1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/236031524/jolenta_helena_of_poland
2 CONT
2 CONT Hungarian princess, nun. She was born around 1244 in Esztergom as the seventh of the ten children of Bela IV and Maria Laskarina. She was raised at her sister's court, who had married Bolesław of Greater Poland. In 1254 she was be
2 CONC trothed to Bolesław of Kalisz. The wedding was celebrated two years later at the Wavel in Kraków. Due to her age (her husband was twenty years older) she remained in Kraków under the care of her sister, but later joined her husban
2 CONC d in Kalisz. Their first daughter was born in 1263. Together with her husband she founded several hospitals, churches, and monasteries. In 1279 Bolesław, returned from an expedition against Brandenburg, already seriously ill, and 
2 CONC died after arriving in Kalisz. Shortly after his death, she joined the Poor Clares and lived with her sister in the Franciscan monastery in Kraków. They later moved to the Franciscan monastery which Kinga had founded in Stary Sącz
2 CONC . Except for a short time during the Tatar invasion, they lived there until Kinga's death in 1292. She then moved to the convent of the Poor Clares in Gniezno where she finished her life twelve years later. She was soon venerated 
2 CONC and was beatified on 26 Sep 1827, but has not yet been canonized like her two sisters.
2 CONT ∼
2 CONT ∼
2 CONT Feast Day – June 11 Yolanda of Poland or Yolanda of Hungary, also Blessed Yolanda (also known as Helen; 1235 – 11 June 1298
2 CONT Blessed Yolanda of Hungary was the daughter of Bela IV, King of Hungary. Her mother, Mary, was the daughter of the Greek emperor of Constantinople. In the year 1240, when Yolanda was scarcely five years old, she arrived at the cou
2 CONC rt of Poland. Her elder sister, Blessed Kinga (Cunigunda), who was married to the duke of Poland, had asked to supervise the child's education. Under such a mistress, Yolanda grew not only in age, but also in virtue and grace befo
2 CONC re God and men.
2 CONT When Yolanda arrived at young womanhood, she was married to Boleslaus, the duke of Greater Poland. But the young duchess was not enamored of the glory and pleasure of this world. It was a greater pleasure for her to do good in her
2 CONC  elevated position. Like a true sovereign, she came to the assistance of the poor and sick, the widows and the orphans. She and her husband built hospitals, convents, and churches, and she was so great an inspiration to him in eve
2 CONC rything that was good and pleasing to God, that he received the surname of the Pious.
2 CONT But Boleslaus was soon to receive the reward of his piety in heaven. After his death and after two of her daughters were married, Yolanda and her third daughter left all the glamor and riches of the world and withdrew to the conve
2 CONC nt of the Poor Clares at Sandec, where, devoted to prayer and mortification, she led a life entirely hidden in Christ. Disturbances resulting from war compelled her after a time to move to the convent at Gniezno, which she herself
2 CONC , assisted by her last consort, had founded.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://hu.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boldog_Jol%C3%A1n
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jolenta_of_Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT Jolenta (Jolanta) of Poland (also known as Blessed Yolande (Yolanda) of Poland and Blessed Helen of Hungary (or of Poland); Polish: Bł. Jolenta) (1235–1298) was the daughter of Béla IV of Hungary and Maria Laskarina. She was the s
2 CONC ister of Saint Margaret of Hungary and Saint Kinga (Cunegunda). One of her father's sisters was the great Franciscan saint, Elizabeth of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a young girl, Jolenta was sent to Poland, to be tutored under the supervision of her sister, Kinga, who was married to the Duke of Poland. There, she was encouraged to marry Boleslaus of Greater Poland, which she did in 1257. T
2 CONC hey had three daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Kalisz (1263 – 28 September 1304). Married Henry V, Duke of Legnica
2 CONT
2 CONT Hedwig of Kalisz (1266 – 10 December 1339). Married Władysław I the Elbow-high, King of Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna of Kalisz (born 1278, date of death unknown). A nun in Gniezno
2 CONT
2 CONT During the time of her marriage, she was noted for her great services to the poor and needy of the country, as well as being a major benefactor of the monasteries, friaries and hospitals connected to them. Her husband gave her so 
2 CONC much support in her charities, that he earned the nickname, "the Pious." She was widowed in 1279.
2 CONT
2 CONT Religious work
2 CONT
2 CONT Following Boleslaus' death, Jolenta and Kinga, along with one of Jolenta's daughters, retired to the Poor Clare monastery Kinga had founded in Sandez. Forced to relocate due to armed conflict in the region, Jolenta founded a new m
2 CONC onastery in Gniezno. She was persuaded to become Abbess of the community shortly before her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Veneration
2 CONT
2 CONT She has been declared as a candidate for sainthood. Her sisters, Kinga and Margaret, have already been canonized.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Beatyfikowana 26.09.1827r.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Jolenta (Jolanta) of Poland (also known as Blessed Yolande (Yolanda) of Poland and Blessed Helen of Hungary (or of Poland); Polish: Bł. Jolenta) (1235–1298) was the daughter of Béla IV of Hungary and Maria Laskarina. She was the s
2 CONC ister of Saint Margaret of Hungary and Saint Kinga (Cunegunda). One of her father's sisters was the great Franciscan saint, Elizabeth of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a young girl, Jolenta was sent to Poland, to be tutored under the supervision of her sister, Kinga, who was married to the Duke of Poland. There, she was encouraged to marry Boleslaus of Greater Poland, which she did in 1257. T
2 CONC hey had three daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Kalisz (1263 – 28 September 1304). Married Henry V, Duke of Legnica Hedwig of Kalisz (1266 – 10 December 1339). Married Władysław I the Elbow-high, King of Poland Anna of Kalisz (born 1278, date of death unknown). A n
2 CONC un in Gniezno
2 CONT
2 CONT During the time of her marriage, she was noted for her great services to the poor and needy of the country, as well as being a major benefactor of the monasteries, friaries and hospitals connected to them. Her husband gave her so 
2 CONC much support in her charities, that he earned the nickname, "the Pious." She was widowed in 1279.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following Boleslaus' death, Jolenta and Kinga, along with one of Jolenta's daughters, retired to the Poor Clare monastery Kinga had founded in Sandez. Forced to relocate due to armed conflict in the region, Jolenta founded a new m
2 CONC onastery in Gniezno. She was persuaded to become Abbess of the community shortly before her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT She has been declared as a candidate for sainthood. Her sisters, Kinga and Margaret, have already been canonized.
