1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andronikos_II_Palaiologos
2 CONT
2 CONT http://finnholbek.dk/getperson.php?personID=I50&tree=2
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos (Greek: Ανδρόνικος Β' Παλαιολόγος) (25 March 1259, Nicaea – February 13, 1332, Constantinople) — also Andronicus II Palaeologus — reigned as Byzantine emperor from 1282 to 1328. He was the eldest survivin
2 CONC g son of Michael VIII Palaiologos and Theodora Doukaina Vatatzina, grandniece of John III Doukas Vatatzes. On 8 November 1273, Andronikos II married Anne of Hungary (1260–1281), daughter of King Stephen V of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos was acclaimed co-emperor in 1261, after his father Michael VIII recovered Constantinople from the Latin Empire, but he was crowned only in 1272. Sole emperor from 1282, Andronikos II immediately repudiate
2 CONC d his father's unpopular Church union with the Papacy (which he had been forced to support while his father was still alive), but was unable to resolve the related schism within the Orthodox clergy until 1310. Andronikos II was al
2 CONC so plagued by economic difficulties and during his reign the value of the Byzantine hyperpyron depreciated precipitously while the state treasury accumulated less than one seventh the revenue (in nominal coins) that it had done pr
2 CONC eviously. Seeking to increase revenue and reduce expenses, Andronikos II raised taxes and reduced tax exemptions, and dismantled the Byzantine fleet (80 ships) in 1285, thereby making the Empire increasingly dependent on the rival
2 CONC  republics of Venice and Genoa. In 1291, he hired 50–60 Genoese ships. Later, in 1320, he tried to resurrect the navy by constructing 20 galleys, but failed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos sought to resolve some of the problems facing the Byzantine Empire through diplomacy. After the death of his first wife Anne of Hungary, he married Yolanda (renamed Eirene) of Montferrat, putting an end t
2 CONC o the Montferrat claim to the Kingdom of Thessalonica. Andronikos II also attempted to marry off his son and co-emperor Michael IX Palaiologos to the Latin Empress Catherine I of Courtenay, thus seeking to eliminate Western agitat
2 CONC ion for a restoration of the Latin Empire. Another marriage alliance attempted to resolve the potential conflict with Serbia in Macedonia, as Andronikos II married off his five-year old daughter Simonis to King Stefan Milutin in 1
2 CONC 298.
2 CONT
2 CONT In spite of the resolution of problems in Europe, Andronikos II was faced with the collapse of the Byzantine frontier in Asia Minor. After the failure of the co-emperor Michael IX to stem the Turkish advance in Asia Minor in 1302 
2 CONC and the disastrous Battle of Bapheus, the Byzantine government hired the Catalan Company of Almogavars (adventurers from Aragon and Catalonia) led by Roger de Flor to clear Byzantine Asia Minor of the enemy. In spite of some succe
2 CONC sses, the Catalans were unable to secure lasting gains. They quarreled with Michael IX, and eventually turned on their Byzantine employers after the murder of Roger de Flor in 1305, devastating Thrace, Macedonia, and Thessaly on t
2 CONC heir road to Latin Greece. There they conquered the Duchy of Athens and Thebes. The Turks continued to penetrate the Byzantine possessions, and Prusa fell in 1326. By the end of Andronikos II's reign, much of Bithynia was in the h
2 CONC ands of the Ottoman Turks of Osman I and his son and heir Orhan. Also, Karesi conquered Mysia region with Paleokastron after 1296, Germiyan conquered Simav in 1328, Saruhan captured Magnesia in 1313 and Aydınoğlu captured Symirna 
2 CONC in 1310.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Empire's problems were exploited by Theodore Svetoslav of Bulgaria, who defeated Michael IX and conquered much of northeastern Thrace in c. 1305–1307. The conflict ended with yet another dynastic marriage, between Michael IX's
2 CONC  daughter Theodora and the Bulgarian emperor. The dissolute behavior of Michael IX's son Andronikos III Palaiologos led to a rift in the family, and after Michael IX's death in 1320, Andronikos II disowned his grandson, prompting 
2 CONC a civil war that raged, with interruptions, until 1328. The conflict precipitated Bulgarian involvement, and Michael Asen III of Bulgaria attempted to capture Andronikos II under the guise of sending him military support. In 1328 
2 CONC Andronikos III entered Constantinople in triumph and Andronikos II was forced to abdicate. He died as a monk in 1332.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1274 Andronikos II married as his first wife Anna of Hungary, a daughter of King Stephen V of Hungary and Elizabeth the Cuman, with whom he had two sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael IX Palaiologos
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine Palaiologos, despotes
2 CONT
2 CONT After Anna died in 1281, in 1284 Andronikos II then married Yolanda (renamed Eirene), a daughter of Marquis William VII of Montferrat, with whom he had:
2 CONT
2 CONT John Palaiologos (c. 1286–1308), despotes, married Eirene Choumnaina, no issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodore I, Marquis of Montferrat (1291–1338)
2 CONT
2 CONT Demetrios Palaiologos (d. after 1343), despotes. Father of Irene Palaiologina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Simonis Palaiologina (1294-after 1336), who married King Stefan Milutin of Serbia
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos II also had at least two illegitimate daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Eirene, who married John II Doukas, ruler of Thessaly
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria, who married Toqta, Khan of the Golden Horde
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos or Andronicus II Palaeologus (Greek: Ανδρόνικος Β' Παλαιολόγος) (25 March 1259, Constantinople – February 13, 1332, Constantinople), reigned as Byzantine emperor 1282–1328. Andronikos II Palaiologos was t
2 CONC he eldest surviving son of Michael VIII Palaiologos and Theodora Doukaina Vatatzina, grandniece of John III Doukas Vatatzes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos was acclaimed co-emperor in 1261, after his father Michael VIII recovered Constantinople from the Latin Empire, but he was crowned only in 1272. Sole emperor from 1282, Andronikos II immediately repudiate
2 CONC d his father's unpopular Church union with the Papacy (which he had been forced to support while his father was still alive), but was unable to resolve the related schism within the Orthodox clergy until 1310. Andronikos II was al
2 CONC so plagued by economic difficulties and during his reign the value of the Byzantine hyperpyron depreciated precipitously while the state treasury accumulated less than one seventh the revenue (in nominal coins) that it had done pr
2 CONC eviously. Seeking to increase revenue and reduce expenses, Andronikos II raised taxes and reduced tax exemptions, and dismantled the Byzantine fleet (80 ships) in 1285, thereby making the Empire increasingly dependent on the rival
2 CONC  republics of Venice and Genoa. In 1291, he hired 50-60 Genoese ships. Later, in 1320, he tried to resurrect the navy by constructing 20 galleys, but unfortunately he failed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos sought to resolve some of the problems facing the Byzantine Empire through diplomacy. After the death of his first wife, he married Yolanda (renamed Eirene) of Montferrat, putting an end to the Montferrat
2 CONC  claim to the Kingdom of Thessalonica. Andronikos II also attempted to marry off his son and co-emperor Michael IX Palaiologos to the Latin Empress Catherine I of Courtenay, thus seeking to eliminate Western agitation for a restor
2 CONC ation of the Latin Empire. Another marriage alliance attempted to resolve the potential conflict with Serbia in Macedonia, as Andronikos II married off his five-year old daughter Simonis to King Stefan Milutin in 1298.
2 CONT
2 CONT In spite of the resolution of problems in Europe, Andronikos II was faced with the collapse of the Byzantine frontier in Asia Minor. After the failure of the co-emperor Michael IX to stem the Turkish advance in Asia Minor in 1300,
2 CONC  the Byzantine government hired the Catalan Company of Almogavars (adventurers from Aragon and Catalonia) led by Roger de Flor to clear Byzantine Asia Minor of the enemy. In spite of some successes, the Catalans were unable to sec
2 CONC ure lasting gains. They quarreled with Michael IX, and eventually turned on their Byzantine employers after the murder of Roger de Flor in 1305, devastating Thrace, Macedonia, and Thessaly on their road to Latin Greece. There they
2 CONC  conquered the Duchy of Athens and Thebes. The Turks continued to penetrate the Byzantine possessions, and Bursa fell in 1326. By the end of Andronikos II's reign, much of Bithynia was in the hands of the Ottoman Turks of Osman I 
2 CONC and his son and heir Orhan.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Empire's problems were exploited by Theodore Svetoslav of Bulgaria, who defeated Michael IX and conquered much of northeastern Thrace in c. 1305-1307. The conflict ended with yet another dynastic marriage, between Michael IX's
2 CONC  daughter Theodora and the Bulgarian emperor. The dissolute behavior of Michael IX's son Andronikos III Palaiologos led to a rift in the family, and after Michael IX's death in 1320, Andronikos II disowned his grandson, prompting 
2 CONC a civil war that raged, with interruptions, until 1328. The conflict precipitated Bulgarian involvement, and Michael Asen III of Bulgaria attempted to capture Andronikos II under the guise of sending him military support. In 1328 
2 CONC Andronikos III entered Constantinople in triumph and Andronikos II was forced to abdicate. He died as a monk in 1332.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Family
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1274 Andronikos II married as his first wife Anna of Hungary, a daughter of King Stephen V of Hungary and Elizabeth the Cuman, with whom he had two sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael IX Palaiologos
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine Palaiologos, despotes
2 CONT
2 CONT After Anna died in 1281, in 1284 Andronikos II then married Yolanda (renamed Eirene), a daughter of Marquis William VII of Montferrat, with whom he had:
2 CONT
2 CONT John Palaiologos (c. 1286-1308), despotes
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodore I, Marquis of Montferrat (1291-1338)
2 CONT
2 CONT Demetrios Palaiologos (d. after 1343), despotes. Father of Irene Palaiologina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Simonis Palaiologina (1294-after 1336), who married King Stefan Milutin of Serbia
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos II also had at least two illegitimate daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Eirene, who married John II Doukas, ruler of Thessaly
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria, who married Toqta, khan of the Golden Horde
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos (Greek: Ανδρόνικος Β' Παλαιολόγος) (25 March 1259, Nicaea – February 13, 1332, Constantinople) — also Andronicus II Palaeologus — reigned as Byzantine emperor from 1282 to 1328. He was the eldest survivin
2 CONC g son of Michael VIII Palaiologos and Theodora Doukaina Vatatzina, grandniece of John III Doukas Vatatzes.
2 CONT
