1 NOTE {geni:about_me} PŘEMYSL OTAKAR of Bohemia, son of WENZEL I King of Bohemia & his wife Kunigunde von Hohenstaufen (1233-killed in battle Dürnkrut 26 Aug 1278, bur Znaim Minoriten).  The Chronica Pragensis (Chronicon Francisci) name
2 CONC s "Wladislaum et Przesemysl" as the two sons of "Rex Wencezslaus"[301].  Associate king of Bohemia 31 Jul 1248-Nov 1249.  Markgraf of Moravia 1251.  He succeeded as OTAKAR Duke of Austria and Steiermark in 1251.  He succeeded his 
2 CONC father in 1253 as OTAKAR PŘEMYSL II King of Bohemia.  Crowned 1261.  His possession of the duchies of Austria and Styria was legalised in 1262 by Richard of Cornwall King of Germany[302].  "Otakarus…rex Boemiæ, dux Austriæ et Stir
2 CONC iæ et marchio Moraviæ" founded Kloster Goldenkron by charter dated 1263[303].  "Otacharus…rex Boemorum dux Austrie et Styrie et marchio Moravie" confirmed the foundation of Kloster Hohenfurt by "Wockonis quondam marschalci regni n
2 CONC ostri" by charter dated 17 Jul 1264[304].  King Richard appointed Otakar as protector of the royal domains east of the Rhine 7 Jan 1267[305].  He succeeded in 1269 as OTAKAR Duke of Carinthia, .  In 1274, King Otakar ignored deman
2 CONC ds for the return to imperial jurisdiction of the duchies of Austria, Carinthia and Styria.  This proposal had been made in line with the policy of Rudolf I King of Germany, promulgated at the Diet of Nürnburg 19 Nov 1274, under w
2 CONC hich all properties unlawfully appropriated since the deposition of Emperor Friedrich II in 1245 were to be returned to the empire.  In accordance with the procedures established, the duchies were deemed forfeited after a year and
2 CONC  a day[306].  King Rudolf declared war on Bohemia, and King Otakar was obliged to abdicate under the temporary peace of 21 Nov 1276, which was confirmed by treaty 6 May 1277.  Rudolf's position was confirmed definitively after he 
2 CONC defeated King Otakar at the battle of Marchfeld near Dürnkrut 26 Aug 1278.  The Notæ Altahenses record that "Otakarus rex Boemie" was killed in battle "1278 VII Kal Sep"[307]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (Hainburg 11 Feb 1252, dispensation 3o and 4o 5 Jul 1253, repudiated 1261) as her second husband, MARGARETA of Austria, widow of HEINRICH VII King of Germany, daughter of LEOPOLD VI Duke of Austria [Babenberg] & his wife
2 CONC  Theodora --- (-Burg Krumau am Kamp 2 Oct 1267, bur Lilienfeld).  The Annales Mellicenses in 1226 record the marriage of "Margaretam filiam Liupoldi ducis Austrie" and "Heinricus filius imperatoris Friderici"[308].  The Notæ Sanct
2 CONC i Emeranni record the marriage in 1225 at Nürnberg of "Heinricus rex" and "Constantiam filiam Liupoldi ducis Austrie"[309].  She was crowned Queen of Germany 28 Mar 1227 at Aachen.  She lived in a Dominican convent at Trier after 
2 CONC the death of her first husband[310].  The Continuatio Garstensis records the second marriage "apud Heimburch" of "Margaretam viduam regis Heinrici, filiam ducis Leupoldi" and "Otakarus marchio Moravie"[311].  The Canonicorum Prage
2 CONC nsium Continuationes Cosmæ records the marriage "in Henburk III Id Feb 1252" of "Prziemysl filius regis Wenceslai" and "Margaretham viduam filiam Leupoldi ducis Austriæ"[312].  The Chronicon Francisci records the marriage in 1252 
2 CONC of "Ottakarus Rex Boemiæ" and "Margaretham, quondam Romanorum Regina"[313].  "Margareta domina regni Boemie ducissa Austrie et Styrie marchionissa Moravie" granted "comitiam in Rakz" to "Wocconi de Rosenberch" by charter dated 126
2 CONC 0[314].  The Altahenses Annales record that "Otaker rex" repudiated his first wife "sine iudicio ecclesie"[315].  The Canonicorum Pragensium Continuationes Cosmæ records that "regina Margareta" left Bohemia for Austria "XV Kal Nov
2 CONC  1261"[316].  The necrology of Lilienfeld records the death "IV Kal Nov" of "Margareta quondam regina Romanorum filia ducis Leupoldi" and her burial next to her father "in Campo Liliorum"[317].  The necrology of Kloster Neuburg re
2 CONC cords the death "IV Kal Nov" of "Margareta filia ducis Liupoldi regina Romanorum ducissa Austrie et Stirie"[318]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly (Pressburg 25 Oct 1261) as her first husband, KUNIGUNDA [Kunguta] Rostislavna, daughter of ROSTISLAV Mikhailovich ex-Grand Prince of Kiev, ex-Prince of Galich, Ban of Mačva & his wife Anna of Hungary (1245-9 Sep 1285, b
2 CONC ur Prague Erlöserkirche).  The Canonicorum Pragensium Continuationes Cosmæ records the marriage "VIII Kal Nov 1261" of "Cunegundem filiam Hostislai ducis Bulgarorum" and the king "in castello Ungarias…Possen"[319].  The Chronica P
2 CONC ragensis (Chronicon Francisci) records the second marriage of "Rex Przsemysl" and "Chungundam, Regis de Mazouia filiam"[320].  The Altahenses Annales record the marriage in 1261 of "Otaker rex" and "Chunigundem filiam Regis Ruscie
2 CONC , neptem Bele regis Ungarie ex filia"[321].  She married secondly (Prague 2 Jun 1284) as his second wife, Zaviš von Rosenberg zu Skalitz und Falkenstein (-beheaded Schloß Frauenberg 24 Aug 1290, bur Hohenfurt).  The Chronica Prage
2 CONC nsis (Chronicon Francisci) records the second marriage of "rex Wencezslaus…mater…eius" and "quondam Baronem…Zauissium" by whom she had a son "in Moravia…Iohannes" who joined "ordine Cruciferorum Christo"[322].  The Cronica Aulæ Re
2 CONC gine records the capture and death of “Zawissium...castrum...Vroburg”[323]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (1): --- Palczierzik, daughter of ---.  The Chronica Pragensis (Chronicon Francisci) names "filium…Nicolaum, et postea filias plures" as the children of "Rex Przsemysl" and his mistress named "Palczierzik"[324].  Pulkawa’
2 CONC s Bohemian Chronicle records (in 1252) “filiam de Kunring de Austria” as mistress of “Przemysl filius regis Wenceslai” and mother of “filium...Nicolauum...postea...ducem Opaviensem” and of “postea tres filias” who married “unam...
2 CONC domino de Strakonicz, secundam de Wartemberg et de Druholz, tertiam...Vockoni de Krawau”[325].  Friess suggests that Pulkawa’s identification of Otakar’s mistress results from confusion with another source stating that the king’s 
2 CONC daughter married “Heinrich V von Kuenring-Weitra” (see below)[326]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (2): ---.  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not been identified. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Otakar Přemysl II & his second wife had five children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         HEINRICH (1262-1263).  The Cronica Aulæ Regine records that “rex Ottacarus” and his second wife had “tres filios”, of whom “duobus mox in infantia defunctis”, while “iunior” succeeded their father[327].  The primary sou
2 CONC rce which confirms his name has not yet been identified. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         son (-young).  The Cronica Aulæ Regine records that “rex Ottacarus” and his second wife had “tres filios”, of whom “duobus mox in infantia defunctis”, while “iunior” succeeded their father[328]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.         KUNIGUNDE (Jan 1265-27 Nov 1321, bur Prague St Veit).  The Chronica Pragensis (Chronicon Francisci) records the baptism of "Rex Przsemysl…filiæ suæ primogenitæ" in 1265[329].  The Chronica Pragensis (Chronicon Francisci
2 CONC ) records that "filia Regis…Chungundis" became a nun "ad sanctum Franciscum" in 1276, but that she was later taken from the monastery by her brother and married to "Duci Mazouiæ", after whose death she became "Abbatissa ad sanctum
2 CONC  Georgium in castro Pragensis"[330].  The primary source which confirms her first betrothal has not yet been identified.  The Chronica Pragensis (Chronicon Francisci) records the death in 1321 of "Regis Przemysl filia, Abbatissa a
2 CONC d sanctum Georgium in castro Pragensi…Chunegundis" and her burial in the monastery[331].  Betrothed ([1276]) to HARTMANN von Habsburg Graf von Kiburg, son of RUDOLF I Graf von Habsburg King of Germany & his first wife Gertrud [Ann
2 CONC a] von Hohenberg [Zollern] (Rheinfelden 1263-drowned between Breisach and Strasbourg 21 Dec 1281, bur Basel Münster).  m (1291, divorced before 19 Dec 1302) as his second wife, BOLESŁAW II Prince of Plock, son of SIEMOWIT I Prince
2 CONC  of Mazovia [Piast] & his wife Pereiaslava Daniilovich of Galich (after 1251-20 Apr 1313).  He succeeded his brother in 1294 as BOLESŁAW II Prince of Mazovia.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.         AGNES (5 Sep 1269-Prague 17 May 1296, bur Prague, Clarissan convent).  The Canonicorum Pragensium Continuationes Cosmæ records the birth "Non Sep 1269" of "regi Otakaro filia", but does not name her[332].  The Chronica 
2 CONC Pragensis (Chronicon Francisci) records the betrothal in 1278 of "Wencezslaus filius regis Boemiæ" and "filiam Domini Rudolphi Electi" and of "filius Electi filiam Regis Boemiæ"[333].  The Dytheri de Helmestat Notæ Historicæ recor
2 CONC d that "rex Rudolfus…filium suum dominum Hartmudum [error for Rudolfum]" married "sororem regis Bohemie"[334].  After her husband died, she lived at Schloß Brugg im Aargau until 1295, thereafter becoming a nun at the Clarissan con
2 CONC vent of Prague.  The Chronicon Francisci records the death "XVI Kal Jun" in 1296 of "Domina Agnes soror Regis Boemiæ, Ducissa Austriæ, relicta Ducis Rudolphi Romanorum Regis filii" and her burial "in Ecclesia sanctæ Claræ ordinis 
2 CONC fratrum minorem apud sanctum Franciscum in Praga"[335].  m (contract Iglau 1278, Mar 1289) RUDOLF II von Habsburg ex-Duke of Austria, son of RUDOLF I Graf von Habsburg King of Germany & his first wife Gertrud [Anna] von Hohenberg 
2 CONC (1270-Prague 10 May 1290, bur Prague Burg, transferred 1293 to St Veit’s Cathedral). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.         WENZEL (17 Sep 1271-21 Jun 1305, bur Prague Königsaal).  The Chronicon Francisci records the birth "in die beatorum Martyrum Cozmæ et Damiani" in 1271 of "Wencezslaus…Rex Boemiæ"[336].  He succeeded in 1283 as WENZEL II
2 CONC  King of Bohemia.  Imperial Elector 1289.  Crowned King of Bohemia 2 Jun 1297.  King of Poland 1300. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -        see below. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Otakar Přemysl II had [seven] illegitimate children by Mistress (1):
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.          NIKOLAUS ([1255]-25 Jul 1318).  The Chronica Pragensis (Chronicon Francisci) names "filium…Nicolaum, et postea filias plures" as the children of "Rex Przsemysl" and his mistress named "Palczierzik", adding that he was 
2 CONC later invested as "Dux Opauiæ"[337].  Pulkawa’s Bohemian Chronicle records (in 1252) “filiam de Kunring de Austria” as mistress of “Przemysl filius regis Wenceslai” and mother of “filium...Nicolauum...postea...ducem Opaviensem” an
2 CONC d of “postea tres filias” who married “unam...domino de Strakonicz, secundam de Wartemberg et de Druholz, tertiam...Vockoni de Krawau”[338].  He was legitimated 6 Oct 1360, but excluded from succession to the throne of Bohemia 20 
2 CONC Oct 1260.  Herr von Troppau 4 Jul 1269.  Ritter 1273.  He was installed in 1281 as NIKOLAUS I Duke of Troppau. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -           DUKES of TROPPAU. 
2 CONT
