2 CONT Izyaslav, Pr of Kursk (1125-29), Pr of Polotsk (1129-32), Pr of Turov (1132-34), Pr of Volynia (1135-42), Pr of Pereyaslav (1142-46), Great Pr of Kiev (1146-49)+(1150)+(1151-54), *Novgorod 1096, +Kiev 13.11.1154; 1m: apparently a 
2 CONC Staufen princess (+1151); 2m: 1154 N (Rusudan?) of Georgia; all children from 1m.:
2 CONT *A1. Mstislav II, Pr of Pereyaslav (1146-49)+(1151-55), Pr of Lutzk (1155-57), Pr of Volynya (1157-70), Great Pr of Kiev (V.1167-XII.1169)+(II.1170-IV.1170), +19.8.1170; m.1151/52 Agnieszka of Poland (*1137 +after 1181)
2 CONT **B1. Roman "the Great", Pr of Novgorod (1168-70), Pr of Volynia (1173-87)+(1188-1205), Pr of Galicia (1187-88)+(1199-1205), Grand Pr of Kiev (1203-05), *ca 1205, +murdered nr Zavikhvost 19.6.1205; 1m: bef 1184 (div 1198) Predisla
2 CONC va Ryurikovna of Smolensk (+after 1204); 2m: ca 1200 a Byzantine noblewoman (+after 1219)
2 CONT *** ...
2 CONT ***C3. Danylo, Pr of Galicia (1211-13)+(1229-64), Pr of Volynia (1215-64), crowned by a papal archbishop in Dorohychyn 1253/55 as 1st King of Galicia, *1201, +1264; 1m: 1218 Anna Mstislavna of Novgorod (+bef 1252); 2m: bef 1252 N,
2 CONC  niece of Duke Mindaugas of Lithuania; all kids by 1m.
2 CONT ****D1. Irakli, *ca 1223, +by 1240
2 CONT ****D2. '''Lev, Duke of Belz (1245-64), Duke of Halicz (1264-69), King of Galicia (1269-1301), he moved his capital from Galich/Halicz to the newly-founded city of Lvov/Lwow (Lemberg), *ca 1228, +ca 1301'''; m.1251/52 Konstancia o
2 CONC f Hungary (*ca 1237, +bef 1300)
2 CONT *****E1. Yuriy I, King of Galicia (1301-08), *24.4.1252, +24.4.1308; 1m: 1282 N Yaroslavna of Tver (+ca 1286); 2m: before 1300 Eufemia of Kujavia (+18.3.1308)
2 CONT ****** ...
2 CONT *****E2. Anastasia, +12.3.1335; m.before 1301 Pr Ziemowit of Poland (+1309/14)
2 CONT *****E3. Svyatoslava, a nun, +1302
2 CONT ****D3. Roman, Duke of Slonim and Novogrudok (1255-60), *ca 1230, +ca 1261; 1m: 27.6.1252 (div 1253) Gertrud of Austria (*ca 1288, +24.4.1299); 2m: ca 1255 Elena Glebovna of Volkovysk (+aft 1288)
2 CONT ***** ...
2 CONT ****D4. Mstislav, Duke of Lutsk (1265-89), Duke of Volynia (1289-aft 1300), +aft 1300; m.ca 1253 N, a dau.of khan Teigak of the Cumans
2 CONT ***** ...
2 CONT ****D5. Ioann Shvarn "the Lightning", Duke of Chelm (1264-69), Great Duke of Lithuania (1268-69), +1269, bur Chelm; m.1255 N, a dau.of Duke Mindaugas of Lithuania
2 CONT ****D6. Pereyaslava, +12.4.1283; m.ca 1248 Pr Ziemowit of Masovia (*1224, +23.6.1262)
2 CONT ****D7. Ustinia; m.1250/51 Pr Andrei II of Vladimir-Suzdal (+1264)
2 CONT ****D8. Sofia; m.1259 Gf Heinrich V von Schwarzburg-Blankenburg (+1287)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lev_I_of_Galicia
2 CONT
2 CONT Alt birth dates: 1269,c. 1228, 1228
2 CONT
2 CONT Lev He moved his father's capital from Halych to the newly-founded city of Lvov (Lviv in modern Ukraine). This city was named after him by its founder, Lev's father King Daniel. In 1247 Lev married Constance, daughter of Béla IV o
2 CONC f Hungary. Unlike his father, who pursued a Western political course, Lev worked closely with the Mongols and together with them invaded Poland. However, although his troops plundered territory as far west as Racibórz, sending man
2 CONC y captives and much booty back to Galicia, Lev did not ultimately gain much territory from Poland. Lev cultivated a particularly close alliance with the Tatar khan Nogai. Lev also attempted, unsuccessfully, to establish his family
2 CONC 's rule over Lithuania. Soon after his brother Shvarno ascended to the Lithuanian throne in 1267, Lev organized the murder of Grand Duke of Lithuania Vaišvilkas. Following his brother Shvarno's loss of the throne in 1269, Lev ente
2 CONC red into conflict with the Lithuania. From 1274-1276 he fought a war with the new Lithuanian ruler Traidenis but was defeated, and Lithuania annexed the territory of Black Ruthenia with its city of Navahrudak. In 1279, Lev allied 
2 CONC himself with king Wenceslaus II of Bohemia and invaded Poland, although his attempt to capture Kraków in 1280 ended in failure. That same year, however, Lev defeated Hungary and annexed part of Transcarpathia, including the city o
2 CONC f Mukachevo. In 1292 he defeated Poland and added Lublin with surrounding areas to the territory of Galicia-Volhynia. At the time of Lev's death in 1301 the state of Galicia-Volhynia was at the height of its power.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Leo I of Galicia (Ukrainian: Лев Дани́лович, Lev Danylovych) (ca. 1228 – ca. 1301) became in turn Knyaz of Belz (1245–1264), Knyaz of Peremyshl, Knyaz of Halych (1264–1269) and Grand Prince of Kiev (1271–1301).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a son of King Daniel of Galicia and his first wife, Anna Mstislavna Smolenskaya (daughter of Mstislav Mstislavich the Bold). As his father, Lev was a member of the senior branch of Vladimir II Monomakh descendents. He was a
2 CONC  first cousin of Alexander Nevsky, as their mothers were sisters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT
2 CONT Lev moved his father's capital from Halych to the newly founded city of Lviv. This city was named after him by its founder, Lev's father, King Daniel of Galicia. In 1247 Lev married Constance, daughter of Béla IV of Hungary. Unlik
2 CONC e his father, who pursued a Western political course, Lev worked closely with the Mongols and together with them invaded Poland. However, although his troops plundered territory as far west as Racibórz in Silesia, sending many cap
2 CONC tives and much booty back to Galicia, Lev did not ultimately gain much territory from Poland. Lev cultivated a particularly close alliance with the Tatar Nogai Khan. He also attempted, unsuccessfully, to establish his family's rul
2 CONC e over Lithuania. Soon after his younger brother Shvarn ascended to the Lithuanian throne in 1267, Lev organized the murder of Grand Duke of Lithuania Vaišvilkas. Following Shvarn's loss of the throne in 1269, Lev entered into con
2 CONC flict with Lithuania. In 1274–1276 he fought a war with the new Lithuanian ruler Traidenis but was defeated, and Lithuania annexed the territory of Black Ruthenia with its city of Navahrudak.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1279, Lev allied himself with King Wenceslaus II of Bohemia and invaded Poland, although his attempt to capture Kraków in 1280 ended in failure. That same year, however, Lev defeated the Kingdom of Hungary and temporarily annex
2 CONC ed part of Transcarpathia, including the town of Mukachevo. In 1292, he defeated Poland and added Lublin with surrounding areas to the territory of Galicia-Volhynia. At the time of Lev's death in 1301, the state of Galicia-Volhyni
2 CONC a was at the height of its power. Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT Lev I married Constance of Hungary, daughter of Béla IV of Hungary and Maria Laskarina. They had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Yuri I of Galicia (24 April 1252/1257 – 18 March 1308).
2 CONT     Svyatoslava Lvovna of Halych (d. 1302), a nun
2 CONT     Anastasia Lvovna of Galicia (d. 12 March 1335), who married Siemowit of Dobrzyń.
