1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ladislaus_IV_of_Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Ladislaus IV (Hungarian: IV. (Kun) László, Croatian: Ladislav IV. (Kumanac), Slovak: Ladislav IV. (Kumánsky); 5 August 1262 – 10 July 1290), also known as Ladislaus the Cuman, was King of Hungary and Croatia from 1272 to 1290. His
2 CONC  mother, Elizabeth, was the daughter of a chieftain from the pagan Cumans who had settled in Hungary. At the age of seven, he married Elisabeth (or Isabella), a daughter of King Charles I of Sicily. Ladislaus was only 9 when a reb
2 CONC ellious lord, Joachim Gutkeled, kidnapped and imprisoned him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ladislaus IV
2 CONT
2 CONT Ladislaus the Cuman's seal
2 CONT King of Hungary and Croatia
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT 6 August 1272 – 10 July 1290
2 CONT Coronation
2 CONT August 1272, Székesfehérvár
2 CONT Predecessor
2 CONT Stephen V
2 CONT Successor
2 CONT Andrew III
2 CONT Born
2 CONT 5 August 1262
2 CONT Died
2 CONT 10 July 1290 (aged 27)
2 CONT Körösszeg (Cheresig), Kingdom of Hungary
2 CONT Burial
2 CONT Cathedral of Csanád (Cenad, Romania)
2 CONT Spouse
2 CONT Elizabeth of Sicily ​(m. 1270)​
2 CONT Dynasty
2 CONT Árpád
2 CONT Father
2 CONT Stephen V of Hungary
2 CONT Mother
2 CONT Elizabeth the Cuman
2 CONT Religion
2 CONT Roman Catholic
2 CONT Ladislaus was still a prisoner when his father Stephen V died on 6 August 1272. During his minority, many groupings of barons – primarily the Abas, Csáks, Kőszegis, and Gutkeleds – fought against each other for supreme power. Ladi
2 CONC slaus was declared to be of age at an assembly of the prelates, barons, noblemen, and Cumans in 1277. He allied himself with Rudolf I of Germany against Ottokar II of Bohemia. His forces had a preeminent role in Rudolf's victory o
2 CONC ver Ottokar in the Battle on the Marchfeld on 26 August 1278.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Ladislaus could not restore royal power in Hungary. A papal legate, Philip, bishop of Fermo, came to Hungary to help Ladislaus consolidate his authority, but the prelate was shocked at the presence of thousands of pagan C
2 CONC umans in Hungary. Ladislaus promised that he would force them to adopt a Christian lifestyle, but they refused to obey the legate's demands. Ladislaus decided to support the Cumans, for which Philip of Fermo excommunicated him. Th
2 CONC e Cumans imprisoned the legate, and the legate's partisans captured Ladislaus. In early 1280, Ladislaus agreed to persuade the Cumans to submit to the legate, but many Cumans preferred to leave Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ladislaus vanquished a Cuman army that invaded Hungary in 1282. Hungary also survived a Mongol invasion in 1285. Ladislaus had, by that time, become so unpopular that many of his subjects accused him of inciting the Mongols to inv
2 CONC ade Hungary. After he imprisoned his wife in 1286, he lived with his Cuman mistresses. During the last years of his life, he wandered throughout the country with his Cuman allies, but he was unable to control the most powerful lor
2 CONC ds and bishops any more. Pope Nicholas IV planned to declare a crusade against him, but three Cuman assassins murdered Ladislaus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT Childhood (1262–1272)
2 CONT edit
2 CONT Ladislaus was the elder son of Stephen V, son of Béla IV of Hungary, and Stephen's wife Elizabeth the Cuman.[1][2] Elizabeth was the daughter of a chieftain of the Cumans who had settled in Hungary.[3] She was born as a pagan and 
2 CONC was baptized before her marriage to Stephen.[4] Ladislaus was born under the sign of Mars in 1262, according to Simon of Kéza, who was his chaplain in the 1270s.[5][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Conflicts between Ladislaus's father and grandfather developed into a civil war in 1264.[5][7] Béla IV's troops, which were under the command of Ladislaus's aunt, Anna, captured the castle of Sárospatak, where Ladislaus and his mo
2 CONC ther were staying, and imprisoned them.[5][8] Ladislaus initially was kept in the Turóc Castle by Andrew Hont-Pázmány, but two months later, he was sent to the court of Boleslaw the Chaste, Duke of Cracow, who was Béla IV's son-in
2 CONC -law.[9] After his grandfather and father made peace in March 1265, Ladislaus was set free and returned to his father.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ladislaus's father made an alliance with King Charles I of Sicily in September 1269.[11][12] According to the treaty, Charles I's daughter, Elizabeth, who was about four years old at that time, was engaged to the seven-year-old La
2 CONC dislaus.[5][12] The children's marriage took place in 1270.[5][12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Béla IV died on 3 May 1270, and Ladislaus's father was crowned king two weeks later;[12][13] the new monarch, however, could not stabilize his rule.[14] Béla IV's closest advisors – Duchess Anna, and Béla IV's former palatine, Hen
2 CONC ry Kőszegi – left Hungary and sought assistance from Anna's son-in-law, King Ottokar II of Bohemia.[12][15] The newly appointed ban of Slavonia, Joachim Gutkeled, also turned against Stephen V and kidnapped Ladislaus in the summer
2 CONC  of 1272.[16] Gutkeled held Ladislaus in captivity in the fortress of Koprivnica in Slavonia.[12] Historian Pál Engel suggests that Joachim Gutkeled planned to force Stephen V to divide Hungary with Ladislaus.[12] Stephen V besieg
2 CONC ed Koprivnica, but could not take it.[12] Stephen fell seriously ill and died on 6 August.[13][12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT edit
2 CONT Minority (1272–1277)
2 CONT edit
2 CONT
2 CONT King Ladislaus IV as depicted in the Chronica Hungarorum
2 CONT Joachim Gutkeled departed for Székesfehérvár as soon as he was informed of Stephen V's death, because he wanted to arrange the boy–king's coronation.[17][18] Ladislaus's mother joined him, infuriating Stephen V's partisans who acc
2 CONC used her of having conspired against her husband.[17][18] Stephen V's master of the treasury, Egidius Monoszló, laid siege to her palace in Székesfehérvár, but Gutkeled's supporters routed him.[17] Monoszló fled to Pressburg (now 
2 CONC Bratislava, Slovakia); he captured the town and ceded it to Ottokar II of Bohemia.[17]
2 CONT
2 CONT A seal depicting a crowned woman who sits on a throne
2 CONT Seal of Ladislaus's mother, Elizabeth the Cuman
2 CONT Archbishop Philip of Esztergom crowned Ladislaus king in Székesfehérvár on about 3 September.[13][17] In theory, the 10-year-old Ladislaus ruled under his mother's regency, but in fact, baronial parties administered the kingdom.[1
2 CONC 9][20] In November of that year, Henry Kőszegi returned from Bohemia and assassinated Ladislaus's cousin, Béla of Macsó.[21][22] Duke Béla's extensive domains, which were located along the southern borders, were divided among Henr
2 CONC y Kőszegi and his supporters.[17][21] In retaliation for Hungarian incursions into Austria and Moravia, Austrian and Moravian troops invaded the borderlands of Hungary in April 1273.[23] They captured Győr and Szombathely, plunder
2 CONC ing the western counties.[23] Joachim Gutkeled recaptured the two forts two months later, but Ottokar II of Bohemia invaded Hungary and seized many fortresses, including Győr and Sopron in the autumn.[24][25]
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Csák and his allies removed Joachim Gutkeled and Henry Kőszegi from power, but Gutkeled and Kőszegi seized Ladislaus and his mother in June 1274.[26][27] Although Peter Csák liberated the king and his mother, Gutkeled and Kő
2 CONC szegi captured Ladislaus's younger brother, Andrew, and took him to Slavonia.[21][26][27] They demanded Slavonia in Duke Andrew's name, but Peter Csák defeated their united forces near Polgárdi at the end of September.[21][26] Kős
2 CONC zegi was killed in the battle.[26] Peter Csák then launched a campaign against Kőszegi's son and Ladislaus accompanied him.[26] At the end of 1274, Rudolf I, the new king of Germany, and Ladislaus concluded an alliance against Ott
2 CONC okar II of Bohemia.[26][28]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ladislaus contracted an unidentified serious illness, but recovered from it.[29] He attributed this recovery to a miracle by his deceased saintly aunt, Margaret, and approached the Holy See to promote her canonization in 1275.[29]
2 CONC  In the same year, a new civil war broke out between Joachim Gutkeled and Peter Csák.[30] Ladislaus took part in Csák's military expedition against the Kőszegis, who were Gutkeled's supporters.[30] However, Gutkeled and his suppor
2 CONC ters removed their opponents from power at an assembly of the barons and noblemen at Buda around 21 June 1276.[30]
2 CONT
2 CONT Taking advantage of the war between Rudolf I and Ottokar II, Ladislaus made an incursion into Austria in the autumn.[31] Sopron soon accepted Ladislaus's suzerainty and Ottokar II promised to renounce of all towns he occupied in w
2 CONC estern Hungary.[31] However, new armed conflicts began in Hungary during 1277: the Transylvanian Saxons captured and destroyed Gyulafehérvár (now Alba Iulia in Romania), the see of the bishop of Transylvania, and the Babonići rose
2 CONC  up in rebellion in Slavonia.[32]
2 CONT
2 CONT First years of majority (1277–1278)
2 CONT edit
2 CONT Battle on the Marchfeld
2 CONT The Battle on the Marchfeld (26 August 1278)
2 CONT Joachim Gutkeled died while battling against the Babonići in April 1277.[32][31] A month later, an assembly of the prelates, barons, noblemen, and Cumans declared Ladislaus to be of age.[21][19] The estates of the realm also autho
2 CONC rized the 15-year-old monarch to restore internal peace with all possible means.[21] Ladislaus then invaded the Kőszegis's domains in Transdanubia, but could not defeat them.[33] He met Rudolf I of Germany in Hainburg an der Donau
2 CONC  on 11 November to confirm their alliance against Ottokar II of Bohemia.[33]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the royal army captured the rebellious Nicholas Geregye's fortress at Adorján (now Adrian in Romania), Ladislaus held a "general assembly" for seven counties along the River Tisza in early summer of 1278.[33] The assembly co
2 CONC ndemned two rebellious local noblemen to death.[33] In Transdanubia, Ivan Kőszegi attempted to play off Ladislaus's father's first cousin, Andrew the Venetian, against Ladislaus.[33] Andrew demanded Slavonia for himself, but retur
2 CONC ned to Venice without success.[33]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ladislaus joined forces with Rudolf I of Germany to launch a campaign against Ottokar II.[21] Ladislaus's troops played a decisive role in Rudolf's victory in the Battle on the Marchfeld on 26 August.[21][34] Ottokar was killed in
2 CONC  the battlefield.[34] After the battle, King Rudolf I gave Ladislaus "his thanks, declaring that through his help all Austria and Styria had been restored to him",[35] according to Ladislaus's chronicler, Simon of Kéza.[36]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Cuman question (1278–1285)
2 CONT edit
2 CONT Ladislaus in Cuman apparel
2 CONT Ladislaus depicted in apparel favored by the Cumans (from the Illuminated Chronicle)
2 CONT Pope Nicholas III sent Philip, bishop of Fermo, to Hungary to help Ladislaus restore royal power on 22 September 1278.[37][38] The papal legate arrived in Hungary in early 1279.[37][39] With the legate's mediation, Ladislaus concl
2 CONC uded a peace treaty with the Kőszegis.[37] Bishop Philip soon realized, however, that most Cumans were still pagans in Hungary.[39][38] He extracted a ceremonious promise from the Cuman chieftains of giving up their pagan customs,
2 CONC  and persuaded the young King Ladislaus to swear an oath to enforce the keeping of the Cuman chieftains' promise.[37] An assembly held at Tétény passed laws which, in accordance with the legate's demand, prescribed that the Cumans
2 CONC  should leave their tents and live "in houses attached to the ground".[39] The Cumans did not obey the laws, however, and Ladislaus, himself a half-Cuman, failed to force them.[39][40] In retaliation, Bishop Philip excommunicated 
2 CONC him and placed Hungary under interdict in October.[41] Ladislaus joined the Cumans and appealed to the Holy See, but the Pope refused to absolve him.[41]
2 CONT
2 CONT On Ladislaus's demand, the Cumans seized and imprisoned Philip of Fermo in early January 1280.[41][40] However, Finta Aba, voivode of Transylvania captured Ladislaus and handed him over to Roland Borsa.[41][40] In less than two mo
2 CONC nths, both the legate and the king were set free and Ladislaus took a new oath to enforce the Cuman laws.[42] However, many Cumans decided to leave Hungary instead of obeying the legate's demands.[43] Ladislaus followed the moving
2 CONC  Cumans as far as Szalánkemén (now Stari Slankamen in Serbia), but could not hinder them from crossing the frontier.[44][45]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ladislaus launched a campaign against Finta Aba and seized his castles in the summer of 1281.[44] According to the Austrian Rhymed Chronicle, Bishop Philip of Fermo left Hungary around the same time, stating that he would never co
2 CONC me back, "not for the sake of the Holy Father".[46] A Cuman army invaded the southern parts of Hungary in 1282.[47] The Illuminated Chronicle writes that Ladislaus, "like the brave Joshua, went out against" the Cumans "to fight fo
2 CONC r his people and his realm."[47][48] He vanquished the invaders's army at Lake Hód, near Hódmezővásárhely, in the autumn of 1282.[39]
2 CONT
2 CONT At the end of 1282, Ladislaus laid siege to Borostyánkő (now Bernstein im Burgenland in Austria), which was held by the Kőszegis.[47][49] The Kőszegis resisted, forcing the king to lift the siege in early 1283.[49] Ladislaus even 
2 CONC reconciled with Ivan Kőszegi and appointed him palatine before 6 July.[49] Ladislaus abandoned his wife, Isabella, and settled among the Cumans by the end of the year.[39]
2 CONT
2 CONT Last years (1285–1290)
2 CONT edit
2 CONT The Mongols of the Golden Horde invaded Hungary under the command of Khans Talabuga and Nogai in January 1285.[50] According to the Illuminated Chronicle, they "spread a terrible devastation of fire throughout the whole country" t
2 CONC o the east of the Danube.[51] Local forces resisted the invaders at many places, including, for example, at Regéc.[49] The invasion lasted for two months before the Mongols withdrew.[51]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ladislaus's favoritism towards the Cumans made him so unpopular that many of his subjects accused him of inciting the Mongols to invade Hungary.[39] In fact, Ladislaus employed Mongol prisoners of war, known as nyögérs, when he su
2 CONC bjugated a rebellion in the Szepesség in September 1285.[52] The king preferred the Cumans' way of life, including their costumes and hairstyle, and took Cuman girls as his mistresses.[39][53] According to Lodomer, archbishop of E
2 CONC sztergom, Ladislaus copulated with his favorite concubine, Aydua, whom the archbishop described as a "poisonous viper", in public.[53]
2 CONT
2 CONT Assassination of Ladislaus
2 CONT Cuman assassins murder Ladislaus in Körösszeg (Cheresig, Romania) on 10 July 1290
2 CONT In September 1286, Ladislaus imprisoned his wife and granted all her revenues to his mistress.[53][52] Archbishop Lodomer liberated the queen the following September.[52] The archbishop summoned the prelates, the barons, and the n
2 CONC oblemen to an assembly in Buda and excommunicated Ladislaus.[52] In response, the infuriated king stated that "beginning with the archbishop of Esztergom and his suffragans, I shall exterminate the whole lot right up to Rome with 
2 CONC the aid of Tartar swords", according to Archbishop Lodomer.[39][54]
2 CONT
2 CONT The barons captured Ladislaus in the Szepesség in January 1288.[55] Although his partisans soon liberated him, he acquiesced in concluding an agreement with Archbishop Lodomer.[55] The archbishop absolved Ladislaus on condition th
2 CONC at the king would live in accordance with Christian morals.[55] However, Ladislaus broke his promise.[55] He abducted his sister, Elizabeth, prioress of the Dominican Monastery of the Blessed Virgin on Rabbits' Island, and gave he
2 CONC r in marriage to a Czech aristocrat, Záviš of Falkenstein.[56] According to Archbishop Lodomer, Ladislaus even stated, "If I had 15 or more sisters in as many cloistered communities as you like, I would snatch them from there to m
2 CONC arry them off licitly or illicitly; in order to procure through them a kin-group who will support me by all their power in the fulfillment of my will".[54]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ladislaus spent the last years of his life wandering from place to place.[57] Hungary's central government lost power because the prelates and the barons ruled the kingdom independently of the monarch.[39][58] For example, Ivan Kő
2 CONC szegi and his brothers waged wars against Albert I, Duke of Austria, but Ladislaus did not intervene, although the Austrians captured at least 30 fortresses along the western borders.[59][60]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Kőszegis offered the crown to Andrew the Venetian, who arrived in Hungary in early 1290.[61] One of their opponents, Arnold Hahót, captured the pretender, however, and surrendered him to Duke Albert.[61][62] Ladislaus appointe
2 CONC d Mizse, who had recently converted from Islam to Christianity, palatine.[61][63] Pope Nicholas IV was even planning to proclaim a crusade against Ladislaus.[64][65] However, Ladislaus, who had always been partial towards his Cuma
2 CONC n subjects, was assassinated by three Cumans, named Arbuz,[66] Törtel, and Kemence, at the castle of Körösszeg (now Cheresig in Romania) on 10 July 1290.[39][60] Mizse and the Cuman Nicholas, who was the brother of Ladislaus's Cum
2 CONC an lover, took vengeance for Ladislaus's death, slaughtering the murderers.[60]
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon Pope Nicholas IV's orders, an inquiry was carried out to find out "whether the king died as a Catholic Christian".[67] The results of the investigation are unknown, but the Chronicon Budense writes that Ladislaus was buried i
2 CONC n the cathedral of Csanád (now Cenad in Romania).[67][60] His successor, Andrew the Venetian, and Pope Boniface VIII recalled Ladislaus as "of renowned memory".[67]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.britannica.com/biography/Ladislas-IV
