1 OCCU Sebastokrator: "Venerable Ruler"
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Isaac Komnenos or Comnenus (Greek: Ισαάκιος Κομνηνός, Isaakios Komnēnos), (c. 1113 – after 1154), was the third son of Emperor John II Komnenos by Piroska of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly before his death in 1143, John II Komnenos designated his fourth son Manuel as his heir, although the third son, Isaac, was still alive. At the time Isaac was conducting the body of his eldest brother, the co-emperor Alexi
2 CONC os Komnenos, back to Constantinople.
2 CONT
2 CONT Consequently Manuel made sure that his men took control of the capital before Isaac learned of his father's death and made his bid for the throne. Although some of the clergy, the people and the military thought that Isaac was bet
2 CONC ter fit to rule, he had to resign himself to his younger brother's accession.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1145–1146 he campaigned with him against the Seljuk Turks in Anatolia. Although the relationship between the brothers remained uneasy, there was never an open conflict, and Isaac enjoyed the court dignity of sebastokratōr. The 
2 CONC marriages of Isaac's daughters served as useful tools of Manuel's foreign policy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT By his first wife, Theodora, Isaac had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexios Komnenos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Irene Komnene, who married an unnamed Doukas Kamateros and became the mother of Isaac Komnenos of Cyprus.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Komnenos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Komnene, who married Constantine Makrodoukas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Komnene, who married King Stephen IV of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his second wife, Irene Synadene, Isaac had two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodora Komnene, who married King Baldwin III of Jerusalem.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudokia Komnene, who married William VIII of Montpellier.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isaac_Komnenos_(d._1154)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac Komnenos (d. 1154)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac Komnenos or Comnenus (Greek: Ισαάκιος Κομνηνός, Isaakios Komnēnos), (c. 1113 – after 1154), was the third son of Emperor John II Komnenos by Piroska of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly before his death in 1143, John II Komnenos designated his fourth son Manuel as his heir, although the third son, Isaac, was still alive. At the time Isaac was conducting the body of his eldest brother, the co-emperor Alexi
2 CONC os Komnenos, back to Constantinople.
2 CONT
2 CONT Consequently Manuel made sure that his men took control of the capital before Isaac learned of his father's death and made his bid for the throne. Although some of the clergy, the people and the military thought that Isaac was bet
2 CONC ter fit to rule, he had to resign himself to his younger brother's accession.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1145–1146 he campaigned with him against the Seljuk Turks in Anatolia. Although the relationship between the brothers remained uneasy, there was never an open conflict, and Isaac enjoyed the court dignity of sebastokratōr. The 
2 CONC marriages of Isaac's daughters served as useful tools of Manuel's foreign policy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Byzantine Empire had a complex system of aristocracy and bureaucracy, which was inherited from the Roman Empire. At the apex of the pyramid stood the Emperor, sole ruler and divinely ordained, but beneath him a multitude of of
2 CONC ficials and court functionaries operated the administrative machinery of the Byzantine state.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sebastokratōr (Σεβαστοκράτωρ) – "Venerable Ruler": a title created by Alexius I as a combination of autokrator and sebastos (see below). The first sebastokrator was Alexius' brother Isaacius. It was essentially a meaningless title
2 CONC , which signified only a close relationship with the emperor, but ranked immediately after the Despotēs. The feminine form was sebastokratorissa. The first foreigner to be called sebastokrator was Stefan Nemanja of Serbia, who was
2 CONC  given the title in 1191. Kaloyan of Bulgaria also used the title.
2 CONT
2 CONT Autokratōr (Αυτοκράτωρ) — "self-ruler": this title was originally equivalent to Imperator, and was used by the emperors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sebastos (Σεβαστός) – "August One" this title is the literal Greek translation of the Latin term Augustus or Augoustos, was sometimes used by the emperors. As a separate title it appeared in the latter half of the 11th century, an
2 CONC d was extensively awarded by Alexios I Komnenos to his brothers and relations. The female version of the title was sebastē. The special title Prōtosebastos ("First Venerable One") was created for Hadrianos, Alexios' second brother
2 CONC , and awarded also to the Doge of Venice and the Sultan of Iconium. During the 12th century. it remained in use for the Emperor's and the Sebastokratōr's children, and senior foreign dignitaries. However, the parallel processes of
2 CONC  proliferation and devaluation of titles during the 12th century resulted in the creation of a bewildering array of often ridiculously large variations, by using the prefixes pan ("all"), hyper ("above"), prōto ("first"): examples
2 CONC  include Pansebastos,
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT By his first wife, Theodora, Isaac had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexios Komnenos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Irene Komnene, who married an unnamed Doukas Kamateros and became the mother of Isaac Komnenos of Cyprus.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Komnenos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Komnene, who married Constantine Makrodoukas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Komnene, who married King Stephen IV of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his second wife, Irene Synadene, Isaac had two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodora Komnene, who married King Baldwin III of Jerusalem.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudokia Komnene, who married William VIII of Montpellier.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT K. Varzos, Ē genealogia tōn Komnēnōn (Thessalonica, 1984) vol. 1 pp. 391-398.
2 CONT
2 CONT P. Magdalino, The Empire of Manuel I Komnenos 1143–1180, Cambridge University Press, 1993.
