1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivan_Asen_III_of_Bulgaria
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ivan Asen III''' (Bulgarian: Иван Асен III, also Йоан Асен III, Ioan Asen III, and in English John Asen III), ruled as emperor (tsar) of Bulgaria 1279-1280. Ivan Asen III was the son of Mitso Asen of Bulgaria and Maria of Bulga
2 CONC ria, a daughter of Ivan Asen II of Bulgaria and Irene of Thessalonica. He was probably born in about 1259/60, and died in exile in 1303.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fearing the rapid success of Ivailo of Bulgaria, the Byzantine Emperor Michael VIII Palaiologos summoned Ivan Asen to his court, granted him the title of despotēs, and married him to his eldest daughter Eirene Palaiologina in 1277
2 CONC  or 1278. Michael VIII then sent several Byzantine armies to attempt to assert Ivan Asen III on the throne of Bulgaria. Although Ivailo defeated several of these attempts, he was blockaded for three months in Drăstăr (Silistra) by
2 CONC  the Mongol allies of Michael VIII. In the interval a Byzantine force besieged the Bulgarian capital Tărnovo and, hearing a rumor of Ivailo's death in battle, the local nobility surrendered and accepted Ivan Asen III as emperor in
2 CONC  1279.
2 CONT
2 CONT To strengthen his position in Tarnovo, Ivan Asen III married his sister Maria (Kira Maria) to the Bulgaro-Cuman nobleman George Terter, but failed to assert himself throughout the country. Ivailo reappeared before the walls of the
2 CONC  capital and defeated two Byzantine attempts to relieve Ivan Asen III. Despairing of success, Ivan Asen III and Eirene Palaiologina secretly fled Tarnovo with choice treasures from the palace treasury, including pieces captured fr
2 CONC om defeated Byzantine Emperors in former victories. Reaching Mesembria (Nesebăr), the imperial couple sailed for Constantinople, where the enraged Michael VIII refused to receive them for days for their cowardice.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1280 or 1281 Ivan Asen III traveled to the Golden Horde, competing with Ivailo in a bid to win support for restoration in Bulgaria. The Mongol chieftain Nogai Khan eventually had Ivailo murdered, but failed to restore Ivan Asen
2 CONC  III in Bulgaria. The latter returned to his family possessions in the Troad, and died in 1303.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan Asen III and Eirene Palaiologina became the progenitors of the large and influential family Asan (or Asanes) in the Byzantine Empire, which prospered in various court and provincial offices until the end of the empire and its
2 CONC  dependencies in the mid-15th century. One of Ivan Asen III's descendants, Eirene Asanina (daughter of his son Andronikos Asan) married the future Byzantine Emperor John VI Kantakouzenos and through their daughter Helena (who marr
2 CONC ied emperor John V Palaiologos), became the ancestress of later Byzantine emperors.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Family
2 CONT
2 CONT By his marriage with Eirene Palaiologina, Ivan Asen III had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT *Michael Asan, titular emperor of Bulgaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Andronikos Asan, father of Irene Asanina, wife of John VI Kantakouzenos.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Isaac Asan.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Manuel Asan.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Constantine Asan.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Theodora Asanina, who married Fernan Jimenez d'Aunez and then Manuel Tagares.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Maria Asanina, who married Roger de Flor.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BYZANTINE%20NOBILITY.htm#IvanAsenIIIMytzesB
2 CONT
2 CONT IVAN ASEN Mytzes of Bulgaria, son of IVAN Mico [Mytzes] Tsar of the Bulgarians & his wife [Tamara] Asenina of Bulgaria (-before 1302).  Pachymeres names "Mytzæ primogenitor Ioanni" when recording his betrothal[93].  He was install
2 CONC ed in 1278 by Emperor Mikhael VIII, who was concerned with the deteriorating stability in Bulgaria, as IVAN ASEN III Mytzes Tsar of the Bulgarians.  With a Byzantine army he besieged Trnovo in Autumn 1278.  The city opened its gat
2 CONC es to him in Feb 1279, during Ivajlo's absence fighting the Tatars, and he was recognised as Tsar[94].  His forces pursued Ivajlo, besieging him in the fortress of Silistria.  To increase his power base, he married his sister to a
2 CONC  leading Trnovo boyar[95].  He was expelled in 1280 by Ivajlo, and fled to the Tatars and then to Constantinople.  Pachymeres records that "Asani" was awarded the title "despot" in Byzantium [in 1284][96].  Pachymeres names "propr
2 CONC iam germanam Irenen, Asanis viduam" when recording that Emperor Andronikos II sent her by fast ship to "Cyzicum", a passage is probably dateable to [1302] which sets Ivan Asen's date of death[97]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (Betrothed [1263], 1278) EIRENE Palaiologina, daughter of Emperor MIKHAEL VIII & his wife Theodora Doukaina Komnene Palaiologina Batatzaina of Nikaia ([1255/58]-).  Pachymeres records the betrothal of "Mytzæ primogenitor I
2 CONC oanni" and "primogenita…imperatoris filia…Irenæ", dated from the context to the early part of her father's reign, a later passage recording the couple's marriage[98].  Pachymeres names "propriam germanam Irenen, Asanis viduam" whe
2 CONC n recording that Emperor Andronikos II sent her by fast ship to "Cyzicum"[99].  Her marriage was agreed as part of the 1263 agreement between Ivan's father and Emperor Mikhael VIII under which the former surrended Mesembria to Byz
2 CONC antium[100]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Tsar Ivan Asen III & his wife had [eight] children, who adopted the surname Asanes/Asanina in Byzantium. 
