2 CONT ------
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stefan_Milutin
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Stefan Uroš II Milutin''' (Serbian Cyrillic: Стефан Урош II Милутин; c. 1253 – 29 October 1321), known as '''Stefan Milutin''' (Стефан Милутин), was the King of Serbia between 1282–1321, a member of the Nemanjić dynasty. He was
2 CONC  one of the most powerful rulers of Serbia in the Middle Ages. Milutin is credited with strongly resisting the efforts of Byzantine Emperor Michael VIII Palaiologos to impose Roman Catholicism on the Balkans after the Union of Lyo
2 CONC ns in 1274. During his regin, Serbian economic power grew rapidly, mostly due to the development of mining. He founded Novo Brdo, which became an internationally important silver mining site. As most of the Nemanjić monarchs, he w
2 CONC as proclaimed a saint by the Serbian Orthodox Church with a feast day on October 30.[1][2][3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Milutin appears in the Dante Alighieri's narrative poem Divine Comedy.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT #	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stefan_Milutin#Early_life Early life]
2 CONT #	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stefan_Milutin#Wars_with_the_Bulgarians_and_Mongols Wars with the Bulgarians and Mongols]
2 CONT #	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stefan_Milutin#Feud_of_the_brothers Feud of the brothers]
2 CONT #	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stefan_Milutin#Battles_and_supreme_leadership Battles and supreme leadership]
2 CONT #	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stefan_Milutin#Family Family] /!\ 
2 CONT
2 CONT -------
2 CONT
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SERBIA.htm#Milutindied1321A
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         STEFAN UROŠ MILUTIN ([1253]-Castle Nerodimlja, Amselfeld 29 Oct 1321, bur Sardika [Sofija]).  “Stephanus Uroš, Serbiæ rex” confirmed the donation by “matre Helena” to “monasterio S. Mariæ Rotaciensis” by charter dated 1
2 CONC 5 Mar [1305/07][200].  “Stephanus Uroš, Serbiæ rex…abnepos sancti Symeonis et filius magni regis Uroš” built “templum in honorem sanctorum et justorum Joachim et Annæ” by charter dated 1314[201].  He succeeded in 1282 on the abdic
2 CONC ation of his brother as STEFAN UROŠ II MILUTIN King of Serbia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT   -        see below.  - http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SERBIA.htm#Milutindied1321B
2 CONT
2 CONT STEFAN UROŠ MILUTIN, son of STEFAN UROŠ I "Veliki/the Great" or "Arapavi/the Holy" King of Serbia & his wife Jelena --- ([1253]-Castle Nerodimlja, Amselfeld 29 Oct 1321, bur Sardika [Sofija]).  “Stephanus Uroš, Serbiæ rex” confirm
2 CONC ed the donation by “matre Helena” to “monasterio S. Mariæ Rotaciensis” by charter dated 15 Mar [1305/07][203].  He took up residence at his mother's court at Shkodra after the accession of his brother in 1276[204].  Ban of Hum unt
2 CONC il 1282.  He succeeded in 1282 on the abdication of his brother as STEFAN UROŠ II MILUTIN King of Serbia and Lord of Primorje.  Serbia attacked Macedonia in 1282, conquering Skopje (which became King Milutin's main residence) eith
2 CONC er then or during the 1290s, and Durazzo in 1296[205].  In retaliation for the invasion of Serbia by Šišman, Lord of Vidin in Bulgaria, King Milutin conquered Vidin in 1292 and forced Šišman to seek refuge with his Tatar overlords
2 CONC .  Peace was restored, but to appease the Tatars King Milutin sent his son as a hostage[206].  He made peace with Emperor Andronikos II in 1299, agreeing the Serbian/Byzantine border through Macedonia, and marrying the emperor's d
2 CONC aughter as his fourth wife, her dowry being deemed to be the Byzantine territory which King Milutin had conquered over the previous years[207].  War with his brother broke out in 1300, lasting until 1312 or 1314.  Milutin funded t
2 CONC he war by increasing production from his mines, minting large quantities of silver coins, although Venice complained about their doubtful silver content.  He also built numerous churches in Serbia during his reign, in return for w
2 CONC hich he received firm ecclesiastical support in his government[208].  “Stephanus Uroš, Serbiæ rex…abnepos sancti Symeonis et filius magni regis Uroš” built “templum in honorem sanctorum et justorum Joachim et Annæ” by charter date
2 CONC d 1314[209].  He imprisoned his nephew Vladislav in [1316/17], confiscated his Serbian lands and conquered his Hungarian territories.  Hungary quickly retaliated and re-established Mačva as a Banate under a Hungarian appointee[210
2 CONC ].  King Stefan Uroš II Milutin died after falling from his bed, without nominating his successor which triggered immediate civil war between his sons and his nephew[211].  A document records the death of “Stephanus Uroš, Serbiæ r
2 CONC ex” dated 29 Oct 1321[212]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Betrothed''' ( [1267/68] ) to ANNA Komnene Palaiologina, daughter of Emperor MIKHAEL VIII & his wife Theodora Dukaina Komnene Palaiologina Batatzaina (1260-[1299/1300]).  Pachymeres records that Emperor Mikhael VIII proposed th
2 CONC e betrothal of "[filiam] secundam Annam" and "cralem Serbiæ Stephanum Uresim, secundo eius filio Melotino", dated from the context to the early part of his reign[213]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' firstly ( [1273/76], repudiated [1283] ) [HELENA] Angelina, daughter of IOANNES Dukas Angelos sébastokrator, Lord of Thessaly & his wife --- of the Vlachs (-[1298/99]).  The primary source which confirms her parentage and 
2 CONC marriage has not yet been identified.  She is named "Helene/Jelena" in Europäische Stammtafeln[214] but the primary source on which this is based has not been identified. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' secondly (Autumn 1284, repudiated [1294] ) as her first husband, ANA of Bulgaria, daughter of GEORGI I Terter Tsar of the Bulgarians & his first wife Maria --- (-after 1304).  Pachymeres records the marriage of "Terteris f
2 CONC iliam, ex sorore genitam Asanis" and "cralis Serbiæ"[215].  The primary source which confirms her name has not yet been identified.  Her birth date range is estimated from the estimated birth dates of her two children by her first
2 CONC  marriage.  If these are correct, Ana must have been born from her father's first marriage irrespective of the report in Pachymeres.  She married secondly (1301) Mikhael [Demetrios] Dukas Komnenos Angelos 'Kutrules' despot (-1304)
2 CONC .  The primary source which confirms her second marriage has not yet been identified. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' thirdly ( [1295], repudiated [1298/99] ) as her second husband, ERSZÉBET of Hungary, widow of ZAVIŠ von Rosenberg, daughter of ISTVÁN V King of Hungary & his wife Ersébet [née ---] of the Kumans (1255-[1313/26]).  Pachymer
2 CONC es records the marriage of "sororem defunctæ Augustæ, matris Augusti iunioris…filia regis Ungariæ…germanam tertiam" and the king of Serbia, despite the couple's consanguinity (due to his brother already being married to her sister
2 CONC )[216].  The primary source which confirms her name and first marriage has not yet been identified. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' fourthly (betrothed 1299, [Oct/Nov] 1300) SIMONIDA Palaiologina, daughter of Emperor ANDRONIKOS II & his second wife Yolanda di Monferrato ([1292/93]-Constantinople after 1336).  Pachymeres records the marriage of "August 
2 CONC filiam…Simonidem" and "cralis Serbiæ", after her aunt Evdokia Palaiologina had refused the marry him[217].  Mistreated by her husband, she refused to return to him after attending the funeral of her mother in Constantinople in 131
2 CONC 7 but was forced to do so by her half-brother Konstantinos[218].  She fled to Constantinople after her husband died and became a nun[219]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Mistress''' (1): ---.  The name of the mistress of Stefan Uroš Milutin is not known. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Stefan Uroš II Milutin & his second wife had two children:
