2 CONT * [https://istorijat.wordpress.com/tag/%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%85%D0%BC%D1%83%D0%B4-%D0%BF%D0%B0%D1%88%D0%B0-%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%92%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%9B/ Махмуд-паша Анђеловић]
2 CONT * [https://digitalna.nsk.hr/?pr=iiif.v.a&id=601954&vwopt=%7B%22pages%22%3A%5B50%5D%2C%22pan%22%3A%7B%22x%22%3A0.558%2C%22y%22%3A0.876%7D%2C%22view%22%3A%22image%22%2C%22zoom%22%3A0.841%7D&fbclid=IwAR0wERd174xYIntcrP_Ac0VTyIM8K2l8N
2 CONC UdT_OPUqA6IDqpqCCjh8CnDLn8_aem_ATXm2UjevuJbpXKjkQ6htAUvo9X3V5OPvv7V9MmMmQJZeWhdFbldWgC17gytlBkeqFTdcOBTgKXH9ai9ZFbSVry2  Znameniti Hrvati Bošnjaci i Hercegovci u Turskoj Carevini / Safvet-beg Bašagić. str. 40-41]
2 CONT * [https://books.google.hr/books?id=ptXG0uA70lAC&printsec=frontcover&hl=hr&source=gbs_ge_summary_r&cad=0#v=onepage&q=MAhmud&f=false The Sultan of Vezirs: The Life and Times of the Ottoman Grand Vezir Mahmud Pasha Angelović (1453-1
2 CONC 474)], Théoharis Stavrides
2 CONT * '''See''': [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philanthropenos Philanthropenos - House of Philanthropenos], [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angelos Angelos - House of Angelos]
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in the Serbian Despotate, he was a descendant of the Byzantine [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angelos Angelos family] that had left Thessaly in 1394. According to biographers, he was conscripted as a child by the Ottomans emp
2 CONC loying the devşirme system.[2] Raised as a Muslim in Edirne, he was a capable soldier and was married to a [https://www.geni.com/people/Sel%C3%A7uk-Hatun/6000000204186323838 daughter] of [https://www.geni.com/people/Zagan-Pasha-12
2 CONC th-Grand-Vizier/6000000017606685980 '''Zaganos Pasha''']. After distinguishing himself at the Siege of Belgrade in 1456, he was raised to the position of Grand Vizier as a reward, succeeding his father-in-law [https://www.geni.com
2 CONC /people/Zagan-Pasha-12th-Grand-Vizier/6000000017606685980 '''Zaganos Pasha''']. Throughout his tenure, he led armies or accompanied Mehmed II on his own campaigns. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note:''' Place of his birth mentioned was as: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Novo_Brdo Novo Brdo (Kosovo, Serbian Despotate)] and Kruševac. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahmud_Pasha_Angelovi%C4%87#Origin_and_early_life Origin and early life]===
2 CONT After the Ottoman conquest of Thessaly in 1394, the ruling Angeloi[3][4] Philanthropenoi family took refuge. The grandchildren of either Alexios or Manuel were Mahmud Pasha and his brother [https://www.geni.com/people/Michael-Ange
2 CONC lovi%C4%87/6000000205117708830 '''Mihailo Anđelović'''].
2 CONT
2 CONT It is estimated that Angelović was born in the early 1420s.[5] Most historians accept that Angelović was born in Novo Brdo in the Serbian Despotate, and that his father [https://www.geni.com/people/Mihailos-Angelovi%C4%87/60000002
2 CONC 05116701962 '''Mihailos'''] was the son of either [https://www.geni.com/people/Caesar-Alexios-Angelos-Philanthropenos/6000000008521882797 '''Alexios Angelos Philanthropenos'''] or his son/nephew/brother [https://www.geni.com/peopl
2 CONC e/Caesar-Manuel-Angelos-Philanthropenos/6000000008521782674 '''Manuel'''], rulers of Thessaly.[6] T. Stavrides views it more probable that [https://www.geni.com/people/Caesar-Manuel-Angelos-Philanthropenos/6000000008521782674 '''M
2 CONC anuel'''] was his grandfather.[5] The only information on his father is that he lived in Serbia in the 1420s.[5] His mother's ancestry is the matter of debate. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laonikos_Chalkokondyles Chalkokondyles]
2 CONC  (1430–1470) called her Serbian, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Critobulus Kritoboulos] (1410–1470), Greek, while there are various theories on her noble ancestry.[7] Angelović had a brother, [https://www.geni.com/people/M
2 CONC ichael-Angelovi%C4%87/6000000205117708830 '''Mihailo Anđelović'''], later a prominent Serbian statesman, after the Turkish conquest.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Tahsin Yazıcı, Angelović was "born to a Greek or Serbian family".[9] Dejan Djokić stated that Angelović was born "to a Serb mother and a Greek refugee father – no less than son of the last Angeloi ruler of Thessaly wh
2 CONC o had emigrated to Serbia in the late fourteenth century".[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laonikos_Chalkokondyles Chalkokondyles] mentions that Angelović was captured by Ottoman horsemen while travelling with his mother from Novo Brdo to Smederevo (the Serbian capital), and taken to the O
2 CONC ttoman court.[11] It is assumed that this took place in 1427, when the Ottomans attacked Serbia.[12] Furthermore, it is unconcluded whether he was captured according to the devşirme (practice, the regular practice of taking childr
2 CONC en certain noble families whose Ottomans have taken lands and making these children high rankings officials) or as a prisoner of war.[13] [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ta%C5%9Fk%C3%B6pr%C3%BCzade Taşköprüzade] (d. 1560) and [http
2 CONC s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A%C5%9F%C4%B1k_%C3%87elebi Aşık Çelebi] (1520–1572) name two other boys led with Angelović on horseback to Edirne, Molla Iyas and Mevlana Abdülkerim, the latter which reached the rank of kadıasker (chief 
2 CONC judge) and şeyhülislam (Islamic scholar).[14] Upon conversion to Islam, he received the name Mahmud.
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known about his activities before 1453. According to T. Stavrides, Angelović and his companions were educated in the palace, probably as içoğlan, and Mahmud then entered service in the Enderûn, later serving prince Mehme
2 CONC d, the future sultan.[15] Sources do not agree on which posts he held at the palace.[16] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahmud_Pasha_Angelovi%C4%87#Life Life]===
2 CONT Mahmud Pasha was a capable soldier. After distinguishing himself at the siege of Belgrade (1456), he was raised to the position of Grand Vizier as a reward, succeeding [https://www.geni.com/people/Zagan-Pasha-12th-Grand-Vizier/600
2 CONC 0000017606685980 '''Zaganos Pasha'''].[17] Throughout his tenure he led armies or accompanied Mehmed II on his own campaigns.[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1458, the Serbian Despot [https://www.geni.com/people/Lazar-%C4%90ur%C4%91evi%C4%87/5122715583450064043 '''Lazar Branković'''] died. Mahmud's brother [https://www.geni.com/people/Michael-Angelovi%C4%87/6000000205117708830 '''Mi
2 CONC hailo'''] became member of a collective regency, but he was soon deposed by the anti-Ottoman and pro-Hungarian faction in the Serbian court. In reaction, Mahmud attacked and seized Smederevo Fortress, although the citadel held out
2 CONC , and seized some additional strongholds in its vicinity. Threatened by a possible Hungarian intervention however he was forced to withdraw south and join the forces of Sultan Mehmed II at Skopje.[19] In 1461, he accompanied Mehme
2 CONC d in his campaign against the Empire of Trebizond, the last surviving fragment of the Byzantine Empire. Mahmud negotiated the surrender of the city of Trebizond with the protovestiarios, the scholar George Amiroutzes, who was also
2 CONC  his cousin.[20]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1463, Mahmud led the invasion and conquest of the Kingdom of Bosnia, even though a peace treaty between Bosnia and the Ottomans had just been renewed. He captured the Bosnian king, [https://www.geni.com/people/Stjepan-Ostoji%C4
2 CONC %87/5130707293240106908 '''Stephen Tomašević'''], at Ključ, and obtained from him the cession of the country to the Empire.[19]
2 CONT
2 CONT Angelović accompanied Mehmed II when he attacked Albania Veneta in the summer of 1467, and ravaged the lands. For 15 days he pursued Skanderbeg, who was a Venetian ally at the time, but failed to find him, as Skanderbeg retreated 
2 CONC into the mountains and then succeeded in fleeing to the coast.[21] According to Tursun Beg and Ibn Kemal, Angelović swam over Bojana, attacked Venetian-controlled Scutari, and plundered the surrounding area.[22]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mahmud was dismissed in 1468 due to the machinations of his successor, Rum Mehmed Pasha, ostensibly due to irregularities regarding the resettlement of the Karamanids in Constantinople following the conquest of Karaman earlier in 
2 CONC that year.[23] He was reinstated in 1472, but his relations with the Sultan were now strained. Mahmud was fired and executed in 1474. The cause was the suspicion that he was involved in the sudden death of [https://www.geni.com/pe
2 CONC ople/Mustafa/4874279355180078893 '''Şehzade Mustafa'''], the favorite son of Sultan [https://www.geni.com/people/Mehmed-II-bin-Murad-Han-The-Conqueror-Ottoman-Sultan/385223927880013853 '''Mehmed II''']. It was said that [https://w
2 CONC ww.geni.com/people/Mustafa/4874279355180078893 '''Şehzade Mustafa'''] had an affair with Mahmud's wife, [https://www.geni.com/people/Sel%C3%A7uk-Hatun/6000000204186323838 '''Selçuk Hatun'''] (sister of [https://www.geni.com/people
2 CONC /Hatice-Hatun/6000000204184572859 '''Hatice Hatun'''], the youngest consort of [https://www.geni.com/people/Mehmed-II-bin-Murad-Han-The-Conqueror-Ottoman-Sultan/385223927880013853 '''Mehmed II''']), and that Mahmud poisoned him fo
2 CONC r it. Mahmud denied it but, even without proof, [https://www.geni.com/people/Mehmed-II-bin-Murad-Han-The-Conqueror-Ottoman-Sultan/385223927880013853 '''Mehmed II'''] still decided to execute him.[24] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahmud_Pasha_Angelovi%C4%87#Literary_output Literary output]===
2 CONT
2 CONT ===[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahmud_Pasha_Angelovi%C4%87#Family Family]===
2 CONT He married [https://www.geni.com/people/Sel%C3%A7uk-Hatun/6000000204186323838 '''Selçuk Hatun'''], daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Zagan-Pasha-12th-Grand-Vizier/6000000017606685980 '''Zaganos Pasha'''] by his first wife [
2 CONC https://www.geni.com/people/Sitti-Hatun/6000000204184970822 '''Sitti Nefise Hatun'''], and they had a son named [https://www.geni.com/people/Ali-Bey-Angelovi%C4%87/6000000205117462832 '''Ali Bey'''] and a daughter named [https://w
2 CONC ww.geni.com/people/Hatice-Hatun-Angelovi%C4%87/6000000205116795894 '''Hatice Hatun''']. His wife became lover to [https://www.geni.com/people/Mustafa/4874279355180078893 '''Şehzade Mustafa'''], son of Sultan [https://www.geni.com/
2 CONC people/Mehmed-II-bin-Murad-Han-The-Conqueror-Ottoman-Sultan/385223927880013853 '''Mehmed II''']. For this reason, Mahmud was suspected of involvement in [https://www.geni.com/people/Mustafa/4874279355180078893 '''Mustafa's death''
2 CONC ']  and executed. 
2 CONT
