1 NOTE {geni:about_me} According to the legend mentioned by Jan Długosz, Florian Saryusz came from the small town of Surdęga (now the village of Majkowice), located 8 km north of Przedbórz, then a royal town. He was a knight in the servi
2 CONC ce of the Sieradz Voivode and participated with the team in the wars waged by Władysław Łokietek. He came from the nobility using the Koźlarogi coat of arms - in the original version, a half-horned goat was placed in the red field
2 CONC  on the coat of arms. Florian became famous during the battle of Płowce in 1331, when he was pierced with three Teutonic copies while defending the endangered king. After the battle, the king, driving around the battlefield, saw F
2 CONC lorian Saryusz pressing his insides back into his stomach. The royal medics managed to save his life, and the king, in recognition of his attitude, granted the knight a new estate and a new coat of arms: Jelita [a], in which half 
2 CONC a goat was moved to the coat of arms jewel, and three golden knight copies were placed in the coat of arms. The figures of the alleged descendants of the legendary knight are better confirmed in the sources. Florian Saryusz was co
2 CONC nsidered one of the ancestors of the family by the Kamocki, Borzobohaci, Myśliborski, Żeromscy, Fabicki, Nowosieleccy, Słowiński families and the special memory of the brave ancestor survived in the Zamoyski family.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Zamoyski family founded a number of towns and villages named after Florian or his coats of arms, incl. Szarogród, Koźlerogi and the former town of Jelitów, as well as the forest settlement of Florianka in the Zamość Estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are no historical documents confirming the participation of Florian Saryusz in the Battle of Płowce. However, members of noble families claiming this figure as their ancestor left a number of material traces of the legend. T
2 CONC hese are, among others, brick mansions in Majkowice and Bąkowa Góra, the Florian oak in the town of Florianka, and literary works, including panegyrics written by Tiedemann Giese and Walenty Schreckius, deriving the Zamoyski famil
2 CONC y from Florian Saryusz.
2 CONT
2 CONT In these families the name Florian was passed down from generation to generation. He wore them, among others Cracow bishop Florian of Morsk from the Koźleróg family. The key scene from the legend of Florian Saryusz was presented i
2 CONC n 1909 by Wojciech Kossak in the painting Łokietek and Florian Saryusz after the Battle of Płowce. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This coat of arms was known earlier, among others, from the seal of Piotr of Mokrsko from 1316. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Niesiecki writes about the coat of arms and its origin: 
2 CONT
2 CONT three golden copies arranged in the shape of a star in a red field, half a goat jumping out of the helmet, front legs raised, in the right shield turned with horns on the head.
2 CONT
2 CONT The beginnings of the coat of arms arranged as described were made in the year of our Lord 1331: for when Władysław Łokietek triumphantly beat the Teutonic Knights, so that only forty and a few of his men lay in the square, and th
2 CONC e next day he was touring the battlefield, among the Polish corpses he attacked one of his Knight Floryan Saryusz, who bravely stumbled in this battle, frayed by many wounds, pressed his intestines with his own hand in the guts.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the King saw him, he said with pity to his own people: What is this good soldier suffering for? for which he, having almost exhausted his last strength, replied: "It is not that what bothers me and afflicts me, what do you se
2 CONC e, King, as a more evil neighbor in one village who lives with me?"
2 CONT
2 CONT Some understand that then his native coat of arms of the goat was taken on the helmet, he placed the three copies with which he saw it on the shield, but Długosz does not say this, and yes, as Paprocki thinks, if he had had such a
2 CONC  variation of the coat of arms, he would not have left the former historians, and Długosz clearly He writes first that he was not hit with three copies, but was chopped with many wounds, and then adds that since then the coat of a
2 CONC rms of Koźleroga received a new name from Szariusz-Jelit.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nothing in the coat of arms was mentioned about the variety.
2 CONT
2 CONT We should know then that this coat of arms, as the Jelitczyks use it today, is earlier than this battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Moreover, if anyone had used the goat in the coat of arms before, then even to this day any descendants who used it and sealed it with such a shape, as when this Saryuszus lived, there were already many houses of this coat of arms
2 CONC , as you will see below. The new coat of arms would not benefit everyone but Sarius' own descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is an ancient coat of arms, and even in the days of the Pagan monarchs in Poland, it is well-deserved in its coat of arms, but it is difficult to guess for its origins from where it had its origins.
2 CONT
2 CONT I know that before that the copy of the mark was of royal dignity.
2 CONT
2 CONT I also know that Sarus the king of the Goths once flourished, who beat Radagas on his head and took his slaves from him, around 406.
2 CONT
2 CONT ? Parisius in Slavia? he understands that the name of Saryusz in our Poland is multiplied by the descendants of Sarus.
2 CONT
2 CONT The same says that this coat of arms acquired from one of the Sarmatians in the war with Romans, pierced with three spears, as he proves that the Polish copies are longer, and the Roman ones are shorter, and to the shape that is i
2 CONC n the coat of arms, more similar, and are called Sarissae .
2 CONT
2 CONT In other countries I do not know that anyone has used a similar coat of arms, and only? Petra Sancta cap. 63? claims that the Carloveuszowie in Britain three copies of gold, with the ends of which are silver in the coat of arms?.
2 CONT
2 CONT So much for Niesiecki
2 CONT
2 CONT I was wondering where the name of the Woźniki estate received for war merits came from.
2 CONT
2 CONT Or maybe this property was named after Woźnik from the Sieradz region, which was owned by Florian Saryusz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our family has the? Herbowe? actually a copy of it.
2 CONT
2 CONT All the Jelitczyk family understand their oldest nest Mojkowice in Sieradzka Ziemia, in the district of Piotrków, next to which there is a castle, not far on the Pilica River, the old brick one, the said Surdega, was already devas
2 CONC tated, and it was the property of the knight Floryan Saryusz, about whom above was Żegota from Mojkowice, ensign Sieradzki and Mikołaj, ensign also Sieradzki. Floryan and Żegota were brothers, heirs in Mojkowice, Paprocki mentions
2 CONC  that Floryan got Wroników and Woźniki, and Żegota and Laski were given to them in 1410 (absurd year). 
2 CONT
2 CONT The medieval key of the knightly estates of the Nagodzic family - Jelitczyk from the center in Majkowice, appears in written sources from 1398. Florian of Majkowice was mentioned then, and historical literature tried to associate 
2 CONC this character with the knight Florian Saryusz, the hero of the Battle of Płowce, but there is no clear evidence for this. Several legible components of this key have been preserved in the Pilica river valley. A large village on a
2 CONC  medieval oval plan, the so-called a cone-shaped hillfort, remains of a medieval brick castle called Surdęga and picturesque ruins of a Renaissance fortified manor house. The defensive seats of knights were created in a clear chro
2 CONC nological sequence. The castle is the second defensive seat.
2 CONT
2 CONT Location and description 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Surdęga Castle was located about 1.5 km north-east of the settlement, right on the left bank of the Pilica River, next to the former river crossing. On the other side, there is a place called Stary Most.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before the research began, it was a slight elevation, about 1.2 m high, with a regular rectangular plan and an area of about 3000 m2, covered with stone and brick debris. The embankment was surrounded by a circumferential moat. Cu
2 CONC rrently, the surfaces of the heavily leveled object are covered with wild vegetation.
2 CONT
2 CONT History 
2 CONT
2 CONT The establishment and use of the castle dates back to the 14th-15th centuries.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1944, the ruins and foundations of the building were pulled down for the purposes of the front fortifications on the Pilica line. Only faint relics of the foundations and a partially preserved moat remain. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Condition and test results 
2 CONT
2 CONT The excavations under the supervision of A. Chmielowska were carried out in 1973 and 1974.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the research, a 120 m2 trench was established and several so-called probing ditches. The remains of the foundations of a brick building made of erratic stones, sandstone and limestone have been documented. It was located in
2 CONC  the central part of the elevation, there were two corners and a perimeter wall 16 m long, the width of the foundation part was 2.0 m. dimensions of about 20 x 16 m. The building was surrounded by an embankment and a wet moat 15 m
2 CONC  wide, supplied from the Pilica River. The author identifies part of the raw material used for the construction with the quarry on Mount Czartoria near Handno, located about 6 km. from the tested object.
2 CONT
2 CONT The acquired movable historical material consists of fragments of finger bricks, some with colored glaze, fragments of late-medieval ceramic vessels, tile tiles with figural ornaments, animal bones and several metal objects. The f
2 CONC inding of 3 lead seals included in the group of commercial seals turned out to be exceptionally interesting. This discovery strengthens the thesis that the building initially guarded the medieval crossing over the Pilica River. Th
2 CONC e castle was dated by A. Chmielowska to the 14th-15th centuries.
2 CONT
2 CONT The monument is available without restrictions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Comp. Zbigniew Lechowicz, Regional Branch of the National Heritage Board of Poland in Łódź, 01-01-2015
