1 NOTE John Tzimisces, who had been co-ruler of the Eastern Roman Empire during the regency of Basil II, had assassinated his co-ruler, Nicephorus II Phocas in 969.  Then in 976 John Tzimisces died suddenly in 976.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tzimisces, and Armenian by birth, became one of the greatest Byzantine generals about 969.  John defeated Boris II of Bulgaria in 971.  John marched against Sviatoslav the Russian and defeated him near Adrianople, and forced 
2 CONC him to evacuate Bulgaria in 972.  John then annexed eastern Bulgaria as far as the Danube.  A feudal insurrection in 971 was put down with difficulty.  Between 972 and 976 John Tzimisces took Edessa and Nezib in 974; and went to J
2 CONC erusalem having taken Damascus and Beirut in 976.  He was finally halted by the Egyptian forces and died suddenly in 976.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Romanus II of the Eastern Roman Empire died suddenly in 963 (said to have been poisoned by his wife, Theophano), General John Tzimisces, then age 39, and General Nicephorus Phokus became co-emperors during the minority of Rom
2 CONC anus II's son Basil.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 971 Emperor John Tzimisces of the Eastern Roman Empire, Regent for Basil II, took to the field against the Bulgarian  army of Boris II who had invaded the Balkans.  John's military strategedy surrounded the Bulgarians, took Bor
2 CONC is prisoner, and made them pledge never to invade the Balkan peninsula again.  Boris was taken to Constantinople as a prisoner, and his kingdom became a Byzantine province.
