2 CONT
2 CONT 8.         '''THEODOROS Batatzes''', son of --- (-killed in battle Neocæsarea 1176).  A seal dated to [1150] names "Theodoros Batatzes"[100].  He took part in the invasion of Cilicia in 1158 with his brother-in-law Emperor Manuel 
2 CONC I and occupied Tarsus[101].  The record of the synod of 1166 records the presence of “imperatore domino Manuele Comneno...consobrinis...nostri regis filiis...generi...eius pansebasto...domino Theodoro Vatatze, et domino Andronico 
2 CONC et domino Alexio...”[102].  Sébastohypertatos.  General.  Dux of Cilicia.  Governor of Cilicia.  Pansébastohypertatos.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' EVDOKIA Komnene, daughter of Emperor IOANNES & his wife [Piroska] [Eirene] of Hungary ([1119]-).  Her marriage is indicated by Ioannes Cinnamos who names "Theodorum Batatzem sororium sum", referring to Emperor Manuel I[103
2 CONC ].  
2 CONT
2 CONT    Theodoros Batatzes & his wife had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The House of Vatatzes or Batatzes (Greek: Βατάτζης) was a noble Byzantine Greek family of the 11th–14th centuries with several branches, which produced several senior generals of the Byzantine army and, after John III Doukas Vatat
2 CONC zes intermarried with the Laskaris family, the ruling line of the Empire of Nicaea until the usurpation of Michael VIII Palaiologos in 1261. The feminine form of the name is Vatatzina or Vatatzaina (Βατατζίνα) or Batatzina or Bata
2 CONC tzaina (Βατατζίνα).[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Origins
2 CONT According to the Greek scholar Konstantinos Amantos, the name Vatatzes is a diminutive form of βάτος, vatos, 'bramble, briar', and suggests that it was given as a nickname signifying a harsh character.[2] Another possible origin i
2 CONC s βατάκι, vataki, 'ray fish'.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT The first member of the family, known simply by his surname, is attested around the year 1000. John Skylitzes (Synopsis Historion, 343.134) records that "Vatatzes with his entire family" was among those "distinguished citizens of 
2 CONC Adrianople" who fled to the Bulgarian emperor Samuel because they were accused of pro-Bulgarian sentiments.[1][4] Over the next centuries, the family remained associated with Adrianople and the surrounding region (the thema of Mac
2 CONC edonia), where their estates were.[1] Likewise the 13th-century chronicler Ephraim the Monk mentions Didymoteichon as the family's native city.[2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 11th–12th centuries
2 CONT Michael Psellos records that in 1047, a John Vatatzes joined the revolt of Leo Tornikios, a relative of Emperor Constantine IX Monomachos (r. 1042–1055).[2] The family became prominent in the 12th century, when several members ros
2 CONC e to high offices. Theodore Vatatzes married Eudokia, the sister of Emperor Manuel I Komnenos (r. 1143–1180) and was named despotes; his sons were Andronikos, Alexios, and John Komnenos Vatatzes, who became megas domestikos (comma
2 CONC nder-in-chief of the Byzantine army).[1][5] The hagiographer of the Nicaean emperor John III Doukas Vatatzes (r. 1221/22–1254), identified John with the latter's grandfather, who supposedly had two sons named Nikephoros and Theodo
2 CONC re, who were persecuted by Andronikos I Komnenos (r. 1183–1185). According to Niketas Choniates, however, John's sons were named Manuel and Alexios. This is most likely the result of a confusion by the hagiographer.[5][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Other contemporary members were Leo Vatatzes, who under Manuel I fought against the Hungarians;[5] a 12th-century seal mentions the "patrikios, hypatos, and stratelates of the West" Bryennios Vatatzes;[7] another seal mentions Nik
2 CONC ephoros Vatatzes, "protoproedros, vestarches, megas doux, and praetor of the Aegean Sea";[8] a Joseph Vatatzes, possibly a monk, is also known through his seal, likely from the 13th century;[8][9] while a Basil Vatatzes is known a
2 CONC s doux and anagrapheus of a province under the Nicaean emperor Theodore I Laskaris (r. 1205–1221/22).[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Rulers of the Empire of Nicaea
2 CONT
2 CONT 15th-century miniature portrait of the Nicaean emperor John III Doukas Vatatzes, founder of the imperial line of the family
2 CONT Another Basil Vatatzes, a man of undistinguished birth according to Choniates, married into the Angelos family and rose to become domestikos of the West under Isaac II Angelos (r. 1185–1195). It is possibly due to this connection 
2 CONC that the 14th-century Chronicle of the Morea calls Isaac II "Sakes Vatatzes" (Σάκης Βατάτζης).[5][10] Modern scholars consider Basil as the father of John III Doukas Vatatzes, who thus had no direct connection to the Komnenian-era
2 CONC  noble family.[11] Basil had two further sons, the sebastokrator Isaac and an anonymous third son. Isaac had a son named John and an unnamed daughter who married Constantine Strategopoulos, while the other brother had a daughter w
2 CONC ho became the wife of the protovestiarios Alexios Raoul.[12][13]
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in c. 1192, John married Irene, daughter of the founder of the Empire of Nicaea, Theodore I Laskaris (r. 1205–1221/22), and succeeded him on the throne, despite the opposition of Theodore's brothers. John proved a capable rul
2 CONC er, defeating the Latin Empire at the Battle of Poimanenon, and expanding his realm into Europe, where he captured Thessalonica in 1246. Remembered for his kindness as well as his ability, he was venerated as a saint after his dea
2 CONC th by the Greeks of Asia Minor.[13][14] John III was succeeded as Emperor of Nicaea by his only son Theodore II (r. 1254–1258), who however preferred his mother's surname, Laskaris. With his wife Helena, he had five children.[15] 
2 CONC Theodore II was succeeded by his only son, John IV Laskaris (r. 1258–1261), but was driven from the throne by Michael VIII Palaiologos (r. 1259–1282) after the reconquest of Constantinople in 1261, blinded, and placed in confineme
2 CONC nt in a remote fortress, where he died some time after 1285.[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT Later members
2 CONT The last prominent member was John Vatatzes, who occupied a succession of high offices in 1333–1345.[7][17] Another John Vatatzes was a landowner at Caesaropolis in 1320–1322;[18] a Constantine Vatatzes was archon in Ioannina in 1
2 CONC 367, before being banished by Thomas Preljubovic;[19] a Basil Vatatzes was a hymn writer sometime before 1433;[20] a Theodore Vatatzes donated to the Agioi Pantes Monastery of Mount Athos in 1447;[8][21] John Vatatzes was a hymn w
2 CONC riter from Crete and protopsaltes (first cantor) at Candia in 1465;[22] the goldsmith Frangiskos Vatatzes is known from a testament of 1497;[8] and in 1563 the priest Konstantinos Vatatzes is attested.[8] Finally, in the 17th cent
2 CONC ury, Vasilios Vatatzes (born 1694) from Therapeia near Constantinople, who travelled extensively in Persia and Russia and wrote about his travels.[8][23]
2 CONT
2 CONT A variant of the family name, Diplovatatzes (Διπλοβατάτζης, "Double Vatatzes"), was used from the mid-13th century on for family members who descended from the Vatatzai on both sides. They too ranked among the senior nobility of t
2 CONC he late Byzantine Empire.[1][8] An Alexios and Manuel Diplovatatzes are mentioned in the 14th century, along with the protovestiarites and lord of Veroia Diplovatatzes;[8] a Theodore Diplovatatzes granted the Vatopedi Monastery la
2 CONC nds on Lemnos in 1430;[8] and the last member of the family was Tommaso Diplovataccio (1468–1541), who became a distinguished jurist and scholar in Renaissance Italy.[8]
2 CONT
