1 NOTE In 1182 A constantinople mob massacred the Latis who rule as agents of the regent Maria of Antioch.  They killed city officials and traders, and proclaimed an uncle of Alexius II comnenus as co-emperor to rule as Andronicus I Comn
2 CONC enus.  In 1183 Alexius is strangled to death by agents of his Uncle Andronicus while at Ere.
1 NOTE Alexios II Komnenos or Alexius II Comnenus (Greek: ??????? ?' ????????, Alexios II Komnenos) (10 September 1169 - October 1183, Constantinople), Byzantine emperor (1180-1183), was the son of Emperor Manuel I Komnenos and Maria, da
2 CONC ughter of Raymond, prince of Antioch.[1] He was the long-awaited male heir, and was named Alexius as a fulfilment of the AIMA prophecy.
2 CONT
2 CONT His reign and death
2 CONT
2 CONT The Empire in 1180 A.D when Alexios II became EmperorOn Manuel's death in 1180, Maria, who became a nun under the name Xene ("foreigner"), took the position of regent (according to some historians). She excluded her young son from
2 CONC  power, entrusting it instead to Alexios the protosebastos (a cousin of Alexios II), who was popularly believed to be her lover. Friends of the young Alexios II now tried to form a party against the empress mother and the protoseb
2 CONC astos; Alexios II's half-sister Maria, wife of Caesar John (Renier of Montferrat), stirred up riots in the streets of the capital.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their party was defeated (May 2, 1182), but Andronikos Komnenos, a first cousin of the late Emperor Manuel, took advantage of these disorders to aim at the crown, entered Constantinople, where he was received with almost divine ho
2 CONC nours, and overthrew the government. His arrival was celebrated by a massacre of 80,000 Latins in Constantinople, especially the Venetian merchants, which he made no attempt to stop. He allowed Alexios II to be crowned, but was re
2 CONC sponsible for the death of most of the young emperor's actual or potential defenders, including his mother, his half-sister and the Caesar, and refused to allow him the smallest voice in public affairs.
2 CONT
2 CONT The betrothal in 1180 of Alexios II to Agnes of France, daughter of Louis VII of France and his third wife Adèle of Champagne and at the time a child of nine, had not apparently been followed by their marriage. Andronikos was now
2 CONC  formally proclaimed as co-emperor, and not long afterwards, on the pretext that divided rule was injurious to the Empire, he caused Alexios II to be strangled with a bow-string (October 1183). During Alexius II's reign, the Byzan
2 CONC tine Empire was invaded by King Bela III losing Syrmia and Bosnia to the Kingdom of Hungary in AD 1181, later even Dalmatia was lost to the Venetians. Kilij Arslan II invaded the empire in AD 1182, defeating the Byzantines at the 
2 CONC Siege of Cotyaeum resulting in the Byzantine Empire losing Cotyaeum and Sozopolis.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Portrayal in fiction
2 CONT Alexios is a character in the historical novel Agnes of France (1980) by Greek writer Kostas Kyriazis. The novel describes the events of the reigns of Manuel I, Alexios II and Andronikos I through the eyes of Agnes.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT ^ An alternative date of birth occasionally given is 1168 (van Dieten 1975, p. 169), based on William of Tyre's statement that Alexios was 13 in 1180. For discussion, concluding that 14 September 1169 is correct, see (Wirth 1956);
2 CONC  (Magoulias 1984, p. 383). 
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT Oxford Dictionary of Byzantium, Oxford University Press, 1991  
2 CONT Magdalino, Paul (1993), The Empire of Manuel I Komnenos  
2 CONT Magoulias, Harry J., translator (1984), O city of Byzantium: annals of Niketas Choniates, Detroit: Wayne State University Press, ISBN 0814317642  
2 CONT van Dieten, J. L., editor (1975), Nicetae Choniatae historia, Berlin: De Gruyter  
2 CONT Wirth, Peter (1956), "Wann wurde Kaiser Alexios II. geboren? [When was Emperor Alexios II born?]", Byzantinische Zeitschrift 49: 65-67  
2 CONT Plate, William (1867). "Alexios II Komnenos". in William Smith. Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology. 1. Boston: Little, Brown and Company. pp. 130. http://quod.lib.umich.edu/cgi/t/text/pageviewer-idx?c=moa;cc=moa
2 CONC ;idno=acl3129.0001.001;q1=demosthenes;size=l;frm=frameset;seq=145;page=root;view=image.  
2 CONT This article incorporates text from the article "Alexius II" in the Encyclopædia Britannica, Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
