1 OCCU Byzantine Emperor, Keiser, Emperador de Bizancio (Constantinopla), Empereur, de Byzance, empereur byzantin, Byzantine Emperor 1185-1195 & 1203-1204, Kejsare i Byzan 1185-1195, Emperador del Imperio Bizantino, Kejsare i Byzan 1185-
2 CONC 95, 1203-04
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Исаак II Ангел (греч. Ισαάκιος Β’ Άγγελος; ок. 1156 — январь 1204) — византийский император в 1185—1195 и 1203—1204 годах. Стал первым представителем династии Ангелов, правившей страной 11 лет.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00027070&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isaac_II_Angelos
2 CONT
2 CONT =The Angelos family=
2 CONT
2 CONT One Manolis Angelos from Philadelphia, had issue:
2 CONT *A1. Konstantinos Angelos, admiral of Sicily, *ca 1085, +after VII.1166; m.ca 1120 Theodora Komnene (*5.1.1096/97)
2 CONT ** ...
2 CONT **B3. Andronikos Dukas Angelos, a military leader in Asia Minor, *ca 1222, +after 1185; m.before 1155 Euphrosyne Kastamonitissa (*ca 1125 +after 1195)
2 CONT ***C2. '''ISAAKIOS II Angelos, Emperor of Byzantium (1185-95)+(1203-04), *1155, +Constantinople 12.4.1204 in prison'''; 1m: NN; 2m: 1185 Margaret of Hungary (*1175 +after 1223)
2 CONT ****D1. [1m.] ALEXIOS IV Angelos, Emperor of Byzantium (1203-04) -cr Hagia Sophia, Constantinople 1.8.1203, *1182/3, +murdered 28.1.1204; was engaged to Eufemia of Tschernigov
2 CONT ****D2. [1m.] a daughter, a nun
2 CONT ****D3. [1m.] Eirene=Maria, *1172/81, +27.8.1208; 1m: 1192 Roger, Duke of Apulia (+1193); 2m: 1197 Duke Philip of Swabia, German King (+1208)
2 CONT ****D4. [2m.] Manuel Angelos, +1212
2 CONT ****D5. [2m.] Ioannes Angelos (Kalojan), Pr of Szerém, Comes de Keve, *ca 1203, +ca 1259; m.NN
2 CONT ***C3. Ioannes Angelos; m.NN
2 CONT **** ...
2 CONT ***C4. Theodoros Angelos, blinded 1184
2 CONT ***C5. Andronikos Angelos, blinded 1184
2 CONT ***C6. Konstantinos Angelos, blinded 1183
2 CONT ***C7. Eirene Angeline; m.before 1170 Ioannes Kantakuzenos
2 CONT ***C8. Theodora Angelina, +after 1195; m.1187 (div before 1190) Mgve Conrad I of Montferrat (+28.4.1192)
2 CONT ** ...
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Isaac II Angelos or Angelus (Greek: Ισαάκιος Β’ Άγγελος, Isaakios II Angelos) (September 1156 – January 1204) was Byzantine emperor from 1185 to 1195, and again from 1203 to 1204'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father Andronikos Dukas Angelos, a military leader in Asia Minor (c. 1122 – aft. 1185), married bef. 1155 Euphrosyne Kastamonitissa (c. 1125 – aft. 1195), was a son of Theodora Komnene (b. January 5, 1096/1097), the youngest d
2 CONC aughter of Emperor Alexios I Komnenos and Eirene Doukaina, by her marriage c. 1120 to Konstantinos Angelos, Admiral of Sicily (c. 1085 – aft. July 1166), son of one Manolis Angelos from Philadelphia. Thus Isaac was a member of the
2 CONC  extended imperial clan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Rising by revolt 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 First reign 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Second reign 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Historical reputation 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7 References 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8 External links 
2 CONT
2 CONT 9 Bibliography 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Rising by revolt
2 CONT
2 CONT During the brief reign of Andronikos I Komnenos, Isaac was involved (alongside his father and brothers) in the revolt of Nicaea and Prousa. Atypically, the Emperor did not punish him for this disloyalty, and Isaac remained at Cons
2 CONC tantinople.
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 11, 1185, during Andronikos' absence from the capital, the latter's lieutenant Stephanos Hagiochristophorites moved to arrest Isaac. Isaac killed Hagiochristophorites and took refuge in the church of Hagia Sophia. And
2 CONC ronikos, in some ways a capable ruler, was hated for his cruelty and his efforts to keep the aristocracy obedient. Isaac appealed to the populace, and a tumult arose which spread rapidly over the whole city. When Andronikos arrive
2 CONC d he found that during his absence he had lost popular support, and that Isaac had been proclaimed emperor. Andronikos attempted to flee by boat but was apprehended. Isaac handed him over to the people of the City, and he was kill
2 CONC ed on September 12, 1185.
2 CONT
2 CONT  First reign
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac II Angelos strengthened his position as emperor with dynastic marriages in 1185 and 1186. His niece, Eudokia Angelina, was married to Stefan, son of Stefan Nemanja of Serbia. Isaac's sister, Theodora, was married to the Ital
2 CONC ian marquis Conrad of Montferrat. In January 1186 Isaac himself married Margaret of Hungary (renamed Maria), daughter of king Béla III. Hungary was one of the empire's largest and most powerful neighbours, and Margaret also had th
2 CONC e benefit of high aristocratic descent, being related to the royal families of Kiev, the Holy Roman Empire, Italy, Provence, and earlier Byzantine dynasties.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac inaugurated his reign with a decisive victory over the Norman King of Sicily William II (on the banks of the Strymon, 7 September 1185), who had invaded the Balkans with 80,000 men and 200 ships towards the end of Andronicus
2 CONC  I's reign. Elsewhere his policy was less successful. In late 1185, he sent a fleet of 80 galleys to liberate his brother Alexius III from Acre, but it was destroyed by the Normans of Sicily. He then sent a fleet of 70 ships, but 
2 CONC it failed in its attempt to recover Cyprus from the rebellious noble Isaac Comnenus, thanks to Norman interference.
2 CONT
2 CONT The oppressiveness of his taxes, increased to pay his armies and finance his marriage, resulted in the Vlach-Bulgarian Rebellion late in 1185. The rebellion led to the establishment of the Second Bulgarian Empire under the Asen dy
2 CONC nasty. In 1187, Alexios Branas, the victor over the Normans, was sent against the rebels but turned his arms against his master, and attempted to seize Constantinople, only to be defeated and slain by Isaac's brother-in-law Conrad
2 CONC  of Montferrat. Also in 1187, an agreement was made with Venice, in which the republic would provide 40-100 galleys at six months' notice in exchange for favorable trading concessions. As each Venetian galley was manned by 140 oar
2 CONC smen, that means there were about 18,000 Venetians still in the empire even after Manuel I's arrests.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The emperor's attention was next demanded in the east, where several claimants to the throne successively rose and fell. In 1189 the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick I Barbarossa sought and obtained permission to lead his troops on th
2 CONC e Third Crusade through the Byzantine Empire; but he had no sooner crossed the border than Isaac, who had meanwhile sought an alliance with Saladin, threw every impediment in his way. In retaliation, Barbarossa's army occupied the
2 CONC  city of Philippopolis and defeated a Byzantine army of 3,000 men that attempted to recapture the city.[2] Thus compelled by force of arms, Isaac II was forced to fulfil his engagements in 1190. By 1196, Isaac II had allowed the o
2 CONC nce powerful Byzantine navy to decline to only 30 galleys.
2 CONT
2 CONT The next five years were disturbed by continued warfare with Bulgaria, against which Isaac led several expeditions in person. In spite of their promise, these ventures had little effect, and on one occasion in 1190 Isaac barely es
2 CONC caped with his life. While preparing for yet another offensive against Bulgaria in 1195, Alexios Angelos, the emperor's older brother, taking advantage of the latter's absence from camp on a hunting expedition, proclaimed himself 
2 CONC emperor, and was readily recognised by the soldiers as Emperor Alexios III. Isaac was blinded and imprisoned in Constantinople.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Second reign
2 CONT
2 CONT After eight years of captivity, he was raised from his dungeon to his throne once more after the arrival of the Fourth Crusade and the flight of Alexios III from the capital. But both mind and body had been enfeebled by confinemen
2 CONC t, and his son Alexios IV Angelos was associated on the throne as the effective monarch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Heavily beholden to the crusaders, Alexios IV was unable to meet his obligations and his vacillation caused him to lose the support of both his crusader allies and his subjects. At the end of January, 1204, the influential court o
2 CONC fficial Alexios Doukas Mourtzouphlos took advantage of riots in the capital to imprison Alexios IV and seize the throne as Emperor Alexios V. At this point Isaac II died, allegedly of shock, while Alexios IV was strangled on Janua
2 CONC ry 28 or 29.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Historical reputation
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac has the reputation of one of the most unsuccessful princes that occupied the Byzantine throne. Surrounded by a crowd of slaves, mistresses and flatterers, he permitted his empire to be administered by unworthy favourites, wh
2 CONC ile he squandered the money wrung from his provinces on costly buildings and expensive gifts to the churches of his metropolis. During his reign the empire lost Lefkada, Kefallonia, and Zakynthos to the Normans in 1185. Then he lo
2 CONC st Bulgaria to the Vlachs and Bulgarians in 1186. After that Cilicia was retaken by the Armenians and Cyprus wrested from the empire by the Franks.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Family
2 CONT
2 CONT The identity of Isaac II's first wife is unknown, but her name, Herina (i.e., Eirene), is found on the necrology of Speyer Cathedral, where their daughter Irene is interred. (It must be noted, however, that it would have been extr
2 CONC emely unusual for a mother and daughter to bear the same name, unless the mother's name was monastic.)[3] Isaac's wife may have been a member of the Palaiologos family.[4] A possible foreign origin is also given to her due to havi
2 CONC ng the same name as her daughter.[5][6] Their third child was born in 1182 or 1183 and she was dead or divorced by 1185, when Isaac remarried. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne Angelina, a nun. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Irene Angelina, married first to Roger III of Sicily, and secondly to Philip of Swabia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexios IV Angelos. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By his second wife, Margaret of Hungary (renamed Maria), Isaac II had two sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT John Angelos (b. ca. 1193 - d. 1259). He migrated to Hungary and ruled over Syrmia and Bacs (1227-42) as a vassal of king Béla IV of Hungary. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Manuel Angelos (b. after 1195 - d. 1212) 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac has the reputation of one of the most unsuccessful princes that occupied the Byzantine throne. Surrounded by a crowd of slaves, mistresses and flatterers, he permitted his empire to be administered by unworthy favourites, wh
2 CONC ile he squandered the money wrung from his provinces on costly buildings and expensive gifts to the churches of his metropolis. During his reign the empire lost Lefkada, Kefallonia, and Zakynthos to the Normans in 1185. Then he lo
2 CONC st Bulgaria to the Vlachs and Bulgarians in 1186. After that Cilicia was retaken by the Armenians and Cyprus wrested from the empire by the Franks.
