1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donika_Kastrioti
2 CONT
2 CONT Donika Arianiti (also known as Donika Kastrioti and Andronika Arianiti)[a], (born 1428 – died 1506) was an Albanian noblewoman and the spouse of Albanian leader and national hero Skanderbeg. She was the daughter of Gjergj Arianiti
2 CONC , an earlier leader in the ongoing revolt against the Ottomans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT The wedding of Donika and Skanderbeg in 1451
2 CONT Donika was born in Kaninë, in 1428. Her father, Gjergj Arianiti was a member of the Arianiti family whose domain stretched across the Shkumbin valley and the old Via Egnatia road and reached to the east today's Bitola. Her mother,
2 CONC  Maria Muzaka was a member of the Muzaka family whose domain was the Myzeqe region.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT A month after the Treaty of Gaeta, on 21 April 1451, Skanderbeg married Donika, and thus strengthened the ties with the Arianiti family,[2] in the Eastern Orthodox Ardenica Monastery,[3][4] in Lushnje, present-day southwestern Alb
2 CONC ania. Later her sister Angelina married Serbian ruler Stefan Branković. She is venerated as a saint in the Serbian Orthodox Church.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Ottoman conquest of Albania, the Kastriotis were given peerage in the Kingdom of Naples.[6] They obtained a feudal domain, the Duchy of San Pietro in Galatina and the County of Soleto (Province of Lecce, Italy).[7] Gjon 
2 CONC Kastrioti II, Donika's and Skanderbeg's only child, married Jerina Branković, the daughter of Lazar Branković, Despot of Serbia.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT  Her name is mentioned as Andronica Comneniates in Gjon Muzaka's work about the Muzaka family.[8] Oliver Jens Schmitt names her Andronika Arianiti in his biographical work on Skanderbeg.[9]
2 CONT References
2 CONT  Anamali, Skënder (2002), Historia e popullit shqiptar në katër vëllime (in Albanian), vol. I, Botimet Toena, pp. 255–257, OCLC 52411919
2 CONT  Frashëri, Kristo (2002), Gjergj Kastrioti Skënderbeu: jeta dhe vepra, 1405–1468 (in Albanian), Botimet Toena, p. 181, ISBN 99927-1-627-4
2 CONT  Elsie, Robert (2000). A dictionary of Albanian religion, mythology, and folk culture. New York University Press. p. 14. ISBN 0-8147-2214-8.
2 CONT  Gjika, Ilirjan. "Manastiri i Ardenices" (in Albanian). Retrieved 28 July 2010.
2 CONT  Elsie, Robert (2001). A dictionary of Albanian religion, mythology and folk culture. C. Hurst. p. 9. ISBN 978-1-85065-570-1. Retrieved 26 November 2010.
2 CONT  Gibbon, Edward (1901) [1802], The decline and fall of the Roman empire, P. F. Collier & Son, p. 467, OCLC 317326240
2 CONT  Runciman, Steven (1990), The fall of Constantinople, 1453, Cambridge University Press, pp. 183–185, ISBN 978-0-521-39832-9
2 CONT  John Musachi: Brief Chronicle on the Descendants of our Musachi Dynasty Archived September 10, 2010, at the Wayback Machine
2 CONT  Schmitt Oliver, Skanderbeg, Der neue Alexander auf dem Balkan, Verlag Friedrich Pustet, 2009, p. 45
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/254764178/andronika_(donika)-kastrioti
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronika Arianiti-Muzaka is the daughter of Gjergj Arianiti, of Albania and Maria Muzaka, of Myzeqe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Donika was born in Kaninë, in 1428. Her father, Gjergj Arianiti was a member of the Arianiti family whose domain stretched across the Shkumbin valley and the old Via Egnatia road and reached to the east today's Bitola. Her mother,
2 CONC  Maria Muzaka was a member of the Muzaka family whose domain was the Myzeqe region.
2 CONT
2 CONT A month after the Treaty of Gaeta, on 21 April 1451, Skanderbeg married Donika, and thus strengthened the ties with the Arianiti family, in the Eastern Orthodox Ardenica Monastery, in Lushnje, present-day southwestern Albania. Lat
2 CONC er her sister Angelina married Serbian ruler Stefan Branković. She is venerated as a saint in the Serbian Orthodox Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Ottoman conquest of Albania, the Kastriotis were given peerage in the Kingdom of Naples. They obtained a feudal domain, the Duchy of San Pietro in Galatina and the County of Soleto (Province of Lecce, Italy). Gjon Kastri
2 CONC oti II, Donika's and Skanderbeg's only child, married Jerina Branković, the daughter of Lazar Branković, Despot of Serbia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Donika had a close friendship with the second wife of King Ferdinand I of Naples, Joanna of Aragon who is also the sister of Ferdinand of Aragon. After the beginning of the Italian War of 1494–1498, Donika was forced to leave Napl
2 CONC es and arrived in Valencia around 1501 along with her grandchild, Alfonso Kastrioti. They found refuge in the royal palace.
2 CONT
