1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_V_of_Denmark '''Eric V Klipping'''] was King of Denmark (1259–1286) and son of King Christopher I of Denmark. From 1259-1266, he ruled under the auspices of his competent mother,
2 CONC  Margaret Sambiria (1230-1282). Between 1261 and 1262, the young King Eric was a prisoner in Holstein following a military defeat. Afterwards, he lived in Brandenburg, where he was initially held captive by John I, Margrave of Bra
2 CONC ndenburg (c. 1213-1266) 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Nickname===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The king’s nickname''' ”Klipping” or ”Glipping” refers to a medieval coin that has become ”clipped” (a "clipped penny") or cut in order to indicate devaluation. The nickname is an unkind reference to his lack of trustworthiness
2 CONC . He "short-changed" his people and the monarchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Regency===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''When his father''' King Christopher I was murdered in 1259, Prince Eric was too young to rule in his own right. The Danish court appointed his mother, Queen Margaret Sambiria (Danish: Sprænghest) as regent. She was the daughter
2 CONC  of Sambor II, Duke of Pomerania and Matilda of Mecklenberg, and was a clever and intelligent woman. Immediately, she had to fight to keep her son on the throne from two powerful enemies; Archbishop Jacob Erlandsen (ca. 1220-1274)
2 CONC  and Erik Abelsøn who was Duke of Schleswig from 1260 until his death in 1272. Archbishop Erlandsen had excommunicated the bishop who had anointed young Eric as king. Duke Eric was a nephew of King Christopher and had been in freq
2 CONC uent conflict with the king. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Taking advantage of''' the situation, Chief Jaromar II of Rügen (c. 1218–1260) gathered an army of Wends and invaded Zealand. Queen Margaret raised an army, but was soundly defeated in 1259 near Ringsted. Jarimar went on to att
2 CONC ack and pillage Copenhagen later that year. He shipped his army to Skåne inorder to continue his campaign. Unfortunately for him, he encountered the wrath of a farmer's wife, who killed him outright. The Wends fled back to Rűgen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Believing the Wendish''' incursion showed the Queen was weak, Duke Eric rebelled. The queen was forced to raise another army and march to Jutland to put the Duke in his place. She defeated the Duke, and while he negotiated a tr
2 CONC uce with her, he gathered allies in northern Germany to help him attack. The combined forces defeated Queen Margaret in 1261 at the Battle of Lohede south of Danevirke in Schleswig-Holstein. She and her son were captured and were 
2 CONC forced to cede royal properties in southern Jutland to secure their release. [6]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1260, Queen Margaret''' had released Archbishop Erlandsen from prison thinking he would be grateful, but he subsequently issued an interdict over all of Denmark trying to force the Queen and her son, King Eric off the throne
2 CONC . In 1263, acting as regent of Denmark, the Queen wrote to Pope Urban IV asking him to intervene with Archbishop Erlandsen. After several years of quibbling, the Pope agreed to several items that the Queen wanted. He issued a disp
2 CONC ensation to alter the terms of the Danish succession that would permit women to inherit the Danish throne. This would make it possible for one of King Eric's sisters to become the reigning Queen of Denmark in the event of his deat
2 CONC h, because he had no children. Although Pope Urban IV gave his consent, it never became an issue. King Eric's son, Prince Eric Menved, eventually succeeded to the Danish throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Reign===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''As an adult ruler''', King Eric V tried to enforce his power over the church and nobility. In the 1270s, the King attacked Småland. His conflict with the church was brought to a satisfying result, with the help of the Pope. By 
2 CONC 1282, he had so offended the nobles throughout Denmark that he was forced to accept a charter (Danish: håndfæstning - a kind of a Danish Magna Carta) which limited his authority and guaranteed the ancient rights and customs that p
2 CONC reserved the power of the nobles. The King signed the charter at Nyborg Castle, recognized as Denmark’s first-ever constitution in existence. However at the time of King Eric's death, the rights and guarantees the 1282 charter wou
2 CONC ld lose their effectiveness, since the next king would not be bound by the same agreement.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Mysterious death===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Legend has it that''' several nobles swore an oath that they would murder Eric in revenge for personal slights or policies the king enforced that they did not like. Chief among the conspirators was marshal (Danish: marsk) Stig 
2 CONC Andersen Hvide and Jacob Nielsen, Count of Halland. They paid Rane Jonsen (1254-1294) one of the king's companions, to keep them informed as to the king's activities, in order to fulfill their oath. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''November 1286 found''' the king at Viborg, in central Jutland. After a long day's hunt in the countryside led by Rane Jonsen, the king and a few attendants couldn't find their way back to the king's farm at Viborg. Rane suggest
2 CONC ed that they take shelter for the night of 22 November 1286 in the church barn in the village of Finderup (Finderup Lade). The assassins, dressed as Franciscan friars, were kept informed as to the kings' whereabouts and waited for
2 CONC  everyone to settle down for the night. Once the king fell asleep, they rushed from their hiding places and stabbed and hacked the king to death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Tradition has it that''' he received 56 stab wounds. The folktale that grew up around this event has Stig Andersen personally striking the first blows in revenge for King Eric's seduction of Stig's wife while Stig himself was o
2 CONC ff with the king's army. Eric's bloody corpse was discovered the next morning.[13][dubious – discuss]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The court immediately''' blamed the nation's most powerful noblemen Stig Andersen Hvide and Count Jacob of Halland and outlawed them and seven others. Only one was accused of killing the king, the others were accused of involve
2 CONC ment. Whether they actually had anything to do with the murder remains a mystery. Stig Hvide fled the country to take up piracy. Certainly Stig Hvide was not the only person who had reason to want to see King Eric eliminated. Vald
2 CONC emar IV, who King Eric was forced to accept as Duke of Schleswig in 1283, as well as many of Archbishop Jakob Erlandsen's appointments to bishoprics, remained bitter enemies of the king until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Issue===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Eric V married''' Agnes of Brandenburg (c. 1257–1304) on 11 November 1273 at Schleswig. She was the daughter of John I, Margrave of Brandenburg (d. 1266) and Brigitte of Saxony. The marriage was probably agreed upon during
2 CONC  King Eric's captivity in Brandenburg by Agnes' father from 1262 to 1264. Tradition claims that the King was released from captivity on his promise to marry Agnes without a dowry.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''They had the following issue:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT *Richeza of Denmark (c.1272 - 27 October 1308), married Lord Nicholas II of Werle. A descendant of her daughter Sophia, her great-great-great-grandson, Christian I, would one day become King of Denmark, Norway, and Sweden.
2 CONT *Eric VI of Denmark (1274–1319)
2 CONT *Christopher II of Denmark (1276–1332)
2 CONT *Martha of Denmark (1278–1341), married King Birger of Sweden.
2 CONT *Katharine of Denmark (c.1281 - 1283), died young.
2 CONT *Valdemar of Denmark
2 CONT *Elisabeth of Denmark
2 CONT
2 CONT /////
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.friesian.com/germania.htm#norse
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Erik "Klipping" of Denmark''' (Estridsøn), King of Denmark, (NB, the only Danish king that was named Estridsen was Svend Estridsen, https://da.wikipedia.org/wiki/Svend_Estridsen, and yes, it doesn't exist any dynasty called tha
2 CONC t either, yet, some curators goes bananas if anyone deletes this totally misguiding suffix Estridsøn in this profile. It doesn't belong here in this profile at all and it does more harm than good, as someone actually might come to
2 CONC  believe that his mother's name was Estrid).
2 CONT
