1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Małgorzata Przemyślidka''' – '''królewna czeska''', '''księżna brzesko-legnicka'''. http://pl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ma%C5%82gorzata_Przemy%C5%9Blidka
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Markéta Přemyslovna''' - byla dcerou českého krále Václava II. a Guty (Judith) Habsburské, od roku 1308 manželkou Boleslava III. Lehnického, '''českou princeznou''' a '''lehnickou kněžnou'''. http://cs.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark%C
2 CONC 3%A9ta_P%C5%99emyslovna
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret of Bohemia (1296-1322) From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret of Bohemia (Czech: Markéta Přemyslovna, Polish: Małgorzata Przemyślidka) (February 21 1296 - April 8, 1322) was a daughter of Wenceslaus II of Bohemia and his first wife, Judith of Habsburg. Her paternal grandparents were
2 CONC  Ottokar II of Bohemia and Kunigunda of Slavonia, her maternal grandparents were Rudolph I of Germany and Gertrude of Hohenburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1308, Margaret married Bolesław III the Generous. The betrothal had been her father's doing. Since Bolesław's arrival to the Bohemian court and after his betrothal to Margaret, the King clearly favored him; this attitude caused
2 CONC  fear between the closest male relatives of the King, who seen the young Duke of Legnica in a potential rival for the throne. Although Wenceslaus II had a son seemed him irrelevant, the sudden death of the King in 1305 and one yea
2 CONC r later the murdered of his son and successor Wenceslaus III in Olomouc gained him an unexpectedly importance. Bolesław began his fight for the Bohemian throne taking the title of "haeres Regni Poloniae" (heir of the Polish Kingdo
2 CONC m). The throne of Bohemia passed to Henry of Carinthia, who was married to Margaret's eldest sister, Anna of Bohemia. Henry and Anna were only on the throne for a year before Rudolf of Habsburg overthrew them. He married Elisabeth
2 CONC  Richeza of Poland; who was Margaret's step-mother. By 1307, Rudolf had died so Henry and Anna were invited back but were still not secure. They turned their attentions to Margaret and Anna's sister Elisabeth. Elisabeth was young 
2 CONC and unmarried, Anna and Henry wanted her to marry Otto of Löbdaburg for political reasons but Elisabeth refused. Instead, Elisabeth married John of Luxembourg who overthrew Henry and Anna once and for all. They went to live in Car
2 CONC inthia were Anna died in 1313, childless. John and Elisabeth became King and Queen of Bohemia. They had many children, among them were Charles IV, Holy Roman Emperor and Bonne of Bohemia; by now any chances of Bolesław and Margare
2 CONC t becoming King and Queen of Bohemia were gone. Margaret died while giving birth to her youngest child. In 1326, Bolesław married secondly with Katharina (d. bef. 5 March 1358), daughter of Mladen III Šubić[1] by his wife Jelena, 
2 CONC in turn daughter of King Stefan Uroš III Dečanski of Serbia[2]. Through her father, Katharina was also first cousin of Stjepan Tvrtko I, the first King of Bosnia[3]. They had no children. In his will, Bolesław left the Duchy of Br
2 CONC ieg to his widow, who ruled until her own death.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret and Boleslaw had three children:[4][5] Wenceslaus I (b. ca. 1318 - d. 2 June 1364). Louis I the Fair (b. ca. 1321 - d. 6/23 December 1398). Nikolaus (b. and d. Hradec Králové, 7 April 1322).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_of_Bohemia,_Duchess_of_Wroclaw
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1308, Margaret married Bolesław III the Generous. The betrothal had been her father's doing.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since Bolesław's arrival at the Bohemian court and after his betrothal to Margaret, the King clearly favored him; this attitude caused fear between the closest male relatives of the King, who saw the young Duke of Legnica as a pot
2 CONC ential rival for the throne. Although Wenceslaus II had a son who seemed to render him irrelevant, the sudden death of the King in 1305 and one year later the murder of his son and successor Wenceslaus III in Olomouc gained him an
2 CONC  unexpected importance. Bolesław began his fight for the Bohemian throne taking the title of "haeres Regni Poloniae" (heir of the Polish Kingdom).
2 CONT
2 CONT The throne of Bohemia passed to Henry of Carinthia, who was married to Margaret's eldest sister, Anna of Bohemia. Henry and Anna were only on the throne for a year before Rudolf of Habsburg overthrew them. He married Elisabeth Ric
2 CONC heza of Poland; who was Margaret's stepmother. By 1307, Rudolf had died so Henry and Anna were invited back but were still not secure. They turned their attentions to Margaret and Anna's sister Elisabeth. Elisabeth was young and u
2 CONC nmarried, Anna and Henry wanted her to marry Otto of Löbdaburg for political reasons but Elisabeth refused. Instead, Elisabeth married John of Luxembourg who overthrew Henry and Anna once and for all. They went to live in Carinthi
2 CONC a were Anna died in 1313, childless. John and Elisabeth became King and Queen of Bohemia. They had many children - among them were Charles IV, Holy Roman Emperor and Bonne of Bohemia; by now any chances of Bolesław and Margaret be
2 CONC coming King and Queen of Bohemia were gone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret died one day after giving birth to her youngest child. Bolesław remarried in 1326, taking Katarina Šubić (d. bef. 5 March 1358) as his second wife.
