1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Ripsimia of Armenia===
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Ripsimia (also known as Hripsime) was an Armenian who married the Bulgarian Count Comita Nikola, probably governor of Sofia and had a son, tsar Samuil of Bulgaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ripsimia_of_Armenia
2 CONT
2 CONT http://bg.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A0%D0%B8%D0%BF%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%8F
2 CONT
2 CONT Rypsimia (in Greek : 'Ριψίμη) is the wife commit Nikola and mother of Comitopuli  - brothers David , Moses , Aaron and Samuel . Little is known about her biography. Her name is learned from the additions of Bishop Mikhail Devolski
2 CONC  to the chronicle of Ioan Skilitsa . She died 993 years ago, dating from the famous Samuel inscription from the village of German , Prespansko, placed by Samuel in memory of his parents and his brother David at their grave.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was named after the particularly popular Saint Ripsime in Armenia , who died martyred in 290 years. This fact serves as an argument for the thesis about the Armenian origin of the Ohrid dynasty. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *1.  Zlatarski, V. “History of the Bulgarian State”. Vol. I, Part 2, pp. 606 - 607 (Sofia 1971). [http://www.promacedonia.org/vz1b/vz1b_6_1.html link]
2 CONT *2.  Andreev, Y. Lazarov, Iv. Pavlov, Pl. Who is who in medieval Bulgaria. Enlightenment, Sofia, 1995, p. 325.
2 CONT *3. Adontz, Nicholas (1938). Samuel l'Armenien, roi des Bulgares. MAR Bclsmp (in French) (39): 37.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Inscription 
2 CONT
2 CONT English: National Historical Museum of Bulgaria, in Sofia:
2 CONT
2 CONT >In the year 6523 (= 1015-1016) since the creation of the world, this fortress, built and made by Ivan, Tsar of Bulgaria, was renewed with the help and the prayers of Our Most Holy Lady and through the intercession of her twelve s
2 CONC upreme Apostles. The fortress was built as a haven and for the salvation of the lives of the Bulgarians. The work on the fortress of Bitola commenced on the twentieth day of October and ended on the [...] This Tsar was Bulgarian b
2 CONC y birth, grandson of the pious Nikola and '''Ripsimia''', son of Aaron, who was brother of Samuil, Tsar of Bulgaria, the two who routed the Greek army of Emperor Basil II at Stipone where gold was taken [...] and in [...] this Tsa
2 CONC r was defeated by Emperor Basil in 6522 (= 1014) since the creation of the world in Klyutch and died at the end of the summer.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT * NOT a known child of https://www.geni.com/people/Ashot-II-the-Iron-king-of-Armenia/6000000002187821487
2 CONT * https://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00332111&tree=LEO cites
2 CONT * Europäische Stammtafeln, J.A. Stargardt Verlag, Marburg, Schwennicke, Detlev (Ed.). 2:168 
2 CONT *I. Mladjov, "Reconsidering Agatha, Wife of Eadward the Exile," The Plantagenet Connection 11 (2003) 1-85.  [https://www.academia.edu/5629765/I._Mladjov_Reconsidering_Agatha_Wife_of_Eadward_the_Exile_The_Plantagenet_Connection_11_
2 CONC 2003_1-85?source=swp_share link] “A partial Armenian descent for Samuil and his brothers is nevertheless likely through their mother Ripsimija (Hripsimē), whose name is typically Armenian, and who may have originated in a communit
2 CONC y of Armenian Paulicians resettled in the Balkans for strategic purposes by the Byzantine emperors.”
2 CONT * https://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p834.htm#i25061 cites
2 CONT *[S54] Middle & Far East Families, Armenia. Maurice Boddy Web Site showing Middle & Far East Families, 7/7/2007. [http://mauriceboddy.org.uk/Armenia.htm link] [ARME262] Ashot VI yerkat [=the iron] was King Ashot II of Armenia (914
2 CONC -28), and later (919) granted the title of Shahanshah (king of kings, or Great King). He married (917) [SION512] Marie of Albania (daughter Prince Isaac Sevada I, see SIOUNIE). He died 928. [ARME272] Hripsime (born after 917, daug
2 CONC hter of Ashot VI) married [BULG271] Nikola (born c.906, see BULGARIA).
2 CONT *[S7908] Unknown author, Journal of Ancient and Medieval Studies, Vol. VI, p. 78; Descents from Antiquity, The Augustan Society, Chart T1. [https://store.augustansociety.org/shopping/product/350 link to purchase]
2 CONT *[S54] Middle & Far East Families, Bulgaria. Maurice Boddy Web Site showing Middle & Far East Families, 7/7/2007.
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Nicholas_(komes)_ compiled errors in interpreting cited sources and submitted to Wikipedia’s “Talk” page.  The cited sources are:
2 CONT * 1. The English Wikipedia article for Samuel of Bulgaria cites Samuel l'Arménien, roi des Bulgares by Samuel Adontz as the source for this connection, but that is incorrect. Adontz writes that her name is a popular Armenian name 
2 CONC but says nothing more (pg. 386 in book, pg. 42) https://archive.org/details/Adontz2018SamuelRoiDesBulgares/page/n41/mode/2up
2 CONT *2. French Wikipedia has: “ Selon Christian Settipani, Achot d'Arménie et Marie de Katchen seraient les parents de Rhipsime, épouse de Nikola Kumet, eux-mêmes parents de Samuel de Bulgarie.”  We asked Mr. Settipani about that on 2
2 CONC 0 February 2020.  He responded: “Unfortunately, I don't know who is the author of the wikipedia notice, but he obviously didn't understand what I wrote at all.  I said: "Rhipsimé, whose name refers as an Armenian, "but not necessa
2 CONC rily a noble one") (p. 282, n. 3).  That is all. I say nothing more on this subject and there is nothing more to say!"  Ref:  Continuité‪ des élites à Byzance durant les siècles obscurs: les princes caucasiens et l'empire du VIe a
2 CONC u IXe siècle.  Christopher Settipani.  De Boccard, 2006 - Princes - 638 page‬s. https://books.google.com/books?id=IVdoAAAAMAAJ&focus=searchwithinvolume&q=Rhipsime+
2 CONT *3. French Wikipedia has: “Selon le chroniqueur arménien Stéphane de Taron, le comte Nikola est originaire de la région arménienne de Derdjan. Marié avec Ripsime d'Arménie, fille d'Achot II Bagratouni et de sa femme Marie de Katch
2 CONC en1,2.  “But there is no mention of children of Ashot ll in de Taron: https://archive.org/details/desstephanosvont00step/page/124/mode/2up
