1 OCCU Цар на Македонија 997-1014, владетел на България (997-1014), Czar, Ruled with brothers until 987, ruled 980-1014, Tsar, de Macedoine, Czar of Macedonia, български болярин
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_of_Bulgaria
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00332107&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign 997 – 6 October 1014 Died 6 October 1014(1014-10-06) Place of death Prilep, First Bulgarian Empire, present-day Republic of Macedonia Predecessor Roman of Bulgaria Successor Gavril Radomir
2 CONT *Consort
2 CONT # Kosara,
2 CONT # Agatha
2 CONT
2 CONT * Offspring
2 CONT # Gavril Radomir
2 CONT # Theodora Kosara
2 CONT # Miroslava
2 CONT # Katun Anastazya
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal House Cometopuli
2 CONT * Father Comita Nikola
2 CONT * Mother Ripsimia of Armenia
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel[1] (also Samuil representing Bulgarian: Самуил, pronounced [samuˈil]) was the Emperor (Tsar) of the First Bulgarian Empire from 997 to 6 October 1014. From 980[2] to 997, he was a general for Roman I of Bulgaria, the second
2 CONC  surviving son of Emperor Peter I of Bulgaria, and co-ruled with him, as Roman bestowed him command of the army and the real authority.[3] Samuel struggled to preserve his country's independence from the Byzantine Empire. His rule
2 CONC  was characterized by constant warfare against the Byzantines and their similarly ambitious ruler Basil II.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his early years Samuel managed to inflict several major defeats on the Byzantines and to launch offensive campaigns on their territory.[4] In the late 10th century, the Bulgarian armies conquered the Serb principality of Duklja
2 CONC [5] and led campaigns against the Kingdoms of Croatia and Hungary. But from 1001, he was forced mainly to defend the Empire against the superior Byzantine armies. Samuel died of a heart attack on 6 October 1014, two months after t
2 CONC he catastrophe at Kleidion, and Bulgaria was fully subjugated by Basil II four years later.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel was considered "invincible in power and unsurpassable in strength".[7][8] Similar comment was made even in Constantinople, where John Kyriotes Geometres penned a poem offering a punning comparison between the Bulgarian Empe
2 CONC ror and a comet which appeared in 989.[9][10]
2 CONT
2 CONT During Samuel's reign, Bulgaria gained control of most of the Balkans (except Thrace) and southern Greece. He moved the capital from Skopje to Ohrid[11][4], which had been the cultural and military centre of southwestern Bulgaria 
2 CONC since Boris I's rule.[12] He also made the city the seat of the Bulgarian Patriarchate. Although Samuel's reign brought the end of the First Bulgarian Empire, he is regarded as a heroic ruler in Bulgaria.[13][14]
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 The Cometopuli
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.1 Death of Aaron and Samuel's ascent to power
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Co-rule with Roman
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Emperor
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.1 War against Serbs and Croats
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.2 Advance of the Byzantines
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.3 War with Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.4 Further Byzantine successes
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.5 Disaster at Kleidion
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Aftermath
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Family
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 Grave
2 CONT
2 CONT 7 Nomenclature
