2 CONT
2 CONT  The Cometopuli
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel was the fourth[15] and youngest son of Comita Nikola, most likely Count of Sredets (Sofia)[16] and the Armenian[17] princess[18] Ripsimia.[19] His father, who had close ties to the royal court in Preslav[20], died in 970. A
2 CONC t that time there was no Emperor in Preslav as Peter I had died on 30 January 970 and his sons Boris and Roman were in Constantinople.[21]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the same year[22] Samuel and his three brothers David, Moses and Aaron rebelled against John I Tzimisces's campaign that aimed to "liberate" Bulgaria from the Kievan Rus', which they saw as an attempt by Byzantium to seize powe
2 CONC r in Bulgaria.[23] Indeed the following year, the Byzantines deceived[24] Boris II and forced him to abdicate in Constantinople.[25] Although Tzimisces announced the annexation[26] of Bulgaria, he only controlled the northeastern 
2 CONC half of the country, including the capital Preslav and the seat of patriarchate Drastar (Silistra).[27][28] The lands west of the Iskar River[29][30] remained under the control of the four brothers, who were called Cometopuli (Kom
2 CONC etopouloi, i.e., "the sons of the Count") in Byzantine sources.[31] In 973, the Cometopuli sent envoys to the Holy Roman Emperor Otto I in Quedlinburg in an attempt to secure the protection of their lands.[32]
2 CONT
2 CONT The brothers ruled together in a tetrarchy.[33] David ruled the southernmost regions and led the defense of one of the most dangerous border areas, around Thessaloniki and Thessaly.[33] The centres of his possessions were Prespa a
2 CONC nd Kastoria. Moses ruled from Strumitsa,[33] which would be a base for attacks on the Aegean coast and Serres. Aaron ruled from Sredets,[33] and was to defend the main road from Adrianople to Belgrade, and to attack Thrace. Samuel
2 CONC  ruled northwestern Bulgaria from the strong fortress of Vidin. He was also to organize the liberation of the conquered areas to the east and of Preslav.[34] Some records suggest that David played a major role in this tumultuous p
2 CONC eriod of Bulgarian history.[35]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Byzantines seize the capital Preslav.Even before the fall of Preslav, the Bulgarians defeated Byzantium's Asian army, led by the eunuch Peter, on the outskirts of Plovdiv in 970.[36] From 971 to 975, there were many skirmishes
2 CONC  and minor battles, and the Bulgarian detachments harassed the Byzantine possessions in the Balkans.[37] After John I Tzimiskes died on 11 January 976, the Cometopuli launched an assault along the whole border. But within the firs
2 CONC t weeks David was killed by Vlach vagrants and Moses was fatally injured by a stone during the siege of Serres.[38]Moses died in 986. [39]
2 CONT
2 CONT Their actions to the south detained many Byzantine troops and eased Samuel's liberation of northeastern Bulgaria; the Byzantine commander was defeated and retreated to Crimea.[40][41] Any Bulgarian nobles and officials who had not
2 CONC  opposed the Byzantine conquest of the region were executed, and the war continued north of the Danube until the enemy was scattered and Bulgarian rule was restored.[42]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Death of Aaron and Samuel's ascent to power
2 CONT
2 CONT After its serious defeats in the Balkans, the Byzantine Empire descended into civil war. The commander of the Asian army, Bardas Scleros, rebelled in Asia Minor and sent troops under his son Romanus in Thrace to besiege Constantin
2 CONC ople. The new Emperor Basil II did not have enough manpower to fight both the Bulgarians and the rebels and resorted to treason, conspiracy and complicated diplomatic plots.[43]
2 CONT
2 CONT During the civil wars in the Byzantine Empire, the Bulgarians not only regained their positions on the Balkans, but advanced deep into enemy territory.Basil II was making many promises to the Bulgarians and Scleros to divert them 
2 CONC from allying against him.[44] Aaron, the eldest living Comitopulus, was tempted by an alliance with the Byzantines and the opportunity to seize power in Bulgaria for himself. He held land in Thrace, where the Byzantines could thre
2 CONC aten it. Basil reached an agreement with Aaron, who asked to marry Basil's sister to seal it. But Basil instead sent the wife of one of his officials with the bishop of Sebaste. The deceit was uncovered and the bishop was killed.[
2 CONC 45]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nonetheless, negotiations proceeded and concluded in a peace agreement. The historian Scylitzes wrote that Aaron wanted sole power and "sympathized with the Romans".[46] Samuel learned of the conspiracy and conflict between the tw
2 CONC o brothers was inevitable. The quarrel broke out in the vicinity of Dupnitsa on 14 June 976 and ended with the annihilation of Aaron's family. Only his son, Ivan Vladislav, survived because Samuil's son Gavril Radomir pleaded on h
2 CONC is behalf.[47] Now, practically all power and authority in the state was with Samuel, and the danger of internal conflict was eliminated.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, another theory suggests that Aaron participated in the battle of the Gates of Trajan which took place ten years later. According to that theory Aaron was killed on 14 June 987 or 988.[48][49]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Co-rule with Roman
2 CONT
2 CONT Main article: battle of the Gates of Trajan
2 CONT
2 CONT The Byzantine cavalry fighting in northern Bulgaria.After the Byzantine plan to use Aaron to cause instability in Bulgaria failed, they tried to encourage the rightful heirs to the throne,[50] Boris II and Roman, to oppose Samuil.
2 CONC  Basil II hoped that they would win the support of the nobles and isolate Samuel or perhaps even start a Bulgarian civil war.[51] Boris and Roman were sent back in 986[52] but while they were passing through a forest around the bo
2 CONC rder, Boris was killed by Bulgarian guards who were misled by his Byzantine clothing. Roman, who was walking some distance behind, managed to identify himself to the guards.[53]
2 CONT
2 CONT Roman was taken to Vidin, where he was proclaimed Emperor of Bulgaria.[54] Samuel became his first assistant and general and together they gathered an army and fought the Byzantines.[55] During his captivity, Roman had been castra
2 CONC ted on the orders of John I Tzimiskes so that he would not have heirs. Thus, his first assistant, Samuel, was certain to eventually succeed Roman. The new emperor entrusted Samuel with state affairs and became occupied with the ch
2 CONC urch and religion.[56]
2 CONT
2 CONT As the main efforts of Basil II were concentrated against the rebel Skleros, Samuel's armies easily attacked all European possessions of the Byzantine Empire. He invaded not only Thrace and the area of Thessaloniki, but also Thess
2 CONC aly, Hellas and Peloponnese. Many Byzantine fortresses fell under Bulgarian rule.[57] Samuel wanted to seize the important fortress of Larissa, which controlled the key routes to Thessaly. From 977 to 983, the area around the town
2 CONC  was blocked. After starvation forced the Byzantines to surrender[11], the population of the town was deported to the interior of Bulgaria and the males were forced to enlist in the Bulgarian army.[58] Although Basil II sent force
2 CONC s to the region, they were defeated, and the conquest of Thessaly marked the loss of the last Byzantine stronghold in that part of the peninsula. With this victory, Bulgaria had gained influence over the southwestern Balkans. From
2 CONC  Larissa, Samuel took the relics of St Achillios, which were laid in a specially built church of the same name on an island in Lake Prespa.[59][60][61]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Bulgarians defeat the Byzantines in the battle of Thessaloniki.The major successes in the west raised justifiable fears in Constantinople, and after serious preparations, Basil II launched a campaign in the very centre of the 
2 CONC Bulgarian Empire[62] to distract Samuel from southern Greece.[63][64] The Byzantine army passed through the mountains around Ihtiman and besieged Sofia in 986. For 20 days, the Byzantine assaults proved fruitless and even disastro
2 CONC us: several times, the Bulgarians came out of the city, slaughtered many enemy soldiers and captured draught animals and horses. Eventually, the Bulgarian troops burned the siege equipment of the invaders. Basil II withdrew to Thr
2 CONC ace, but on 17 August 986,[65] while passing through the mountains, the Byzantine army was thoroughly routed at the Trajan's Gate Pass. This was a significant blow for Basil,[66][67] who was one of the few to return to Constantino
2 CONC ple; his personal treasure was captured by the victors.[68][69]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the defeat, the Byzantine Empire descended into a civil war after the rebellion of Bardas Phocas.[70][71][72] Samuil seized the opportunity and began to exert pressure on Thessaloniki.[73][74] Basil II sent a large army to t
2 CONC he town and appointed a new governor, Gregorios Taronites,[75] but he was powerless to stop the Bulgarian advance. By 989, the Bulgarian troops had penetrated deep into Byzantine territory[76] and seized many fortresses, including
2 CONC  such important cities as Veria and Servia. In the south, the Bulgarians marched throughout Epirus and in the west they seized the area of modern Durrës on the Adriatic Sea.[77][78][79]
2 CONT
2 CONT The defeat at Spercheios.In 989, Phocas was killed and his followers surrendered, and the following year Basil II reached an agreement with Skleros.[80] The Byzantines focused their attention on Bulgaria,[81] and in 991 counter-at
2 CONC tacked.[82][83] The Bulgarian army was defeated and Roman was captured while Samuel managed to escape.[84] The Byzantines conquered some areas; but in 995, the Arabs invaded Asia Minor and Basil II was forced to move many of his t
2 CONC roops to combat this new threat. Samuil quickly regained the lost lands and advanced south. In 996, he defeated the Byzantines in the battle of Thessaloniki. During the battle, Thessaloniki's governor, Gregorios, perished and his 
2 CONC son Ashot was captured.[85] Inspired, the Bulgarians continued south. They marched through Thessaly, overcame the defensive wall at Thermopylae and entered the Peloponnese, devastating everything on their way.[86]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Byzantines recovered and an army under Nikephorus Uranos was sent after the Bulgarians. The two armies met near the flooded river of Spercheios. The Byzantines found a place to ford, and on 19 July 996, they surprised the unpr
2 CONC epared Bulgarian army and routed it in the battle of Spercheios.[87] Samuel's arm was wounded and he barely escaped captivity; he and his son allegedly pretended to be dead.[88] After nightfall they headed for Bulgaria and walked 
2 CONC 400 kilometres (249 mi) home. Research of Samuel's grave suggests that the bone in his arm healed at an angle of 140° but remained crippled.[89]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Emperor
2 CONT
2 CONT In 997, Roman died in captivity in Constantinople, ending the line of rulers started by Krum. Because of the war with Byzantium, it was dangerous to leave the throne vacant for long, and Samuel was chosen as the new Emperor of Bul
2 CONC garia because he had the closest relations to the deceased emperor and was Roman's long-standing military commander.[90] The presbyter of Duklja also marked the event: "By that time among the Bulgarian people rose one Samuel, who 
2 CONC proclaimed himself Emperor. He led a long war against the Byzantines and expelled them from the whole territory of Bulgaria, so that the Byzantines did not dare to approach it."[91]
2 CONT
2 CONT "Above the comet scorched the sky, below the comet[oupolos] (Samuil) burns the West."
2 CONT
2 CONT Byzantine writer John Kyriotes Geometres.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantinople would not recognize the new emperor, as for the Byzantines Boris II's abdication symbolized the official end of Bulgaria and Samuel was considered a rebel. Instead Samuel sought recognition from the Pope, which woul
2 CONC d be a serious blow to the position of the Byzantines in the Balkans and would lessen the influence of the Patriarch of Constantinople thereby benefiting both Rome and Bulgaria. Samuil possibly received his imperial crown from Pop
2 CONC e Gregory V.[92]
2 CONT
2 CONT  War against Serbs and Croats
2 CONT
2 CONT In 998, Samuel launched a major campaign against the Serbian principality of Duklja to prevent an alliance between Prince Jovan Vladimir and the Byzantines. When the Bulgarian troops reached Duklja, the Serbian prince and his peop
2 CONC le withdrew to the mountains. Samuel left part of the army at the foot of the mountains and led the remaining soldiers to besiege the Ulcinj coastal fortress. In an effort to avoid bloodshed he asked Jovan Vladimir to surrender. A
2 CONC fter the prince refused, some Serb nobles offered their services to the Bulgarians and, when it became clear that further resistance was fruitless, the Serbs surrendered. Jovan Vladimir was exiled to Samuel's palaces in Prespa.[93
2 CONC ]
2 CONT
2 CONT The wedding of Ashot and Samuil's daughter Miroslava.The Bulgarian troops next went through Dalmatia, took control of Kotor, and journeyed to Dubrovnik. Although they failed to take Dubrovnik, they devastated the surrounding villa
2 CONC ges. The Bulgarian army then attacked Croatia in support of rebel princes Krešimir III and Gojslav and advanced northwest to Split, Trogir and Zadar, then northeast through Bosnia and Raška and returned to Bulgaria.[93] This Croat
2 CONC o-Bulgarian War allowed Samuel to install friendly monarchs in Croatia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel's daughter Theodora Kosara fell in love with the captive Jovan Vladimir. The couple married after gaining Samuil's approval, and Jovan returned to his lands as a Bulgarian official along with his uncle Dragomir, whom Samuel
2 CONC  trusted.[94] Meanwhile, Princess Miroslava fell in love with the Byzantine noble captive Ashot and threatened to commit suicide if she was not allowed to marry him. Samuel conceded and appointed Ashot as governor of the Drach Pro
2 CONC vince.[95] Samuel also gained an alliance with the Kingdom of Hungary when his eldest son and heir, Gavril Radomir, married the daughter of the Hungarian Ruling Prince Géza.[96]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Advance of the Byzantines
2 CONT
2 CONT The beginning of the new millennium saw a change in Byzantine-Bulgarian relations.[97] Basil II had amassed an army larger and stronger than that of the Bulgarians. Determined to definitively conquer Bulgaria, he moved much of the
2 CONC  battle-seasoned war potential from the eastern campaigns against the Arabs to the Balkans[98][99] and Samuel was forced to defend rather than attack.[100]
2 CONT
2 CONT "Even if the sun would have come down, I would have never thought that the Moesian [Bulgarian] arrows were stronger than the Avzonian [Roman, Byzantine] spears.
2 CONT
2 CONT ... And when you, Featon [Sun], descend to the earth with your gold-shining chariot, tell the great soul of the Caesar: The Danube [Bulgaria] took the crown of Rome. The arrows of the Moesians broke the spears of the Avzonians."
2 CONT
2 CONT Byzantine writer John Kyriotes Geometres on the battle of the Gates of Trajan.[101]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1001, Basil II sent a large army under the patrician Theodorokanos and Nicephorus Xiphias to the north of the Balkan Mountains to seize the main Bulgarian fortress in the area. The Byzantine troops seized Preslav and Pliska,[10
2 CONC 2] putting north-eastern Bulgaria under Byzantine rule for a second time. The following year, they struck in the opposite direction, marching through Thessaloniki to tear off Thessaly and the southernmost parts of the Bulgarian Em
2 CONC pire. Although the Bulgarian commander of the fortress of Veroia, Dobromir, was married to one of Samuel's nieces, he voluntarily surrendered the fort and joined the Byzantines.[103] The Byzantines also captured the fortress of Ko
2 CONC lidron without a fight, but its commander Dimitar Tihon managed to retreat with his soldiers and join Samuel.[104] The next town, Servia, did not fall so easily. Its governor Nikulitsa organized the defenders well. They fought bra
2 CONC vely until the Byzantines penetrated the walls and forced them to surrender.[105] Nikulitsa was taken to Constantinople and given the high court title of patricius, but he soon escaped and joined the Bulgarians. He tried to retake
2 CONC  Servia, but the siege was unsuccessful and he was captured again and imprisoned.[106]
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, Basil II's campaign seized many towns in Thessaly. He forced the Bulgarian population of the conquered areas to resettle in the Voleron area between the Mesta and Maritsa rivers. Edessa resisted for weeks but after a lo
2 CONC ng siege was conquered. The population was moved to Voleron and its governor Dragshan was taken to Thessaloniki, where he was betrothed to the daughter of a local noble. Unwilling to be married to an enemy, Dragshan three times tr
2 CONC ied to flee to Bulgaria and was eventually executed.[107]
2 CONT
2 CONT  War with Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Main article: Bulgarian-Hungarian Wars
2 CONT
2 CONT Map of Bulgaria in its largest extension during Samuel's reign.[32]The Byzantine-Bulgarian conflict reached its height in 1003 when Hungary became involved. Since the beginning of the 9th century, the Bulgarian territory had stret
2 CONC ched beyond the Carpathian Mountains as far as the Tisza River and the middle Danube. During the reign of Samuel, the governor of these northwestern parts was Duke Ahtum, the grandson of Duke Glad, who was defeated by the Hungaria
2 CONC ns in 930s. Ahtum commanded a strong army and firmly defended the northwestern borders of the Empire. He also built many churches and monasteries through which he spread Christianity in Transylvania.[108][109]
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Gavril Radomir's marriage to the daughter of the Hungarian ruler had established friendly relation between the two strongest states in the Danube area, the relationship deteriorated after Géza's death. The Bulgarians supp
2 CONC orted Gyula and Koppány as rulers instead of Géza's Stephen I. As a result of the conflict, the marriage between Gavril Radomir and the Hungarian princess was dissolved. The Hungarians then attacked Ahtum, who had directly backed 
2 CONC the pretenders for the Hungarian crown. Stephen I convinced Hanadin, Ahtum's right-hand man, to help in the attack. When the conspiracy was uncovered Hanadin fled and joined the Hungarian forces.[110] At the same time, a strong By
2 CONC zantine army besieged Vidin, Ahtum's seat. Although many soldiers were required to participate in the defense of the town, Ahtum was occupied with the war to the north. After several months he died in battle when his troops were d
2 CONC efeated by the Hungarians.[111] As a result of the war, Bulgarian influence to the northwest of the Danube diminished.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Further Byzantine successes
2 CONT
2 CONT The first decade of the new millennium was abundant in constant bloody battles, sieges and skirmishes. This picture shows a Byzantine victory in that period.The Byzantines took advantage of Bulgarian difficulties in the north. In 
2 CONC 1003, Basil II led a large army to Vidin, northwestern Bulgaria's most important town. After an eight-month siege, the invaders captured the fortress by force.[112] The commanders of the town repulsed all attempts to break its def
2 CONC ence, including the use of Greek fire.[37] While Basil's forces were engaged there, Samuel struck in the opposite direction: on 15 August he attacked Adrianople and plundered the area.[113] The Byzantines continued the siege of Vi
2 CONC din and finally gained the town after a betrayal by the local bishop.[114]
2 CONT
2 CONT Basil II then decided to return to Constantinople, but, fearing an encounter with the Bulgarian army on the main road to his capital, he used an alternate route. The Byzantines marched south through the Morava valley and reached a
2 CONC  key Bulgarian city, Skopje, in 1004. The Bulgarian army was camping on the opposite side of the Vardar River. After finding a ford and crossing the river Basil II attacked and defeated Samuel's unsuspecting army, repeating tactic
2 CONC s used in the Battle of Spercheios.[115] The Byzantines continued east and besieged the fortress of Pernik. Its governor, Krakra, was not seduced by Basil's promises of a noble title and wealth. Krakra defended the fortress and th
2 CONC e Byzantines withdrew to Thrace after suffering heavy losses.[116][117]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the same year, Samuel undertook a march against Thessaloniki. He ambushed and captured its governor, Ioannes Chaldus,[37][118] but this success could not compensate for the losses the Bulgarians had suffered in the past four ye
2 CONC ars. The setbacks in the war demoralized some of Samuil's military commanders, especially the captured Byzantine nobles. Samuel's son-in-law Ashot, the governor of Dyrrhachium, made contact with the local Byzantines and the influe
2 CONC ntial Ioannes Chryselios, Samuel's father-in-law. Ashot and his wife boarded one of the enemy ships that beleaguered the town, and fled to Constantinople. Meanwhile Chryselios surrendered Dyrrhachium to the Byzantine chief Eustath
2 CONC ios Daphnomeles in 1005, securing the title of patricius for his sons.[119][120]
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1006 to 1013, neither side had much success and the balance of power remained unchanged. But in 1006–1007, Basil II penetrated deep into Bulgarian territory[121] and in 1009 Samuel's forces were defeated at Kreta east of Thes
2 CONC saloniki.[122] Basil launched annual campaigns into Bulgarian territory, devastating everything on his way.[123] Although there was still no decisive battle, it was clear that the end of the Bulgarian resistance was imminent; the 
2 CONC evidence was the fierceness of the military engagements and the constant campaigns of both sides which devastated the Bulgarian and Byzantine lands.[124][122]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Disaster at Kleidion
2 CONT
2 CONT Main article: Battle of Kleidion
2 CONT
2 CONT The battle of Kleidion.Since the Byzantines usually used the valley of the Strumitsa River for their invasions into Bulgaria, Samuel built a thick wooden wall in the gorges around the village of Klyuch (Kleidion, "key") to bar the
2 CONC  enemy's way. When Basil II launched his next campaign in the summer of 1014, his army suffered heavy casualties in assaults against the wall.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile Samuel sent forces under his general Nestoritsa to attack Thessalonica to distract Basil's attention. Nestoritsa was defeated near the city[125] by its governor Botaniates who later joined the main Byzantine army near Kl
2 CONC yuch.[126] After several days of trying to break through the wall, one Byzantine commander, the governor of Plovdiv Nicephorus Xiphias, found a by-pass and on 29 July attacked the Bulgarians from the rear.[127] Despite the despera
2 CONC te resistance and the personal bravery of Gavril Radomir, the Byzantines overwhelmed the Bulgarian army and captured around 14,000 soldiers.[128] According to some sources the number was 15,000.[129] Basil II immediately sent forc
2 CONC es under his favourite commander Theophylactus Botaniates to pursue the surviving Bulgarians, but they were defeated after an audacious counter-attack by Gavril Radomir, who personally killed Botaniates.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then, under order of Basil II the captured men were blinded; one of every 100 men was left with one eye to lead the others home.[130][131] They were sent back to Samuel, who had a heart attack on seeing the blinded soldiers. He di
2 CONC ed two days later, on 15 October 1014.[132] This savagery gave the Byzantine emperor his byname Boulgaroktonos ("Bulgar-slayer" in Greek: Βουλγαροκτόνος). Some historians theorize it was the death of his favourite commander that i
2 CONC nfuriated Basil II to blind the captured soldiers.[133]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Aftermath
2 CONT
2 CONT Themes in the Byzantine Empire under Basil IIThe battle of Kleidion had major political consequences. Although Samuel's son and successor, Gavril Radomir, was a talented military leader, he was unable to restore the Bulgarian Empi
2 CONC re's previous power. After Samuel's death, many of his subordinates, including Krakra, surrendered to the Byzantines. In the deep north-northwest, the duke of Syrmia, Sermon, was deceived and killed by the Byzantines.[134] After m
2 CONC any other bloody battles, The Bulgarian Empire was thoroughly conquered by the end of 1018, only four years after Samuel's death.[135] Most of the territory was incorporated in the new Theme of Bulgaria with Skopje as capital.[136
2 CONC ] It was more than 150 years before Bulgaria was restored, with the rebellion of brothers Peter and Asen in 1185.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Detail form Samuil's garment.Samuel married Agatha, whose origin is unknown. They had five children: Gavril Radomir and four daughters—Theodora Kosara, Miroslava, Katun Anastasiya and Agatha. Gavril Radomir married twice, to Ilona
2 CONC  of Hungary and Irina from Larisa; Kosara married the Prince of Duklya, Jovan Vladimir; Miroslava married the captured Byzantine noble Ashot and Katun Anastasiya married the Hungarian noble Vazul.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the fall of Bulgaria, Samuel's descendants assumed important positions in the Byzantine court after they were resettled and given lands in Asia Minor and Armenia. Оne of his granddaughters, Catherine, became Empress of Byzan
2 CONC tium. Another grandchild, Peter II Delyan, tried to restore the Bulgarian Empire after a major uprising in 1040–1041. Two other women of the dynasty became Byzantine Empresses,[137] while many nobles were strategos and governors o
2 CONC f different provinces.
2 CONT
2 CONT               Comita
2 CONT
2 CONT Nikola       Ripsimia
2 CONT
2 CONT of Armenia
2 CONT
2 CONT David   Moses   Aron   Samuil
2 CONT
2 CONT of Bulgaria       Agatha
2 CONT
2 CONT   Katun
2 CONT
2 CONT Anastazya     Theodora
2 CONT
2 CONT Kosara     Miroslava     Gavril
2 CONT
2 CONT Radomir
2 CONT
2 CONT There is also another version about Samuel’s origin. The 11th century historian Stepanos Asoghik wrote that Samuel had only one brother, and they were Armenians from the district Derjan (Armenian land incorporated into the Byzanti
2 CONC ne Empire). They were sent to fight the Bulgarians in Macedonia but ended up joining them.[138] This version is supported by the historian Nicholas Adontz who analyzes the events and facts of the century and concludes that Samuel 
2 CONC had only one brother, David.[139] Asoghik's version is also supported by the historian Jordan Ivanov,[140] and according to Samuil's Inscription he had only one brother called David.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yahya says that the son of Samuel – Gabriel (Gavril) was assassinated by the boss of the Bulgarians, son of Aaron, because Aaron belonged to the race that reigned over Bulgaria. Asoghik and Yahya clearly distinguish the race of Sa
2 CONC muel from the one of Aaron or the race of the Comitopouli from the royal race. Moses and Aaron are not from the family of the Comitopouli. David and Samuel were of Armenian origin. Moses and Aaron were Armenians from their mother’
2 CONC s side.[141]Check out:
2 CONT
2 CONT Simeon I of Bulgaria had several daughters and four sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Michael. 2.Peter I of Bulgaria (married to an Armenian Irina (Maria)/ Boris, Roman. 3.Ivan (married to an Armenian in Caesarea/ Moses, Aaron/ John (Ivan) Vladislav. 4.Benjamin (Bajan).
2 CONT
2 CONT Comes (comita) Nikola/ David, Samuel/ Gabriel (Gavril Radomir) and several other children.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Grave
2 CONT
2 CONT The remains of the Basilica of Agios Achillius in Lake Prespa where Samuil's grave was found.Samuel's grave was found in 1965 by Greek professor Nikolaos Moutsopoulos, on an island in Lake Prespa, in the Church of St Achillios. Sa
2 CONC muel had built the church for the relics of the saint of the same name.[142] The coat of arms of the Comitopuli house, two perched parrots, was embroidered on his funeral garment.
2 CONT
2 CONT His remains are kept in a secret location in Greece, but according to a recent agreement, they may be returned to Bulgaria and buried in the SS. Forty Martyrs Church in Veliko Tarnovo by April 2007, to rest with the remains of emp
2 CONC erors Kaloyan and Michael Shishman.[143]
2 CONT
2 CONT The skull was reconstructed to restore the appearance of the 70-year-old Bulgarian ruler. According to the reconstruction he was sharp-faced man, bald-headed with white beard and moustache.[144]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Nomenclature
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel's empire had its heartlands about Ohrid, west and southwest of the earlier centers of the First Bulgarian Empire. Thus the White Russian-Yugoslav scholar George Ostrogorsky distinguished Samuel's Empire sharply from the ear
2 CONC lier Bulgarian Empire, as being a "Macedonian kingdom".[145] Some of his school, such as Anastasijević, claimed that Samuel ruled a separate Slavic Empire,[146] founded as an anti-Bulgarian rebellion of the Comitopuli, not a conti
2 CONC nuation of the Bulgarian state. This theory is now held only in the Republic of Macedonia; although Pirivatrić rejects it.[147] The official publications of the Republic even refer to a "Macedonian Slavic" or even only "Macedonian
2 CONC " Empire.[148]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel is among the most renowned Bulgarian rulers. His military struggle with the Byzantine empire is marked as an epic period of Bulgarian history. The great number of monuments and memorials in Bulgaria and Republic of Macedoni
2 CONC a, such as the ones in Petrich and Ohrid signify the trail this historical figure has left in the memory of people. Four Bulgarian villages bear his name as well as Samuel Point[149] on Livingston Island, Antarctica. Samuel is the
2 CONC  main figure in at least three major Bulgarian novels by authors Dimitar Talev[150], Anton Donchev and Stefan Tsanev and is mentioned in the verse of Ivan Vazov[151], Pencho Slaveykov[152] and Atanas Dalchev.[153]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel of Bulgaria
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel[1] (also Samuil representing Bulgarian: Самуил, pronounced [samuˈil]) was the Emperor (Tsar) of the First Bulgarian Empire from 997 to 6 October 1014. From 980[2] to 997, he was a general for Roman I of Bulgaria, the second
2 CONC  surviving son of Emperor Peter I of Bulgaria, and co-ruled with him, as Roman bestowed him command of the army and the real authority.[3] Samuel struggled to preserve his country's independence from the Byzantine Empire. His rule
2 CONC  was characterized by constant war against the Byzantium and its similarly ambitious ruler Basil II.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his early years Samuel managed to inflict several major defeats on the Byzantines and to launch offensive campaigns on their territory.[4] In the late 10th century, the Bulgarian armies conquered the Serb principality of Duklja
2 CONC [5] and led campaigns against the Kingdoms of Croatia and Hungary. But from 1001, he was forced mainly to defend the Empire against the superior Byzantine armies. Samuel died of heart attack on 6 October 1014, two months after the
2 CONC  catastrophe at Kleidion, and Bulgaria was fully subjugated by Basil II four years later.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel was considered "invincible in power and unsurpassable in strength".[7][8] Similar comment was made even in Constantinople, where John Kyriotes Geometres penned a poem offering a punning comparison between the Bulgarian Empe
2 CONC ror and a comet which appeared in 989.[9][10]
2 CONT
2 CONT During Samuel's reign, Bulgaria gained control of most of the Balkans (except Thrace) and southern Greece. He moved the capital from Skopje to Ohrid[11][4], which had been the cultural and military centre of southwestern Bulgaria 
2 CONC since Boris I's rule.[12] He also made the city the seat of the Bulgarian Patriarchate. Although Samuel's reign brought the end of the First Bulgarian Empire, he is regarded as a heroic ruler in Bulgaria.[13][14]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Cometopuli
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel was the fourth[15] and youngest son of Comita Nikola, most likely Count of Sredets (Sofia)[16] and the Armenian[17] princess[18] Ripsimia.[19] His father, who had close ties to the royal court in Preslav[20], died in 970. A
2 CONC t that time there was no Emperor in Preslav as Peter I had died on 30 January 970 and his sons Boris and Roman were in Constantinople.[21]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the same year[22] Samuel and his three brothers David, Moses and Aaron rebelled against John I Tzimisces's campaign that aimed to "liberate" Bulgaria from the Kievan Rus', which they saw as an attempt by Byzantium to seize powe
2 CONC r in Bulgaria.[23] Indeed the following year, the Byzantines deceived[24] Boris II and forced him to abdicate in Constantinople.[25] Although Tzimisces announced the annexation[26] of Bulgaria, he only controlled the northeastern 
2 CONC half of the country, including the capital Preslav and the seat of patriarchate Drastar (Silistra).[27][28] The lands west of the Iskar River[29][30] remained under the control of the four brothers, who were called Cometopuli (Kom
2 CONC etopouloi, i.e., "the sons of the Count") in Byzantine sources.[31] In 973, the Cometopuli sent envoys to the Holy Roman Emperor Otto I in Quedlinburg in an attempt to secure the protection of their lands.[32]
2 CONT
2 CONT The brothers ruled together in a tetrarchy.[33] David ruled the southernmost regions and led the defense of one of the most dangerous border areas, around Thessaloniki and Thessaly.[33] The centres of his possessions were Prespa a
2 CONC nd Kastoria. Moses ruled from Strumitsa,[33] which would be a base for attacks on the Aegean coast and Serres. Aron ruled from Sredets,[33] and was to defend the main road from Adrianople to Belgrade, and to attack Thrace. Samuel 
2 CONC ruled northwestern Bulgaria from the strong fortress of Vidin. He was also to organize the liberation of the conquered areas to the east and of Preslav.[34] Some records suggest that David played a major role in this tumultuous pe
2 CONC riod of Bulgarian history.[35]
2 CONT
2 CONT Even before the fall of Preslav, the Bulgarians defeated Byzantium's Asian army, led by the eunuch Peter, on the outskirts of Plovdiv in 970.[36] From 971 to 975, there were many skirmishes and minor battles, and the Bulgarian det
2 CONC achments harassed the Byzantine possessions in the Balkans.[37] After John I Tzimiskes died on 11 January 976, the Cometopuli launched an assault along the whole border. But within the first weeks David was killed by Vlach vagrant
2 CONC s and Moses was fatally injured by a stone during the siege of Serres.[38]Moses died in 986. [39]
2 CONT
2 CONT Their actions to the south detained many Byzantine troops and eased Samuel's liberation of northeastern Bulgaria; the Byzantine commander was defeated and retreated to Crimea.[40][41] Any Bulgarian nobles and officials who had not
2 CONC  opposed the Byzantine conquest of the region were executed, and the war continued north of the Danube until the enemy was scattered and Bulgarian rule was restored.[42]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Death of Aaron and Samuel's ascent to power
2 CONT
2 CONT After its serious defeats in the Balkans, the Byzantine Empire descended into civil war. The commander of the Asian army, Bardas Scleros, rebelled in Asia Minor and sent troops under his son Romanus in Thrace to besiege Constantin
2 CONC ople. The new Emperor Basil II did not have enough manpower to fight both the Bulgarians and the rebels and resorted to treason, conspiracy and complicated diplomatic plots.[43]
2 CONT
2 CONT Basil II was making many promises to the Bulgarians and Scleros to divert them from allying against him.[44] Aaron, the eldest living Comitopulus, was tempted by an alliance with the Byzantines and the opportunity to seize power i
2 CONC n Bulgaria for himself. He held land in Thrace, where the Byzantines could threaten it. Basil reached an agreement with Aaron, who asked to marry the Basil's sister to seal it. But Basil instead sent the wife of one of his officia
2 CONC ls with the bishop of Sebaste. The deceit was uncovered and the bishop was killed.[45]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nonetheless, negotiations proceeded and concluded in a peace agreement. The historian Scylitzes wrote that Aron wanted sole power and "sympathized with the Romans".[46] Samuel learned of the conspiracy and conflict between the two
2 CONC  brothers was inevitable. The quarrel broke out in the vicinity of Dupnitsa on 14 June 976 and ended with the annihilation of Aaron's family. Only his son, Ivan Vladislav, survived because Samuil's son Gavril Radomir pleaded on hi
2 CONC s behalf.[47] Now, practically all power and authority in the state was with Samuel, and the danger of internal conflict was eliminated.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, another theory suggests that Aron participated in the battle of the Gates of Trajan which took place ten years later. According to that theory Aron was killed on 14 June 987 or 988.[48][49]
2 CONT
2 CONT Co-rule with Roman
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Byzantine plan to use Aron to cause instability in Bulgaria failed, they tried to encourage the rightful heirs to the throne,[50] Boris II and Roman, to oppose Samuil. Basil II hoped that they would win the support of th
2 CONC e nobles and isolate Samuel or perhaps even start a Bulgarian civil war.[51] Boris and Roman were send back in 986[52] but while they were passing through a forest around the border, Boris was killed by Bulgarian guards who were m
2 CONC isled by his Byzantine clothing. Roman, who was walking some distance behind, managed to identify himself to the guards.[53]
2 CONT
2 CONT Roman was taken to Vidin, where he was proclaimed Emperor of Bulgaria.[54] Samuel became his first assistant and general and together they gathered an army and fought the Byzantines.[55] During his captivity, Roman had been castra
2 CONC ted on the orders of John I Tzimiskes so that he would not have heirs. Thus, his first assistant, Samuel, was certain to eventually succeed Roman. The new emperor entrusted Samuel with state affairs and became occupied with the ch
2 CONC urch and religion.[56]
2 CONT
2 CONT As the main efforts of Basil II were concentrated against the rebel Skleros, Samuel's armies easily attacked all European possessions of the Byzantine Empire. He invaded not only Thrace and the area of Thessaloniki, but also Thess
2 CONC aly, Hellas and Peloponnese. Many Byzantine fortresses fell under Bulgarian rule.[57] Samuel wanted to seize the important fortress of Larissa, which controlled the key routes to Thessaly. From 977 to 983, the area around the town
2 CONC  was blocked. After starvation forced the Byzantines to surrender[11], the population of the town was deported to the interior of Bulgaria and the males were forced to enlist in the Bulgarian army.[58] Although Basil II sent force
2 CONC s to the region, they were defeated, and the conquest of Thessaly marked the loss of the last Byzantine stronghold in that part of the peninsula. With this victory, Bulgaria had gained influence over the southwestern Balkans. From
2 CONC  Larissa, Samuel took the relics of St Achillios, which were laid in a specially built church of the same name on an island in Lake Prespa.[59][60][61]
2 CONT
2 CONT The major successes in the west raised justifiable fears in Constantinople, and after serious preparations, Basil II launched a campaign in the very centre of the Bulgarian Empire[62] to distract Samuel from southern Greece.[63][6
2 CONC 4] The Byzantine army passed through the mountains around Ihtiman and besieged Sofia in 986. For 20 days, the Byzantine assaults proved fruitless and even disastrous: several times, the Bulgarians came out of the city, slaughtered
2 CONC  many enemy soldiers and captured draught animals and horses. Eventually, the Bulgarian troops burned the siege equipment of the invaders. Basil II withdrew to Thrace, but on 17 August 986,[65] while passing through the mountains,
2 CONC  the Byzantine army was thoroughly routed at the Trajan's Gate Pass. This was a significant blow for Basil,[66][67] who was one of the few to return to Constantinople; his personal treasure was captured by the victors.[68][69]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the defeat, the Byzantine Empire descended into a civil war after the rebellion of Bardas Phocas.[70][71][72] Samuil seized the opportunity and began to exert pressure on Thessaloniki.[73][74] Basil II sent a large army to t
2 CONC he town and appointed a new governor, Gregorios Taronites,[75] but he was powerless to stop the Bulgarian advance. By 989, the Bulgarian troops had penetrated deep into Byzantine territory[76] and seized many fortresses, including
2 CONC  such important cities as Veria and Servia. In the south, the Bulgarians marched throughout Epirus and in the west they seized the area of modern Durrës on the Adriatic Sea.[77][78][79]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 989, Phocas was killed and his followers surrendered, and the following year Basil II reached an agreement with Skleros.[80] The Byzantines focused their attention on Bulgaria,[81] and in 991 counter-attacked.[82][83] The Bulga
2 CONC rian army was defeated and Roman was captured while Samuel managed to escape.[84] The Byzantines conquered some areas; but in 995, the Arabs invaded Asia Minor and Basil II was forced to move many of his troops to combat this new 
2 CONC threat. Samuil quickly regained the lost lands and advanced south. In 996, he defeated the Byzantines in the battle of Thessaloniki. During the battle, Thessaloniki's governor, Gregorios, perished and his son Ashot was captured.[8
2 CONC 5] Inspired, the Bulgarians continued south. They marched through Thessaly, overcame the defensive wall at Thermopylae and entered Peloponnese, devastating everything on their way.[86]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Byzantines recovered and an army under Nikephorus Uranos was sent after the Bulgarians. The two armies met near the flooded river of Spercheios. The Byzantines found a place to ford, and on 19 July 996, they surprised the unpr
2 CONC epared Bulgarian army and routed it in the battle of Spercheios.[87] Samuel's arm was wounded and he barely escaped captivity; he and his son allegedly pretended to be dead.[88] After nightfall they headed for Bulgaria and walked 
2 CONC 400 kilometres (249 mi) home. Research of Samuel's grave suggests that, the bone in his arm healed at an angle of 140° but remained crippled.[89]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Emperor
2 CONT
2 CONT In 997, Roman died in captivity in Constantinople, ending the line of rulers started by Krum. Because of the war with Byzantium, it was dangerous to leave the throne vacant for long, and Samuel was chosen as the new Emperor of Bul
2 CONC garia because he had the closest relations to the deceased emperor and was Roman's long-standing military commander.[90] The presbyter of Duklja also marked the event: "By that time among the Bulgarian people rose one Samuel, who 
2 CONC proclaimed himself Emperor. He led a long war against the Byzantines and expelled them from the whole territory of Bulgaria, so that the Byzantines did not dare to approach it."[91]
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantinople would not recognize the new emperor, as for the Byzantines Boris II's abdication symbolized the official end of Bulgaria and Samuel was considered a rebel. Instead Samuel sought recognition from the Pope, which woul
2 CONC d be a serious blow to Byzantine positions in the Balkans and lessen the influence of the Patriarch of Constantinople, which would benefit both Rome and Bulgaria. Samuil possibly received his imperial crown from Pope Gregory V.[92
2 CONC ]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]War against Serbs and Croats
2 CONT
2 CONT In 998, Samuel launched a major campaign against the Serbian principality of Duklja to prevent an alliance between Prince Jovan Vladimir and the Byzantines. When the Bulgarian troops reached Duklja, the Serbian prince and his peop
2 CONC le withdrew to the mountains. Samuel left part of the army at the foot of the mountains and led the remaining soldiers to besiege the Ulcinj coastal fortress. In an effort to avoid bloodshed he asked Jovan Vladimir to surrender. A
2 CONC fter the prince refused, some Serb nobles offered their services to the Bulgarians and, when it became clear that further resistance was fruitless, the Serbs surrendered. Jovan Vladimir was exiled to Samuel's palaces in Prespa.[93
2 CONC ]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Bulgarian troops next went through Dalmatia, took control of Kotor, and journeyed to Dubrovnik. Although they failed to take Dubrovnik, they devastated the surrounding villages. The Bulgarian army then attacked Croatia in supp
2 CONC ort of rebel princes Krešimir III and Gojslav and advanced northwest to Split, Trogir and Zadar, then northeast through Bosnia and Raška and returned to Bulgaria.[93] This Croato-Bulgarian War allowed Samuel to install friendly mo
2 CONC narchs in Croatia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel's daughter Theodora Kosara fell in love with the captive Jovan Vladimir. The couple married after gaining Samuil's approval, and Jovan returned to his lands as a Bulgarian official along with his uncle Dragomir, whom Samuel
2 CONC  trusted.[94] Meanwhile, Princess Miroslava fell in love with the Byzantine noble captive Ashot and threatened to commit suicide if she was not allowed to marry him. Samuel conceded and appointed Ashot as governor of the Drach Pro
2 CONC vince.[95] Samuel also gained an alliance with the Kingdom of Hungary when his eldest son and heir, Gavril Radomir, married the daughter of the Hungarian Ruling Prince Géza.[96]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Advance of the Byzantines
2 CONT
2 CONT The beginning of the new millennium saw a change in Byzantine-Bulgarian relations.[97] Basil II had amassed an army larger and stronger than that of the Bulgarians. Determined to definitively conquer Bulgaria, the he moved much of
2 CONC  the battle-seasoned war potential from the eastern campaigns against the Arabs to the Balkans[98][99] and Samuil was forced to defend rather than attack.[100]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1001, Basil II sent a large army under the patrician Theodorokanos and Nicephorus Xiphias to the north of the Balkan Mountains to seize the main Bulgarian fortress in the area. The Byzantine troops seized Preslav and Pliska,[10
2 CONC 2] putting north-eastern Bulgaria under Byzantine rule for a second time. The following year, they struck in the opposite direction, marching through Thessaloniki to tear off Thessaly and the southernmost parts of the Bulgarian Em
2 CONC pire. Although the Bulgarian commander of the fortress of Veroia, Dobromir, was married to one of Samuel's nieces, he voluntarily surrendered the fort and joined the Byzantines.[103] The Byzantines also captured the fortress of Ko
2 CONC lidron without a fight, but its commander Dimitar Tihon managed to retreat with his soldiers and join Samuel.[104] The next town, Servia, did not fall so easily. Its governor Nikulitsa organized the defenders well. They fought bra
2 CONC vely until the Byzantines penetrated the walls and forced them to surrender.[105] Nikulitsa was taken to Constantinople and given the high court title of patricius, but he soon escaped and joined the Bulgarians. He tried to retake
2 CONC  Servia, but the siege was unsuccessful and he was captured again and imprisoned.[106]
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, Basil II's campaign seized many towns in Thessaly. He forced the Bulgarian population of the conquered areas to resettle in the Voleron area between the Mesta and Maritsa rivers. Edessa resisted for weeks but after a lo
2 CONC ng siege was conquered. The population was moved to Voleron and its governor Dragshan was taken to Thessaloniki, where he was betrothed to the daughter of a local noble. Unwilling to be married to an enemy, Dragshan three times tr
2 CONC ied to flee to Bulgaria and was eventually executed.[107]
2 CONT
2 CONT War with Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT The Byzantine-Bulgarian conflict reached its height in 1003 when Hungary became involved. Since the beginning of the 9th century, the Bulgarian territory had stretched beyond the Carpathian Mountains as far as the Tisza River and 
2 CONC the middle Danube. During the reign of Samuel, the governor of these northwestern parts was Duke Ahtum, the grandson of Duke Glad, who was defeated by the Hungarians in 930s. Ahtum commanded a strong army and firmly defended the n
2 CONC orthwestern borders of the Empire. He also built many churches and monasteries through which he spread Christianity in Transylvania.[108][109]
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Gavril Radomir's marriage to the daughter of the Hungarian ruler had established friendly relation between the two strongest states in the Danube area, the relationship deteriorated after Géza's death. The Bulgarians supp
2 CONC orted Gyula and Koppány as rulers instead of Géza's Stephen I. As a result of the conflict, the marriage between Gavril Radomir and the Hungarian princess was dissolved. The Hungarians then attacked Ahtum, who had directly backed 
2 CONC the pretenders for the Hungarian crown. Stephen I convinced Hanadin, Ahtum's right-hand man, to help in the attack. When the conspiracy was uncovered Hanadin fled and joined the Hungarian forces.[110] At the same time, a strong By
2 CONC zantine army besieged Vidin, Ahtum's seat. Although many soldiers were required to participate in the defense of the town, Ahtum was occupied with the war to the north. After several months he died in battle when his troops were d
2 CONC efeated by the Hungarians.[111] As a result of the war, Bulgarian influence to the northwest of the Danube diminished.
2 CONT
2 CONT Further Byzantine successes
2 CONT
2 CONT The Byzantines took advantage of Bulgarian difficulties in the north. In 1003, Basil II led a large army to Vidin, northwestern Bulgaria's most important town. After an eight-month siege, the invaders captured the fortress by forc
2 CONC e.[112] The commanders of the town repulsed all attempts to break its defence, including the use of Greek fire.[37] While Basil's forces were engaged there, Samuel struck in the opposite direction: on 15 August he attacked Adriano
2 CONC ple and plundered the area.[113] The Byzantines continued the siege of Vidin and finally gained the town after a betrayal by the local bishop.[114]
2 CONT
2 CONT Basil II then decided to return to Constantinople, but, fearing an encounter with the Bulgarian army on the main road to his capital, he used an alternate route. The Byzantines marched south through the Morava valley and reached a
2 CONC  key Bulgarian city, Skopje, in 1004. The Bulgarian army was camping on the opposite side of the Vardar River. After finding a ford and crossing the river Basil II attacked and defeated Samuel's unsuspecting army, repeating tactic
2 CONC s used in the Battle of Spercheios.[115] The Byzantines continued east and besieged the fortress of Pernik. Its governor, Krakra, was not seduced by Basil's promises for a noble title and wealth. Krakra defended the fortress and t
2 CONC he Byzantines withdrew to Thrace after suffering heavy losses.[116][117]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the same year, Samuel undertook a march against Thessaloniki. He ambushed and captured its governor, Ioannes Chaldus,[37][118] but this success could not compensate for the losses the Bulgarians had suffered in the past four ye
2 CONC ars. The setbacks in the war demoralized some of Samuil's military commanders, especially the captured Byzantine nobles. Samuel's son-in-law Ashot, the governor of Dyrrhachium, made contact with the local Byzantines and the influe
2 CONC ntial Ioannes Chryselios, Samuel's father-in-law. Ashot and his wife boarded one of the enemy ships that beleaguered the town, and fled to Constantinople. Meanwhile Chryselios surrendered Dyrrhachium to the Byzantine chief Eustath
2 CONC ios Daphnomeles in 1005, securing the title of patricius for his sons.[119][120]
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1006 to 1013, neither side had much success and the balance of power remained unchanged. But in 1006–1007, Basil II penetrated deep into Bulgarian territory[121] and in 1009 Samuel's forces were defeated at Kreta east of Thes
2 CONC saloniki.[122] Basil launched annual campaigns into Bulgarian territory, devastating everything on his way.[123] Although there was still no decisive battle, it was clear that the end of the Bulgarian resistance was imminent; the 
2 CONC evidence was the fierceness of the military engagements and the constant campaigns of both sides which devastated the Bulgarian and Byzantine lands.[124][122]
2 CONT
2 CONT Disaster at Kleidion
2 CONT
2 CONT Since the Byzantines usually used the valley of the Strumitsa River for their invasions into Bulgaria, Samuel built a thick wooden wall in the gorges around the village of Klyuch (Kleidion, "key") to bar the enemy's way. When Basi
2 CONC l II launched his next campaign in the summer of 1014, his army suffered heavy casualties in assaults against the wall.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile Samuel sent forces under his general Nestoritsa to attack Thessalonica to distract Basil's attention. Nestoritsa was defeated near the city[125] by its governor Botaniates who later joined the main Byzantine army near Kl
2 CONC yuch.[126] After several days of trying to break through the wall, one Byzantine commander, the governor of Plovdiv Nicephorus Xiphias, found a by-pass and on 29 July attacked the Bulgarians from the rear.[127] Despite the despera
2 CONC te resistance and the personal bravery of Gavril Radomir, the Byzantines overwhelmed the Bulgarian army and captured around 14,000 soldiers.[128] According to some sources the number was 15,000.[129] Basil II immediately sent forc
2 CONC es under his favourite commander Theophylactus Botaniates to pursue the surviving Bulgarians, but they were defeated after an audacious counter-attack by Gavril Radomir, who personally killed Botaniates.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then, under order of Basil II the captured men were blinded; one of every 100 men was left with one eye to lead the others home.[130][131] They were sent back to Samuel, who had a heart attack on seeing the blinded soldiers. He di
2 CONC ed two days later, on 15 October 1014.[132] This savagery gave the Byzantine emperor his byname Boulgaroktonos ("Bulgar-slayer" in Greek: Βουλγαροκτόνος). Some historians theorize it was the death of his favourite commander that i
2 CONC nfuriated Basil II to blind the captured soldiers.[133]
2 CONT
2 CONT The battle of Kleidion had major political consequences. Although Samuel's son and successor, Gavril Radomir, was a talented military leader, he was unable to restore the Bulgarian Empire's previous power. After Samuel's death, ma
2 CONC ny of his subordinates, including Krakra, surrendered to the Byzantines. In the deep north-northwest, the duke of Syrmia, Sermon, was deceived and killed by the Byzantines.[134] After many other bloody battles, The Bulgarian Empir
2 CONC e was thoroughly conquered by the end of 1018, only four years after Samuel's death.[135] Most of the territory was incorporated in the new Theme of Bulgaria with Skopje as capital.[136] It was more than a 150 years before Bulgari
2 CONC a was restored, with the rebellion of brothers Peter and Asen in 1185.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel married Agatha, whose origin is unknown. They had five children: Gavril Radomir and four daughters—Theodora Kosara, Miroslava, Katun Anastasiya and Agatha. Gavril Radomir married twice, to Ilona of Hungary and Irina from La
2 CONC risa; Kosara married the Prince of Duklya, Jovan Vladimir; Miroslava married the captured Byzantine noble Ashot and Katun Anastasiya married the Hungarian noble Vazul.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the fall of Bulgaria, Samuel's descendants assumed important positions in the Byzantine court after they were resettled and given lands in Asia Minor and Armenia. Оne of his granddaughters, Catherine, became Empress of Byzan
2 CONC tium. Another grandchild, Peter II Delyan, tried to restore the Bulgarian Empire after a major uprising in 1040–1041. Two other women of the dynasty became Byzantine Empresses,[137] while many nobles were strategos and governors o
2 CONC f different provinces.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is also another version about Samuel’s origin. The 11th century historian Stepanos Asoghik wrote that Samuel had only one brother, and they were Armenians from the district Derjan (Armenian land incorporated into the Byzanti
2 CONC ne Empire). They were sent to fight the Bulgarians in Macedonia but ended up joining them.[138] This version is supported by the historian Nicholas Adontz who analyzes the events and facts of the century and concludes that Samuel 
2 CONC had only one brother, David.[139] Asoghik's version is also supported by the historian Jordan Ivanov,[140] and according to Samuil's Inscription he had only one brother called David.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yahya says that the son of Samuel – Gabriel (Gavril) was assassinated by the boss of the Bulgarians, son of Aaron, because Aaron belonged to the race that reigned over Bulgaria. Asoghik and Yahya clearly distinguish the race of Sa
2 CONC muel from the one of Aaron or the race of the Comitopouli from the royal race. Moses and Aaron are not from the family of the Comitopouli. David and Samuel were of Armenian origin. Moses and Aaron were Armenians from their mother’
2 CONC s side.[141]Check out:
2 CONT
2 CONT Simeon I of Bulgaria had several daughters and four sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Michael. 2.Peter I of Bulgaria (married to an Armenian Irina (Maria)/ Boris, Roman. 3.Ivan (married to an Armenian in Caesarea/ Moses, Aaron/ John (Ivan) Vladislav. 4.Benjamin (Bajan).
2 CONT
2 CONT Comes (comita) Nikola/ David, Samuel/ Gabriel (Gavril Radomir) and several other children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grave
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel's grave was found in 1965 by Greek professor Nikolaos Moutsopoulos, on an island in Lake Prespa, in the Church of St Achillios. Samuel had built the church for the relics of the saint of the same name.[142] The coat of arms
2 CONC  of the Comitopuli house, two perched parrots, was embroidered on his funeral garment.
2 CONT
2 CONT His remains are kept in a secret location in Greece, but according to a recent agreement, they may be returned to Bulgaria and buried in the SS. Forty Martyrs Church in Veliko Tarnovo by April 2007, to rest with the remains of emp
2 CONC erors Kaloyan and Michael Shishman.[143]
2 CONT
2 CONT The skull was reconstructed to restore the appearance of the 70-year-old Bulgarian ruler. According to the reconstruction he was sharp-faced man, bald-headed with white beard and moustache.[144]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Nomenclature
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel's empire had its heartlands about Ochrid, west and southwest of the earlier centers of the First Bulgarian Empire. Thus the White Russian-Yugoslav scholar George Ostrogorsky distinguished Samuel's Empire sharply from the ea
2 CONC rlier Bulgarian Empire, as being a "Macedonian kingdom".[145] Some of his school, such as Anastasijević, claimed that Samuel ruled a separate Slavic Empire,[146] founded as an anti-Bulgarian rebellion of the Comitopuli, not a cont
2 CONC inuation of the Bulgarian state. This theory is now held only in the Republic of Macedonia; although Pirivatrić rejects it.[147] The official publications of the Republic even refer to a "Macedonian Slavic" or even only "Macedonia
2 CONC n" Empire.[148] But Samuel called himself Bulgarian Emperor, and claimed the prestige of the Bulgarian patriarchate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel is among the most renowned Bulgarian rulers. His military struggle with the Byzantine empire is marked as an epic period of Bulgarian history. The great number of monuments and memorials in Bulgaria and Republic of Macedoni
2 CONC a, such as the ones in Petrich and Ohrid signify the trail this historical figure has left in the memory of people. Four Bulgarian villages bear his name as well as Samuel Point[149] on Livingston Island, Antarctica. Samuel is the
2 CONC  main figure in at least three major Bulgarian novels by authors Dimitar Talev[150], Anton Donchev and Stefan Tsanev and is mentioned in the verse of Ivan Vazov[151], Pencho Slaveykov[152] and Atanas Dalchev.[153]
2 CONT
