2 CONT Vorgänger 	Amt 	Nachfolger Heinrich II. 	Herzog von Österreich Altösterreich Adalbert Babenberger Stammbaum.svg 1177–1194 	Friedrich I. Ottokar IV. 	Herzog der Steiermark Blason Ducs de Styrie.svg 1192–1194 	Leopold VI. Normdaten:
2 CONC  PND: 137082118
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Hertug av Østerrike 1177-1194. Leopold utmerket seg på det tredje korstog ved inntagelsen av Ptolemais. Under besettelsen av Akka, var Leopold den første som plantet sin fane på byens citadell. Men midt under hærens gjallende jube
2 CONC lrop over at Akka igjen tilhørte de kristne, grep Richard Løvehjerte av pur misunnelse hertugens fane, kastet den i bakken og satte sin egen opp istedet. Hertugen bet imidlertid harmen i seg og utsatte hevnen til et gunstigere tid
2 CONC spunkt.
2 CONT
2 CONT For vågehalsen Richard Løvehjerte gjorde imidlertid hjemferden slett ikke slutt på eventyrene. De ble tvert imot verre enn noensinne. Først ble skipet hans drevet ut av kurs av storm og strandet til slutt på nordkysten av Adriater
2 CONC havet. Med tanke på Filip August og andre fiender som lurte på ham for å ta ham til fange, forkledte hans seg nå som kjøpmann og fortsatte reisen til lands. Men i nærheten av Wien ble han oppdaget, falt i hendene på sin dødsfiende
2 CONC  Leopold og ble holdt som fange i nesten to år, helt til hans trofaste vasaller greide å skaffe til veie den veldige løsesummen som ble forlangt for ham. Den skal ha beløpet seg til mer enn to års inntekter for den engelske krone.
2 CONT
2 CONT I 1192 knyttet Leopold Steiermark til sitt hertugdømme.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han ble begravet i Heilingenburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tekst: Tore Nygaard
2 CONT
2 CONT Kilder: Carl Grimberg: Menneskenes liv og historie, bind 8, side 236, 241-242. Erich Brandenburg: Die Nachkommen Karls des Grossen, Leipzig 1935. Allgemein deutsche Biographie. Mogens Bugge: Våre forfedre, nr. 1127. Bent og Vidar 
2 CONC Billing Hansen: Rosensverdslektens forfedre, side 18, 23. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Hertig av Ostmark (Österrike) från 1177, av huset Babenberg. Leopold deltog i tredje korståget och blev ovän med Rikard I Lejonhjärta, som han tillfångatog på hemvägen från Palestina men senare tvangs utlämna till kejsar Henrik VI
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Källa: Nationalencyklopedin.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leopold_V,_Duke_of_Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT Leopold V (1157 – December 31, 1194), the Virtuous, was a Babenberg duke of Austria from 1177 and of Styria from 1192 until his death. He was the son of the Austrian duke Henry II Jasomirgott and his Byzantine wife, Theodora Comne
2 CONC na, a daughter of Andronicus Comnenus, the second eldest son of the Emperor John II Komnenos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke of Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT Leopold succeeded his father as Duke of Austria upon his death on 13 January 1177. Soon after becoming Duke, Leopold lent his support to Frederick of Bohemia in his struggle against Duke Soběslav II, who had campaigned in the Aust
2 CONC rian duchy, and in 1179, Leopold reached a peace agreement with Bohemia. On 17 August 1186, he negotiated the Georgenberg Pact with Ottokar IV of Styria, by which Styria and the central part of Upper Austria were amalgamated into 
2 CONC the Duchy of Austria after 1192. This was the first step towards the creation of modern Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Third Crusade and aftermath
2 CONT
2 CONT Leopold is mainly remembered outside Austria for his participation in the Third Crusade. He arrived to take part in the siege of Acre in spring 1191, having sailed from Zadar on the Adriatic coast. He took over command of what rem
2 CONC ained of the imperial forces after the death of Frederick VI, Duke of Swabia in January. According to legend, his tunic was blood-soaked after the fights and when he doffed his belt, a white stripe appeared. Emperor Henry VI grant
2 CONC ed him the privilege to adopt these colours as his new banner, that later would become the flag of Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Acre had surrendered, the banners of the Kingdom of Jerusalem, Richard I of England, Philip II of France and Leopold's ducal flag were raised in the city by Leopold's cousin, Conrad of Montferrat. However, Richard removed Le
2 CONC opold's colours (see Siege of Acre) and the duke wrathfully left for his Austrian home, where he arrived by the end of 1191. Richard was also suspected of involvement in the murder of Conrad, shortly after his election as King of 
2 CONC Jerusalem in April 1192.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his journey back that winter, Richard, travelling in disguise, shortly before Christmas 1192 had to stop near Vienna, where he was recognized (supposedly because of his signet ring) and arrested in Erdberg (modern Landstraße di
2 CONC strict). For some time the king was imprisoned in Dürnstein, and in March 1193 was brought before Emperor Henry VI at Trifels Castle, accused of Conrad's murder. Leopold's share of the immense ransom, supposedly six thousand bucke
2 CONC ts—about 23 tons—of silver, became the foundation for the mint in Vienna, and was used to build new city walls for Vienna, as well as to found the towns of Wiener Neustadt and Friedberg in Styria. However, the duke was excommunica
2 CONC ted by Pope Celestine III for having taken a fellow crusader prisoner.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1172, Leopold married Helena, a daughter of King Géza II of Hungary. By her, Leopold had at least two children;
2 CONT
2 CONT  Frederick I (d. 16 April 1198)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Leopold VI (d. 28 July 1230)
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1194, Leopold's foot was crushed when his horse fell on him at a tournament in Graz. He died of gangrene, still under excommunication, and was buried at Heiligenkreuz Abbey.
2 CONT
