1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secondotto,_Marquess_of_Montferrat
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Secondotto, Marquess of Montferrat==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Secondotto Palaeologus''' '''(also Otho or Ottone; died 16 December 1378)''' was the Margrave of Montferrat[1] from 1372 to his death, the third of the Greek Palaeologan dynasty. His name Secondotto may derive from his being th
2 CONC e second Otto to rule Montferrat in his own right, though he would really be Otto III. More probably it is derived from Saint Secundus (San Secondo in Italian), the patron saint of Asti, which his father treated as the capital of 
2 CONC the marquisate.[2] The Otto may be in honour of Otto, Duke of Brunswick-Grubenhagen, a close ally of his father.[3]
2 CONT He was '''born around 1360''' as the first son of ''John II and Isabella of Majorca''.[3] In December 1361, as part of a peace deal made between John and Galeazzo II Visconti, co-lord of Milan, he was betrothed to the latter’s fou
2 CONC r year old daughter Maria. The city of Asti, on which Galeazzo had also had designs, was to count as part of Maria’s dowry, and Montferrat would be allowed to retain control of it. The peace was short-lived, however, as Maria died
2 CONC  in the May of the following year.[3][4] He succeeded as a child of around 12, and ruled originally under the co-regency of his uncle Otto, Duke of Brunswick-Grubenhagen, and Amadeus VI of Savoy. His father’s will had stipulated t
2 CONC hat he should remain under his uncle’s tutelage until the age of 25; however Otto left for Naples in 1376 to marry Queen Joan I.[5] Weak and inept, Secondotto could not carry the weight of government which devolved on his shoulder
2 CONC s with the absence of his uncle. Secondotto thus decided to marry Violante (2 August 1377), the daughter of Gian Galeazzo Visconti, and widow of Lionel of Antwerp, 1st Duke of Clarence, and affirm an alliance with that family agai
2 CONC nst the House of Savoy, Piedmont, and Achaea.[5] When Otto of Brunswick's brother attacked and seized Asti, Secondotto called in the aid of his father-in-law, who, with a huge force, retook Asti and maintained it under Milanese co
2 CONC ntrol. Secondotto realised at that juncture the danger of his Milanese alliance, but by then it was too late. He gathered a force and led it against the Milanese troops and was defeated. Probably out of fear, he retreated to an un
2 CONC known destination. He died at Langhirano in the vicinity of Parma in obscure circumstances: he may simply have been the loser in a brawl (he was famously ill-tempered and violent), or he may have been assassinated by an agent of t
2 CONC he Visconti. His body was carried to Parma and buried before the high altar of the cathedral.[5][6] When his uncle received news of his death, he returned at once to the margraviate to handle the succession. He placed Secondotto's
2 CONC  brother John III on the Montferrat throne. Despite the involvement of Emperor-elect Wenceslaus, the negotiations which Otto opened with Gian Galeazzo over the recovery of Asti were fruitless.[5]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT =Sommaire=
2 CONT * Marquis de Montferrat (1372-1378) :
2 CONT ** précédé de [http://www.geni.com/people/Giovanni-II-Palaiologos/6000000006404266080 Jean II]
2 CONT ** succédé par [http://www.geni.com/people/Joan-III-de-Montferrat/6000000003979222156 Jean III]
2 CONT * Mort sans lignée.
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT * [http://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000027609064898 Moreri, L., ''Le Grand Dictionnaire Historique -- Les Montferrat'', 1759] (ci-joint)
2 CONT * [http://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marchesato_del_Monferrato#Periodo_Paleologo Marchesato del Monferrato, Wikipédia]
2 CONT * [http://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ottone_III_del_Monferrato Ottone III del Monferrato, Wikipédia]
