1 TITL King of Sweden
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birger,_King_of_Sweden '''Birger Magnusson'''] was King of Sweden from 1290 to 1318.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Magnus III of Sweden and Hedwig of Holstein, Queen of Sweden 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?Sokord=Svenske&EndastDigitaliserat=false&Fritext=Svenske&AvanceradSok=True&page=90&FacettState=undefined%3Ac%7C&postid=sdhk_2030&tab=post#tab '''The Swedish Diplomatarium's Main Catalog of Medieval L
2 CONC etters: SDHK no: 2030'''] Dating 1303 July 29, in Alsnow (Alsnö). Swedish king's marshal Tyrgils Knutsson announces that he has made a land exchange with King B(irger) of Sweden, whereby he has given the king the island and the fa
2 CONC rm Ön in Dalarna (in Folkärna parish) with all additions, "Hjortæthorp" with additions, his entire possession in Avesta with the fishing in the stream there . Skira (Svennevad parish) in Närke, the mills in Röttle by Gränna and th
2 CONC e three mills in Torshälla, of which he received two from Mr. Ulf Karlsson and one from Bishop Isar in Strängnäs. In exchange, the king had handed over to him and his heirs his entire share of the commons, i.e. the royal castle (K
2 CONC ungstedingen), in the Diocese of Linköping and Växjö, the farm Tuna (Tuna parish, Tunaläns hrad) in Tjust and the property "Bry...l" in Östergötland with all additions. This also specifies conditions for how to proceed with, for e
2 CONC xample, demarcated and divested parts of the so-called royal castle or parts of the estate that Tyrgils Knutsson left to the king, which he could be prevented from taking possession of. Sigillant is the issuer, Dukes Erik of Swede
2 CONC n and Valdemar of Finland and others. of the Swedish king's councillors.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Background=
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Birger was the son''' of King [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnus_III_of_Sweden Magnus III of Sweden] and [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hedwig_of_Holstein Hedwig of Holstein]. He was hailed king of Sweden when he was four ye
2 CONC ars old. This was done by his father in order to secure the succession. In 1275, King Magnus had led a rebellion against his elder brother, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valdemar,_King_of_Sweden King Valdemar], and ousted him fro
2 CONC m the throne. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Before his death''', King Magnus ordered his kinsman, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torkel_Knutsson Torgils Knutsson], the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lord_High_Constable_of_Sweden Constable of the Realm], to be the guardia
2 CONC n of his son Birger. In 1293, Birger was crowned at Söderköping after marrying [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martha_of_Denmark Martha of Denmark], the daughter of King [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_V_of_Denmark Eric V of De
2 CONC nmark].
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Reign===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Birger was only ten''' years old when his father died, at which time Torgils Knutsson was the most influential statesman in Sweden. In 1293, Torgils Knutsson led the Swedes to a victory which won a part of western [https://en.w
2 CONC ikipedia.org/wiki/Karelia Karelia]. This expedition has traditionally been dubbed as the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Third_Swedish_Crusade Third Swedish Crusade]. When Torgils Knutsson returned from leading the crusade in Finla
2 CONC nd, a feud had developed between the brothers. Torgils Knutsson supported King Birger.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Birger came of age''' when there was a conflict within the Church of Sweden over interpretation of the Privileges of 1280, which had been the cost of the support of the Church for his father's usurpation. The king's brothers [h
2 CONC ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_Magnusson_(duke Erik Magnusson], Duke of Södermanland and [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valdemar,_Duke_of_Finland Valdemar Magnusson], Duke of Finland took advantage of this conflict. Duke Eric t
2 CONC ried to establish an independent kingdom around Bohuslän, which he had received as part of his marriage to the Norwegian princess Ingeborg, and Halland at the boundary between Sweden, Norway and Denmark. A civil war broke out, but
2 CONC  by 1306 emotions had cooled to the point where the dukes acknowledged the son of Birger, Magnus Birgersson, as the successor to the throne. Torkel Knutsson, who was Duke Valdemar's father-in-law, was executed in 1306 as a token o
2 CONC f reconciliation between King Birger and his brothers. The same year, in an event known as the Håtuna games (Håtunaleken), Birger was taken captive by his brothers on the Håtuna royal estate in Uppland and taken as prisoner to Nyk
2 CONC öping Castle (Nyköpingshus).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1308, Eric and''' Valdemar were forced by the Danish king to release King Birger, but they did so under humiliating conditions. When King Birger was free, he sought aid in Denmark, and the strife began anew. Birger remained 
2 CONC king in name, but had to give up the Royal Domain, exchanging it for eastern Uppland, Närke, his brother Erik's former Duchy Södermanland, Östergötland, Gotland and the Castle of Viborg.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1312, Duke Eric''' married [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ingeborg_of_Norway Ingeborg of Norway], daughter of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haakon_V_of_Norway King Haakon V] of Norway in a double wedding in Oslo. At the sam
2 CONC e time, Eric's brother Duke Valdemar married [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ingeborg_Eriksdottir_of_Norway Ingeborg Eriksdottir of Norway], the daughter of King [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_II_of_Norway Eric II of Norway].
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Duke Erik also held''' Bohuslän from Norway as well as northern Halland and was creating a separate kingdom centered on Göta älv. In 1317 however, Birger captured his brothers during the Nyköping Banquet (Nyköpings gästabud), w
2 CONC hich led to their death. According to Eric's Chronicle (Erikskrönikan), the dukes were starved to death in a cellar of Nyköping Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Birger was ousted by''' his brothers' supporters in 1318 and went into exile to his brother-in-law King [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_VI_of_Denmark Eric VI of Denmark], taking the Royal Archives with him. His son, Prince 
2 CONC Magnus Birgersson, was executed at Stockholm. In 1319, the three-year-old son of Duke Erik, King Magnus VII of Norway, was hailed [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnus_IV_of_Sweden King Magnus IV of Sweden] under the Regency of hi
2 CONC s grandmother Queen Helvig and his mother Duchess Ingeborg.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Magnus Birgersson (1300–1320)'''
2 CONT *'''Eric Birgersson'''
2 CONT *'''Agnes Birgersdotter'''
2 CONT *'''Katarina Birgersdotter'''
2 CONT
2 CONT =Links=
2 CONT
2 CONT * http://runeberg.org/sverhist/1/0522.html
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Original:  Or. perg. DRA (C 6, Adk. Sweden) Photocopy:  RA and DK
2 CONT * Writings in the Kiöbenhavnske Selskab af Lærdoms och Videnskabs Elskere fremlagte og oplæste (Copenhagen 1745), 7:315 
2 CONT * Dipl. Dal. No. 3 (by Langebek) Printed regest
2 CONT * Norberg through 600 years (1958), p. 155 
2 CONT * H. Schück, Rikets brev og register (1976), p. 46 no. 30 (see also p. 53)
2 CONT * Photo in Norberg through 600 years (1958), after p. 156.
2 CONT * Literature and commentary
2 CONT * On Tuna see KA Holmberg, De svenska Tuna-namnen (1969), p. 238 with note 2.
2 CONT * On Torkel Knutsson's land holdings, see Bjarne Beckman, Matts Kättilmundsson og hans tid, part 1 (1953), pp. 338f.
2 CONT
