1 OCCU 1332-1350 deželni glavar Kranjske, 1337-1352 advokat, 1341-1359 grof, , count
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Frederic I, count of Celje, a son of Ulric I of Sannegg and Katharina, countess of Heunburg, continued the Sanneg dynasty and is regarded to be the founding father of the lineage of the Counts of Celje. In various 
2 CONC deeds from 1313 to 1341 he keeps being referred to as Frederic of Sanek, baron. In later documents he designation changes to "count of Zili" (source: Altes Hofkammerarchiv, Austria). In 1331 there appears a settlement deed wherein
2 CONC  the Celje estate is deeded to Frederic I for the price of 250 silver marks. The deed was approved by Albert, duke of Austria. Frederic receives the Celje estate as part of his inheritance from the Heunburg dynasty. In 1332 Freder
2 CONC ic is being given the post of Captain of Crain by Henry, duke of Carinthia. The promotion is subsequently officially confirmed by Otto, duke of Austria. Thereafter he is also gifted the estate of Lemberg (Lengburg) by Bertrand, pa
2 CONC triarch of Aquileia. In 1341 he receives further recognition by Louis IV, Holy Roman Emperor. Louis creates a new hereditary title "Graf von und zu Cili". This key Latin document has been dated: "Monachii die lunae post Septimanam
2 CONC  Pafehae Anno 1341" and according to Wissgrill (1795) was archived at: "Altes Hofkammerarchiv zu Graz, Fasc. 43, Rum. 4". In 1343 Frederic is asked by Otto and Frederic, counts of Ortenburg, to act as a referee in their dispute wi
2 CONC th the Auersberg dynasty (Repertorium Archivi Domest. Fam. Auersperg Rum. 46). Frederic dies Aug. 9, 1359 and is buried in the Minorite church in Celje, whereby the church had previously been set up by him as his own charitable fo
2 CONC undation. Frederic was married twice. His first marriage was to Anna, daughter of John, duke of Saxony and Helena, daughter of the duchess of Schleswig. His second marriage was to Diedmud von Walsee.  His first marriage produces t
2 CONC wo daughters, Anna and Catharina. His second marriage produced two sons: Ulric II and Herman I. Between 1359 and 1360 both sons appear various times as witnesses in deeds for Rudolph IV, duke of Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Please note that there may be additional information in the Slovenian-language version of this profile.
