1 NOTE Originaly named Jagiello.  Also grand duke of Lithuania (1377-1401).  He 
2 CONT founded the Jagiellon dynasty and made Poland a great power in eastern 
2 CONT Europe. Jagiello succeeded his father in 1377. Lithuania was then a pagan 
2 CONT country, but when Jagiello married Jadwiga, queen of Poland, and ascended 
2 CONT the Polish throne, he accepted the Roman Catholic faith of the Poles, 
2 CONT taking the name Wladyslaw II. The following year Christianity was 
2 CONT officially introduced in Lithuania, and Wladyslaw himself destroyed the 
2 CONT idols he had worshiped. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1392 Wladyslaw made his cousin and rival Witold (1350-1430) vice-regent 
2 CONT of Lithuania, and in 1401 he recognized him as duke, forming a firm 
2 CONT alliance with him. With Witold's help, he fought the Teutonic Knights for 
2 CONT many years, inflicting on them a major defeat at the Battle of Tannenberg 
2 CONT in northeastern Poland in 1410. The wars lasted intermittently until 1432, 
2 CONT irreversibly weakening the order's military and financial power.
1 NOTE Grand Duke of Lithuania (1377-1401), King of Poland 1386-1434
