1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Ruprecht''' (Rupert) I – w latach 1364-1409 razem z braćmi '''książę legnicki''' (do 1373 regencja), w latach 1397-1401 '''regent w księstwie głogowsko-żagańskim'''. http://pl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruprecht_I_legni
2 CONC cki
2 CONT
2 CONT Rupert I of Legnica From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Rupert I of Legnica (Polish: Ruprecht I Legnicki; b. 27 March 1347 - d. by 12 January 1409), was a Duke of Legnica since 1364 until his death, and also regent over half of 
2 CONC the Duchy of Głogów-Żagań during 1397-1401. He was the eldest son of Wenceslaus I, Duke of Legnica, by his wife Anna, daughter of Casimir I, Duke of Cieszyn. He was born after eight years of childless marriage. [edit]Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Rupert's father died in 1364, leaving him and his four siblings under the guardianship of his uncle Louis I the Fair. One year later, with his cousin Henry VIII with a Scar -Louis's son-, Rupert joined to the Emperor Charles IV's 
2 CONC trip to be crowned King of Arles. Rupert visited then another French cities, including Avignon, were he obtained from the Pope Urban V the anullment of the excommunication over his late father. In 1370 he participated in the Sejm 
2 CONC Reich in Nuremberg, were at the first time, Rupert manifest his political views. The regency of Louis I the Fair was longer: only in 1373 Rupert began his personal rule over Legnica. Despite the inclusion of his brother Wenceslaus
2 CONC  II as a co-rule, Rupert maintain the full government over the Duchy, but always with the close cooperation of his uncle. Thanks to the pressure of Louis, Rupert signed an agreement with his younger brothers on 2 December 1372, un
2 CONC der wich they accepted not divided the Duchy of Legnica between them for the next ten years. The agreement was extended in subsequent years, and allowed Rupert to exercise full control over Legnica at expense of his younger brothe
2 CONC rs, who, although they are co-rulers, none of them had any real power. In later years, Rupert was actively involved in the dynastic struggles over the Silesian inheritance. On 21 May 1379 was concluded a treaty between Rupert and 
2 CONC King Wenceslaus IV, who stipulated that after Rupert's homage to Wenceslaus as his vassal, the King garantized his total possesion over the inheritance of all the descendants of Bolesław III the Generous. Unfortunately, on 6 Janua
2 CONC ry 1383, Rupert had to give up his claims over the Duchies of Wroclaw, Schweidnitz and Jawor. After the death of Henry VIII the Sparrow, Duke of Głogów-Żagań on 14 March 1397, Rupert took the regency on behalf of his minor sons, u
2 CONC ntil 1401. The successive deaths of Louis I the Fair (6 December 1398) and his son Henry VIII (11 July 1399) left Rupert as the Head of the Silesian Legnica-Brieg branch, who allowed him to act as a mediator in the disputes of his
2 CONC  Silesian relatives (for example in 1399 between the Dukes of Opole and the Bishop of Lubusz, Jan Borschnitzem, and in 1400, with the sons of Henry VIII, who wanted to share their dominions). In the internal politics, the two main
2 CONC  problems of Rupert was to pay the debts of both father and grandfather (who were succesfully cancelled) and avoid problems with the Church (who was finally ended with the election of his younger brother Wenceslaus II as Bishop of
2 CONC  Wroclaw). Like his uncle Louis I, Rupert was a generous protector of the arts. One of the artist supported by him was the Brieg canon Piotr Byczyny, who write the "Chronicles of Polish Dukes". Rupert died around 12 January 1409 a
2 CONC nd was buried in the Church of God (pl: Bożego Grobu) of Legnica. [edit]Marriage and Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT By 10 February 1372, Rupert married with Hedwig (b. ca. 1350 - d. 27 March 1390), daughter of Henry V of Iron, Duke of Żagań and widow of King Casimir III the Great of Poland. They had two daughters: Barbara (b. ca. 1384 - d. Treb
2 CONC itz, 9 May 1436), married on 6 March 1396 to Rudolph III, Duke of Saxe-Wittenberg and Elector of Saxony. Through this union, Rupert was a direct ancestor of the Kings of Denmark and the House of Gonzaga, rulers of Mantua and Montf
2 CONC errato. Agnes (b. bef. 1385 - d. aft. 7 July 1411), a nun in Wroclaw. [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT SILESIA Complete Genealogy of the House of Piast (Silesia) RUPERT (RUPRECHT) I LEGNICKI This article was translated by his Polish Wikipedia version. ________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rupert_I_of_Legnica
