1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Samuel of Bulgaria
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel married '''Agatha, whose origin is unknown.''' They had five children: Gavril Radomir and four daughters—Theodora Kosara, Miroslava, Katun Anastasiya and Agatha. Gavril Radomir married twice, to Ilona of Hungary and Irina f
2 CONC rom Larisa; Kosara married the Prince of Duklya, Jovan Vladimir; Miroslava married the captured Byzantine noble Ashot and Katun Anastasiya married the Hungarian noble Vazul.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_of_Bulgaria
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family, grave and legacy===
2 CONT Samuel's wife was called Kosara. The name of his second wife was Agatha. Samuel had five children: the successor Gavril Radomir and four daughters: Theodora Kosara, Miroslava, Katun Anastasiya and Agatha. Gavril Radomir married tw
2 CONC ice, to Ilona of Hungary and Irina from Larissa; Kosara married the Prince of Duklja, Jovan Vladimir; Miroslava married the captured Byzantine noble Ashot and Katun Anastasiya married the Hungarian noble Vazul.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the fall of Bulgaria, Samuel's descendants assumed important positions in the Byzantine court after they were resettled and given lands in Asia Minor and Armenia. Оne of his granddaughters, Catherine, became empress of Byzan
2 CONC tium. Another grandchild, Peter II Delyan, led an attempt to restore the Bulgarian Empire after a major uprising in 1040 – 1041. Two other women of the dynasty became Byzantine empresses,[121] while many nobles served in the army 
2 CONC as strategos or became governors of various provinces.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://bg.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A1%D0%B0%D0%BC%D1%83%D0%B8%D0%BB#.D0.9F.D0.BE.D0.BB.D0.B8.D1.82.D0.B8.D1.87.D0.B5.D1.81.D0.BA.D0.B8_.D1.81.D0.B2.D0.B0.D1.82.D0.B1.D0.B8
2 CONT
2 CONT /Translation: Marriage Agatha Descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabriel Radomir Theodora Kosara Miroslava two daughters and illegitimate son, known by name
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Kosara or Cossara (Bulgarian: Косара)[1] was a Bulgarian Empress, the wife of Tsar Samuel of Bulgaria. She married about 970.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father was John Chryselios from Dyrrhachium. His name and his heresy drive us towards the middle of the Armenians Paulicians[2].
2 CONT
2 CONT In contemporary Bulgaria, Kosara is a popular name traditionally associated with the Emperor Samuel’s family. Today, there is a village Kosara in Northern Bulgaria founded in 1750 by Armenians.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kosara of Bulgaria had many children. Some of them that we know are:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gavril Radomir of Bulgaria, her son, was married to the Hungarian princess. He married to another women - Irena, after devorsing her. Gavril Radomir ruled the First Bulgarian Empire from October 1014 to September 1015.
2 CONT
2 CONT Miroslava of Bulgaria, her daughter, fell in love with the Byzantine noble captive from Armenian origin Ashot of Taron and married to him around 998. Later she escaped with him in Byzantium.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodora Kosara of Bulgaria, her daughter, fell in love with Jovan Vladimir of Doclea who was prisoner of her father Samuel of Bulgaria. She married him. Doclea is a present day Monte Negro.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kosara_of_Bulgaria
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agatha,_wife_of_Samuil
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------
