2 CONT b)        ''' ['''daughter (-before 1182).  The Continuator of Guillaume de Tyre records that Isaakios married "Toros de la Montaigne…sa fille"[449].  Rüdt-Collenberg suggests that the same daughter of Thoros II married Hethum of 
2 CONC Lampron and Isaakios Dukas, highlighting that Armenian sources only mention one daughter[450] (the Chronicle of Sempad, which records the marriage of the unnamed daughter of Thoros with Hethum son of Oshin[451]).  This would not b
2 CONC e impossible from a chronological point of view.  Rüdt-Collenberg dates the marriage to the start of the governorship of Isaakios in Cilicia and before the battle of Myriokephalon in Sep 1176, which marked the end of the brief all
2 CONC iance between Armenia and Byzantium[452].  According to Rüdt-Collenberg, she is not mentioned by the sources which describe the transfer of her husband and children to Antioch in 1182[453], suggesting that she had died by that dat
2 CONC e.  Benedict of Peterborough records that "Ysaac vero imperator de Cipre" killed his first wife[454] but corroboration for this has not yet been found in any other source.  
2 CONT
