1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Unidentified / Unnamed Daughter of Theodore Branas & Agnes / Ann Capet
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna survived Emperor Andronikos' fall and is next heard of in 1193, when she is said by a Western chronicler to have become the lover of Theodore Branas, a military leader who fought on the empire's northern frontier.  They did n
2 CONC ot a first marry............After the fall of Constantinople in 1204, Agnes derived respect from the Latin barons due to her being a former empress.  According to Robert of Clari, Agnes had a bad reputation and could only talk thr
2 CONC ough a translator because she did not know French and had spent most of her life in the Byzantine court............Anna and Theodore eventually married, at the urging of Latin Emperor Baldwin I of Constantinople, in the summer of 
2 CONC 1204.  Theodore Branas continued to fight for the Latin Empire and is last heard of in 1219, by which time Agnes had already disappeared from the historical record.  They had at least one daughter, who married Narjot de Toucy.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BYZANTIUM%2010571204.htm#BranainaMNarjotToucy
2 CONT
2 CONT i)          --- Branaina (-before 1239).  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines refers to the daughter of "sororem regis Francorum imperatricem" marrying "Nargaldo de Toceio, Guidonis de Dampetro consobrinus", in a later pas
2 CONC sage recording that she was "filia Livernes et sororis regis Francie"[947].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' as his first husband, NARJOT [III] de Toucy Seigneur de Bazarne, son of NARJOT [II] Seigneur de Toucy & his wife Agnès de Dampierre-sur-l'Aube (-1241).  Regent of the Latin Empire of Constantinople 1228-1231 and 1238-1239.
