2 CONT
2 CONT    Emperor Alexios III had one illegitimate son by Mistress (1):.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.    '''      ANDRONIKOS Megas Komnenos''' (Nov 1355-[murdered] [14] Mar 1376, bur Theoskepastos).  The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos records that "the emperor had a son, the Lord Andronikos, by another woman and not the empress
2 CONC ", dated to 1355 from the context[139].  The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos records that "the son of the emperor, the despot Lord Andronikos the Grand Komnenos fell from the palace" of the emperor 14 Mar 1376, was carried into the
2 CONC  palace where he died, and was buried "in the Theoskepastos"[140].  According to Europäische Stammtafeln, he was killed by being thrown from a tower[141], but the chronicle of Michael Panaretos does not state that Andronikos was m
2 CONC urdered.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Betrothed'' to GULKHAN Khatun of Georgia, daughter of DAVIT IX King of Georgia & his wife Sindukhtar of Samtzkhe (-2 May 1395).  The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos records that "the son of the emperor, the despot Lord Andronikos
2 CONC  the Grand Komnenos" had been betrothed to "the king of Tbilisi´s daughter, the niece on the sister´s side of Achpougas", that she was then betrothed to "the younger and legitimate and lawful son of the emperor, the younger empero
2 CONC r Lord Manuel the Grand Komnenos", arrived in Trebizond "around…Sep 5" in 1377, was "crowned in the imperial [chapel] and named ''Eudokia'', as she was previously called Koulkanchat" and married 6 Sep by the "metropolitan of Trebi
2 CONC zond Theodosios"[142].  She adopted the name ''EVDOKIA in Byzantium''.  
2 CONT She later married Manuel Achpugas Megas Komnenos, who later succeeded as MANUEL III Emperor in Trebizond. 
2 CONT
