1 OCCU Herzog von Braunschweig-Göttingen
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Otto II, Herzog von Braunschweig-Göttingen=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Ernst I, Herzog von Braunschweig-Göttingen and Elisabeth von Hessen
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otto_I,_Duke_of_Brunswick-G%C3%B6ttingen Otto I, Duke of Brunswick-Göttingen, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto the Evil (c. 1340 – 13 December 1394, Hardegsen) was a member of the House of Guelph. He was a Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, from 1367 Duke in the Principality of Göttingen.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT His father was Ernest I (1305–1367), who assumed power in the Principality of Göttingen in 1345. After several years of co-regency in 1367, Otto took up government in this small and economically weak principality. Otto initially r
2 CONC esided in the city of Göttingen, where he also held several large jousting tournaments. Over time, however, he had a number of disputes with the citizens of the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto's contemporaries called him der Quade, which is Low German for "the evil". He received this nickname due to the almost unbroken series of feuds he was involved in. Otto has been described as a prominent representative of the 
2 CONC former knighthood. He often allied himself with noble Knights to fight against other princes or against the cities, whose burgeoning power was anathema to him. During these clashes, Otto often changed sides. Sometimes, he fought s
2 CONC everal feuds simultaneously.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1367 onwards, he pursued hereditary claims to the Landgraviate of Hesse. He tried to enforce his claims in a military alliance with the knights in the Star League. At the same time, he fought in the Lüneburg War of Succession
2 CONC . At first, he fought on the side of Magnus II against the House of Ascania. He was able to secure the reign of Brunswick from 1374 to 1381. Ultimately, he failed in both Hesse and Brunswick and had to withdraw in exchange for fin
2 CONC ancial compensation.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1387, he tried to impose his influence on the city of Göttingen, but had little success. In April, the citizens of Göttingen stormed the ducal castle inside the city walls. In return, Otto devastated villages and farmsteads in 
2 CONC the area. In July, the citizens under captain Moritz von Uslar defeated him in a pitched battle between Rosdorf and Grone. In August, Otto was forced to recognize the freedom of Göttingen's possessions in the area.
2 CONT
2 CONT After he was expelled from Göttingen, Otto had to reside in Hardegsen, where he had acquired Hardeg Castle in 1379 from the Lords of Rosdorf. By this time, he had been excommunicated, which is why he was buried in unhallowed groun
2 CONC d north of the church of Wiebrechtshausen monastery at Northeim. He was later posthumously released from the excommunication. After that, a tomb was erected over his burial place and a chapel was built around it. The chapel was la
2 CONC ter connected to the church.
2 CONT
2 CONT The final verdict on Otto I is negative, because he overestimated his powers and left himself weakened when he fought too many fights at once. He left a heavily indebted and politically disorganized country to his only son, Otto I
2 CONC I.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage and issue'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1379, he married Margarete (c. 1364 – 18 July 1442), a daughter of Duke William VII of Jülich-Berg.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''They had two children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * son, '''Otto II, who succeeded his father.'''
2 CONT * daughter, '''Elisabeth, who married Eric I, Duke of Brunswick-Grubenhagen.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources= 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Paul Zimmermann (1887). "Otto der Quade" . Allgemeine Deutsche Biographie (in German). Vol. 24. Leipzig: Duncker & Humblot. pp. 677–682.
2 CONT * Dietrich Denecke, Helga-Maria Kühn (eds.): Göttingen. Geschichte einer Universitätsstadt, vol. 1, Göttingen, 1987, ISBN 3-525-36196-3.
2 CONT * Paul Ehrenpfordt: Otto der Quade, Herzog von Braunschweig zu Göttingen (1367–1394), Geibel, Hannover, 1913.
2 CONT * Edgar Kalthof: Geschichte des südniedersächsischen Fürstentums Göttingen und des Landes Calenberg im Fürstentum Calenberg 1285–1584, Verlag Otto Zander, Herzberg (Harz)-Pöhlde, 1982, ISBN 3-923336-03-9.
2 CONT * Joachim Lehrmann: Raubritter zwischen Heide, Harz und Weser, Lehrte 2007, ISBN 978-3-9803642-6-3.  
2 CONT * The House of Guelph - http://www.welfen.de/
