1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Tautvilas''', baptismal name Konrad (born around 1350, died 1390 or 1395/1397) - Lithuanian prince from the Gediminids dynasty, son of Kestutis, brother of Vytautas and Sigismund.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Tautvilas was one of the younger sons of the Lithuanian Grand Duke Kestutis. After the death of Kestutis (1382), who was murdered during the fight with Jogaila, his son Vytautas and his henchmen left Lithuania and first went to hi
2 CONC s brother-in-law, Prince Janusz, who ruled Mazovia. At that time, Tautvilas, granted by Grand Duke Jogaila, became the ruler of Navahrudak. However, Tautvilas quickly followed his older brother, although it is not entirely certain
2 CONC  whether Tautvilas left Lithuania with Vytautas or whether the brothers left separately. When Vytautas, in search of a stronger ally in his fight against Jogaila for the Lithuanian grand-ducal throne, left Mazovia and came to the 
2 CONC Teutonic Knights, then Tautvilas probably stayed in Mazovia, where he was baptized and took the name of Konrad. The baptismal name of the prince referred to the names of the dukes of Mazovia. Although the Grand Master of the Teuto
2 CONC nic Order, Konrad von Zollner, intervened with Jogaila in the matter of the Kestutis' sons deprived of patrimony, in a letter from January 1383, Jogaila flatly refused to demand the return of the lands they had previously ruled.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the efforts to crown the Polish kingdom, Jogaila, fighting with the Teutonic Knights, concluded an agreement with Vytautas, who soon abandoned his Teutonic allies and, in 1384, returned to Lithuania, receiving Grodno. Howev
2 CONC er, the real power in the Lithuanian principality was still exercised by Jogaila, appointing his brother Skirgaila as his governor. After a few years, in 1389, Vytautas, dissatisfied with his inferior position, initiated a revolt 
2 CONC against the Lithuanian ruler and again turned to the Teutonic Knights for help. Tautvilas joined Vytautas again with his wife, who had previously been abducted by Jogaila from Vytautas' supporters. In 1390 Tautvilas took part in t
2 CONC he Teutonic invasion of Vilnius, defended by the Lithuanian-Polish crew.
2 CONT
2 CONT In August 1390, the Teutonic army led by Marshal Englehard Rabe began the siege of the Lithuanian capital. According to the chronicler Wigand of Marburg, during the siege in September of that year, Prince Konrad (Tautvilas) was hi
2 CONC t by a bullet and died a few weeks later. After Tautvilas' death, he was buried in the Vilnius cathedral.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Polish historian Jan Tęgowski, it was not Prince Tautvilas who died in 1390 during the siege of Vilnius, but his nephew Konrad, son of Prince Vaidotas, and Tautvilas himself died after February 2, 1395, and before 139
2 CONC 8. The view of J. Tęgowski is considered unconvincing by historian Jarosław Nikodem.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Jan Długosz claimed that the wife of the Lithuanian boyar Moniwid was Julianna, the sister of Princess Anna, Vytautas' first wife, and the widow of Prince Narymunt, killed in 1390 near the walls of Vilnius. In turn, the Chronicle 
2 CONC of Bychowiec claims that Julianna was the wife of Vidmantas, Birute's uncle, murdered by Skirgaila. On this basis, the nineteenth-century historian Józef Wolff, finding Długosz's mistake as to the name of the Lithuanian prince kil
2 CONC led in 1390, concluded that Julianna was, in fact, the widow of Tautvilas, who died in 1390 near Vilnius.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tautvilas' daughter was Jadviga (Hedwig), who was named in honor of the Polish queen. On September 27, 1396, in Krakow, Jadviga married the Duke of Slupsk, Barnim V of the Pomeranian Griffin dynasty. The marriage was aimed at brin
2 CONC ging closer ties between Poland and the Slupsk princes, who took a different position in the ongoing conflict between the Polish Crown and the Teutonic Order. Jadviga's husband died before 1403.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Remarks'''
2 CONT
2 CONT According to J. Tęgowski, Kestutis had only one wife, Birute. In turn, J. Nikodem believes that the mother of Kestutis' younger sons - Vytautas, Tautvilas, and Sigismund - was his second wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Bibliography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Jan Tęgowski: The first generation of Giedyminowicz. Poznań-Wrocław: Historical Publishing House, 1999. ISBN 978-83-7730-051-0.
2 CONT * Jarosław Nikodem: Witold. Grand Duke of Lithuania (1354 or 1355 - October 27, 1430). Krakow: Avalon, 2013. ISBN 978-83-7730-051-0.
2 CONT * Józef Wolff: Lithuanian-Ruthenian princes from the end of the 14th century. Warsaw: J. Filipowicz, 1895. 
