1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the son of https://www.geni.com/people/Ioann-Korotopol-prince-of-Pronsk-Ryazan/6000000069474817905?through=6000000013301302772 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Oleg Ivanovich, prince of Ryazan' '' (baptized as Yakov / Jacob, later monastic name Joachim) (died in 1402) - Grand Duke of Ryazan in 1351-1371.
2 CONT
2 CONT Oleg Ivanovich inherited the rule of Ryazan' after the death of Ivan or Vasily Alexandrovich. He is traditionally considered the son of Ivan Korotopolis, but according to other research and his own writing, he is considered the so
2 CONC n of Ivan Alexandrovich, whose origins are also debatable.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Origin'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Ryazan-Moscow treaty letters, '''Ivan Yaroslavich''' is invariably called '''Oleg's grandfather''', whose origin is beyond doubt, the brother of Mikhail, the founder of a separate Pron dynasty, and in a generation, two prin
2 CONC ces are called between them at once: '''Ivan Ivanovich (Korotopol)''' and '''Ivan Alexandrovich'''. Just as in the generation between Dmitry Donskoy and his grandfather Ivan Kalita, Dmitry's uncle Semyon the Proud and father Ivan 
2 CONC Krasny (all one after the other who ruled in Moscow) are called in the same letters. Ivan Yaroslavich was killed in the Horde in 1327, Ivan Korotopol was expelled from Ryazan in 1342, and Ivan Aleksandrovich died before 1353 (the 
2 CONC first mention of Oleg).
2 CONT
2 CONT Oleg himself, in a letter of commendation to the Olgov Monastery for the village of Arestovskoye, calls Ivan Alexandrovich his father (''prayer of his father, the great prince Ivan Alexandrovich''). Therefore, researchers are incl
2 CONC ined to believe that Oleg was the son of Ivan Alexandrovich, and not Ivan Korotopol.
2 CONT
2 CONT As for Ivan Alexandrovich, Alexander Mikhailovich Pronsky, who was killed by Ivan Korotopopol, is considered his father. However, this version is not compatible with the fact that Ivan Korotopol and Ivan Alexandrovich in the lette
2 CONC rs belong to the same generation of princes, and also with the fact that Oleg's daughter was married to Vladimir, the son of Yaroslav Aleksandrovich Pronsky.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan Alexandrovich is mentioned only in letters, but is not mentioned in chronicles. Vasily Alexandrovich, on the contrary, is not mentioned in the letters, but is mentioned in the annals, and in Nikonovskaya even as the Grand Duk
2 CONC e of Ryazan. However, the news of the chronicle regarding the Ryazan events is usually questioned by historians, since the chronicle was created under the leadership of Metropolitan Daniel, a Ryazanian by birth. Ilovaisk, Dobrolyu
2 CONC bov, and Ekzemplyarsky considered Ivan and Vasily to be one person, in this case, the year of Vasily's death (1351) may mean the year of Ivan's death and the beginning of Oleg's reign. Most historians consider the Ivan and Vasily 
2 CONC brothers of Yaroslav Alexandrovich. He is not named Ryazan prince in letters, although he owned a label for the Ryazan reign and drove Ivan Korotopopol out of Ryazan. After the capture of Ryazan, he moved his residence (they talk 
2 CONC about the transfer of the capital) from Pereyaslavl-Ryazansky to Rostislavl-Ryazansky, and died in Pronsk. Therefore, Presnyakov admits that Ivan Alexandrovich could rule in Ryazan during the life of Yaroslav, from 1342.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In the era of the “great commemoration” in the Golden Horde, before the concentration of power in the hands of Mamai, Oleg, in alliance with Vladimir Pronsky and Tit Kozelsky, defeated the Horde prince Tagai near the Shishevsky fo
2 CONC rest in 1365.
2 CONT
2 CONT The period from 1370 to 1387 includes persistent attempts by Oleg to defend the independence of his principality bordering on the steppe, the most vulnerable to Horde raids, at a time when national interests demanded the unificati
2 CONC on of Russian forces in the fight against the Horde. Oleg in 1370 became one of the organizers of the gathering of troops to help Moscow besieged by Olgerd, but in 1371 he was defeated by the troops of Dmitry of Moscow under the c
2 CONC ommand of Prince Dmitry Mikhailovich Bobrok-Volynsky in the battle of Skornishchev, Ryazan reign was occupied by Vladimir Pronsky (by 1373, to the invasion Mamaia, Oleg was able to regain his reign with the help of the Tatar Murza
2 CONC  Solokhmir; the Moscow army went to the northern bank of the Oka). In 1377, Ryazan was ravaged by the Horde prince Arapsha, and Oleg barely escaped capture. In 1378, Dmitry withdrew his troops beyond the Oka and, in alliance with 
2 CONC Danila Pronsky, defeated the Horde army in the battle on the Vozha. In 1379, Ryazan was again ravaged by Mamai himself.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1380, Oleg entered into negotiations with Mamai and Jagiello against Dmitry, which is traditionally interpreted as his betrayal of the all-Russian cause, but by some historians - as a diplomatic game in order to protect their l
2 CONC ands from repeated devastation, to persuade Dmitry to meet Mamai even before he came to Ryazan land and even as a deliberate misleading of Mamai and Jogaila regarding a possible connection with them on the Oka. Dmitry withdrew his
2 CONC  troops beyond the Oka, nevertheless not striking at Ryazan, but passing west of the mainlands of the Ryazan principality. The “Zadonshchina” even mentions the death of 70 Ryazan boyars from the Russian side, however, this informa
2 CONC tion does not inspire confidence because of the fact that this fact is not mentioned in the annals and because of the impartiality of the author (Zefony Ryazanets).
2 CONT
2 CONT After Dmitry's victory on the Kulikovo field, Oleg left the capital for the southern borders of the principality and stayed there until Dmitry returned to Moscow, while his other detachments attacked the troops returning from the 
2 CONC Kulikovo field, robbed the carts, and took prisoners. In 1381, Dmitry conducted a retaliatory campaign, and Oleg Ryazansky recognized himself as a “younger brother” and concluded an anti-Horde agreement with Dmitry, similar to the
2 CONC  Moscow-Tver agreement of 1375, and promised to return the prisoners captured after the Battle of Kulikovo.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1382, Tokhtamysh suddenly attacked Russia, Dmitry did not have time to gather strength, and Oleg, in order to save his lands from being devastated by the Horde, showed them the fords on the Oka, but they nevertheless plundered 
2 CONC Ryazan on the way back. In addition, in the autumn of the same year, Dmitry also conducted a punitive campaign against Ryazan. In 1385, Oleg, taking advantage of the weakening of Moscow after the invasion of Tokhtamysh, captured K
2 CONC olomna and defeated the Moscow army led by Vladimir Andreevich the Brave in the battle of Perevitsk. Through the mediation of Sergius of Radonezh, an eternal peace was concluded with Dmitry Donskoy, and in 1387 Oleg married his so
2 CONC n Fedor to Dmitry's daughter Sofya.
2 CONT
2 CONT In subsequent years, Oleg supported his son-in-law, Prince Yuri Svyatoslavich of Smolensk, against Vitovt of Lithuania, who sought to capture Smolensk. Clashes took place on Lithuanian and Ryazan territory (1393-1401). Oleg manage
2 CONC d for some time to return the throne to Yuri Svyatoslavich, who had fled from Smolensk captured by the Lithuanians. However, in 1402, the Ryazan Principality was defeated in the battle of Lubutsk, while the son of Prince Rodoslav 
2 CONC Olgovich, who led the army, was captured. This undermined the strength of Oleg Ivanovich.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before the very end of his life, Oleg accepted monasticism and schema under the name Joachim in the Solotchinsky Monastery founded by him 18 miles from Ryazan. His wife, Princess Euphrosyne,  also ended her life in the monastery.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Political career evaluation'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Oleg had a difficult and controversial fate and bad fame posthumoustly, which was created by Moscow chroniclers and has come down to our days. The prince, who was dubbed the "second Svyatopolk" in Moscow, but whom the peopl
2 CONC e of Ryazan loved and were faithful to him both in victories and after defeats, was a bright and significant figure in the life of Russia in the XIV century. A noteworthy fact is that in the final letter (1375) between Dmitry Ivan
2 CONC ovich Donskoy and Mikhail Alexandrovich Tversky - the main competitors for dominance and the great reign of Vladimir - Prince Oleg Ryazansky is indicated as an arbitrator in controversial cases. This indicates that Oleg was at tha
2 CONC t time an authoritative figure, the Grand Duke, who did not stand either on the side of Tver or on the side of Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Burials of Prince Oleg Ivanovich and Princess Efrosinya'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Duke Oleg Ivanovich Ryazansky died on July 5, 1402, and was initially buried in a stone coffin in the Intercession Church of the Solotchinsky Monastery. He is revered by Orthodox people as a locally revered saint. His wife E
2 CONC uphrosyne (Evpraksia) († December 05, 1404/06) was a locally revered saint in the rank of reverend. By decree of the Holy Synod (October 06, 1769) No.1955, the remains of Grand Duke Oleg Ivanovich and Princess Euphrosyne were tran
2 CONC sferred to the Church of the Nativity of the Most-Holy Theotokos of the Solotchinsky Monastery. After the closing of the monastery in 1923, the honest remains of the Grand Duke and his wife were seized and transferred to the Ryaza
2 CONC n Provincial Museum, and on July 13, 1990, the remains of Oleg Ivanovich were transferred to St. John the Theological Monastery, after which on June 22, 2001, the remains again transferred to the Solotchinsky monastery. Subsequent
2 CONC ly, the couple was reburied in the Mother of God-Nativity Cathedral of the Ryazan Kremlin.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Coat of arms of Ryazan'''
2 CONT
2 CONT “In a golden field, a prince standing, holding a sword in his right hand, and a scabbard in his left; he is wearing a scarlet coat, and a green dress and hat, overlaid with sables ”(Winkler, p. 131). According to Ryazan legends, t
2 CONC he Grand Duke Oleg Ivanovich of Ryazan himself is depicted on the coat of arms.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family and Children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sister: Anastasia.
2 CONT * First wife unknown, second wife: Efrosinya (Evpraksia) of Lithuania (died December 5, 1404).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT from the first wife NN: 
2 CONT * Anastasia - married to Dmitry-Kaributas Olgerdovich, Prince of Novgorod-Seversky and Zbarazh
2 CONT
2 CONT from the second wife Efrosinya:
2 CONT * Agrippina - since 1377 married to Ivan Titovich Kozelsky;
2 CONT * Alena - married to Yuri Svyatoslavich Smolensky;
2 CONT * Fedor (d.1427) - Grand Duke of Ryazan (1402-1427);
