1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Zofia''' (Sonka) '''Holszańska''' herbu Hippocentaurus – księżniczka litewska, królowa Polski (od 1424). [http://pl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zofia_Holsza%C5%84ska Wikipedia PL]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophia of Halshany (Belarusian: Соф'я Гальшанская, Sofja Halšanskaja; Lithuanian: Sofija Alšėniškė; Polish: Zofia Holszańska) (c. 1405-1461), was a Lithuanian princess of Halshany, Queen of Poland from (1422-1461), and the last wi
2 CONC fe of Jogaila.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vytautas the Great helped arrange the marriage between Sophia and Jogaila. Sophia was a daughter of Vytautas's brother-in-law, Andrew of Halshany. Jogaila was already at the advanced age of 71 when, on February 24 (or 7), 1422, he
2 CONC  married Sophia, his fourth wife, who was then at the approximate age of 17. The marriage took place in the city of Navahrudak and was carried out by Matthias of Trakai, Bishop of Samogitia. Finally, she bore him the male heirs he
2 CONC  desired. From previous marriages he had two daughters, one who died as a baby, the other, Jadwiga of Lithuania, the last of his children with Piast blood, died in 1431 at 23, amidst rumors of poisoning. Sophia was the mother of:
2 CONT
2 CONT Wladislaus III of Poland (1424-1444), King of Poland 1434-1444 and Hungary 1440-1444  Casimir IV of Poland (1427-1492), Grand Duke of Lithuania 1440-1492, King of Poland 1447-1492  Another son, also named Casimir, died at the age 
2 CONC of one. Initially there was resistance from the Polish nobility to recognise Jogaila's and Sophia's sons as the heirs to the Polish throne. Jogaila had to make concessions and grant them new privileges in exchange for this recogni
2 CONC tion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophia sponsored the translation of the Bible into the Polish language (1453–1461). She was the grandmother of Saint Casimir.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sophia of Halshany From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophia of Halshany (Belarusian: Соф'я Гальшанская, Sofja Halšanskaja; Lithuanian: Sofija Alšėniškė; Polish: Zofia Holszańska) (c. 1405-1461), was a Lithuanian princess of Halshany, Queen of Poland from (1422-1461), and the last wi
2 CONC fe of Jogaila.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Vytautas the Great helped arrange the marriage between Sophia and Jogaila. Sophia was a daughter of Vytautas's brother-in-law, Andrew of Halshany. Jogaila was already at the advanced age of 71 when, on February 24 (or 7), 1422, he
2 CONC  married Sophia, his fourth wife, who was then at the approximate age of 17. The marriage took place in the city of Navahrudak and was carried out by Matthias of Trakai, Bishop of Samogitia. Finally, she bore him the male heirs he
2 CONC  desired. From previous marriages he had two daughters, one who died as a baby, the other, Jadwiga of Lithuania, the last of his children with Piast blood, died in 1431 at 23, amidst rumors of poisoning. Sophia was the mother of: 
2 CONC Wladislaus III of Poland (1424-1444), King of Poland 1434-1444 and Hungary 1440-1444 Casimir IV of Poland (1427-1492), Grand Duke of Lithuania 1440-1492, King of Poland 1447-1492 Another son, also named Casimir, died at the age of
2 CONC  one. Initially there was resistance from the Polish nobility to recognise Jogaila's and Sophia's sons as the heirs to the Polish throne. Jogaila had to make concessions and grant them new privileges in exchange for this recogniti
2 CONC on. Sophia sponsored the translation of the Bible into the Polish language (1453–1461). She was the grandmother of Saint Casimir.
