1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Fedor (Fyodor, Teodor, Fedko, Fedka Nesvitski), son of Korybut, is mentioned in multiple documents and letters since 1422. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the letter dated Feb. 8 1432 from Švitrigaila to Grand Duke of Volkovysk, is mentioned Fedor Korybutowicz, foreman of Podolia (starosta Podolski - PL).
2 CONT
2 CONT Fedor participated in the Battle of Vilkomir (now Ukmergė, Lithuania), in which he was captured. Princes Zbarazhsky and Vishnevetsky (who used the coat of arms of Korybut) considered themselves descendants of Fedor, and therefore 
2 CONC used the family crest Korybut, but no information about Fedor’s sons was preserved.
2 CONT
2 CONT * foreman of Podolia GDL (now Ukraine) under prince Švitrigaila;
2 CONT * in 1433 Švitrigaila gives to Fedor Korybut the Lutsk GDL (now Ukraine); 
2 CONT * ruler of Nesvizh (Nyasvizh);
2 CONT * foreman of Kremenets GDL (now Ukraine);
2 CONT * foreman of Braslavl GDL (now Belarus);
2 CONT * in 1434 joined the Polish side in the conflict of Švitrigaila, and lost all his lands.
