2 CONT This individual was found on GenCircles at: http://www.gencircles.com/users/mpbennett/1/data/5153
1 NOTE Died having issue, {Burke's Peerage} [GADD.GED]
1 NOTE The eldest daughter of King Edward III of England and Phillipa of Hainaut was named Isabelle.  Her father's mother was Isabella of France, probably her namesake.  But also, since her parents were 2nd cousins, perhaps also for thei
2 CONC r common grandmother Isabella of Aragon, wife of Phillip III of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella of England, wife of Enguerrand de Coucy, although separated at the time, and living in England, died in 1379.  Leaving her husband free to re-marry.  She was also survived by her daughters, Marie de Coucy now of Foix, and
2 CONC  Phillipa, living in England with her mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Early in March 1347 the King and Queen of England (Edward III and Phillipa) traveled from England to Calais to Flanders with their oldest daughter, Isabelle."  The betrothal (of Isabelle to Louis de Male of Nevers) took place in 
2 CONC great ceremony.  However, it was not in agreement with the proposed bridegroom, who at fifteen years old stated he would not agree nor wed her whose father had slain his. Louis was the "put in courteous prison by his Fleming couns
2 CONC elors until he should agree.  After several months he reluctanly agreed, but at the first chance at liberty he rode for the border of France and escaped to the King of France Philip IV.  Leaving the fifteen year old bride-to-be at
2 CONC  the altar.  This was not the first nor last of Isabella's bethroals that went astray.  When she was 3 her father proposed her marriage to Pedro of Castile, but negotiantions fell through.  Isabell's sister Joanna was to take her 
2 CONC place when Joanna died at Bordeaux in 1348.  A second match for Isabella was to the reluctant Louis of Flanders (above).  Two years later she was to be matched with Charles IV of Bohemia.  In 1351 she was 19 and to marry Berard d'
2 CONC Albret, son of Bernard-Ezi, Sire d'Albret, of Gascony.  Five ships were to carry Isabella and her retinue, she changed her mind at dockside, and went home to London, jilting d'Albret.   Her next love became Enguerrand d'Coucy.  wh
2 CONC om she did finally marry in 1365.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite the rampage of the black death, the plague, the Queen (Phillipa of Hainaut) and her daughter, Isabella, attended the ceremonial of the Order of the Garter in 1349.
