1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Jeannne (Joan) de Bretagne''' born 1319, died at Guingamp, France 10 Sep 1384, buried Guingamp, France, église des Cordeliers.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Countess de Goello et de Penthièvre.  She succeeded in 1341 as Duchess of Brittany, until 1364.  Vicomtesse de Limoges 1341-1369.  She renounced her rights by the Treaty of Guérande 1365, ending the Breton wars.  The necrology of 
2 CONC Notre-Dame-de-Beauport records the death "IV Id Sep" in 1384 of "domine Johanne ducisse Britannie, uxoris bone memorie Caroli domini du Plexeix"
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Joanna of Penthievre or Joanna the Lame (in French Jeanne de Penthièvre, Jeanne la Boiteuse) (1319 – September 10, 1384) was reigning Duchess of Brittany (in her own right) together with her husband Charles of Blois between 1341 a
2 CONC nd 1364. She was also Countess of Penthièvre in her own right.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was one of the protagonists of the Breton War of Succession. Joanna was the only daughter of Guy of Brittany, count of Penthièvre, and thus niece and an heiress of duke John III.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1337, she married Charles of Blois in Paris and in 1341 on the death of John III, the couple assumed the rule of the duchy of Brittany, being supported by most of the local nobility and administration. However, John of Dreux, b
2 CONC orn from the second marriage of duke Arthur II to Yolande de Montfort, did not agree to let go of his own rights.
2 CONT
2 CONT The result was the Breton War of Succession, which would end in 1364 with the death of Charles of Blois in battle and the victory of the Montfort branch. During the war, the military successes varied and both claimants held the ru
2 CONC le of some parts of the country. Joanna's party was stronger at least about 1345, and possibly they held larger part of the duchy than the rival between the entire 1341-64 period.
2 CONT
2 CONT they had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT John I of Blois-Châtillon (1340–1404)
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry (d. 1400)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie (1345-1404), Lady of Guise, married in 1360 Louis I of Naples
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite, married in 1351 Charles de la Cerda (d. 1354)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT She was one of the protagonists of the Breton War of Succession. Joanna was the only daughter of Guy of Brittany, count of Penthièvre, and thus niece and an heiress of duke John III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal Seal of Jeanne, Duchess of Brittany upon the year 1369.In 1337, she married Charles of Blois in Paris and in 1341 on the death of John III, the couple assumed the rule of the duchy of Brittany, being supported by most of the
2 CONC  local nobility and administration. However, John of Dreux, born from the second marriage of duke Arthur II to Yolande de Montfort, did not agree to let go of his own rights.
2 CONT
2 CONT When John died in 1345 in the midst of the Breton War of Succession, his wife Joanna of Flanders took arms to protect the rights of her son John V, Duke of Brittany against the party led by Charles and Joan. Joanna organized resis
2 CONC tance and made use of diplomatic means to protect her family and country. In the siege of Hennebont, she took up arms and, dressed in armour, conducted the defence of the town. Joanna even led a raid of knights outside the walls t
2 CONC hat successfully fired and destroyed one of the enemy's rear camps. After this she became known as "Jeanne la Flamme". She even captured Joan's husband Charles. However Joanna was eventually forced to retreat to England, where she
2 CONC  became mentally ill leaving her young son in the care of the English court. Charles and Joan by this time controlled most of Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT After these initial successes, Charles was taken prisoner by the English in 1347. Thomas Dagworth was the official captor of Charles of Blois. He was released nine years afterwards against a ransom of about half a million ecús, an
2 CONC d resumed the war against the Montforts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles died in the Battle of Auray which determined the end of the war and the victory of the Montforts. He was canonized as saint for his devotion to religion, but the process was made null by Pope Gregory XI by request of Duke 
2 CONC John V of Brittany. Subsequently, in 1904, he was beatified. Joan was left a widow.
2 CONT
2 CONT After this defeat, Joan had to sign the first treaty Guérande, ceding sovereignty over Brittany, while retaining the ducal title for life.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1379, when John V had been forced into exile in England, Joan was shocked that King Charles V of France wished to annex Brittany as part of France in violation of her rights and those of her son. Both local factions united to i
2 CONC nvite John V back from his exile in England and retake control of the Duchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Charles V, she signed on 15 January 1381 the second treaty of Guérande by which she received a substantial pension and the opportunity for her heirs to recover the duchy if John V had no descendants. The problem
2 CONC  of succession was finally settled.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan died on 10 September 1384 and she was buried at the church of the Friars Minor of Guingamp.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT En mi nuevo libro LA SORPRENDENTE GENEALOGÍA DE MIS TATARABUELOS, encontrarán a este y muchos otros de sus ancestros con un resumen biográfico de cada uno. El libro está disponible en: amazon.com            barnesandnoble.com     
2 CONC         palibrio.com.  Les será de mucha utilidad y diversión.  Ramón Rionda
2 CONT
2 CONT In my new book LA SORPRENDENTE GENEALOGÍA DE MIS TATARABUELOS, you will find this and many other of your ancestors, with a biography summary of each of them. The book is now available at:  amazon.com            barnesandnoble.com 
2 CONC             palibrio.com.  Check it up, it’s worth it. Ramón Rionda
