1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John I, Duke of Alençon
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT John I of Alençon, called the Sage (1385, Château d'Essay, – October 25, 1415, Azincourt), was the son of Peter II of Alençon and Marie de Chamaillard. In 1404, he succeeded his father as Count of Alençon and Perche. He was made D
2 CONC uke of Alençon in 1414.
2 CONT
2 CONT He commanded the second division of the French army at the Battle of Agincourt. When the English broke through the first division, he led a countercharge. He is sometimes credited with killing Edward, Duke of York, wounding Humphr
2 CONC ey, Duke of Gloucester, and cutting an ornament from the crown of Henry V of England; but he was then overpowered by the King's bodyguard, and slain before he could yield himself.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Family
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1396, he married Marie of Brittany (1391–1446), daughter of John V, Duke of Brittany. They had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter (1407, Argentan – 1408)
2 CONT
2 CONT John II of Alençon (1409–1476)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie (1410, Argentan, – 1412, Argentan)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne (1412, Argentan – 1420)
2 CONT
2 CONT Charlotte (1413, Argentan – 1435, Lamballe)
2 CONT
2 CONT He also had two illegitimate children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter (d. 1424, Battle of Verneuil), Lord of Gallandon
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite, married Jean de St-Aubin, Lord of Preaux
