1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Count Palatine and Elector of the Palatinate
2 CONT
2 CONT WIKIPEDIA PAGE:  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rupert_II,_Elector_Palatine
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Rupert II of the Rhine (German: Ruprecht II., der Harte (der Ernste)) (12 May 1325, Amberg – 6 January 1398, Amberg). He was the Elector Palatine of the Rhine from the house of Wittelsbach in 1390–1398.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the elder son of Adolf, Count Palatine of the Rhine and Countess Irmengard von Öttingen. On 13 February 1338 the Palatinate was divided between Rupert II and his uncle Rudolf II, Duke of Bavaria.[citation needed] After the 
2 CONC death of his other uncle, the Elector Rupert I (who had succeeded Rudolf II), on 16 February 1390 he was proclaimed Elector Palatine with the consent of Wenceslaus, King of the Romans. In 1391 he banished Jews and prostitutes from
2 CONC  the Palatinate, confiscated their property, and bequeathed it to the Ruprecht Karl University of Heidelberg. In 1395 he promulgated the so-called Rupertinische Konstitution which was intended to provide for unity of the Palatinat
2 CONC e. Among other provisions, he incorporated to his realm the former Imperial Free City Neckargemünd.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried in a Cistercian cloister in Heidelberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Family and children He was married in 1345 to Beatrix of Sicily and Aragon, daughter of King Peter II of Sicily. They had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna (1346 – 30 November 1415), married in 1363 to Duke Wilhelm VII of Jülich and Berg.  Friedrich (1347 – ca. 1395).  Johann (1349 – ca. 1395).  Mechthild (b. 1350), married to Landgrave Sigost of Leuchtenberg.  Elisabeth (ca. 13
2 CONC 51 – 1360).  King Rupert of Germany (1352 – 1410).  Adolf (1355 – 1 May 1358). 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruprecht_II._(Pfalz)
2 CONT =Ruprecht II., der Harte oder der Ernste=
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rupert_II,_Elector_Palatine
2 CONT =Rupert II, Elector Palatine=
2 CONT Rupert II of the Rhine (German: Ruprecht II., der Harte (der Ernste)) (12 May 1325, Amberg – 6 January 1398, Amberg). He was the Elector Palatine of the Rhine from the house of Wittelsbach in 1390–1398.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the elder son of Adolf, Count Palatine of the Rhine and Countess Irmengard von Öttingen. On 13 February 1338 the Palatinate was divided between Rupert II and his uncle Rudolf II, Duke of Bavaria.[citation needed] After the 
2 CONC death of his other uncle, the Elector Rupert I (who had succeeded Rudolf II), on 16 February 1390 he was proclaimed Elector Palatine with the consent of Wenceslaus, King of the Romans. In 1391 he banished Jews and prostitutes from
2 CONC  the Palatinate, confiscated their property, and bequeathed it to the Ruprecht Karl University of Heidelberg. In 1395 he promulgated the so-called Rupertinische Konstitution which was intended to provide for unity of the Palatinat
2 CONC e. Among other provisions, he incorporated to his realm the former Imperial Free City Neckargemünd.
2 CONT
2 CONT *He was buried in a Cistercian cloister in Heidelberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family and children===
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married in 1345 to '''Beatrix of Sicily and Aragon''', daughter of King Peter II of Sicily. They had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Anna (1346 – 30 November 1415), married in 1363 to William VII of Jülich, 1st Duke of Berg.
2 CONT # Friedrich (1347 – ca. 1395).
2 CONT # Johann (1349 – ca. 1395).
2 CONT # Mechthild (b. 1350), married to Landgrave Sigost of Leuchtenberg.
2 CONT # Elisabeth (ca. 1351 – 1360).
2 CONT # King Rupert of Germany (1352 – 1410).
2 CONT # Adolf (1355 – 1 May 1358).
