1 OCCU цар на България (1040-1041)
1 TITL Tsar
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Peter Delyan (reigned 1040–1041) (Bulgarian Петър Делян) was the leader of the Bulgarian uprising against the Byzantine Empire started in thema of Bulgaria during summer of 1040 - (now region Pomoravlje in modern S
2 CONC erbia).[1] He was proclaimed emperor (tsar) of Bulgaria (as Samuil's grandson) in Belgrade (summer 1040). His original name may have been simply Delyan, in which case he assumed the name Peter II upon his accession, commemorating 
2 CONC the sainted Emperor Peter I (Petăr I), who had died in 970.''' The year of his birth is uncertain, but probably not long after 1000, and before 1014; he may have died in 1041.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Origin
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.1 Delyan as Radomir's son
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.2 Delyan as local Bulgarian
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Peter Delyan as leader of Bulgarian uprising
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Family tree
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 References
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.1 See also
2 CONT
2 CONT  Origin
2 CONT
2 CONT His origin is not clear. He claimed that he was son of emperor Gavril Radomir and grandson of Samuil, but he could also be a some local who became leader of uprising and used the story that he's Samuil's grandson to justify his pr
2 CONC oclamation for tsar.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Delyan as Radomir's son
2 CONT
2 CONT Those who believe he actually was Radomir's son, think that he was born from Radomir's marriage with Marguerite, sister of King Stephen I of Hungary (996/997). Peter's mother was expelled while pregnant from the Samuil's court bef
2 CONC ore the accession of Gavril Radomir, but given Delyan's subsequent career, it is likely that he had been born and remained in Bulgaria with his father (see Fine, reference below).
2 CONT
2 CONT After Ivan Vladislav murder of Gavril Radomir in 1015 and the conquest of Bulgaria by the Byzantine Empire in 1018, Delyan was taken captive to Constantinople and became a servant of an unidentified member of the Byzantine aristoc
2 CONC racy. He later escaped and went to his mother's country of Hungary, from which he re-entered Bulgaria and raised a revolt against Byzantine rule, taking advantage of the discontent over the imposition of taxes in coin by the Byzan
2 CONC tine government.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Delyan as local Bulgarian
2 CONT
2 CONT Those who oppose the theory that he was Samuil's grandson and believe he was merely a local Bulgarian, claim that he was proclaimed as tsar in Belgrade not because it was a border town between the Byzantine Empire and Hungary and 
2 CONC he crossed the border when rebels liberated town to join them as a prince of royal blood, but only because it was the first important town rebels have liberated.
2 CONT
2 CONT They also claim that it is highly unlikely that Ivan Vladislav who in 1015 murdered his brother Gavril Radomir (Delyans supposed father) and his current wife Maria to came to the throne, wouldn't kill Radomir's sons and heirs, if 
2 CONC he had them, in order to secure his ruling. Especially if it's known that he also ordered killing of Serbian prince of Duklja Jovan Vladimir, who was Samuil's son-in-law (married with his daughter Theodora/Kosara), because he thou
2 CONC ght Vladimir was interested in position of Slavonic tsar.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Peter Delyan as leader of Bulgarian uprising
2 CONT
2 CONT Main article: Bulgarian uprising against the Byzantine Empire (1040-1041)
2 CONT
2 CONT During the summer of 1040 in thema of Bulgaria local people raised rebellion against Byzantine Empire. There were two main causes:
2 CONT
2 CONT Replacing Bulgarian Archbishopric of Ohrid with Greek (1037) and beginning of process of Hellenisation
2 CONT
2 CONT Imposition of taxes in coin for local people by the Byzantine government
2 CONT
2 CONT Uprising spread fast and rebels very quickly took over control over northern part of Pomoravlje and liberated Belgrade. Leader of the rebellion Delyan was proclaimed emperor (Tsar) of Bulgaria in Belgrade under the name Peter II b
2 CONC y being raised atop a shield by leaders of the resistance, and perhaps enjoyed some support from Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bulgaria under Petar II Delyan
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter II Delyan took Niš and Skopje, first co-opting and then eliminating another potential leader in the person of one Tihomir, who had led a rebellion in the region of Durazzo. After this Peter II marched on Thessalonica, where 
2 CONC the Byzantine Emperor Michael IV was staying. Michael fled, leaving his treasury to a certain Michael Ivac. The latter, who was probably a son of Ivac, a general under Samuil, promptly turned over the bulk of the treasury to Peter
2 CONC  outside the city. Thessalonica remained in Byzantine hands, but Macedonia, Durazzo, and parts of northern Greece were taken by Peter II's forces. This inspired further Slavic revolts against Byzantine rule in Epirus and Albania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter II Delyan's successes ended, however, with the interference of his cousin Alusian. Alusian, whose father Ivan Vladislav had murdered Peter's father Gavril Radomir in 1015, joined Peter II's ranks as an apparent deserter from
2 CONC  the Byzantine court, where he had been disgraced. Alusian was welcomed by Peter II, who gave him an army with which to attack Thessalonica. The siege, however, was raised by the Byzantines, and the army was defeated. Alusian bare
2 CONC ly escaped and returned to Ostrovo.
2 CONT
2 CONT One night, during dinner, Alusian took advantage of Peter II's inebriation, and cut off his nose and blinded him with a kitchen knife in 1041. Since Alusian was of the blood of Samuil of Bulgaria, he was quickly proclaimed emperor
2 CONC  in Peter II's place by his troops, but he conspired to defect to the Byzantines. As the Bulgarian and Byzantine troops were preparing for battle, Alusian deserted to the enemy and headed for Constantinople, where his possessions 
2 CONC and lands were restored to him, and he was rewarded with the high courtly rank of magistros.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, though blind, Peter II Delyan resumed command of the Bulgarian forces, but the Byzantine Emperor Michael IV determined to take advantage of the situation and advanced against them. In an obscure battle of Ostrovo, the B
2 CONC yzantines defeated the Bulgarian troops and Peter II Delyan was captured and taken to Constantinople, where he was perhaps executed. According some legends he was blinded and later exiled in a monastery in Iskar gorge in Balkan Mo
2 CONC untains where he died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Norse sagas refer to the participation of the future Norwegian King Harald Hardråda, who allegedly cut down Peter II in the field of battle as a member of the Varangian Guard. This tradition may be supported by a laconic reference
2 CONC  in the so-called "Bulgarian Apocryphal Chronicle". In either case, Peter II Delyan may have perished in 1041.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Delyan
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Delyan (reigned 1040–1041) (Bulgarian Петър Делян) was the leader of the Bulgarian uprising against the Byzantine Empire started in thema of Bulgaria during summer of 1040 - (now region Pomoravlje in modern Serbia).[1] He wa
2 CONC s proclaimed emperor (tsar) of Bulgaria (as Samuil`s grandson) in Belgrade (summer 1040). His original name may have been simply Delyan, in which case he assumed the name Peter II upon his accession, commemorating the sainted Empe
2 CONC ror Peter I (Petăr I), who had died in 970. The year of his birth is uncertain, but probably not long after 1000, and before 1014; he may have died in 1041.
2 CONT
2 CONT Origin
2 CONT
2 CONT His origin is not clear. He claimed that he was son of emperor Gavril Radomir and grandson of Samuil, but he could also be a some local who became leader of uprising and used the story that he's Samuil's grandson to justify his pr
2 CONC oclamation for tsar.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Delyan as Radomir's son
2 CONT
2 CONT Those who believe he actually was Radomir's son, think that he was born from Radomir's marriage with Marguerite, sister of King Stephen I of Hungary (996/997). Peter's mother was expelled while pregnant from the Samuil's court bef
2 CONC ore the accession of Gavril Radomir, but given Delyan's subsequent career, it is likely that he had been born and remained in Bulgaria with his father (see Fine, reference below).
2 CONT
2 CONT After Ivan Vladislav murder of Gavril Radomir in 1015 and the conquest of Bulgaria by the Byzantine Empire in 1018, Delyan was taken captive to Constantinople and became a servant of an unidentified member of the Byzantine aristoc
2 CONC racy. He later escaped and went to his mother's country of Hungary, from which he re-entered Bulgaria and raised a revolt against Byzantine rule, taking advantage of the discontent over the imposition of taxes in coin by the Byzan
2 CONC tine government.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Delyan as local Bulgarian
2 CONT
2 CONT Those who oppose the theory that he was Samuil's grandson and believe he was merely a local Bulgarian, claim that he was proclaimed as tsar in Belgrade not because it was a border town between the Byzantine Empire and Hungary and 
2 CONC he crossed the border when rebels liberated town to join them as a prince of royal blood, but only because it was the first important town rebels have liberated.
2 CONT
2 CONT They also claim that it is highly unlikely that Ivan Vladislav who in 1015 murdered his brother Gavril Radomir (Delyans supposed father) and his current wife Maria to came to the throne, wouldn't kill Radomir's sons and heirs, if 
2 CONC he had them, in order to secure his ruling. Especially if it's known that he also ordered killing of Serbian prince of Duklja Jovan Vladimir, who was Samuil's son-in-law (married with his daughter Theodora/Kosara), because he thou
2 CONC ght Vladimir was interested in position of Slavonic tsar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Delyan as leader of Bulgarian uprising
2 CONT
2 CONT Main article: Bulgarian uprising against the Byzantine Empire (1040-1041)
2 CONT
2 CONT During the summer of 1040 in thema of Bulgaria local people raised rebellion against Byzantine Empire. There were two main causes:
2 CONT
2 CONT Replacing Bulgarian Archbishopric of Ohrid with Greek (1037) and beginning of process of Hellenisation
2 CONT
2 CONT Imposition of taxes in coin for local people by the Byzantine government
2 CONT
2 CONT Uprising spread fast and rebels very quickly took over control over northern part of Pomoravlje and liberated Belgrade. Leader of the rebellion Delyan was proclaimed emperor (Tsar) of Bulgaria in Belgrade under the name Peter II b
2 CONC y being raised atop a shield by leaders of the resistance, and perhaps enjoyed some support from Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter II Delyan took Niš and Skopje, first co-opting and then eliminating another potential leader in the person of one Tihomir, who had led a rebellion in the region of Durazzo. After this Peter II marched on Thessalonica, where 
2 CONC the Byzantine Emperor Michael IV was staying. Michael fled, leaving his treasury to a certain Michael Ivac. The latter, who was probably a son of Ivac, a general under Samuil, promptly turned over the bulk of the treasury to Peter
2 CONC  outside the city. Thessalonica remained in Byzantine hands, but Macedonia, Durazzo, and parts of northern Greece were taken by Peter II's forces. This inspired further Slavic revolts against Byzantine rule in Epirus and Albania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter II Delyan's successes ended, however, with the interference of his cousin Alusian. Alusian, whose father Ivan Vladislav had murdered Peter's father Gavril Radomir in 1015, joined Peter II's ranks as an apparent deserter from
2 CONC  the Byzantine court, where he had been disgraced. Alusian was welcomed by Peter II, who gave him an army with which to attack Thessalonica. The siege, however, was raised by the Byzantines, and the army was defeated. Alusian bare
2 CONC ly escaped and returned to Ostrovo.
2 CONT
2 CONT One night, during dinner, Alusian took advantage of Peter II's inebriation, and cut off his nose and blinded him with a kitchen knife in 1041. Since Alusian was of the blood of Samuil of Bulgaria, he was quickly proclaimed emperor
2 CONC  in Peter II's place by his troops, but he conspired to defect to the Byzantines. As the Bulgarian and Byzantine troops were preparing for battle, Alusian deserted to the enemy and headed for Constantinople, where his possessions 
2 CONC and lands were restored to him, and he was rewarded with the high courtly rank of magistros.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, though blind, Peter II Delyan resumed command of the Bulgarian forces, but the Byzantine Emperor Michael IV determined to take advantage of the situation and advanced against them. In an obscure battle of Ostrovo, the B
2 CONC yzantines defeated the Bulgarian troops and Peter II Delyan was captured and taken to Constantinople, where he was perhaps executed. According some legends he was blinded and later exiled in a monastery in Iskar gorge in Balkan Mo
2 CONC untains where he died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Norse sagas refer to the participation of the future Norwegian King Harald Hardråda, who allegedly cut down Peter II in the field of battle as a member of the Varangian Guard. This tradition may be supported by a laconic reference
