2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zavida
2 CONT
2 CONT =Завида - Белу Урош / Zavida or Beli Uroš=
2 CONT
2 CONT Zavida (Serbian: Завида) or Beli Uroš (White Uroš) was a 12th-century Serbian royal member who briefly ruled as Župan of Zahumlje and later held the title "Lord of Ribnica".
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a close kinsman or even a son of Uroš I of Rascia, although this has not been clarified (Stefan Nemanja's descendants are named Vukan and Uroš in several generations).[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He ruled the province of Zahumlje before getting into conflict with his brothers[1], resulting in him being "exiled"[1] (before 1113), to the Duklja region where he would hold the title of Lord of Ribnica (Part of present-day Podg
2 CONC orica). After the death of George of Duklja in the 1130s, the family of Zavida regained some of its power in Serbia.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first born child of Zavida, Tihomir was chosen to rule over Rascia as a Grand Župan (highest title) following Byzantiums division of the Serb lands by Manuel I, his other sons were given česti[2] (parts): Stracimir ruled West 
2 CONC Morava, Miroslav ruled Zahumlje and Travunia, Stefan Nemanja was given Toplica, Ibar, Rasina and Reke.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family===
2 CONT
2 CONT #Tihomir († 1169), oldest son, ruled as Grand župan of Raška (1163.-(1166) 1168).
2 CONT #Stracimir († after 1189), ruled the region of Western Morava (1163-(1166) 1168, 1169-?).
2 CONT #Miroslav († 1196 or 1199), ruled Zahumlje (1163-(1166) 1168, and 1169 to his death).
2 CONT #Nemanja (born 1113 - † 1199), youngest son, ruled as Grand župan of Raška ((1166) 1168 - 25.03. 1196).
2 CONT #Daughter(s), (as Nemanja and his brothers are registered as maternal uncles of Mihailo III of Duklja)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * According to Tibor Zivkovic (Portreti srpskih vladara, Beograd, 2006) '''zupan Zavida was the only son of Vukan.''' 
2 CONT * Other version of his father: '''Урош I / Uroš I''' (see: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uro%C5%A1_I_of_Rascia)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogy.euweb.cz/balkan/balkan5.html
