1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==='''  Holy Roman Empress Consort & Regent '''===
2 CONT
2 CONT Although there is some doubt about her ancestry, most of the available sources point out to her actual parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Theophanu''' (Greek: Θεοφανώ, Theophano; c. 955 – June 15, 991), also spelled '''Theophania''' (Θεοφάνια), '''Theophana''' or '''Theophano''', was an Empress consort of the Holy Roman Empire by marriage to Holy Roman Emperor Ot
2 CONC to II, and regent of the Holy Roman Empire during the minority of her son from 983 until her death in 991. (Wikpedia)
2 CONT
2 CONT Her ancestry has been uncertain for many years. A good overview of the different points of view is given by: (in German)
2 CONT
2 CONT link: http://www.manfred-hiebl.de/mittelalter-genealogie/_byzanz/t/theophano_sklerina_deutsche_koenigin_991/glocker_theophano.html
2 CONT
2 CONT She is described in contemporary documents as a niece of [https://www.geni.com/people/John-I-Tzimiskes-Eastern-Roman-Emperor/6000000002187823066 Emperor Ioannes Tzimiskes]. There are differing suggestions about her parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT ""Davids suggests that she was the daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Konstantinos-Skleros/6000000002187952983 Konstantinos Skleros] and his wife [https://www.geni.com/people/Sophia-Phokaina/6000000002187952991 Sofia][1424],
2 CONC  who was probably the sister* of the [https://www.geni.com/people/Maria-Skleraina/385944807780003976 first wife] of [https://www.geni.com/people/John-I-Tzimiskes-Eastern-Roman-Emperor/6000000002187823066 Emperor Ioannes]. An indic
2 CONC ation that this may be correct is that '''Theofano's''' second daughter was named [https://www.geni.com/people/Sophie-I-Abbess-of-Gandersheim/6000000010885538132 Sophie], normal Byzantine practice being to name the first daughter 
2 CONC after the paternal grandmother and the second after the maternal grandmother[1425]. It also appears to be chronologically sustainable. However, too little is known about the families of Emperor Ioannes and his wife to propose this
2 CONC  as the only plausible hypothesis, especially as the word "neptis" could cover a wide variety of relationships. In addition, it cannot even be assumed that the wife of Konstantinos Skleros was the only individual named Sofia in th
2 CONC ese families at the time." (MedLands)
2 CONT -------------
2 CONT ''not exactly, but from the reconstructions here on GENI the nephew relationship would still be there:''<br/>
2 CONT *[https://www.geni.com/people/Maria-Skleraina/385944807780003976 first wife] of [https://www.geni.com/people/John-I-Tzimiskes-Eastern-Roman-Emperor/6000000002187823066 Emperor Ioannes] is sister of [https://www.geni.com/people/Kon
2 CONC stantinos-Skleros/6000000002187952983 Konstantinos Skleros] married with [https://www.geni.com/people/Sophia-Phokaina/6000000002187952991 Sofia] 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT == Wikipedia ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Theophanu Skleraina''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Statue at St Dionysius Church, Eschwege 
2 CONT
2 CONT Empress consort of the Holy Roman Empire 
2 CONT Tenure 972–983  Coronation 14 April 972 
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse Otto II, Holy Roman Emperor 
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue: 
2 CONT * Sophie I, Abbess of Gandersheim 
2 CONT * Adelheid I, Abbess of Quedlinburg 
2 CONT * Matilda, Countess Palatine of Lorraine 
2 CONT * Otto III, Holy Roman Emperor 
2 CONT
2 CONT Theophanu (Greek: Θεοφανώ Σκλήραινα, Theophano Skleraina; circa 960 – June 15, 991), also spelled Theophania (Θεοφάνια), Theophana or Theophano, was the niece of the Byzantine Emperor John I Tzimiskes. By her marriage with Holy Ro
2 CONC man Emperor Otto II, she was Empress consort of the Holy Roman Empire and held regency as Empress dowager upon her husband's death in 983. Her name is derived from Medieval Greek Theophaneia (Θεοφάνεια), "appearance of God" (Theop
2 CONC hany).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage certificate,
2 CONT
2 CONT Lower Saxon State Archive, WolfenbüttelAccording to the marriage certificate issued on 14 April 972—a masterpiece of the Ottonian Renaissance—Theophanu is identified as the neptis (niece or granddaughter) of Emperor John I Tzimisk
2 CONC es (925–976). She was nevertheless of distinguished noble heritage: recent research has established her as the daughter of Tzimiskes' brother-in-law (from his first marriage) Constantine Skleros (c. 920–989), and of Sophia Phokain
2 CONC a, Tzimiskes' cousin as the daughter of Kouropalatēs Leo Phokas, brother of Emperor Nikephoros II (c. 912–969).
2 CONT
2 CONT Holy Roman Emperor Otto the Great had requested a Byzantine princess for his son, Otto II, to seal a treaty between the Holy Roman Empire and the Eastern Roman Empire. An unwise reference by the Pope to Emperor Nikephoros II as "G
2 CONC reek" in a letter[citation needed] while Otto's ambassador, Bishop Liutprand of Cremona, was at the Byzantine court, had destroyed the first round of negotiations[citation needed]. With the ascension of John I Tzimiskes, who had n
2 CONC ot been personally referred to other than as Roman Emperor, the treaty negotiations were able to resume. However, not until a third delegation led by Archbishop Gero of Cologne arrived in Constantinople, were they successfully com
2 CONC pleted.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Saxon chronicler Bishop Thietmar of Merseburg, Theophanu was not the ''virgo desiderata'', the anticipated imperial princess, as the Ottonian dynasty had marked out Anna Porphyrogenita, a daughter of late Emperor 
2 CONC Romanos II. Nevertheless, when Archbishop Gero conducted her to Rome, Emperor Otto knew that he could not refuse the offer. The 12-year-old princess duly arrived in grand style in 972, with a magnificent escort including Byzantine
2 CONC  artists, architects and artisans, and bearing great treasure.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage and children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto II and Theophanu, crowned and blessed by Jesus Christ, Byzantine ivory relief, Musée de Cluny, ParisTheophanu and Otto were married by Pope John XIII on April 14, 972 at Saint Peter's and she was crowned empress the same day 
2 CONC in Rome. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sophie I, Abbess of Gandersheim and Essen, born 975, died 1039.
2 CONT * Adelheid I, Abbess of Quedlinburg and Gandersheim, born November or December 977, died 1040.
2 CONT * Matilda, born 979, died 1025, married Ezzo, count palatine of Lotharingia
2 CONT * Otto III, Holy Roman Emperor, born June or July 980
2 CONT * A daughter, a twin to Otto III, who died before October 8, 980
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life as Empress'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto II succeeded his father on 8 May 973. Theophanu accompanied her husband on all his journeys, and she is mentioned in ca. one quarter of the emperor's formal documents, evidence of her privileged position, influence and intere
2 CONC st in affairs of the empire. It is known that she was frequently at odds with her mother-in-law, Adelaide of Italy, which caused an estrangement between Otto II and Adelaide. According to Abbot Odilo of Cluny, Adelaide was very ha
2 CONC ppy when "that Greek woman" died.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Benedictine chronicler Alpert of Metz describes Theophanu as being an unpleasant and talkative woman. Theophanu was also criticized for her decadence, which manifested in her bathing once a day and introducing luxurious garmen
2 CONC ts and jewelry into Germany. She is credited with introducing the fork to Western Europe - chronographers mention the astonishment she caused when she "used a golden double prong to bring food to her mouth" instead of using her ha
2 CONC nds as was the norm." The theologian Peter Damian even asserts that Theophanu had a love affair with John Philagathos, a Greek monk who briefly reigned as Antipope John XVI.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto II died suddenly on 7 December 983 at the age of 28, probably from malaria. His three-year-old son, Otto III, had already been appointed King of the Romans during a diet held on pentecost of that year at Verona. On Christmas 
2 CONC Theophanu had him crowned by the Mainz archbishop Willigis at Aachen Cathedral, with herself ruling as Empress Regent on his behalf. Upon the death of Emperor Otto II, Bishop Folcmar of Utrecht released his cousin, the Bavarian du
2 CONC ke Henry the Quarrelsome from custody. Duke Henry allied with Archbishop Warin of Cologne and seized his nephew Otto III in spring 984, while Theophanu was still in Italy. Nevertheless he was forced to surrender the child to his m
2 CONC other, who was backed by Archbishop Willigis of Mainz and Bishop Hildebald of Worms.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theophanu officially took over regency in May 985 and reigned the Holy Roman Empire until her death in 991, including the lands of Italy and Lotharingia. By her prudent policies, she also was able to conclude peace with Duke Henry
2 CONC 's former supporter Duke Mieszko I of Poland and to safeguard her minor son's interests. Like the Byzantine empress regnants Irene of Athens (752–803) and Theodora (815–867), who also had ruled for their minor sons, she issued dip
2 CONC lomas in her own name as imperator augustus, "Emperor", the years of her reign counted from the accession of her husband in 972.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died at Nijmegen and was buried in the Church of St. Pantaleon near her wittum in Cologne. The chronicler Thietmar eulogized her as follows: "Though [Theophanu] was of the weak sex she possessed moderation, trustworthiness, an
2 CONC d good manners. In this way she protected with male vigilance the royal power for her son, friendly with all those who were honest, but with terrifying superiority against rebels."
2 CONT
2 CONT Because Otto III was still a child, his grandmother Adelaide of Italy took over the regency until Otto III became old enough to rule on his own.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Disputed Ancestry ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Her parentage is not certain. She has been called a daughter of Roman II or of Konstantinos Skleros, patrikios. If the daughter of Skleros, then it is not certain whether by his wife Sophia Prokas or his wife Agatha Taronitissa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wagner gives her parents as possibly Roman II, Byzantine Emperor, and his wife Theophana, daughter of Romanus I, Byzantine Emperor. Theophana as daughter of Roman II is logical but in doubt. It appears in Weis, ￼''Ancestral Roots'
2 CONC '￼ (3rd edition), as well as ''￼Complete Peerage￼'', but was dropped from the 4th and 5th editions, based on a manuscript by Moriarty showing Theophana's probable descent as a daughter of Sophia Prokas, daughter of Leo Prokos, and
2 CONC  her husband Konstantinos Skleros, brother of Marie, first wife of the Emperor John Tsimices. (''Ancestral Roots''￼, 6th edition, accepts the Moriarty material._ The Moriarty manuscript cites "Wer War die Kaiserin Theophano" (1939
2 CONC ). -- adapted from Stuart, pp. 123, 133, 174.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The parentage of Theophano, wife of the Emperor Otto II, has been much disputed. The evidence and arguments are summaried and discussed in a recent article by Count Rüdt-Collenberg, "Wer war Theophana" [Who was Theophana]. Prince
2 CONC  Toumanoff (in a letter of 5 November 1972) comments that though this well sums up all the arguments, it fails, in his view, to draw the correct conclusions. "That she is referred to as a niece of John Tsimisces, instead of as a d
2 CONC aughter of Romanus II is no argument, because at the moment of her marriage she was indeed the niece of the then reigning Emperor, that is, John I." Vasiliev has shown that Romanus and Theophano indeed had another child, so that t
2 CONC here is room for Theophano in their family. Her name, rare enough, is that of Romanus II's low-born wife, assumed after her elevation.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Finally, there is only one serious argument, cited by Rüdt-Collenberg, namely that of affinity and no trace of dispensation, in connexion with the marriage, or just bethrothal, of Romanus II's granddaughter Zoë to Theophano's son
2 CONC  Otto III, that is, her first cousin. But the situation is not as simple as that. On the Western side, the fact (adduced by Rüdt-Collenberg) that we have no trace of a dispensation for such a marriage is rather an argument from si
2 CONC lence, and silence complicated by the presence of an anti-Pope; he was a Byzantine creature and it was he who, prior to becoming anti-Pope, carried on the negotiations for the marriage (C.M.H., 6, (1966), p.184); he quite obviousl
2 CONC y would have acceded to the wishes of his Imperial protector at Constantinople. On the Byzantine side, one notices very often that the intransigence of the clergy withers before a forceful Emperor such as Basil II. Indeed, Zoë lat
2 CONC er married her second cousin (once removed) Romanus Argyrus and no questions were asked. In the view of all this, I personally consider Theophano a daughter of Romanus II, unless stronger argument to the contrary is produced." (Si
2 CONC r Anthony Wagner, ''￼Pedigree and Progress￼'' (London, 1975), p. 258).
2 CONT
2 CONT "I would answer that arguing from a cited relationship is not conclusive in either direction. On the other hand, while an argument from silence is scarcely a strong argument against Theophana being a daughter of Romanus II, it is 
2 CONC equally weak to argue that an anti-Pope, just because he conducted the negotiations, would have been likely to overlook the relationship. Just because a Pope (or anti-Pope) apparently permitted Zoë to marry her second cousin once 
2 CONC removed with no known dispensation does not necessarily mean that there was no dispensation. Further, it is no evidence that he would or could have allowed her to marry her first cousin - a much more obvious relationship - in the 
2 CONC same way. The argument that this Pope was a creature of the Emperor would be equally valid if it were argued that a dispensation would surely have been easily granted if one had been needed. Given the universal tendency to inflate
2 CONC  the parentage of women when there is some question - and in this case, an understandable desire that the brilliant Byzantine marriage of the Emperor Otto have been made with the daughter of an Emperor rather than merely with a ni
2 CONC ece - I am inclined to accept the lesser (and equally plausible) line."
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Theophanu (Greek: Θεοφανώ Σκλήραινα, Theophano Skleraina; c. 955 – June 15, 991), also spelled Theophania (Θεοφάνια), Theophana or Theophano, was the niece of the Byzantine Emperor John I Tzimiskes. By her marriage with Holy Roman
2 CONC  Emperor Otto II, she was Empress consort of the Holy Roman Empire and held regency as Empress dowager upon her husband's death in 983. Her name is derived from Medieval Greek Theophaneia (Θεοφάνεια), "appearance of God" (Theophan
2 CONC y).
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the marriage certificate issued on 14 April 972—a masterpiece of the Ottonian Renaissance—Theophanu is identified as the neptis (niece or granddaughter) of Emperor John I Tzimiskes (925–976) who was of Armenian descen
2 CONC t. She was nevertheless of distinguished noble heritage: recent research speculates that she was the daughter of Tzimiskes' brother-in-law (from his first marriage) Constantine Skleros (c. 920–989), and of Sophia Phokaina, Tzimisk
2 CONC es' cousin as the daughter of Kouropalatēs Leo Phokas, brother of Emperor Nikephoros II (c. 912–969).[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Holy Roman Emperor Otto the Great had requested a Byzantine princess for his son, Otto II, to seal a treaty between the Holy Roman Empire and the Eastern Roman Empire. An unwise reference by the Pope to Emperor Nikephoros II as "G
2 CONC reek" in a letter [3] while Otto's ambassador, Bishop Liutprand of Cremona, was at the Byzantine court, had destroyed the first round of negotiations.[4] With the ascension of John I Tzimiskes, who had not been personally referred
2 CONC  to other than as Roman Emperor, the treaty negotiations were able to resume. However, not until a third delegation led by Archbishop Gero of Cologne arrived in Constantinople, were they successfully completed.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Saxon chronicler Bishop Thietmar of Merseburg, Theophanu was not the virgo desiderata, the anticipated imperial princess, as the Ottonian dynasty had marked out Anna Porphyrogenita, a daughter of late Emperor Roma
2 CONC nos II. Nevertheless, when Archbishop Gero conducted her to Rome, Emperor Otto knew that he could not refuse the offer. The young princess duly arrived in grand style in 972, with a magnificent escort including Byzantine artists, 
2 CONC architects and artisans, and bearing great treasure.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theophanu and Otto were married by Pope John XIII on April 14, 972 at Saint Peter's and she was crowned empress the same day in Rome. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophie I, Abbess of Gandersheim and Essen, born c 975, died 1039.
2 CONT Adelheid I, Abbess of Quedlinburg and Gandersheim, born November or December 977, died 1040. Matilda, born 979, died 1025, married Ezzo, count palatine of Lotharingia Otto III, Holy Roman Emperor, born June or July 980 A daughter,
2 CONC  a twin to Otto III, who died before October 8, 980 Theophanu is not to be confused with her granddaughter Theophanu, Abbess of Essen Abbey from 1039–1058, daughter of Otto III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto II succeeded his father on 8 May 973. Theophanu accompanied her husband on all his journeys, and she is mentioned in ca. one quarter of the emperor's formal documents, evidence of her privileged position, influence and intere
2 CONC st in affairs of the empire. It is known that she was frequently at odds with her mother-in-law, Adelaide of Italy, which caused an estrangement between Otto II and Adelaide. According to Abbot Odilo of Cluny, Adelaide was very ha
2 CONC ppy when "that Greek woman" died.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Benedictine chronicler Alpert of Metz describes Theophanu as being an unpleasant and talkative woman. Theophanu was also criticized for her decadence, which manifested in her bathing once a day and introducing luxurious garmen
2 CONC ts and jewelry into Germany. She is credited with introducing the fork to Western Europe - chronographers mention the astonishment she caused when she "used a golden double prong to bring food to her mouth" instead of using her ha
2 CONC nds as was the norm." The theologian Peter Damian even asserts that Theophanu had a love affair with John Philagathos, a Greek monk who briefly reigned as Antipope John XVI.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto II died suddenly on 7 December 983 at the age of 28, probably from malaria. His three-year-old son, Otto III, had already been appointed King of the Romans during a diet held on Pentecost of that year at Verona. At Christmas,
2 CONC  Theophanu had him crowned by the Mainz archbishop Willigis at Aachen Cathedral, with herself ruling as Empress Regent on his behalf. Upon the death of Emperor Otto II, Bishop Folcmar of Utrecht released his cousin, the Bavarian d
2 CONC uke Henry the Quarrelsome from custody. Duke Henry allied with Archbishop Warin of Cologne and seized his nephew Otto III in spring 984, while Theophanu was still in Italy. Nevertheless he was forced to surrender the child to his 
2 CONC mother, who was backed by Archbishop Willigis of Mainz and Bishop Hildebald of Worms.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theophanu officially took over regency in May 985 and reigned the Holy Roman Empire until her death in 991, including the lands of Italy and Lotharingia. By her prudent policies, she also was able to conclude peace with Duke Henry
2 CONC 's former supporter Duke Mieszko I of Poland and to safeguard her minor son's interests. Like the Byzantine empress regnants Irene of Athens (752–803) and Theodora (815–867), who also had ruled for their minor sons, she issued dip
2 CONC lomas in her own name as imperator augustus, "Emperor", the years of her reign counted from the accession of her husband in 972.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died at Nijmegen and was buried in the Church of St. Pantaleon near her wittum in Cologne. The chronicler Thietmar eulogized her as follows: "Though [Theophanu] was of the weak sex she possessed moderation, trustworthiness, an
2 CONC d good manners. In this way she protected with male vigilance the royal power for her son, friendly with all those who were honest, but with terrifying superiority against rebels."
2 CONT
2 CONT Because Otto III was still a child, his grandmother Adelaide of Italy took over the regency until Otto III became old enough to rule on his own.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theophano_Sklerina_of_Byzantium
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Theophanu (960 – June 15, 991) (Greek: Θεοφανώ Theophano), also spelled Theophania, Theophana or Theophano, was born in Constantinople, and was the wife of Otto II, Holy Roman Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto I, Holy Roman Emperor had requested a Byzantine princess for his son, Otto, to seal a treaty between the Holy Roman Empire and the Eastern Roman Empire. The unwise reference by the pope to the ruler in Constantinople as "Gree
2 CONC k"[citation needed] in a letter[citation needed] while Otto's ambassador, Liudprand of Cremona, was in the Byzantine court, had destroyed the first round of negotiations[citation needed]. With the ascension of a new emperor who ha
2 CONC d not been personally been referred to other than as Roman Emperor, the treaty negotiations were able to resume. Theophanu duly arrived in 972, arriving in grand style with a magnificent escort and bearing great treasure. However,
2 CONC  according to the chronicler Thietmar, she was not the ''virgo desiderata'', the Imperial princess, that was expected. Theophanu is identified in the marriage contract as the neptis (niece or granddaughter) of Emperor John I Tzimi
2 CONC sces (Ιωάννης Ι Τσιμισκής). John Tzimisces married secondly Theodora, the sister of Romanus II.
2 CONT
2 CONT At one time it was believed[who?] Theophanu was the daughter of the Emperor Romanos II and his consort Theophano, but no mention is made of her being porphyrogenita (πορφυρογέννητη), born-in-the-purple, nor are her parents identif
2 CONC ied. It is unlikely that Theophanu was the daughter of any emperor. Current research holds that her father was Konstantinos Skleros (Κωνσταντίνος Σκληρός), brother of the pretender Bardas Skleros (Βάρδας Σκληρός) and her mother wa
2 CONC s Sophia Phokaina (Σοφία Φώκαινα), niece of Emperor Nikephoros II (Νικηφόρος ΙΙ). Her father's sister, Maria Skleraina (Μαρία Σκλήραινα), was the first wife of Tzimisces'. Accordingly[who?] she was of Armenian descent and a relati
2 CONC ve of the imperial families only by marriage, not blood[citation needed].
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT Theophanu and Otto were married by Pope John XIII on April 14, 972 at Saint Peter's and she was crowned empress the same day in Rome. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Sophie I, Abbess of Gandersheim and Essen, born 975, died 1039.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Adelheid I, Abbess of Quedlinburg and Gandersheim, born November or December 977, died 1040.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Matilda, born 979, died 1025; who married Ezzo, count palatine of Lotharingia
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Otto III, Holy Roman Emperor, born June or July 980
2 CONT
2 CONT     * A daughter, a twin to Otto, who died before October 8, 980
2 CONT
2 CONT Life as Empress
2 CONT
2 CONT Theophanu accompanied her husband on all his journeys, and issued diplomas in her own name as Empress. It is known she was frequently at odds with her mother-in-law, Adelaide of Italy, and this caused an estrangement between Otto 
2 CONC II and Adelaide. According to Odilo, Abbot of Cluny, Adelaide was very happy when "that Greek woman" died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert of Metz describes Theophanu as being an unpleasant and talkative woman. Theophanu was also criticized for her decadence, which manifested in her bathing once a day and introducing luxurious garments and jewelry into Germany
2 CONC . She is credited with introducing the fork to Western Europe - chronographers mention the astonishment she caused when she "used a golden double prong to bring food to her mouth" instead of using her hands as was the norm. "The t
2 CONC heologian Peter Damian even asserts that Theophanu had a love affair with John Philagathos, a Greek monk who briefly reigned as Antipope John XVI.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarcophagus of Empress Theophanu
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto II died suddenly on December 7, 983 and was buried in Rome. That Christmas Theophanu had their three-year-old son crowned as Otto III, with herself ruling as Empress Regent on his behalf. Henry II, Duke of Bavaria seized Otto
2 CONC  in spring 984, but was forced to surrender the child to his mother. With the cooperation of Willigis, Archbishop of Mainz, and Hildebald, Bishop of Worms, Theophanu reigned until her death in 991.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was buried in the Church of St. Pantaleon at Cologne. The chronicler Thietmar eulogized her as follows: "Though [Theophanu] was of the weak sex she possessed moderation, trustworthiness, and good manners. In this way she prote
2 CONC cted with male vigilance the royal power for her son, friendly with all those who were honest, but with terrifying superiority against rebels."
2 CONT
2 CONT Because Otto III was still a child, his grandmother Adelaide of Italy took over the regency until Otto III became old enough to rule on his own.
2 CONT
