1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Narjot_de_Toucy_(died_1241) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Narjot de Toucy (died 1241), lord of Bazarnes, was the son of Narjot II of Toucy (France) and of his wife Agnes de Dampierre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alongside his father-in-law Theodore Branas and Geoffroy de Merry, Narjot de Toucy formed part of the council that briefly governed Constantinople from 17 December 1219 (on the death of Conon de Béthune until the arrival of the de
2 CONC signated Emperor, Robert of Courtenay, in 1220. He served as Regent of the Latin Empire in 1228-1231, during the minority of Baldwin II of Constantinople and in 1238-1239 during the Emperor's fund-raising journey in western Europe
2 CONC . 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1241.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Narjot de Toucy's first wife was the daughter of Theodore Branas and the dowager Empress Anna (Agnes of France). They had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Philip of Toucy, who was Regent of the Latin Empire from 1245 to 1247 and Admiral of Sicily in 1271.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Anselin de Toucy, lord of Mottola.
2 CONT
2 CONT * An unnamed daughter, who in 1239 married William II Villehardouin, prince of Achaea.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Marguerite de Toucy, who married Leonardo of Veruli, Chancellor of the Principality of Achaea.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1239 or 1240, presumably after his first wife's death, Narjot de Toucy married the daughter of Jonas, King of the Cumans. She became a nun after his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Narjot_de_Toucy_(died_1241)_
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/burgdauxer.htm#NarjotToucydied1241B
2 CONT
2 CONT NARJOT [III] de Toucy, son of NARJOT [II] Seigneur de Toucy & his wife Agnes de Dampierre (-1241).  “Yterius dominus de Tociaco et Baiserna” confirmed a donation to Rigny abbey made by “Raynaldus cognomento Caprarius”, with the su
2 CONC pport of “fratres mei Ansericus et Narjotus”, by charter dated 1201[399].  Seigneur de Bazarnes.  Regent of the Latin Empire of Constantinople 1228/31 and 1238/39. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' firstly --- Branaina, daughter of THEODOROS Branas & his wife Agnes de France (-before 1239).  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines refers to the daughter of "sororem regis Francorum imperatricem" marrying "Nargaldo
2 CONC  de Toceio, Guidonis de Dampetro consobrinus", in a later passage recording that she was "filia Livernes et sororis regis Francie"[400]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' secondly ([1239/40]) --- of the Kumans, daughter of JONAS of the Kumans (-Constantinople after 1241).  The primary source which confirms her origin and marriage has not been identified.  She became a nun after her husband 
2 CONC died. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Narjot [III] & his first wife had four children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Narjot III de Toucy (died 1241), lord of Bazarnes''', was the son of''' Narjot II of Toucy''' (France) and his wife '''Agnes de Dampierre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Narjot de Toucy's first wife was the daughter of T'''heodore Branas and the dowager Empress Anna (Agnes of France).''' They had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Philip of Toucy, who was regent of the Latin Empire from 1245 to 1247 and Admiral of Sicily in 1271.[1]
2 CONT # Anselin of Toucy, lord of Mottola.[1]
2 CONT # An unnamed daughter, who in 1239 married William II of Villehardouin, prince of Achaea.
2 CONT # Margaret of Toucy, who married Leonardo of Veruli, Chancellor of the Principality of Achaea.
2 CONT In 1239 or 1240, presumably after his first wife's death, Narjot de Toucy married the daughter of Jonas, King of the Cumans. She became a nun after his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''
2 CONT Narjot de Toucy (died 1241), lord of Bazarnes, was the son of Narjot II of Toucy (France) and of his wife Agnes de Dampierre. Alongside his father-in-law Theodore Branas and Geoffroy de Merry, Narjot de Toucy formed part of the co
2 CONC uncil that briefly governed Constantinople from 17 December 1219 (on the death of Conon de Béthune until the arrival of the designated Emperor, Robert of Courtenay, in 1220. He served as Regent of the Latin Empire in 1228–1231, du
2 CONC ring the minority of Baldwin II of Constantinople and in 1238–1239 during the Emperor's fund-raising journey in western Europe. He died in 1241.[1] Narjot de Toucy's first wife was the daughter of Theodore Branas and the dowager E
2 CONC mpress Anna (Agnes of France). They had four children: Philip of Toucy, who was Regent of the Latin Empire from 1245 to 1247 and Admiral of Sicily in 1271. Anselin of Toucy, lord of Mottola. An unnamed daughter, who in 1239 marrie
2 CONC d William II Villehardouin, prince of Achaea. Margaret of Toucy, who married Leonardo of Veruli, Chancellor of the Principality of Achaea. In 1239 or 1240, presumably after his first wife's death, Narjot de Toucy married the daugh
2 CONC ter of Jonas, King of the Cumans. She became a nun after his death. Notes[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump up ^ These details from the French Wikipedia entries fr:Empire latin de Constantinople and fr:Baudouin II de Courtenay. Bibliography[edit]
