2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=1ee9f0c7-bbf6-4029-9ba2-8f914dc7827e&tid=6959821&pid=-1168763166
1 NOTE     ES II:62-3
1 NOTE He was called The Saint.  In 1217 Ferdinand's mother, Berengaria, 
2 CONT renounced her title to the Castilian throne in favor of her son. Alfonso 
2 CONT IX, who had himself expected to acquire Castile, was angered at his wife's 
2 CONT action, and, aided by a group of Castilian nobles favorable to his claim, 
2 CONT made war upon his newly crowned son. Ferdinand, however, with the wise 
2 CONT counsel of his mother, proved more than a military match for Alfonso, who 
2 CONT at length was forced to abandon his plan of conquering Castile. Through 
2 CONT the good offices of Berengaria, Ferdinand was able to effect the peaceful 
2 CONT union of León and Castile upon the death of his father in 1230. Ferdinand 
2 CONT devoted his energies to prosecuting the war against the Moors, conquering 
2 CONT Cordoba in 1236 and Seville in 1248. He was rigorous in his suppression of 
2 CONT the heretical Albigenses, a fact largely responsible for his canonization 
2 CONT more than two centuries later. In 1242 Ferdinand reestablished at 
2 CONT Salamanca the university originally founded by his grandfather.
1 NOTE Canonized in 1671.
2 CONT Fought the Moors consolidated the Reconquista. King of Castile.
1 NOTE Saint Ferdinand III (July 30 or August 5, 1199 - May 30, 1252), was the King of Castile from 1217 and King of León from 1230. Through his second marriage he was also Count of Aumale. He finished the work done by his maternal gran
2 CONC dfather Alfonso VIII and consolidated the Reconquista. In 1231, he permanently united Castile and León. He was canonized in 1671 and, in Spanish, he is Fernando el Santo or San Fernando.
2 CONT
2 CONT St Ferdinand was the son of Alfonso IX of León and Berenguela of Castile. He was born at the monastery of Valparaíso (Peleas de Arriba, Zamora) in 1198 or 1199. His parents' marriage was annulled by order of Pope Innocent III in
2 CONC  1204, due to consanguinity. Berenguela took their children, including Ferdinand, to the court of her father. In 1217, her younger brother Henry I died and she succeeded him to the Castilian throne, but she immediately surrendered
2 CONC  it to her son Ferdinand, for whom she initially acted as regent. When Alfonso died in 1230, Ferdinand also inherited León, though he had to fight for it with Alfonso's designated heirs, Sancha and Dulce, the daughters of his fir
2 CONC st wife. He thus became the first sovereign of both kingdoms since the death of Alfonso VII in 1157.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early in his reign, Ferdinand had to deal with a rebellion of the House of Lara. He also established a permanent border with the Kingdom of Aragon by the Treaty of Almizra (1244).
2 CONT
2 CONT St Ferdinand spent much of his reign fighting the Moors. Through diplomacy and war, exploiting the internal dissensions in the Moorish kingdoms, he triumphed in expanding Castilian power over southern Iberian Peninsula. He capture
2 CONC d the towns of Úbeda in 1233, Córdoba in 1236, Jaén in 1246, and Seville in 1248, and occupied Murcia in 1243, thereby reconquering all Andalusia save Granada, whose king nevertheless did homage to Ferdinand. Ferdinand divided 
2 CONC the conquered territories between the Knights, the Church, and the nobility, whom he endowed with great latifundias. When he took Córdoba, he ordered the Liber Iudiciorum to be adopted and observed by its citizens, and caused it 
2 CONC to be rendered, albeit inaccurately, into Castilian.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT United arms of Castile and León which Ferdinand first used.The capture of Córdoba was the result of an uneven and uncoordinated process whereby parts (the Ajarquía) of the city first fell to the independent almogavars of the Si
2 CONC erra Morena to the north, which Ferdinand had not at the time subjugated.[1] Only in 1236 did Ferdinand arrive with a royal army to take Medina, the religious and administrative centre of the city.[1] Ferdinand set up a council of
2 CONC  partidores to divide the conquests and between 1237 and 1244 a great deal of land was parcelled out to private individuals and members of the royal family as well as the Church.[2] On 10 March 1241, Ferdinand established seven ou
2 CONC tposts to define the boundary of the province of Córdoba.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the domestic front, he strengthened the University of Salamanca and founded the current Cathedral of Burgos. He was a patron of the newest movement in the Church: that of the friars. Whereas the Benedictines and then the Cister
2 CONC cians and Cluniacs had taken a major part in the Reconquista up til then, Ferdinand founded Dominican, Franciscan, Trinitarian, and Mercedarian houses in Andalusia, thus determining the religious future of that region. Ferdinand h
2 CONC as also been credited with sustaining the convivencia in Andalusia.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primera Crónica General de España asserts that, on his death bed, Ferdinand commended his son "you are rich in lands and in many good vassals - more so than any other king in Christendom," probably in recognition of his expa
2 CONC nsive conquests.[4] He was buried within the Cathedral of Seville by his son Alfonso X. His tomb is inscribed with four languages: Arabic, Hebrew, Latin, and an early incarnation of Castilian.[5] St Ferdinand was canonized by Pope
2 CONC  Clement X in 1671. Several places named San Fernando were founded across the Spanish Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT The symbol of his power as a king was his sword Lobera.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages and family
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1219, Ferdinand married Elisabeth of Hohenstaufen (1203-1235), daughter of the German king Philip of Swabia and Irene Angelina. Elisabeth was called Beatriz in Spain. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso X, his successor 
2 CONT Fadrique 
2 CONT Ferdinand (1225-1243/1248) 
2 CONT Eleanor (born 1227), died young 
2 CONT Berenguela (1228-1288/89), a nun at Las Huelgas 
2 CONT Henry 
2 CONT Philip (1231-1274). He was promised to the Church, but was so taken by the beauty of Princess Kristina of Norway, daughter of Haakon IV of Norway, who had been intended as a bride for one of his brothers, that he abandoned his hol
2 CONC y vows and married her. She died in 1262, childless. 
2 CONT Sancho, Archbishop of Toledo and Seville (1233-1261) 
2 CONT Juan Manuel, Lord of Villena 
2 CONT Maria, died an infant in November 1235 
2 CONT After he widowed, he married Jeanne of Dammartin, Countess of Ponthieu, before August 1237. They had four sons and one daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand (1239-1269), Count of Aumale 
2 CONT Eleanor (c.1241-1290), married Edward I of England 
2 CONT Louis (1243-1269) 
2 CONT Simon (1244), died young and buried in a monastery in Toledo 
2 CONT John (1245), died young and buried at the cathedral in Córdoba 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT ^ a b Edwards, 6. 
2 CONT ^ Ibid, 7. 
2 CONT ^ Ibid, 182. 
2 CONT ^ Ibid, 1. 
2 CONT ^ Menocal, 47. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT González, Julio. Reinado y Diplomas de Fernando III, i: Estudio. 1980. 
2 CONT Menocal, María Rosa. The Ornament of the World. Little, Brown and Company: Boston, 2002. ISBN 0316168718 
2 CONT Edwards, John. Christian Córdoba: The City and its Region in the Late Middle Ages. Cambridge University Press: 1982
