1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_of_Villehardouin
2 CONT
2 CONT William II of Villehardouin, (Guillaume II de Villehardouin) (died May 1, 1278) was the last Villehardouin prince of Achaea and Andravida and ruled the principality at the height of its power and influence.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was the son of Geoffrey I Villehardouin. In 1236 he aided the Latin Empire against the Byzantine Empire of Nicaea, and was rewarded with the overlordship of the Venetian Duchy of the Archipelago and other Venetian territor
2 CONC ies in the Aegean Sea. In 1239 he married the daughter of Narjot de Toucy and of Narjot's first wife (who was the daughter of the dowager empress Anna). William came to power in Achaea in 1246 when his brother Geoffrey II Villehar
2 CONC douin died.
2 CONT
2 CONT As prince he conquered the remaining territory of the Peloponnese (known at the time as Morea) and built the fortress of Mistra near Sparta. In 1249 he captured Monemvasia with help from his Euboeote vassals, and later that year a
2 CONC ccompanied Louis IX of France on the Seventh Crusade, joining him in Cyprus with 400 knights and 28 ships. Louis also gave him a license to mint coins in the style of royal French money.
2 CONT
2 CONT Uner William's rule the Duchy of the Archipelago, the Duchy of Athens, and the Lombard lords ("triarchs") of Euboea recognized him as their lord. In 1255 his Venetian second wife Carintana dalle Carceri died, leading to a dispute 
2 CONC over the inheritance of a fief in Euboea, and war broke out between Venice and Achaea (the "War of the Euboeote Succession" terciers being the three Lombard lords of the island). William won the war and also defeated the Duke of A
2 CONC thens in 1258, reaffirming his influence over the duchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1259 he married Anna Komnene Doukaina, daughter of Michael II of Epirus, forming an alliance with the Byzantine Despotate of Epirus against Nicaea, an alliance which also included Manfred of Sicily. In September of that year he
2 CONC  led the Achaean forces at the Battle of Pelagonia against the Nicaeans, but the Epirote army deserted and William was defeated. He fled the field and hid under a haystack, where he was captured and brought to Nicaea. He remained 
2 CONC in captivity until 1262, and was forced to hand over Grand Maigne, Monemvasia and Mistra to the Byzantine Empire, which had been restored in Constantinople the previous year.
2 CONT
2 CONT William had now lost all of his previous power, as had his former lord, Baldwin II of Constantinople, whose Latin Empire was lost with the Byzantine restoration. William and Baldwin both acknowledged Charles of Anjou as lord of Ac
2 CONC haea under the Treaty of Viterbo in 1267; Charles had earlier defeated and killed William's old ally Manfred. As a vassal of Charles, William and 400 Achaean knights fought against Conradin at the Battle of Tagliacozzo in 1268.
2 CONT
2 CONT William and Anna had two daughters, Isabella and Margaret; Isabella, the elder daughter, married Charles's son Philip of Sicily, who, however, predeceased his father. Charles personally succeeded William in 1278, ending the Villeh
2 CONC ardouin dynasty and setting up Angevin rule, with the principality governed essentially as a province of the Kingdom of Naples. With the decreasing power and influence of Achaea, the Duchy of Athens became the most powerful state 
2 CONC in Greece.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was also noted as a poet and troubadour, and the Manuscrit du Roi, containing two of his own compositions, was written in Achaea during his reign. He was fluent in both French and Greek.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/LATIN%20LORDSHIPS%20IN%20GREECE.htm#GuiilaumeIIVillehardouindied1278
2 CONT
2 CONT '''GUILLAUME de Villehardouin''', son of GEOFFROY I Prince of Achaia & his wife Elisabeth [de Chappes] (Kalamata Castle after [1208][97]-Kalamata Castle[98] 1 May 1278, bur Andravida, church of St James).  The Livre de la Conquest
2 CONC e de la Morée names “Goffrey et Guillerme” as the two sons of Geoffroy I Prince of Achaia, adding that Geoffroy inherited the principality and that Guillaume was granted “la chastellanie de Calamate” after the death of their fathe
2 CONC r[99].  He succeeded his brother in [1246] as GUILLAUME II "le grand Dent" Prince of Achaia.  He captured Monemvasia, with Venetian naval help, after a three year siege and built the fortresses of Mistra (to protect the plain of S
2 CONC parta), Old Maina (near Cape Matapan) and Levtro (also known as Beaufort, near Kisternes) to gain the submission of the Slavonic Meling tribe[100].  He was in Cyprus in May 1249 to join the Fifth Crusade of Louis IX King of France
2 CONC .  Claiming control over Eubœa, he invaded the island in 1256 and captured Negroponte, although it was recovered by Venice in 1258[101].  He invaded Athens in 1258, in response to the coalition against him formed by Guy Lord of At
2 CONC hens, and defeated Guy at the pass of Mount Karydi[102].  He joined the alliance formed by Manfredo King of Sicily and Mikhael [II] Angelos Lord of Epirus to attack Nikaia under Emperor Mikhael VIII.  Under the sébastokrator Ioann
2 CONC es Palaiologos, the emperor's troops defeated the alliance in the valley of Pelagonia in Autumn 1259 and captured Guillaume.  He was released end 1261, in return for swearing allegiance to Emperor Mikhael and ceding the towns of M
2 CONC onemvasia, Mistra, Maina and Hierakion to Byzantium.  Once back in Morea, he recaptured some of the lost territory and defeated the Byzantine army near Andravida in 1263 and at Nikli in 1264[103].  Hoping to gain support against a
2 CONC ny renewed Byzantine attack, he swore allegiance to Charles I King of Sicily [Anjou-Capet] under the first Treaty of Viterbo 24 May 1267, the agreement being confirmed by the betrothal of his daughter to the king's son[104].  On G
2 CONC uillaume's death, the principality of Achaia passed under the direct authority of the king of Sicily, who appointed a series of baillies as his governors.  This state of affairs continued until 1289 when Guillaume's daughter Isabe
2 CONC lle was installed as Pss of Achaia at the time of her second marriage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (1239) [AGNES] de Toucy, daughter of NARJOT de Toucy Lord of Bazarne & his first wife --- Branaina.  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines records the marriage of "Guielmus" and "filiam Nargaldi natam de filia Live
2 CONC rnes et sororis regis Francie" but does not name his wife[105].  The primary source which confirms her name has not yet been identified.  [1239/52]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [106]m secondly ( [1246] ) CARINTANA dalle Carceri, daughter of RIZZARDO dalle Carceri of Eubœa & his wife --- (before 1220-1255).  She was baroness in her own right of the northern third of the island of Eubœa, which her husband 
2 CONC claimed on her death[107].  Sturdza states that "recent research" has established that Prince Guillaume did not marry Carintana but gives no further details[108]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m thirdly ( [1258] ) as her first husband, ANNA Komnenodukaina of Epirus, daughter of MIKHAEL [II] Komnenos Dukas Angelos Lord of Epirus & his wife Theodora Dukaina Petraliphaina Basilissa (-4 Jan 1286).  Georgius Akropolites reco
2 CONC rds the marriage of "Michaelis…filia sua Anna" and "Achaiæ principe"[109].  Georgius Phrantzes names "Manfredum Siculorum rege et principem Peloponnesi et Achaiæ" as the two sons-in-law of "Aetolorum et Epirotarum despota Michaele
2 CONC "[110].  The Livre de la Conqueste de la Morée records the marriage of “le prince Guillerme” and “la suer dou despot”[111].  The Istoria of Marino Sanudo Torsello records that "il principe Guglielmo" married "Nicephoro…sorella" an
2 CONC d that her dowry was "il castello della Liconia"[112].  She adopted the name AGNES.  After her husband died, she received the castles of Kalamata and Khlemutsi for life, although she later exchanged them for other land in Morea[11
2 CONC 3].  She married secondly (before 1280) Nicolas [II] de Saint-Omer Joint Lord of Thebes (-1294).  The Livre de la Conqueste de la Morée records that, after the death of Prince Guillaume, “la princesses a feme…suer…de Quir Niccifor
2 CONC e” married “le baron monseignor Nicole de Saint Omer le veillart, li sires de la moitié d´Estives”[114].  The late 13th century genealogy by Balduinus de Avennis records that "Nicolaum", son of "Bilas", married "Achayæ principissa
2 CONC m"[115]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT   Guillaume & his first wife had [two] children:  
2 CONT   Guillaume & his third wife had two children: 
