1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keratsa_of_Bulgaria
2 CONT
2 CONT *http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00570281&tree=LEO
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kera_Tamara '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Kera Tamara''' (Bulgarian: Кера Тамара) was the daughter of the Bulgarian Emperor Ivan Alexander and his second wife Sarah-Theodora. Kera Tamara was a sister of Ivan Shishman and Ivan Sratsimir. She was born in the 1340s and or
2 CONC iginates from the Shishman dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first husband of Kera Tamara was despot Constantine. According to one theory he was the despot of Velbazhd Constantine Dragash whose daughter Helena Dragash married the Byzantine Emperor Manuel II and became mother of the last
2 CONC  Byzantine Emperor Constantine XI. However, that theory has been dismissed by the historians because in 1371 Kera Tamara was already a widow while Constantine Dragash died in 1395. Therefore, despot Constantine who was depicted in
2 CONC  the Tetraevangelia of Ivan Alexander next to the Bulgarian princess was another man.
2 CONT
2 CONT As early as 1371 when Ivan Alexander died and Ivan Shishman inherited the throne, in the capital Tarnovo arrived ambassadors from the Ottoman Sultan Murad I to arrange his relations with the new Emperor of Bulgaria. The Sultan who
2 CONC  was obviously familiar with the beauty of Kera Tamara and the fact that she was a widow demanded her to become his wife as a guarantee for the peace between the two counties.[1] Ivan Shishman managed to divert his demand and prol
2 CONC onged his decision for seven years. On that occasion an anonymous Bulgarian chronicle from the 15th century records:
2 CONT
2 CONT >''...And on the throne came Shishman, son of Alexander. Amorat [Murad] sent to him men to ask for his sister, but he did not want to give his sister, Tsarina Kera Tamara...[1]''
2 CONT
2 CONT However, in 1378 when his attempts to stop the Turks failed, Ivan Shishman reluctantly sent Kera Tamara in the harem of the Sultan in the Ottoman capital Bursa.[1] She kept her Christian faith. In the Boril Synadnik her fate was p
2 CONC raised as a self-sacrifice:
2 CONT
2 CONT >''...For Kera Tamara, daughter of the great Emperor Ivan Alexander, great princess, wife of the great emir Amurat, to whom she was given for the sake of the Bulgarian people. And she, when she arrived there, kept her True faith, 
2 CONC freed her people, lived well and pious and died in peace, may her memory live forever...[1]''
2 CONT
2 CONT The grave of Kera Tamara remains today in Bursa in the family tomb of the Ottoman dynasty next to the grave of Murad I and the visitor know it as the place of "the Bulgarian Empress Maria". According to Kera Tamara's will, her tom
2 CONC b remained uncovered and barley was sowed on her grave.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources===
2 CONT
2 CONT #  Павлов, Пламен. Търновските царици. В.Т.:ДАР-РХ, 2006.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Kera Tamara (Bulgarian: Кера Тамара) was the daughter of the Bulgarian Emperor Ivan Alexander and his second wife Sarah-Theodora. Kera Tamara was a sister of Ivan Shishman and Ivan Sratsimir. She was born in the 1340s and originat
2 CONC es from the Shishman dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first husband of Kera Tamara was despot Constantine. According to one theory he was the despot of Velbazhd Constantine Dragash whose daughter Helena Dragash married the Byzantine Emperor Manuel II and became mother of the last
2 CONC  Byzantine Emperor Constantine XI. However, that theory has been dismissed by the historians because in 1371 Kera Tamara was already a widow while Constantine Dragash died in 1395. Therefore, despot Constantine who was depicted in
2 CONC  the Tetraevangelia of Ivan Alexander next to the Bulgarian princess was another man.
2 CONT
2 CONT As early as 1371 when Ivan Alexander died and Ivan Shishman inherited the throne, in the capital Tarnovo arrived ambassadors from the Ottoman Sultan Murad I to arrange his relations with the new Emperor of Bulgaria. The Sultan who
2 CONC  was obviously familiar with the beauty of Kera Tamara and the fact that she was a widow demanded her to become his wife as a guarantee for the peace between the two counties. Ivan Shishman managed to divert his demand and prolong
2 CONC ed his decision for seven years. On that occasion an anonymous Bulgarian chronicle from the 15th century records:
2 CONT
2 CONT ...And on the throne came Shishman, son of Alexander. Amorat [Murad] sent to him men to ask for his sister, but he did not want to give his sister, Tsarina Kera Tamara...
2 CONT
2 CONT However, in 1378 when his attempts to stop the Turks failed, Ivan Shishman reluctantly sent Kera Tamara in the harem of the Sultan in the Ottoman capital Bursa. She kept her Christian faith. In the Boril Synadnik her fate was prai
2 CONC sed as a self-sacrifice:
2 CONT
2 CONT ...For Kera Tamara, daughter of the great Emperor Ivan Alexander, great princess, wife of the great emir Amurat, to whom she was given for the sake of the Bulgarian people. And she, when she arrived there, kept her Orthodox faith,
2 CONC  freed her people, lived well and pious and died in peace, may her memory live forever...
2 CONT
2 CONT The grave of Kera Tamara remains today in Bursa in the family tomb of the Ottoman dynasty next to the grave of Murad I and the visitor know it as the place of "the Bulgarian Empress Maria". According to Kera Tamara's will, her tom
2 CONC b remained uncovered and barley was sowed on her grave.
