1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murad_I
2 CONT
2 CONT Murad I (nick-named Hüdavendigâr - from Persian: خداوندگار Khodāvandgār - "the God-like One") (Serbian: Мурат 1. / Murat I) (Turkish: I. Murat Hüdavendigâr) (March or June 29, 1326, Sogut or Bursa – June 15, 1389, Battle of Kosovo
2 CONC ) (Ottoman Turkish: مراد اول) was the ruler of the Ottoman Empire, Sultan of Rûm, from 1362 to 1389. He was the son of Orhan I and the Valide Sultan Nilüfer Hatun (whose name means Water lily in Turkish), daughter of the Prince of
2 CONC  Yarhisar or Byzantine princess Theodora Kantakouzene (also named Nilüfer), who was of ethnic Greek descent and became the ruler following his father's death in 1362.
2 CONT
2 CONT He established the Empire by building up a society and government in the newly conquered city of Adrianople (Edirne in Turkish) and by expanding the realm in Europe, bringing most of the Balkans under Ottoman rule and forcing the 
2 CONC Byzantine emperor to pay him tribute. It was Murad who established the former Osmanli tribe into an empire. He established the title of sultan in 1383 and the corps of the janissaries and the devşirme recruiting system. He also or
2 CONC ganised the government of the Divan, the system of timars and timar-holders (timariots) and the military judge, the kazasker. He also established the two provinces of Anadolu (Anatolia) and Rumeli (Europe).
2 CONT
2 CONT Wars
2 CONT
2 CONT Murad fought against the powerful emirate of Karaman in Anatolia and against the Serbs, Bulgarians and Hungarians in Europe. In particular, a Serb expedition to expel the Turks from Adrianople led by King Vukasin and Despot Ugljes
2 CONC a, both being Serbian magnates, was defeated on September 26, 1371, by Murad's capable second lieutenant Lala Şâhin Paşa, the first governor (beylerbey) of Rumeli. In 1385 Sofia fell to the Ottomans. In 1386 Prince Lazar Hrebeljan
2 CONC ović defeated a small Ottoman force at the Battle of Pločnik. The Ottoman army did not suffer heavy casualties, and was unable to capture Niš on the way back. In 1389 Murad's army defeated the Serbian Army and its allies under the
2 CONC  leadership of Lazar at the Battle of Kosovo.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are different accounts from different sources about when and how Murad I was assassinated. The contemporary sources mainly noted that the battle took place and that both Prince Lazar and the Sultan lost their lives in the ba
2 CONC ttle. The existing evidence of the additional stories and speculations as to how Murad I died were disseminated and recorded in the 15th century and later, decades after the actual event. One Western source states that during firs
2 CONC t hours of the battle, Murad I was assassinated by Serbian nobleman and knight Miloš Obilić by knife. Most Ottoman chroniclers (including Dimitrie Cantemir)  state that he was assassinated after the finish of the battle while goin
2 CONC g around the battlefield. Others state that he was assassinated in the evening after the battle at his tent by the assassin who was admitted to ask a special favour. His older son Bayezid, who was in charge of the left wing of the
2 CONC  Ottoman forces, took charge after that. His other son, Yakub Bey, who was in charge of the other wing, was called to the Sultan's command center tent by Bayezid, but when Yakub Bey arrived he was strangled, leaving Bayezid as the
2 CONC  sole claimant to the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the earliest preserved Christian record, a letter of Florentine senate to the King Tvrtko I of Bosnia, dated 20 October 1389, Murad I's killing was described. Milos Obilic, a Serbian warrior had managed to get through the Ottom
2 CONC an army and kill Murad I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fortunate, most fortunate are those hands of the twelve loyal lords who, having opened their way with the sword and having penetrated the enemy lines and the circle of chained camels, heroically reached the tent of Amurat himself.
2 CONC  Fortunate above all is that one who so forcefully killed such a strong vojvoda by stabbing him with a sword in the throat and belly. And blessed are all those who gave their lives and blood through the glorious manner of martyrdo
2 CONC m as victims of the dead leader over his ugly corpse.
2 CONT
2 CONT Therefore, the earliest account suggests a coordinated attack of a group of noblemen who managed to break through and behind enemy lines, rather than a solitary act of deception by a single person.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sultan Murad's internal organs were buried in Kosovo field and remains to this day on a corner of the battlefield in a location called Meshed-i Hudavendigar which has gained a religious significance by the Muslims (which had been 
2 CONC renamed Obilić by the Serbs). It has recently been renovated. His other remains were carried to Bursa, his Anatolian capital city, and were buried in a tomb at the complex built in his name.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages and progeny
2 CONT
2 CONT Murad I, oil on canvas by Haydar Hatemi
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages of Murad I:
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1359 Valide Sultan Gülçiçek Hatun- of Greek origin
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1370 Maria Thamara Hatun - sister of Bulgarian Czar Ivan Shishman
2 CONT
2 CONT Pasha Melek Hatun - daughter of Kizil Murad Bey
2 CONT
2 CONT Fulane Hatun - daughter of Candaroglu
2 CONT
2 CONT Progeny of Murad I:
2 CONT
2 CONT Yakub Celebi (? - d. 1389) - son. In the first recorded fratricide in the history of the Ottoman dynasty, Bayezid I had Yakup killed during or following the Battle of Kosovo at which their father had been killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sultan Bayezid I (1354-1402)- son of Gulcicek Hatun
2 CONT
2 CONT Savci Bey - son. He and his lover, Byzantyne emperor John V Palaeologus' son Andronicus,[7] rebelled against their fathers. Murad had Savci killed. Andronicus, who had surrendered to his father, was imprisoned and blinded at Murad
2 CONC 's insistence.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ibrahim Bey - son
2 CONT
2 CONT Yahshi Bey - son of Gulcicek Hatun
2 CONT
2 CONT Halil Bey - son
2 CONT
2 CONT Nefise - daughter
2 CONT
2 CONT Sultan - daughter
2 CONT
2 CONT Sultan Murad in literature
2 CONT
2 CONT In William Shakespeare's History play Henry V,
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Harry refers to Murad as "Amurath" in Act V Scene 2 when he succeeds his father, King Henry IV, in 1412:
2 CONT
2 CONT Chief Justice. Good morrow, and God save your majesty!
2 CONT
2 CONT King Henry V. This new and gorgeous garment, majesty,
2 CONT
2 CONT Sits not so easy on me as you think.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brothers, you mix your sadness with some fear:
2 CONT
2 CONT This is the English, not the Turkish court;
2 CONT
2 CONT Not Amurath an Amurath succeeds,
2 CONT
2 CONT But Harry Harry!
2 CONT
2 CONT Murad (as "Amurath the First") is the subject of Thomas Goffe's play The Courageous Turk, published in 1632.
2 CONT
