1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/daughter-of-Alexios-IV-of-Trebizond/6000000004094974286?through=6000000126169972892
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/TREBIZOND.htm#dauAlexiosIVMKaraYuluk
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.         daughter .  Her parentage and marriage are confirmed by the Historia Byzantina of Michælis Ducæ Nepotis which names "Karaiulucus...Alexii Comneni imperatoris Trapezuntini gener" as ruler in "vicinus Lazis ac Persis"[195
2 CONC ].  Kuršankis dates the marriage to [1422] when he says that Kara Ilük conquered Karahisar, Tercan, Bayburt et Akşehir and besieged Erzincan with the help of Emperor Alexios IV[196].  If this dating is correct, her father must hav
2 CONC e been Emperor Alexios IV.  There is no indication whether this daughter was legitimate or illegitimate.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' ([1422]) KARA YÜLÜK, son of --- (-[Aug/Sep] 1435).  
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT A third ''unnamed daughter'', who married Qara Osman, chieftain of the Aq Qoyunlu
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexios_III_of_Trebizond
2 CONT *Not listed in ML
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT Williams, Kelsey Jackson.  ''A Genealogy of the Grand Komnenoi of Trebizond'' (1/2007)< [https://research-repository.st-andrews.ac.uk/handle/10023/8570 link] > Page 183.  PDF page 13.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexios IV, Emperor of Trebizond, 1417-1429 … 
2 CONT
2 CONT Kuršankis (1979, p.246) summarizes by stating that he believes Alexios to have had five children: Ioannes, Alexander, David, Maria and the anonymous wife of Qara Yoluq. He doubts the existence of the other three daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue by Theodora Kantakouzene (Nicol, 1968, pp.169-170): 
2 CONT
2 CONT v. (possibly) Anonyma I. She was married to Qara Yoluq ‘Uthmān ibn Qutlugh, emir of the Āq Quyūnlū and son of Qutlugh ibn Tūr ‘Alī (although whether by his Trapezuntine wife is uncertain), prior to 1421 (Magoulias 1975, pp.127, 29
2 CONC 0-291). She has been previously claimed as a daughter of Alexios III, but Kuršankis (1979, p.245) demonstrates that she must have been a daughter of Alexios IV (cf., however, Ganchou, 2000a) instead and suggests that Anonyma III’s
2 CONC  existence may be owed to the confusion that has arisen over the many intermarriages between the Āq Quyūnlū and the Trapezuntine Komnenoi. Qara Yoluq died of wounds sustained in battle against the Qarā Quyūnlū in August or Septemb
2 CONC er 1435 (Woods, 1976, p.64).
