2 CONT From: https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/158575575/elisabeth-von_ziegenhain
2 CONT
2 CONT ___________________________
2 CONT '''Ulrich V, Lord of Hanau''' (c. 1370–1419 in Schaafheim) was the ruling Lord of Hanau from 1380 to 1404.
2 CONT Childhood
2 CONT His exact date and even the year of his birth were not recorded, because in the medieval view the date the death of a person was far more important, as that was the date a memorial mass would be read. Nevertheless, the exact date 
2 CONC of his death was not recorded either, because his family was silent about him after he was deposed.
2 CONT He was born around 1370, as the eldest son of Ulrich IV and his wife, Elisabeth of Wertheim. He inherited Hanau already in 1380.
2 CONT One of the sons of Ulrich IV was enrolled at the University of Heidelberg in 1390. The enrollment entry does not mention a first name — it only names a domicellus de Hanaw.[1][2] Since '''Ulrich V''' was already ruling Hanau at th
2 CONC is time, it would be unlikely that he was this student.
2 CONT Regency
2 CONT Since he was a minor when he inherited Hanau in 1380, a regency was set up. The father of his fiancée, Count Gottfried VIII of Ziegenhain acted as regent until c. 1388.
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT In 1389, he fought on the side of Elector Palatine Rupert I in the Cities War, a conflict between the emerging cities and the nobility, and on the victorious side in the Feud of Kronberg. In 1400, he was among the Burgmanns of Fri
2 CONC edberg who were sent to the newly elected King Rupert of the Romans, to negotiate about the homage, after King Wenceslaus had been deposed.
2 CONT Government crisis and deposition
2 CONT There were two main reasons why '''Ulrich V''' was deposed by his relatives:
2 CONT The absence of a male heir threatened the survival of the family. In 1375, his father had confirmed the primogeniture decree, which Ulrich II had issued in 1339. The threat was reduced by family treaty of 1391, which allowed his b
2 CONC rother Reinhard II to marry if '''Ulrich V still had no male heir ten years later''', that is, in 1401.
2 CONT '''Ulrich V''' ran into economic difficulties from 1394, and even more so after 1396, even though he had received compensation payments from the city of Frankfurt after the Feud of Kronberg. Apparently, he had overstretched himsel
2 CONC f in his "foreign policy". He mortgaged the village of Hochstadt to Frankfurt, the district of Joßgrund to the Lords of Thüngen, and in the end, he even mortgaged the two major of the four cities in his territory, Hanau and Babenh
2 CONC ausen to his neighbor, political rival and first cousin once removed, Archbishop John II of Mainz, effectively making him co-ruler of the Lordship of Hanau.
2 CONT From c. 1395, a coalition consisting of Reinhard II and his younger brother, John began acting independently and even against '''Ulrich V.''' This led to an open dispute, which was settled by a compromise in 1398. However, the dis
2 CONC pute emerged again, and grew to the level of a feud. From 1400, and more so from 1402, Reinhard II and John sought support from Archbishop John II of Mainz, who eventually changed sides and dropped his support of '''Ulrich V.'''
2 CONT In 1404, '''Ulrich V''' gradually lost power. On 20 February 1404, Reinhard II and John took up government; on 20 November 1404, '''Ulrich''' formally abdicated and ordered his subjects to pay homage to his brothers. '''His wife h
2 CONC ad already renounced any claims she might have on Hanau earlier that year, in return for a monetary compensation, without Ulrich V being even mentioned in the contract.'''
2 CONT Historians in the 18th and 19th century have suggested that '''Ulrich V''' may have suffered from a mental disorder. This disorder would have justified setting aside the primogeniture decree for just one generation. However, there
2 CONC  is no contemporary evidence to support this theory.
2 CONT Reinhard II and John ruled jointly at first. After John died in 1411, Reinhard II ruled alone.
2 CONT Death
2 CONT Little is known about '''Ulrich V''' after his abdication. He is reported to have resided in Frankfurt for a while, and later in the district of Babenhausen. He probably died in 1419 in Schaafheim.
2 CONT '''Marriage and issue'''
2 CONT '''In 1394, Ulrich V married Elisabeth (c. 1375 – 1 December 1431), the daughter''' of Count Gottfried VIII of Ziegenhain. '''They had three daughters''':
2 CONT 1. Elisabeth (d. 1475), married in 1413 to Albert I of Hohenlohe (d. 15 June 1429)
2 CONT 2. Agnes (d. 22 November 1446), abbess of Klarenthal Abbey
2 CONT 3. Adelaide (d. 13 November 1440), a nun at Klarenthal Abbey
2 CONT Ancestors
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ulrich_V,_Lord_of_Hanau
2 CONT
2 CONT ___________________________
2 CONT C.      HERREN von HOHENLOHE in WEIKERSHEIM
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT The following reconstruction is an incomplete outline only, added mainly to show hyperlinks to other families in Medieval Lands.  The primary sources which confirm the family relationships have not been identified unless otherwise
2 CONC  stated. 
2 CONT ALBRECHT von Hohenlohe, son of KRAFT [III] Herr von Hohenlohe-Weikersheim & his wife Anna von Leuchtenberg (-1429). 
2 CONT m (before 11 Feb 1413) ELISABETH von Hanau, '''daughter of ULRICH [V] von Hanau & his wife Elisabeth von Ziegenhain''' (-1475).  Heiress of Ziegenhain. 
2 CONT Albrecht & his wife had children: 
2 CONT *1.         KRAFT [V] von Hohenlohe (-21 Mar 1472).  Graf von Hohenlohe-Weikersheim.  m (1431) MARGARETA von Oettingen, daughter of FRIEDRICH [III] Graf von Oettingen & his second wife Euphemia von Münsterberg (-24 Feb 1472).  Kra
2 CONC ft [V] & his wife had children: 
2 CONT **a)         GOTTFRIED [IV] von Hohenlohe (-1497).  Graf von Hohenlohe-Weikersheim. 
2 CONT **-        GRAFEN von HOHENLOHE-WEIKERSHEIM und SCHILLINGSFÜRST. 
2 CONT **b)         KRAFT [VI] von Hohenlohe (-2 Aug 1503).  Graf von Hohenlohe-Neuenstein.  m (Waldenburg 26 Feb 1476) HELENA von Württemberg, daughter of ULRICH V "der Vielgeliebte" Graf von Württemberg-Stüttgart & his third wife Margu
2 CONC erite de Savoie (-19 Feb 1506, bur Öhringen Stiftskirche).  Kraft [VI] & his wife had children: 
2 CONT ***i)          GEORG von Hohenlohe (-1551).  Graf von Hohenlohe-Waldenburg. 
2 CONT ***-        GRAFEN von HOHENLOHE-WALDENBURG, HOHENLOHE-NEUENSTEIN, HOHENLOHE-LANGENBURG. 
2 CONT ***ii)         MARGARETA von Hohenlohe (30 Jul 1480-Zweibrücken 3 Sep 1522).  m (Zweibrücken 21 Jan 1499) ALEXANDER Pfalzgraf von Zweibrücken und Veldenz, son of LUDWIG Pfalzgraf von Zweibrücken und Veldenz & his wife Jeanne de Cr
2 CONC oÿ (26 Nov 1462-Zweibrücken 31 Oct 1514, bur Zweibrücken St Nikolaus, later removed to Alexanderkirche). 
2 CONT ***iii)        ELISABETH von Hohenlohe (-1540).  m WOLFGANG Graf von Löwenstein, son of LUDWIG Graf von Löwenstein & his first wife Elisabeth von Montfort (-15 Apr 1512).  m secondly GEORG Freiherrr von Hewen, son of --- (-1542). 
2 CONT *2.         ELISABETH von Hohenlohe (-1488).  m firstly (Feb 1441) LUDWIG [V] von Lichtenberg, son of LUDWIG [IV] von Lichtenberg & his wife Anna von Baden (12 May 1417-25 Feb 1471).  m secondly (before 31 Aug 1476) HUGO Graf von 
2 CONC Montfort, son of --- (-16 Oct 1491). 
2 CONT From: https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/FRANCONIA.htm#AlbrechtHohenlohedied1429B
2 CONT
