1 NOTE {geni:about_me} -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/LATIN%20LORDSHIPS%20IN%20GREECE.htm#NiccoloCarceredied1383
2 CONT
2 CONT (a)       NICOLÒ [III] dalle Carceri (-murdered Naxos 1383).  His parentage is confirmed by the 16th century Annali Veneti of Stefano Magno which names "Fiorenza Sanudo…fiola…de Marco Sanudo fradelo fu de Januli Sanudo ducha de Ar
2 CONC chipielago" as mother of "Nicolò dale Carcere ducha del Arcipielago" when recording the latter´s death "in la citade de Nicosia" in 1383[583].  He succeeded his father as Triarch of Eubœa (central part), under the guardianship of 
2 CONC his mother.  He succeeded his mother in 1371 as NICOLÒ II Duke of Naxos and the Archipelago.  He continued to reside in Eubœa, appointing Januli Gozzadini of Anaphe as his regent in Naxos.  He plotted to extend his influence in Eu
2 CONC bœa, planning the capture of Negroponte in 1380 with assistance from the Navarrese Company.  He aroused resentment among his subjects by his extorsion and was murdered, apparently with the connivance of Francesco Crispo who was im
2 CONC mediately accepted as his successor[584].  On his death, Venice confiscated the two thirds of the island of Eubœa which he held[585].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (1372) as her first husband, PETRONILLA Tocco, daughter of LEONARDO I Count of Kefalonia & his wife Maddalena Buondelmonti (-[22 Jun 1409/27 Sep 1410]).  After her first husband's death, she was compensated by property nea
2 CONC r the hot baths of Aedepsos on the island of Eubœa.  She married secondly ([1383]) Nicola Vernier, of Venice[586].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicolò had one illegitimate child by an unknown mistress: 
2 CONT
2 CONT *(1)        FRANCESCO dalle Carceri (-1427).  Treasurer of Venice at Navplion.  He left descendants on Naxos, extinct in the 17th century[587].  
