2 CONT [edit] Cultural references
2 CONT
2 CONT The Romantic French poet Gérard de Nerval wrote a poem dedicated to the king: "Rêverie de Charles VI"[10].
2 CONT
2 CONT The novel The Notebooks of Malte Laurids Brigge by Rainer Maria Rilke describes the old age of Charles VI at length.
2 CONT
2 CONT The story "Hop-Frog, or The Eight Chained Ourang-Outangs" by Edgar Allan Poe involves a scene strikingly similar to the Bal des Ardents. (Full text at Wikisource)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Edith Pattou novel East mentions Charles of France's second son, Charles, to be the white bear.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Charles VI, and his madness, are mentioned at length in the historical novel Het Woud der Verwachting/Le Foret de Longue Attente/In a Dark Wood Wandering (1949) by Hella S. Haasse.
2 CONT
2 CONT Christine de Pisan dedicates a poem to King Charles VI "Prière pour le roi Charles" in which she pleas for the health of her king.
2 CONT
