1 NOTE Duc d'Anjou
2 CONT Comte de Provence
2 CONT Roi de Naples
2 CONT Sources: Sebastien AVY
1 NOTE Il ráegne tout d'abord sous la râegence de sa máere, Marie de Blois.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il tente la reconquãete du royaume de Naples sur Ladislas, fils de Charles III de Duras, roi de Hongrie (entre 1409 et 1411). Il est vainqueur áa la bataille de Rocca Secca en 1411 mais ne râeussit pas áa âevincer Ladislas.
1 NOTE DUKE OF ANJOU
2 CONT
2 CONT "OF ANJOU"
1 NOTE Louis II of Anjou (1377-April 29, 1417), was the rival of Ladislas as King of Naples. He was a member of the House of Valois-Anjou.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT Born in Toulouse, Louis II was the son of Louis I of Anjou, King of Naples, and came into his Angevin inheritance, which included Provence, in 1384, with his rival Charles of Durazzo (father of Ladislas), of the senior Angevin lin
2 CONC e, in possession of Naples.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1386, the underage Ladislas was expelled soon after his father died. Louis II was crowned King of Naples by the Avignonese antipope Clement VII on November 1, 1389 and took possession of Naples the following year. He was ousted
2 CONC  in turn by his rival in 1399.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1409, Louis liberated Rome from Ladislas' occupation; in 1410, as an ally of the antipope John XXIII he attacked Ladislas and defeated him at Roccasecca (1411). Eventually Louis lost his Neapolitan support and had to retire. Hi
2 CONC s claim to Naples passed to his son, Louis III.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married his first cousin once removed Yolande of Aragon (1384-1443) in Arles in 1400, giving him a possibility of inheriting the throne of Aragon through her right. Her father, King John I of Aragon had died in 1396, and her un
2 CONC cle king Martin I of Aragon died in 1410.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis II died at his chateau of Angers, the heart of Anjou; he is buried there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT Louis and Yolande had five surviving children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis III of Anjou, titular King of Naples, Duke of Anjou, etc. 
2 CONT René of Anjou, King of Naples, Duke of Anjou, etc. 
2 CONT Charles of Le Maine (1414-1472), Count of Maine 
2 CONT Marie of Anjou (1404 - 1463), married 1422, Bourges Charles VII of France 
2 CONT Yolande (1412, Arles - 1440), married 1431, Nantes Francis I, Duke of Brittany 
1 NOTE The Angevin French prince, Louis II of Anjou (1377–1417) was the rival of Ladislas as King of Naples.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis II was the son of Louis I of Anjou, King of Naples, and came into his Angevin inheritance, which included Provence, in 1384, with his rival Charles of Durazzo (father of Ladislas), of the senior Angevin line, in possession o
2 CONC f Naples.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1386, the underage Ladislas was expelled soon after his father died. Louis II was crowned King of Naples by the Avignonese antipope Clement VII on November 1, 1389 and took possession of Naples the following year. He was ousted
2 CONC  in turn by his rival in 1399.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1409, Louis liberated Rome from Ladislas' occupation; in 1410, as an ally of the antipope John XXIII he attacked Ladislas and defeated him at Roccasecca (1411). Eventually Louis lost his Neapolitan support and had to retire. Hi
2 CONC s claim to Naples passed to his son, Louis III.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Yolande of Aragon (1384–1443) in Arles in 1400, giving him a possibility of inheriting the throne of Aragon through her right. Her father, King John I of Aragon had died in 1396, and her uncle king Martin I of Aragon di
2 CONC ed in 1410.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis II died at his chateau of Angers, the heart of Anjou; he is buried there. Louis and Yolande had five surviving children.
