1 OCCU Countess of Flanders, Countess of Flanders (as Margaret III), Countess of Artois and Countess Palatine of Burgundy (as Margaret II) and twice Duchess consort of Burgundy, d. 3-16-1404/1405, Condesa de Flandes
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ='''MARGUERITE de Flandre''' (Mâle near Bruges 1350, chr 13 Apr 1350 - Arras 16 Mar 1405, bur Lille, église Saint-Pierre) 
2 CONT '''Ctss of Flanders, Ctss d'Artois, Ctss de Nevers and Rethel, Ctss Palatine of Burgundy. Dss of Brabant and Limburg, Markgravine of Antwerp, Dame de Malines 1404'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ==History==
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter and heir of [https://www.geni.com/people/Louis-II-van-Vlaanderen-Comte-de-Flandres-Flanders/6000000185363088822 '''Louis de Mâle'''], Count of Flanders, Count of Nevers, and Count of Rethel (1346–1384); and hi
2 CONC s wife [https://www.geni.com/people/Margaretha-de-M%C3%A2le-Flanders-Nevers/6000000001504983220?through=6000000185363088822  '''Margueritte of Brabant'''] . Through his mother, Margaret I, Louis was also Count of Burgundy and Coun
2 CONC t of Artois (1382–1384).
2 CONT
2 CONT In '''1357''' the young  [https://www.geni.com/people/Marguerite-Ctss-Flanders-d-Artois-de-Nevers-Burgundy/6000000004532537004?through=6000000006101415412  '''Margueritte'''], then seven, married [https://www.geni.com/people/Phili
2 CONC ppe-de-Rouvres-duc-de-Bourgogne/6000000006101415412?through=6000000004532537004 '''Philip of Rouvres'''], grandson and heir of Eudes IV, Duke of Burgundy. Philip was eleven years old and her second cousin. He was count of Burgundy
2 CONC  and Artois (1347–1361), Duke of Burgundy (1350–1361), and became yet Count of Auvergne and Boulogne (1360–1361). The marriage was unconsumated, and Margaret was widowed in 1361, when Philip died in a riding accident.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 19 Oct '''1364''', Margaret was betrothed to  [https://www.geni.com/people/Edmund-of-Langley-1st-Duke-of-York/6000000016643963931 '''Edmund of Langley'''], s/o Edward III King of England & his wife Philippa de Hainaut (Abbot’s 
2 CONC Langley, Hertfordshire 5 Jun 1341-King’s Langley, Hertfordshire 1 Aug 1402, bur King’s Langley, Church of the Dominican Friars). This betrothal was arranged under the Treaty of Dover 19 Oct 1364, but the French persuaded Pope Urba
2 CONC n V to refuse a dispensation on grounds of consanguinity. 
2 CONT
2 CONT With the death of  '''Philip of Rouvres''' the duchy of Burgundy was inherited by John II of France (as John I of Burgundy). In 1363 Philip the Bold, John's youngest son, had been granted the duchy as [https://www.geni.com/people/
2 CONC Philip-II-the-Bold-Duke-of-Burgundy/6000000004526559011?through=6000000004532537004 '''Philip II of Burgundy'''], and he subsequently married its former duchess (Margaret) in '''1369'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000205447000863&size=medium}}
2 CONT
2 CONT ''[https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=91925448 Marriage of duke Philip the Bold and Margaret III, Countess of Flanders - Grandes Chroniques de France Paris, Bibliothèque nationale de France http://expositions.bnf.fr/f
2 CONC lamands/grand/fla_459.htm, Public Domain]''
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip had earlier been Duke of Touraine (1359–1363), and became later also count of Charolais (1390–1404).
2 CONT
2 CONT When Margaret's father died in '''1384''', she and Philip inherited the counties of Artois, Burgundy, Flanders, Nevers, and Rethel. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip died in '''1404''', and Margaret died the next year.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Guizot, her father had not wished her to marry a Frenchman, being more friendly to England; he was persuaded to agree to the marriage by his mother, Margaret of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret and Philip had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''John the Fearless''' (1371–1419, murdered at Montereau), his eldest son and successor as Duke of Burgundy
2 CONT * '''Charles''' (1372–1373)
2 CONT * '''Margaret of Burgundy''' (October 1374 – March 8, 1441, Le Quesnoy), Countess of Mortain married William VI, Count of Holland and Duke of Bavaria-Straubing
2 CONT * '''Louis''' (1377–1378)
2 CONT * '''Catherine of Burgundy''' (April 1378, Montbard – January 24, 1425, Grey-sur-Saone), married Leopold IV, Duke of Austria
2 CONT * '''Bonne''' (1379–1399, Arras)
2 CONT * '''Antoine, Duke of Brabant''' (August, 1384 – October 25, 1415, at Agincourt)
2 CONT * '''Mary of Burgundy''' (September 1380, Dijon – October 2, 1422, Thonon-les-Bains), married Amadeus VIII, Duke of Savoy
2 CONT * '''Philip II, Count of Nevers and Rethel''' (1389–1415, at Agincourt)
2 CONT
2 CONT Her eldest son, John the Fearless, succeeded her in 1405 as Duke of Burgundy, Count of Burgundy, Count of Artois, and Count of Flanders. In 1406 her younger son Anthony inherited Brabant and Limburg. Nevers was at first, in her li
2 CONC fetime, given to their eldest son John, but after his accession to duchy, went later to her youngest son Philip.
2 CONT
2 CONT
