2 CONT ==Marie de Valois-Bourgogne1 
2 CONT '''F, #113799, b. September 1386, d. 8 October 1422'''
2 CONT Last Edited=2 Jul 2020
2 CONT Consanguinity Index=2.28%
2 CONT '''Marie de Valois-Bourgogne was born in September 1386 at Dijon, France.2,3 She was the daughter''' of Philippe II de Valois, Duc de Bourgogne and Marguerite de Flandre, Comtesse de Flandre.1 '''She married Amadeo VIII Duca di Sa
2 CONC voia''', son of Amadeo VII Conte di Savoia and Bonne de Berry, Vicomtesse de Carlat et de Murat, '''on 30 October 1401 at Arras, FranceG.2,3 She died on 8 October 1422 at age 36 at Chateau of Thonon les Bains.'''2,3
2 CONT '''Children of Marie de Valois-Bourgogne and Amadeo VIII Duca di Savoia'''
2 CONT 1. Margaret di Savoia2 b. 1405, d. 1418
2 CONT 2. Anthony di Savoia2 b. 1407
2 CONT 3. Anthony di Savoia2 b. 1408
2 CONT 4. Marie di Savoia2 b. 1411, d. 1469
2 CONT 5. Amadeus di Savoia, Prince of Piedmonte2 b. 1412, d. 1431
2 CONT 6. Ludovico I Duca di Savoia+2 b. 21 Feb 1412/13, d. 29 Jan 1465
2 CONT 7. Bonne di Savoia2 b. 1415, d. 1430
2 CONT 8. Philip di Savoia, Comte de Genève2 b. 1417, d. 1444
2 CONT 9. Margherita Principessa di Savoia-Achaea+2 b. 1420, d. 30 Sep 1479
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1. [S16] Jirí Louda and Michael MacLagan, Lines of Succession: Heraldry of the Royal Families of Europe, 2nd edition (London, U.K.: Little, Brown and Company, 1999), table 75. Hereinafter cited as Lines of Succession.
2 CONT 2. [S130] Wikipedia, online http;//www.wikipedia.org. Hereinafter cited as Wikipedia.
2 CONT 3. [S3268] Hans Harmsen, "re: Chester Family," e-mail message to Darryl Roger Lundy, 21 August 2008. Hereinafter cited as "re: Chester Family."
2 CONT https://www.thepeerage.com/p11380.htm#i113799
2 CONT
2 CONT _________________________________
2 CONT '''Amadeus VIII''' (4 September 1383 – 7 January 1451), nicknamed the Peaceful, was Count of Savoy from 1391 to 1416 and Duke of Savoy from 1416 to 1440. He was the first to hold the ducal title, granted by Emperor Sigismund. Know
2 CONC n for his diplomatic temperament and administrative reforms, he strengthened the state's institutions and fostered internal peace.
2 CONT He also claimed the papacy from 1439 to 1449 as Antipope Felix V,[a] in opposition to Popes Eugene IV and Nicholas V, becoming the last historical antipope recognised by a significant portion of the Catholic clergy.
2 CONT Count and duke
2 CONT '''Amadeus''' was born in Chambéry on 4 September 1383,[1] the son of Amadeus VII, Count of Savoy and Bonne of Berry.[2] Even as a boy, he suffered from strabismus, which one of his father's physicians, Jean de Grandville, claimed
2 CONC  he could cure.[3] He became Count of Savoy in 1391 after his father's death on 2 November 1391, caused (it was said) by poisoning, or at least bad treatment at the hands of his physicians.[4] In his testament,[5] the Red Count, A
2 CONC madeus VII, had appointed his mother Bonne of Bourbon rather than his wife Bonne of Berry, niece of King Charles VI of France, as regent for '''Amadeus VIII'''. This set off a struggle at the court between the grandmother and the 
2 CONC mother, eventually involving the dukes of Berry, Bourbon, Orléans, and King Charles VI of France.[6] '''Amadeus VIII's''' grandmother acted as regent until 1397, during his minority.[1]
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT '''Amadeus''' died in Geneva on 7 January 1451,[32] and was buried at Ripaille on 9 January.[33] Pope Nicholas V wrote an apostolic brief, dated 30 April 1451, in appreciation of the life and career of Cardinal Amadeus.[34]
2 CONT '''Marriage and issue'''
2 CONT '''Count Amadeus married Mary of Burgundy (1386–1428), daughter''' of Philip the Bold, Duke of Burgundy, '''at Arras in 1401.[35] However, she did not arrive in Savoy until 1403.[36] They had nine children, of whom only four survi
2 CONC ved to adulthood''':
2 CONT 1. Margaret of Savoy (13 May 1405 – 1418).
2 CONT 2. Anthony of Savoy (September 1407 – before 12 December 1407).
2 CONT 3. Anthony of Savoy (1408 – after 10 October 1408).
2 CONT 4. Marie of Savoy (end of January 1411 – 22 February 1469), married Filippo Maria Visconti, Duke of Milan.[37]
2 CONT 5. Amadeus of Savoy (26 March 1412 – 17 August 1431), Prince of Piedmont, heir apparent until his premature death.
2 CONT 6. Louis (24 February 1413 – 29 January 1465), succeeded his father as Duke of Savoy in 1440.
2 CONT 7. Bonne of Savoy (September 1415 – 25 September 1430).
2 CONT 8. Philip of Savoy (1417 – 3 March 1444), Count of Genève.
2 CONT 9. Margaret of Savoy (7 August 1420 – 30 September 1479), married firstly Louis III, Duke of Anjou and titular King of Naples;[38] secondly Louis IV, Count Palatine of the Rhine;[39] and thirdly Ulrich V, Count of Württemberg.
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT * a. When numbering of the popes began to be used, Antipope Felix II was counted as one of the popes of that name. The second official Pope Felix is thus known by the number III, and the third was given the number IV. It also affe
2 CONC cted the name taken by Amadeus, who would have been the fourth Pope Felix.
2 CONT References
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amadeus_VIII,_Duke_of_Savoy
