1 OCCU Queen of the medieval Iberian kingdom of Aragon, Queen consort of Aragon 1380 - July 3, 1431, AKA "Yolande"
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Violant_of_Bar
2 CONT https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yolande_de_Bar
2 CONT
2 CONT Yolande (c. 1365 – 3 July 1431 ) was a member of the House of Bar, who became Duchess of Girona and Countess of Cervera (1380–1387) through her marriage to the heir to the Crown of Aragon, the future John I, and then Queen of Arag
2 CONC on, of Valencia, Majorca, Sardinia and (titular) of Corsica, and Countess of Barcelona, Roussillon and Cerdinia (1387-1395).
2 CONT
2 CONT Origins
2 CONT Yolande, according to the excerpt entitled L'Histoire Latine du Roy Charles VI (1404), taken from the Histoire généalogique de la maison royale de Dreux (Paris), Luxembourg, Preuve de l'histoire de la maison de Bar-le Duc, was the
2 CONC  second of the eleven children of Robert I, count and then duke of Bar, and Marie, daughter of Jean II le Bon, King of France and Bonne of Luxembourg, and therefore sister of King Charles V. Robert I was the eldest son of Henry IV
2 CONC , Count of Bar and Mousson and his wife, Yolande of Flanders, herself the daughter of Robert, Lord of Marle and Cassel and his wife, Jeanne de Bretagne. Yolande was therefore the niece of Robert III, Count of Flanders, Yolande of 
2 CONC Burgundy, Countess of Nevers and Arthur II, Duke of Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT At the age of 15, in 1380, she married John I of Aragon (1350 † 1395), then Duke of Girona and Count of Cervera, and heir to the Crown of Aragon, in Perpignan. Her husband was 30 years old and had been a widower for two years: he 
2 CONC had been betrothed to Jeanne de France, posthumous daughter of King Philip VI, but she having died while joining him in Béziers, he had then married Mathe, daughter of Count Jean I d'Armagnac, with whom he had had five children, a
2 CONC mong whom only one daughter, Jeanne will reach adulthood.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the union of Yolande with King John Iwere born 7 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT James (1382 † 1388), Dauphin of Girona and Count of Cervera
2 CONT Yolande or Violante (1383 † 1442), married in 1400 to Louis II (1377 † 1417), Duke of Anjou, Count of Maine and Provence ;
2 CONT Ferdinand (1389 † 1389), Duke of Girona and Count of Cervera
2 CONT Antonie (1391 † 1392);
2 CONT Eleanor (1393 † 1393);
2 CONT Peter (1394 † 1394), Duke of Girona and Count of Cervera
2 CONT Jeanne (1396 † 1396).
2 CONT Once married, Yolande quickly sought a political role at court and she clashed with her father-in-law, King Peter IV the Ceremonious, and especially with his fourth wife, Queen Consort Sibylle de Fortià, not least because the roya
2 CONC l couple was opposed to this union.
2 CONT
2 CONT John became king in 1387 and was soon given the nickname "John the Hunter". Of frail health and probably suffering from epilepsy but also because of his lack of will and his preference for hunting over royal duties, he was frequen
2 CONC tly forced to delegate royal authority to his wife: in November 1388 she met the representatives of the City in the Cortes of Monzon and managed to avoid the crisis that was threatening. As queen-lieutenant, she governed the Crown
2 CONC  of Aragon in this capacity for seven years, during which she transformed her court into a centre of French culture, which became the meeting place for the most talented Provençal troubadours.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Pedro IV died, his wife had been deprived of all her pensions and had fled the court to take refuge in Sant Martí Sarroca. His relatives, including the Count of Empúries, rebelled and allied themselves with Count John III of 
2 CONC Armagnac, nephew of the first wife of King John I, who sent his troops to invade Catalonia. In 1389-90, the attacks reached as far as Girona, but the invaders were soon repulsed and defeated by the Catalan troops and Sibylle, who 
2 CONC had been captured, was also deprived of her freedom and locked up in the castle of Montcada. She did, however, receive a pension in exchange for relinquishing all her prerogatives.
2 CONT
2 CONT Widow of John I, who died in a hunting accident in 1396, she devoted herself to the education of her only remaining daughter, Yolande. In the absence of direct male heirs, the throne of Aragon went to Martin I, the younger brother
2 CONC  of John I.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1417, she brought a lawsuit before the Parliament of Paris against her brother, the cardinal-bishop Louis de Bar, from whom she claimed a share of their parents' inheritance. On 30 September she obtained an annual annuity of 1,
2 CONC 500 livres tournois, and Louis ceded the duchy of Bar to René d'Anjou, his sister's grandson, to whom he married Isabella of Lorraine, daughter and heiress of Duke Charles II.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in Barcelona on 13 August 1431 at the age of 66 and was buried next to her husband in the Monastery of Santa Maria de Poblet, the necropolis of the kings of the Crown of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/De_Bar-2
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/246981077/yolande-de_bar
2 CONT
2 CONT Royalty. Sometimes listed as Violant de Bar. Born before 1365 as the oldest daughter of Robert de Bar and Marie de France. She married Juan de Aragon in 1380 and bore him at least six children, of which only one survived their chi
2 CONC ldhood.
