0 @N6000000002188294064@ NOTE Degna Djan
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Degna Djan was an Emperor of Aksum (9th or 10th centuries). Paul B. Henze states that his throne name was "'Anbasa Wedem", which tradition states was his oldest son's name.[1] His younger son was Dil Na'od.
1 CONT
1 CONT E. A. Wallis Budge provides an account of the most familiar tradition about Degna Djan, that upon his deathbed he asked Abuna Peter to decide which of his two sons should succeed him. Abuna Peter selected Del Na'od, but upset with
1 CONC  the decision 'Abasa Wedem is said to have bribed an Egyptian monk Mennas to go to Alexandria and convince the Patriarch of Alexandria to remove Abuna Peter so 'Anbasa Wedem could claim the throne. Mennas returned with forged pape
1 CONC rs that made him Abuna, and he consecrated 'Anbasa Wedem as king. Del Na'od's supporters thereupon collected troops and deposed 'Anasa Wedem; upon learning the truth, Patriarch Cosmas excommunicated Mennas -- but Mennas had died b
1 CONC y that time. [2]
1 CONT
1 CONT Taddesse Tamrat repeats traditions that Degna Djan both led military expeditions as far south as Ennarea, and commanded missionary activities in the highlands of Angot and the modern region of Amhara.[3] Because the Gadla of Tekle
1 CONC  Haymanot states that Degna Djan lived 18 generations -- or 400-600 years -- before the saint (c.1215), "this brings Digna-Jan to the first half of the ninth century."[4]
1 CONT
1 CONT Taddesse Tamrat also mentions a tradition that makes him, not his son Dil Na'od, the last king of Axum.[5]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Paul B. Henze, Layers of Time (New York: Palgrave, 2000), p.49. 
1 CONT ^ E. A. Walis Budge, A History of Ethiopia: Nubia and Abyssinia, 1928 (Oosterhout, the Netherlands: Anthropological Publications, 1970), p. 276. Budge identifies the Patriarch as Cosmas II; however, Taddesse Tamrat (Church and Sta
1 CONC te in Ethiopia [Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1972], pp. 40f) identifies him with Cosmas III. 
1 CONT ^ Taddesse Tamrat, Church and State, pp. 35f. 
1 CONT ^ Taddesse Tamrat, Church and State, p. 36. 
1 CONT ^ Taddesse Tamrat, Church and State, p. 66n 
0 @N6000000002188294046@ NOTE Dil Na'od
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Dil Na'od was the last negus of Axum before the Zagwe dynasty of Ethiopia. He lived in either the 9th or 10th centuries. Dil Na'od was the younger son of Ged'a Jan (or Degna Djan), and succeeded his older brother 'Anbasa Wedem as 
1 CONC negus.[1] According to Wallis Budge, "The reign of Delna'ad was short, perhaps about ten years."[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT Dil Na'od is recorded as both campaigning in the Ethiopian highlands south of Axum, and sending missionaries into that region. With Abuna Salama I, he helped to build the church of Debre Igziabher overlooking Lake Hayq.[3]
1 CONT
1 CONT He was defeated by Mara Takla Haymanot, a prince from Lasta province, who married Dil Na'od's daughter, Masaba Warq. According to tradition, a son of Dil Na'od was carried to Amhara, (possibly to present day Ambassel, near Lake Ha
1 CONC yq) where he was harbored, until his descendants overthrew the Zagwe, and reëstablished the Solomonic dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ Taddesse Tamrat, Church and State in Ethiopia (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1972), p. 55 n.3. 
1 CONT ^ E. A. Walis Budge, A History of Ethiopia: Nubia and Abyssinia, 1928 (Oosterhout, the Netherlands: Anthropological Publications, 1970), p. 276. 
1 CONT ^ Paul B. Henze, Layers of Time (New York: Palgrave, 2000), pp. 47f. 
0 @N6000000002188294027@ NOTE Mara Takla Haymanot
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Mara Takla Haymanot was N?gusä nägäst of Ethiopia, and the founder of the Zagwe dynasty. Some King Lists give his name simply as "Mararah", and other King Lists as "Takla Haymanot".
1 CONT
1 CONT Mara was born in the province of Lasta, which was his powerbase. Originally a general of Dil Na'od, whose daughter Masoba Warq became his wife, Mara overthrew his father-in-law to found the new dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT There is some disagreement over the exact time when he came to the throne: there are two different traditions for how long the Zagwe dynasty ruled: the more common tradition states that it was for 333 years, while a less common on
1 CONC e gives the time as 133 years. The Italian scholar Carlo Conti Rossini accepted the shorter period, and working back from the accepted date of 1270 for the end of the Zagwe dynasty, claims that this dynasty started around 1137. He
1 CONC  supported this theory with the recorded exchange between Patriarch John V of Alexandria and an unnamed king of Ethiopia, who asked for a new abuna because the current one was too old; Conti Rossini argued that the actual reason w
1 CONC as that the abuna refused to condone the coup which resulted in Mara Takla Haymanot gaining the throne.1
1 CONT
1 CONT The extent of his kingdom was much smaller than the later Solomonic dynasty came to rule, embracing parts of Lasta, Wag, Tigray, and perhaps northern Begemder.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Taddesse Tamrat. "The Legacy of Aksum and Adafa" in Church and State in Ethiopia. Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1972, pp. 53-64. 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Dil Na'od Emperor of Ethiopia Succeeded by
1 CONT Tatadim 
0 @N6000000002188292991@ NOTE Jan Seyum
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Jan Seyum was negus of Ethiopia, and a member of the Zagwe dynasty. Taddesse Tamrat states that he was a son of Mara Takla Haymanot, a younger brother of king Tatadim, and the father of Yemrehana Krestos.1 His name does not appear
1 CONC  in the longer king lists.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Taddesse Tamrat, Church and State in Ethiopia, (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1972), p. 56n. 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Tatadim Emperor of Ethiopia Succeeded by
1 CONT Germa Seyum 
0 @N6000000002188292980@ NOTE Gebre Mesqel Lalibela
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gebre Mesqel Lalibela (also called simply "Lalibela", which means "the bees recognise his sovereignty" in Old Agaw) was negus or king of Ethiopia, and a member of the Zagwe dynasty; he is also considered a saint by the Ethiopian c
1 CONC hurch. According to Taddesse Tamrat, he was the son of Jan Seyum and brother of Kedus Harbe. Tradition states that he reigned for 40 years.[1] According to Getachew Makonnen Hasen, his reign was from 1189 to 1229.[2] He is best kn
1 CONC own as the king who either built or commissioned the monolithic churches of Lalibela.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Lalibela was born at either Adefa or Roha (it was later named Lalibela after him) in Bugna. He was given the name "Lalibela" due to a swarm of bees said to have surrounded him at his birth, which his mother took as a sign of 
1 CONC his future reign as Emperor of Ethiopia. Tradition states that he went into exile due to the hostility of his uncle Tatadim and his brother king Kedus Harbe, and was almost poisoned to death by his half-sister. Because Lalibela ca
1 CONC me to power during his brother's lifetime, Taddesse Tamrat suspects that he came to power by force of arms.[3]
1 CONT
1 CONT Lalibela is said to have seen Jerusalem in a vision and then attempted to build a new Jerusalem as his capital in response to the capture of old Jerusalem by Muslims in 1187. As such, many features of the town of Lalibela have Bib
1 CONC lical names - even the town's river is known as the River Jordan. It remained the capital of Ethiopia from the late 12th century and into the 13th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT Details about the construction of his 11 monolithic churches at Lalibela have been lost. The later Gadla Lalibela, a hagiography of the king, states that he carved these churches out of stone with only the help of angels.[4]
1 CONT
1 CONT His chief queen was Masqal Kibra, about whom a few traditions have survived. She induced Abuna Mikael to make her brother Hirun bishop, and a few years later the Abuna left Ethiopia for Egypt, complaining that Hirun had usurped hi
1 CONC s authority.[5] Another tradition states that she convinced king Lalibela abdicate in favor of his nephew Na'akueto La'ab, but after 18 months of his nephew's misrule she convinced Lalibela to resume the throne. Taddesse Tamrat su
1 CONC spects that the end of Lalibela's rule was not actually this amiable, and argues that this tradition masks a brief usurpation of Na'akueto La'ab, whose reign was ended by Lalibela's son, Yetbarak.[6] Getachew Mekonnen credits her 
1 CONC with having one of the rock-hewn churches, Bet Aba Libanos, built as a memorial for Lalibela after his death. [7]
1 CONT
1 CONT Unlike the other Zagwe kings, a sizeable amount of written material has survived about his reign, besides the Gadla Lalibela. An embassy from the Patriarch of Alexandria visited his court around 1210, and have left an account of h
1 CONC im, and Na'akueto La'ab and Yetbarak.[8] The Italian scholar Carlo Conti Rossini has edited and published the several land grants that survive from his reign.[9]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ Taddesse Tamrat, Church and State in Ethiopia (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1972), p. 56n. 
1 CONT ^ Getachew Mekonnen Hasen, Wollo, Yager Dibab (Addis Ababa: Nigd Matemiya Bet, 1992), p. 22. 
1 CONT ^ Taddesse Tamrat, p. 61. 
1 CONT ^ The portion of his Gadla describing his construction of these churches has been translated by Richard K. P. Pankhurst in his The Ethiopian Royal Chronicles (Addis Ababa: Oxford University Press), 1967. 
1 CONT ^ Taddesse Tamrat, pp. 59f. 
1 CONT ^ Taddesse Tamrat, pp. 62f. 
1 CONT ^ Getachew Mekonnen, p. 24. 
1 CONT ^ Taddesse Tamrat, p. 62. 
1 CONT ^ A bibliography for these can be found at Taddesse Tamrat, p. 59. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT J. Perruchon. Vie de Lalibala, roi d'éthiopie: texte éthiopien et traduction française. Paris 1892. (Online version in Gallica website at the "Bibliothèque National Française") 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Kedus Harbe Emperor of Ethiopia Succeeded by
1 CONT Na'akueto La'ab 
0 @N6000000002188293000@ NOTE Kedus Harbe
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Kedus Harbe was negus of Ethiopia, and a member of the Zagwe dynasty. According to Taddesse Tamrat, he was the son of Jan Seyum, the brother of Tatadim.[1] Some authorities date his reign to the years 1079 - 1119.
1 CONT
1 CONT Huntingford does not include him in his list of kings of the Zagwe dynasty.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ Taddesse Tamrat, Church and State in Ethiopia (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1972), p. 56n. 
1 CONT ^ G.W.B. Huntingford, Historical Geography of Ethiopia (London: British Academy, 1989), p. 68. 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Yemrehana Krestos Emperor of Ethiopia Succeeded by
1 CONT Gebre Mesqel Lalibela 
0 @N6000000002188294016@ NOTE Yemrehana Krestos
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Yemrehana Krestos (called "Abraham" by Francisco Álvares) was negus of Ethiopia, and a member of the Zagwe dynasty. According to Taddesse Tamrat, he was the son of Germa Seyum, the brother of Tatadim; however the Italian scholar C
1 CONC arlo Conti Rossini published in 1902 a document that stated Yemrehana Krestos was the successor of Na'akueto La'ab, and succeeded by Yetbarak.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT Taddesse Tamrat describes him as the king of Ethiopia closest to a priest, noting that he insisted on ruling Ethiopia according to Apostolic canons.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT Yemrehana Krestos built the first stone-hewn church in Ethiopia, which is named for him and is located 12 miles northeast from Lalibela. Alvarez left a description of what the church looked like in the early 16th century, in his P
1 CONC rester John of the Indies.[3] Taddesse suggests that construction of this church is related to the record of an Ethiopian delegation that came to Caliph Saladin in 1173, and is recorded as presenting a letter and many gifts to the
1 CONC  Caliph; in the Gadla Yemrehana Krestos, there is a passage that relates how he obtained the door from the Caliph's palace to use in his church.[4] Paul B. Henze provides a list of several other rock-hewn churches attributed to th
1 CONC is king.[5]
1 CONT
1 CONT Alvarez also recorded the tradition that Yemrehana Krestos began the tradition of confining rival heirs to the Imperial throne at Amba Geshen.[6]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ Quoted in E.A. Wallis Budge, A History of Ethiopia: Nubia and Abyssinia, 1928 (Oosterhout, the Netherlands: Anthropological Publications, 1970), p. 277. 
1 CONT ^ Taddesse Tamrat, Church and State in Ethiopia (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1972), p. 61n.3. 
1 CONT ^ Francisco Alvarez, The Prester John of the Indies translated by C.F. Beckingham and G.W.B. Huntingford (Cambridge: Hakluyt Society, 1961), chapter 53. 
1 CONT ^ Taddesse Tamrat, p. 58. 
1 CONT ^ Paul B. Henze, Layers of Time, A History of Ethiopia (New York: Palgrave, 2000), p. 51. 
1 CONT ^ Alvarez, chapter 59. 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Germa Seyum Emperor of Ethiopia Succeeded by
1 CONT Kedus Harbe 
