0 @N6000000000945061282@ NOTE Irene utøvet stor innflytelse, da hennes svigermor trakk seg tilbake. I 1118 søkte hun å 
1 CONT bringe sin ærgjerige datter, Anna Komnenas, mann Nikeforos, på tronen i stedet for sin sønn
1 CONT Johannes. Dette strandet imidlertid på motstand fra Alexios, noe som fikk henne til å trekke seg
1 CONT tilbake til klosteret ?Vierge Plaine de Grâce? som hun hadde stiftet.
1 CONT Hennes ekteskap var helt politisk. Hun var mor til tre sønner, Johannes II, Andronikos
1 CONT og Isak, og fire dødre, Anna, Maria, Eudoxia og Theodora.
0 @N6000000002187821095@ NOTE Irene Ducaena
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Irene Ducaena (1066 – February 19, 1133) was the wife of Byzantine emperor Alexius I Comnenus, and the mother of the emperor John II Comnenus and the historian Anna Comnena.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Succession of Alexius and Irene 
1 CONT 2 Character 
1 CONT 3 Death of Alexius 
1 CONT 4 Children 
1 CONT 5 Sources 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Succession of Alexius and Irene
1 CONT Irene was born in 1066 to Andronicus Ducas and Maria of Bulgaria. Andronicus was a nephew of Emperor Constantine X and a cousin of Michael VII. Irene married Alexius in 1078, when she was still eleven years old. For this reason th
1 CONC e Ducas family supported Alexius in 1081 when a struggle for the throne erupted after the abdication of Nicephorus III. Alexius' mother, Anna Dalassena, a lifelong enemy of the Ducas family, pressured her son to divorce the young 
1 CONC Irene and marry Maria Bagrationi, the former wife of both Michael VII and Nicephorus III. Irene was in fact barred from the coronation ceremony, but the Ducas family convinced the Patriarch of Constantinople, Cosmas I, to crown he
1 CONC r as well, which he did one week later. Anna Dalassena consented to this only by forcing Cosmas to resign immediately afterwards; he was succeeded by Eustathius Garidas. Anna continued to live in the imperial palace and meddle in 
1 CONC Alexius' affairs until her death 20 years later; Maria Bagrationi may have also lived in the palace, and there were rumours that Alexius carried on an affair with her. Anna Comnena vociferously denied this, although she herself wa
1 CONC s not born until December 1, 1083, two years later.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Character
1 CONT Anna may have been whitewashing her family history; she has nothing but praise for both of her parents. She describes her mother in great detail:
1 CONT
1 CONT "She stood upright like some young sapling, erect and evergreen, all her limbs and the other parts of her body absolutely symmetrical and in harmony one with another. With her lovely appearance and charming voice she never ceased 
1 CONC to fascinate all who saw and heard her. Her face shone with the soft light of the moon; it was not the completely round face of an Assyrian woman, nor long, like the face of a Scyth, but just slightly oval in shape. There were ros
1 CONC e blossoms on her cheeks, visible a long way off. Her light-blue eyes were both gay and stern: their charm and beauty attracted, but the fear they caused so dazzled the bystander that he could neither look nor turn away...Generall
1 CONC y she accompanied her words with graceful gestures, her hands bare to the wrists, and you would say it was ivory turned by some craftsman into the form of fingers and hand. The pupils of her eyes, with the brilliant blue of deep w
1 CONC aves, recalled a calm, still sea, while the white surrounding them shone by contrast, so that the whole eye acquired a peculiar lustre and a charm which was inexpressible."
1 CONT
1 CONT It "would not have been so very inappropriate," Anna writes, to say that Irene was "Athena made manifest to the human race, or that she had descended suddenly from the sky in some heavenly glory and unapproachable splendour."
1 CONT
1 CONT Irene was shy and preferred not to appear in public, although she was forceful and severe when acting officially as basilissa. She preferred to perform her household duties, and enjoyed reading hagiographic literature and making c
1 CONC haritable donations to monks and beggars. Although Alexius may have had Maria as a mistress early in his reign, during the later part of his reign he and Irene were genuinely in love (at least according to their daughter Anna). Ir
1 CONC ene often accompanied him on his expeditions, including the expedition against Bohemund I of Antioch in 1107 and to the Chersonese in 1112. On these campaigns she acted as a nurse for her husband when he was afflicted with gout in
1 CONC  his feet. According to Anna she also acted as a sort of guard, as there were constant conspiracies against Alexius. When she remained behind in Constantinople, she acted as regent, with Nicephorus Bryennius, Anna's husband, as a 
1 CONC counselor.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Death of Alexius
1 CONT Irene frequently suggested that Alexius name Nicephorus and Anna as his heirs, over their own younger son John. According to Nicetas Choniates, who depicts her more as a nagging shrew than a loving wife, she "...threw her full inf
1 CONC luence on the side of her daughter Anna and lost no opportunity to calumniate their son John...mocking him as rash, pleasure-loving, and weak in character." Alexius, preferring to create a stable dynasty through his own son, eithe
1 CONC r ignored her, pretended to be busy with other matters, or, at last, lost his temper and chastized her for suggesting such things.
1 CONT
1 CONT Irene nursed Alexius on his deathbed on 1118, while at the same time still scheming to have Nicephorus and Anna succeed him. Alexius had already promised the throne to John, and when John took his father's signet ring Irene accuse
1 CONC d him of treachery and theft. When Alexius finally died, she felt genuine grief, and wore the mourning clothes of her daughter Eudocia, whose own husband had died previously. However, she soon conspired with Anna and Nicephorus ag
1 CONC ainst John, but their plots were unsuccessful and both Irene and Anna were then forced into exile at the monastery of Kecharitomene, which Irene had founded a few years previously. It was not a harsh exile, and Irene lived there i
1 CONC n peace, distributing food to the poor and educating young orphan girls.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Children
1 CONT Irene died on February 19, in either 1123 or 1133, most likely the latter. With Alexius she had seven children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Anna Comnena (1083-1153) 
1 CONT Maria Comnena 
1 CONT John II Comnenus (1087-1143) 
1 CONT Andronicus Comnenus 
1 CONT Isaac Comnenus 
1 CONT Eudocia Comnenus 
1 CONT Theodora Comnenus 
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Anna Comnena, The Alexiad, trans. E.R.A. Sewter. Penguin Books, 1969. 
1 CONT Nicetas Choniates, O City of Byzantium: Annals of Niketas Choniates, trans. Harry J. Magoulias. Wayne State University Press, 1984. 
1 CONT Georgina Buckler, Anna Comnena: A Study. Oxford University Press, 1929. 
1 CONT Thalia Goumia-Peterson, "Gender and Power: Passages to the Maternal in Anna Komnene's Alexiad", in Anna Komnene and Her Times, ed. Thalia Goumia-Peterson. Garland Publishing, 2000. 
1 CONT Warren Treadgold, A History of the Byzantine State and Society. Stanford University Press, 1997. 
1 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Eirene Doukaina, basilissa Rhomaiôn
1 CONT b. 1066, d. 19 February 1127, #5631
1 CONT Pedigree 
1 CONT      Described by her daughter, Anna, in her Alexiad thusly: "She was just like some young, ever-blooming plant, all her limbs and features were perfectly symmetrical, each being broad or narrow in due proportion. She was so charm
1 CONC ing to look at as well as listen to that eyes and ears seemed unable to get their fill of seeing and hearing. Her face too shone with the soft glamour of the moon, it was not fashioned in a perfect circle like the faces of the Ass
1 CONC yrian women, nor again was it very long like those of the Scythians, but it was just slightly modified from a perfect round. And the bloom of her cheeks was such that their rosy hue was visible even to those who stool afar off. He
1 CONC r eyes were blue, yet in spite of their gaiety, they were somewhat awe-inspiring, so that though by their gladness and beauty they attracted the eyes of all beholders, yet these felt constrained to close their eyes so that they kn
1 CONC ew neither how to resist looking at her nor how to look."1 Eirene Doukaina, basilissa Rhomaiôn was the little daughter of Andronicus, the eldest son of the Caesar, and of illustrious lineage, for she traced her descent from the fa
1 CONC mous houses of Andronicus and Constantine Ducas.1 She was born in 1066. She was the daughter of Andronikos Doukas and Marija Kometopoulos.2,3 She married Alexios I Komnenos, basileus Rhomaiôn, son of domestikos ton scholon Ioannes
1 CONC  I Komnenos and Anna Dalassena, in 1078; His 2nd.3,2,4 She died on 19 February 1127 at age 61 years. Died a nun.  
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT      Children of Eirene Doukaina, basilissa Rhomaiôn and Alexios I Komnenos, basileus Rhomaiôn: 
1 CONT   Barbara Komnene   b. c 1080 
1 CONT   Anna Komnene+   b. 1 Dec 1083, d. a 1148 
1 CONT   Ioannes II Kaloiannis Komnenos, basileus Rhomaiôn+   b. 13 Sep 1087, d. 8 Apr 1143 
1 CONT   Theodora Komnene+   b. c 1090 
1 CONT   sebastokrator Isaakios Komnenos+   b. a 1 Jan 1093, d. 1175 
1 CONT
1 CONT [S1274] Anna Komnene, The Alexiad, before 1148, Elizabeth A. Dawes, translator (London, Routledge, Kegan, Paul, 1928), Book 3, III. Hereinafter cited as Alexiad. 
1 CONT [S269] C. W. Previté-Orton The Shorter Cambridge Medieval History, Volume 1, the Later Roman Empire to the Twelfth Century, 1 (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1978), pg. 536, genealogy table 15, (a) the House of Ducas and C
1 CONC omnenus.. Hereinafter cited as sCMH I. 
1 CONT [S25] J. M. Hussey, editor, The Cambridge Medieval History, Volume IV The Byzantine Empire, Part 1 Byzantium and Its Neighbours (Bentley House, 200 Euston Road, London: The Syndics of the Cambridge University Press, 1966), pg. 793
1 CONC . Hereinafter cited as Cambridge Medieval History, Vol 4, Part 1. 
1 CONT [S862] Various Encyclopædia Britannica 2001 Standard Edition CD-ROM (U.S.A.: Britannica.com Inc.
1 CONT , 1994-2000), Alexius I Comnenus (Byz. emp.) . Hereinafter cited as EB CD 2001. 
0 @N6000000003051208513@ NOTE After her husband became favored, discord erupted between her and her mother-in-law, Anna, who urged her son to divorce Irene to win over the faction that had gathered around Maria and her little son, consequently, her husband was
1 CONC  crowned alone. His personal sentiments were with Constantine, but to divorce and exclude her required the cooperation of the Patriarch Cosmas, the representative of the Ducas faction, who refused either to collaborate or resign, 
1 CONC so Alexius followed the path of political expediency and had her crowned Empress.
1 CONT In an attempt to gain the throne for Anna, her favorite, she tried to induce her husband to degrade John and name Anna's husband Emperor.
