0 @N6000000002188002318@ NOTE Andronikos II Palaiologos
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos or Andronicus II Palaeologus (Greek: ??d??????? ?' ?a?a???????) (1259/1260 – February 13, 1332), reigned as Byzantine emperor 1282–1328. Andronikos II Palaiologos was the eldest surviving son of Michael V
1 CONC III Palaiologos and Theodora Doukaina Vatatzina, grandniece of John III Doukas Vatatzes.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life
1 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos was acclaimed co-emperor in 1261, after his father Michael VIII recovered Constantinople from the Latin Empire, but he was crowned only in 1272. Sole emperor from 1282, Andronikos II immediately repudiate
1 CONC d his father's unpopular Church union with the Papacy, but was unable to resolve the related schism within the Orthodox clergy until 1310. Andronikos II was also plagued by economic difficulties and during his reign the value of t
1 CONC he Byzantine hyperpyron depreciated precipitously while the state treasury accumulated more than seven times less revenue (in nominal coins) than it had done previously. Seeking to increase revenue and reduce expenses, Andronikos 
1 CONC II raised taxes and reduced tax exemptions, and dismantled the Byzantine fleet in 1285, thereby making the Empire increasingly dependent on the rival republics of Venice and Genoa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos sought to resolve some of the problems facing the Byzantine Empire through diplomacy. After the death of his first wife, he married Yolanda (renamed Eirene) of Montferrat, putting an end to the Montferrat
1 CONC  claim to the Kingdom of Thessalonica. Andronikos II also attempted to marry off his son and co-emperor Michael IX Palaiologos to the Latin Empress Catherine I of Courtenay, thus seeking to eliminate Western agitation for a restor
1 CONC ation of the Latin Empire. Another marriage alliance attempted to resolve the potential conflict with Serbia in Macedonia, as Andronikos II married off his five-year old daughter Simonis to King Stefan Milutin in 1298.
1 CONT
1 CONT In spite of the resolution of problems in Europe, Andronikos II was faced with the collapse of the Byzantine frontier in Asia Minor. After the failure of the co-emperor Michael IX to stem the Turkish advance in Asia Minor in 1300,
1 CONC  the Byzantine government hired the Catalan Company of Almogavars (adventurous from Aragon and Catalonia) led by Roger de Flor to clear Byzantine Asia Minor from the enemy. In spite of some successes, the Catalans were unable to s
1 CONC ecure lasting gains. They quarreled with Michael IX, and eventually turned on their Byzantine employers after the murder of Roger de Flor in 1305, devastating Thrace, Macedonia, and Thessaly on their road to Latin Greece. There th
1 CONC ey conquered the Duchy of Athens and Thebes. The Turks continued to pentrate the Byzantine possessions, and Prousa fell in 1326. By the end of Andronikos II's reign, much of Bithynia was in the hands of the Ottoman Turks of Osman 
1 CONC I and his son and heir Orhan.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Empire's problems were exploited by Theodore Svetoslav of Bulgaria, who defeated Michael IX and conquered much of northeastern Thrace in c. 1305–1307. The conflict ended with yet another dynastic marriage, between Michael IX's
1 CONC  daughter Theodora and the Bulgarian emperor. The dissolute behavior of Michael IX's son Andronikos III Palaiologos led to a rift in the family, and after Michael IX's death in 1320, Andronikos II disowned his grandson, prompting 
1 CONC a civil war that raged, with interruptions, until 1328. The conflict precipitated Bulgarian involvement, and Michael Asen III of Bulgaria attempted to capture Andronikos II under the guise of sending him military support. In 1328 
1 CONC Andronikos III entered Constantinople in triumph and Andronikos II was forced to abdicate. He died as monk in 1332.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family
1 CONT In 1274 Andronikos II married as his first wife Anna a daughter of King Stephen V of Hungary, with whom he had two sons:
1 CONT
1 CONT Michael IX Palaiologos 
1 CONT Constantine Palaiologos, despotes 
1 CONT After Anna died in 1281, in 1284 Andronikos II then married Yolanda (renamed Eirene), a daughter of Marquis William IX of Montferrat, with whom he had:
1 CONT
1 CONT John Palaiologos (c. 1286–1308), despotes 
1 CONT Theodore I, Marquis of Montferrat (1291–1338) 
1 CONT Demetrios Palaiologos (d. after 1343), despotes 
1 CONT Simonis Palaiologina (1294–after 1336), who married King Stefan Milutin of Serbia 
1 CONT Andronikos II also had at least two illegitimate daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Eirene, who married John II Doukas, ruler of Thessaly 
1 CONT Maria, who married Toqta, khan of the Golden Horde 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Michael VIII Byzantine Emperor
1 CONT 1282–1328
1 CONT with Michael IX in 1294/1295–1320
1 CONT with Andronikos III in 1325–1328 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Andronikos III 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Oxford Dictionary of Byzantium, Oxford University Press, 1991. 
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
0 @N6000000002188002347@ NOTE Eudokia Palaiologina
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eudokia Palaiologina or Eudocia Palaeologina (Greek: ??d???a ?a?a???????a) (c. 1265 – 1302) was the third daughter of Byzantine Emperor Michael VIII Palaiologos and his wife, Theodora Doukaina Vatatzina, a grandniece of Emperor Jo
1 CONC hn III Doukas Vatatzes of Nicaea.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1282 Eudokia married in Constantinople John II Megaskomnenos, Emperor of Trebizond with whom she had two sons, Alexios and Michael. In 1298, after her husband's death and ascension of her son Alexios II, she took the younger so
1 CONC n with her and returned to her brother's court at Constantinople.
1 CONT
1 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos received his sister well, promising to send her back whenever she wished to her elder son, whose position — such was the official Byzantine version — he did all in his power to consolidate.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eudokia had become entirely devoted to Trapezuntine interests. She refused a second marriage with King Stefan Uroš II Milutin of Serbia, as she wished to keep her troth to her first consort, and secretly opposed her brother’s plan
1 CONC  of marrying Alexios II to a daughter of the high court official Nikephoros Choumnos. She pretended, however, to approve this match, so that, under pretence of making arrangements for her son’s wedding, she might return to Trebizo
1 CONC nd.
1 CONT
1 CONT Meanwhile, Alexios II decided for himself to marry an Iberian princess. His uncle Andronikos II wanted this marriage annulled, so Eudokia, on the pretext of inducing her son to dissolve the marriage, succeeded in returning to Treb
1 CONC izond in 1301, where she advised her son to keep his Iberian wife. Eudokia died in the following year, and was perhaps buried in the church of Saint Gregory.
