0 @N6000000002187821377@ NOTE Ivan Vladislav of Bulgaria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ivan Vladislav was the ruler of Bulgaria from August or September 1015 to August or September 1018. He was the son of Aron, the brother of Samuil. His reign oversaw the annexation of Bulgaria by the Byzantine Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Samuil ordered Aron and his family to be killed on suspicion of treason, Ivan ended up surviving only due to the insistance of his cousin Gavril Radomir. The Byzantines, who were having trouble dealing with Gavril Radomir upo
1 CONC n his rise to power, persuaded Ivan to kill the Bulgarian ruler, promising him the throne. He murdered Radomir near Ostrovo, and took his place as ruler of Bulgaria. However, despite swearing an oath of loyalty to Byzantine Empero
1 CONC r Basil II, the conflict with the Greeks continued. The Byzantines devastated Bulgaria, and finally took the capital Ohrid in 1018. Ivan himself was killed during the siege of Duras that same year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ivan had a son, Alusian, that would play an influential role in Bulgaria's struggle for independence several decades later.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT History of Bulgaria 
1 CONT Comitopuli dynasty 
1 CONT Bitola inscription 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Gavril Radomir List of Bulgarian monarchs Succeeded by:
1 CONT None 
