0 @N6000000003949871632@ NOTE  "The Empire of Manuel I Komnenos, 1143-1180" Cambridge U. Press.1993 ISBN0-521-3071-3 {hardback} by Paul Magdolino some  of Manuels distractions as it were are brought to light.
1 CONT He did overreach himself drastically where Italy was concerned and he could have curbed his disputes with the Hungarians some by focusing on them exclusively and ignoring the Normans. The best way for manuel to have dealt with Ita
1 CONC ly was to try and keep Frederick I Barbarossa and the Norman kings of Sicily locked on each other. That way both the Norman threat from south Italy and the possible German threat against the Empire which Magdolino discusses could 
1 CONC have been contained. Manuel could have kept them divided for years with the added bonus of conserving all the money he wasted 1) trying to conquer Norman Sicily and south Italy 2) Trying to get himself recognized as Emperor in the
1 CONC  west over Frederick I ie sole Roman Emperor in the world.
1 CONT Manuel succeeded in forging many marriage alliances and did manage to put 'his man' in power in Hungary, ally with the 'right' Pope (Alexander III), and achieve considerable influence both over the Crusader States and Turkish Ikon
1 CONC ia, albeit for a relatively short period of time. 
1 CONT Manuel overextended an empire that had not fully recovered from Manzikirt and which was being economically bled to death by Venice. Furthermore, he did not extend the empire where it truly needed to be re-established, i.e. in the 
1 CONC east by winning back Anatolia
1 CONT before it became turkified. Rather he "extended the weakness" of the Byzantine Empire by trying a fruitless march into Italy whcih could not add any strength to the empire, but instead, ensured that he created a host of enemies wh
1 CONC ich would come back to ruin the empire when he died.
1 CONT Nevertheless, he is perhaps the most personally admirable of the Comneni and is an appropriate member of the trinity of the great Comneni emperors that also included Alexius I and John II. Alas, he lacked the caution that made the
1 CONC se emperors more effective. He finally took on the turkish problem but suffered a horrible defeat at Myriochephalon where much of his army was lost (although much of the Suljuk army died as well). Perhaps given an earlier offensiv
1 CONC e against the Suljuks with a larger, better supplied army (his suffered a bout of dysentry that enfebbled it just before the battle) he might have blotted out the Seljuks and gone a long way to truly restoring the empire. 
