0 @N6000000002187820577@ NOTE Andronikos I Komnenos
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Andronikos I Komnenos or Andronicus I Comnenus (Greek: ??d??????? ?’ ??µ?????, Andronikos I Komnenos) (c. 1118 – September 12, 1185) was a Byzantine emperor (r. 1183-1185), son of prince Isaac Komnenos. His paternal grandparents w
1 CONC ere Emperor Alexios I Komnenos and Eirene Doukaina.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Biography 
1 CONT 1.1 Early years 
1 CONT 1.2 Exile 
1 CONT 1.3 Emperor 
1 CONT 1.4 Death 
1 CONT 2 Family 
1 CONT 3 Portrayal in fiction 
1 CONT 4 Notes 
1 CONT 5 References 
1 CONT 6 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Biography
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early years
1 CONT Andronikos Komnenos was born early in the twelfth century, around 1118. He was 'endowed by nature with the most remarkable gifts both of mind and body: he was handsome and eloquent, but licentious; and, at the same time, active, h
1 CONC ardy, courageous, a great general and an able politician'.
1 CONT
1 CONT Andronikos' early years were spent in alternate pleasure and military service. In 1141 he was taken captive by the Seljuk Turks and remained in their hands for a year. On being ransomed he went to Constantinople, where was held th
1 CONC e court of his cousin, the Emperor Manuel I Komnenos, with whom he was a great favourite. Here the charms of his niece, the princess Theodora, attracted him and she became his mistress.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1152, accompanied by Eudoxia, he set out for an important command in Cilicia. Failing in his principal enterprise, an attack upon Mopsuestia, he returned, but was again appointed to the command of a province. This second post h
1 CONC e seems also to have left after a short interval, for he appeared again in Constantinople, and narrowly escaped death at the hands of the brothers of Eudoxia.
1 CONT
1 CONT About this time (1153) a conspiracy against the emperor, in which Andronikos participated, was discovered and he was thrown into prison. There he remained for about twelve years, during which time he made repeated but unsuccessful
1 CONC  attempts to escape.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Exile
1 CONT At last, in 1165, he was successful in escaping. After passing through many dangers, he reached the court of Prince Yaroslav of Galicia (Ruthenia). While under the protection of the prince, Andronikos brought about an alliance bet
1 CONC ween him and the emperor Manuel I, and so restored himself to the emperor's favour. With a Russian army he joined Manuel in the invasion of Hungary and assisted at the siege of Semlin.
1 CONT
1 CONT After a successful campaign Manuel I and Andronikos returned together to Constantinople (1168); but a year later, Andronikos refused to take the oath of allegiance to the future king Béla III of Hungary, whom Manuel desired to bec
1 CONC ome his successor. He was removed from court, but received the province of Cilicia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Being still under the displeasure of the emperor, Andronikos fled to the court of Raymond, prince of Antioch. While residing here he captivated and seduced the beautiful daughter of the prince, Philippa, sister of the empress Mari
1 CONC a. The anger of the emperor was again roused by this dishonour, and Andronikos was compelled to flee.
1 CONT
1 CONT He took refuge with King Amalric I of Jerusalem, whose favour he gained, and who invested him with the Lordship of Beirut. In Jerusalem he saw Theodora Komnene, the beautiful widow of the late King Baldwin III and niece of the emp
1 CONC eror Manuel. Although Andronikos was at that time fifty-six years old, age had not diminished his charms, and Theodora became the next victim of his artful seduction.
1 CONT
1 CONT To avoid the vengeance of the Emperor, she fled with Andronikos to the court of Nur al-Din, the Sultan of Damascus; but not deeming themselves safe there, they continued their perilous journey through Persia and Turkestan, round t
1 CONC he Caspian Sea and across the Caucasus, until at length they settled in the ancestral lands of the Komnenoi at Oinaion, on the shores of the Black Sea, between Trebizond and Sinope.
1 CONT
1 CONT While Andronikos was on one of his incursions, his castle was surprised by the governor of Trebizond, and Theodora and her two children were captured and sent to Constantinople. To obtain their release Andronikos in early 1180 mad
1 CONC e abject submission to the Emperor and, appearing in chains before him, besought pardon. This he obtained, and was allowed to retire with Theodora into banishment at Oinaion.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Emperor
1 CONT In 1180 the Emperor Manuel died and was succeeded by his 10 year old son Alexios II Komnenos, who was under the guardianship of his mother and acting Regent Empress Maria. Her Latin origins and culture however led to creeping rese
1 CONC ntment from her Greek subjects (who felt insulted enough by the late Manuel's Western tastes, let alone being ruled by his Western wife), building up to an explosion of rioting that almost became a full civil war. This gave Andron
1 CONC ikos the opportunity to seize the crown for himself, leaving his retirement in 1182 and marching to Constantinople with an army that (according to non-Byzantine sources) included Muslim contingents.[1] His arrival was soon followe
1 CONC d by a massacre of the Latin inhabitants of the city, which was focused on the Venetian merchants who virtually controlled the economy of the city. The massacre resulted in the deaths of 80,000 "Latins". He was believed to have ar
1 CONC ranged the poisoning of Alexios II's elder sister Maria the Porphyrogenita and her husband Renier of Montferrat, although Maria herself had encouraged him to intervene. The poisoner was said to be the eunuch Pterygeonites. Soon af
1 CONC terwards he had the empress Maria imprisoned and then killed (forcing a signature from the child Emperor Alexius to put his mother to death), by Pterygeonites and the hetaireiarches Constantine Tripsychos. Alexios II was compelled
1 CONC  to acknowledge Andronikos as colleague in the empire and was then quickly put to death in turn; the killing was carried out by Tripsychos, Theodore Dadibrenos and Stephen Hagiochristophorites.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT Andronikos, now (1183) sole emperor, married Agnes of France, a child twelve years of age, formerly betrothed to Alexios II. Agnes was a daughter of King Louis VII of France and his third wife Adèle of Champagne. By November 1183,
1 CONC  Andronikos associated his younger legitimate son John Komnenos on the throne. A Venetian embassy visited Constantinople in 1184 and an agreement was reached that compensation of 1500 gold pieces would be paid for the losses incur
1 CONC red in 1171.
1 CONT
1 CONT His short reign was characterized by strong and harsh measures. He resolved to suppress many abuses, but above all things, to check feudalism and limit the power of the nobles, who were rivals for his throne. The people, who felt 
1 CONC the severity of his laws, at the same time acknowledged their justice and found themselves protected from the rapacity of their superiors who had grown corrupt under the opulent and mercurial rule of Manuel I. However, as Andronik
1 CONC os' rule went on, the Emperor became increasingly paranoid and violent - in September 1185, Andronikos ordered the execution of all prisoners, exiles and their families for collusion with the invaders - and the Byzantine Empire de
1 CONC scended into a terror state. The aristocrats in turn were infuriated against him. There were several revolts, the stories of chaos leading to an invasion by King William of the Norman Sicilians. William (with a fleet of 200 ships)
1 CONC  landed in Epirus with a strong force (80,000 men including 5,000 knights), and marched as far as Thessalonica, which he took and pillaged ruthlessly (7,000 Greeks died). Andronikos hastily assembled five different armies to stop 
1 CONC the Sicilian army from reaching Constantinople, but none of these five smaller armies would stand against the Sicilian forces and retreated to the outlying hills. Andronikos also assembled a fleet of 100 ships to stop the Norman f
1 CONC leet from entering the Sea of Marmara. The invaders were finally driven out in 1186 by his successor, Isaac Angelos.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death
1 CONT
1 CONT A medieval depiction of the death of Andronikos. Original in the Bibliothèque Nationale, France.Andronikos seems then to have resolved to exterminate the aristocracy, and his plans were nearly crowned with success. But on Septembe
1 CONC r 11, 1185, during his absence from the capital, Stephen Hagiochristophorites moved to arrest Isaac Angelos, whose loyalty was suspect. Isaac killed Hagiochristophorites and took refuge in the church of Hagia Sophia. He appealed t
1 CONC o the populace, and a tumult arose which spread rapidly over the whole city.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Andronikos arrived he found that his authority was overthrown: Isaac had been proclaimed Emperor. The deposed Emperor attempted to escape in a boat with his wife Agnes and his mistress, but was captured (note that by some, An
1 CONC dronikos not only survived, but also managed to escape to the then self-proclaimed Kingdom of Cyprus). Isaac handed him over to the city mob and for three days he was exposed to their fury and resentment, remaining for that period
1 CONC  tight to a post and beaten up. His right hand was cut off, his teeth and hair were pulled out, one of his eyes was gouged out, and, among many other sufferings, boiling water was thrown in his face, punishment probably associated
1 CONC  to his handsomness and life of licentiosity. At last, led to the Hippodrome of Constantinople, he was hung up by the feet between two pillars, and two Latin soldiers competed as to whose sword would penetrate his body more deeply
1 CONC , and finally his body, according to the representation of his death, was torn apart. He died on September 12, 1185. At the news of the emperor's death, his son and co-emperor John was murdered by his own troops in Thrace.
1 CONT
1 CONT Andronikos I was the last of the Komnenoi to rule Constantinople, although his grandsons Alexios and David founded the Empire of Trebizond in 1204. Their branch of the dynasty was known as the "Great Komnenoi" (Megaskomnenoi).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT Andronikos I Komnenos was married twice and had numerous mistresses. By his first wife, whose name is not known, he had three children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Manuel Komnenos (born 1145), who married Rusudan of Georgia and was the father of Emperor Alexios I and David Komnenos 
1 CONT John Komnenos (apparently born 1159 or 1160), who was co-emperor with his father from 1183 to 1185 and was killed in that year 
1 CONT Maria Komnene 
1 CONT By his mistress Theodora Komnene, Andronikos I had the following issue:
1 CONT
1 CONT Alexios Komnenos, an alleged forefather of the Georgian noble family of Andronikashvili.[3] 
1 CONT Eirene Komnene, who was briefly married to Alexios Komnenos, a son of Emperor Manuel I Komnenos by Theodora Batatzina; also married to Isaac II Angelos and gave birth to Alexios IV Angelos and Irene Angelina, wife of Roger III of 
1 CONC Sicily, then wife of Philip of Swabia. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Portrayal in fiction
1 CONT Andronikos is said to be a character in Michael Arnold's Against the Fall of Night (Garden City, NY: Doubleday, 1975). He is among the main characters of the historical novel Agnes of France (1980) by Greek writer Kostas Kyriazis 
1 CONC (b. 1920). The novel describes the events of the reigns of Manuel I, Alexios II and Andronikos I through the eyes of Agnes. The novel ends with the death of Andronikos.
1 CONT
1 CONT Andronikos was also portrayed in the novel Baudolino by Umberto Eco, with much detail being given to his grisly end.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Ibn Jubayr p. 355 Broadhurst (Turks and Arabs); William of Tyre, Historia Transmarina 22.11 (innumeras Barbararum nationum secum trahens copias); Walter Map, De Nugis Curialium 2.18 (Turks). 
1 CONT ^ Niketas Choniates, Histories pp. 260-274 van Dieten. 
1 CONT ^ Kelsey Jackson Williams (2006), A Genealogy of the Grand Komnenoi of Trebizond. Foundations - the Journal of the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy – Vol. 2, No. 3. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT The Oxford Dictionary of Byzantium, Oxford University Press, 1991. 
1 CONT K. Varzos, E genealogia ton Komnenon (Thessalonica, 1984) vol. 1 pp. 493-638. 
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition article "Andronicus I. (Comnenus)", a publication now in the public domain. 
1 CONT
