0 @N6000000002188002426@ NOTE Abaqa Khan
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Abaqa Khan (1234–1282), the son of Hulagu and Oroqina Khatun, a Mongol Christian. As the second Il-Khan emperor in Persia, he reigned from 1265–1282.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his reign, Abaqa, a devout Buddhist, attempted to convert the Muslims and harassing them mercilessly by promoting Nestorian and Buddhist interests ahead of the Muslims, by sending embassies to Pope Gregory X and Edward I of
1 CONC  England. During his harsh reign, many Muslims had attempted to assassinate Abaqa. In 1265, upon his succession, he received the hand of Maria Despina Palaiologina, the illegitimate daughter of Emperor Michael VIII Palaeologus, in
1 CONC  marriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the time of Hulagu's death from illness in 1265, Abaqa, on his father's behalf, continued to fight the Golden Horde forces of Berke Khan until 1267, the time of Berke's death. However, Barrak Khan tried to annex Iran in 1270 an
1 CONC d thus starting war against Abaqa in the city of Herat. Abaqa successfully defended Iran, leaving his people victorious. Abaqa was succeeded by his Muslim brother Tekuder.
1 CONT
1 CONT Abaqa was later assassinated. The assassin viewed Maria as part of his rightful inheritance, but she fled back to Constantinople where her father, apparently wishing to spare his capital the fate that befell Baghdad, tried to marr
1 CONC y her off again to another Mongol khan. Maria could not accept the offer, became a nun and founded The Church of Panagia Mouchliotissa around 1285.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT Khan genealogy 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Hulagu Ilkhanid Dynasty
1 CONT 1265–1282 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Tekuder 
0 @N6000000002187576487@ NOTE Stefan Dragutin of Serbia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT  Stefan Dragutin, king of Serbia (1276-1282), king of Srem (1282-1316), fresco, detail, 1296 Kingdom of SremStefan Dragutin (died March 12, 1316) was King of Serbia from 1276 to 1282 and king of Srem from 1282 to 1316.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Life 
1 CONT 2 Family 
1 CONT 3 See also 
1 CONT 4 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life
1 CONT Dragutin was the eldest son of king Stefan Uroš I by Helena. He married Katarina, daughter of Hungarian king Stephen V. He was the leading Hungarophile in Serbian politics, and because of this he came in conflict with his father, 
1 CONC from whom he then usurped the throne in 1276 with help of the Hungarians. He began to attack the Byzantine Empire but had no success.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1282 he broke his leg while hunting and became ill; he hence gave the throne to his younger brother Stefan Milutin (the council at Deževo 1282) while keeping for himself some northern parts of the country. Since his son Vladisl
1 CONC av married the cousin of Hungarian king Andrija II, Dragutin in 1284 gained from Hungarian king areas of Macva with Belgrade, and the Bosnian areas Soli and Usora, which he ruled until 1316, establishing family relations with Bosn
1 CONC ian ban Stefan I Kotromanic. His new state was named Kingdom of Srem. First capital of his state was Debrc (between Belgrade and Šabac), and latter he moved his residence to Belgrade. He is the first of the Serb rulers who ruled f
1 CONC rom the capital of Belgrade.
1 CONT
1 CONT In that time the name Srem was designation for two territories: Upper Srem (present day Srem) and Lower Srem (present day Macva). Kingdom of Srem under the rule of Stefan Dragutin was actually Lower Srem. Some historical sources m
1 CONC ention that Stefan Dragutin also ruled over Upper Srem and Slavonija, but the other sources mention another local ruler who ruled over Upper Srem. The name of this ruler was Ugrin Cak.
1 CONT
1 CONT Near the end of the 13th century Stefan Dragutin expanded his territory, adding Branicevo and Kucevo. He came into conflict with his brother Milutin when Milutin wanted to create stronger political ties with the Byzantine Empire a
1 CONC nd obviously did not intend to give the throne to Dragutin's son Vladislav as agreed. Conflict between the brothers was calmed by the clergy in 1313. Near the end of his life he separated from his Hungarian friends and strengthene
1 CONC d his connections in Serbia. He later became a monk and changed his name to Teoktist. He died in 1316 and was buried in the Ðurdevi Stupovi monastery near Novi Pazar. After Dragutin died, new ruler of the Kingdom of Srem became hi
1 CONC s son Stefan Vladislav II.
1 CONT
1 CONT His legacy includes some splendid monasteries, most notable among them: monastery Mala Remeta on Fruška Gora, dedicated to the Shroud of the Mother of God; St. Achilleos church near Arilje (1296); and a monastery Ðurdevi stupovi w
1 CONC ith St. George church in Ras (now Novi Pazar), where he was buried.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family
1 CONT His marriage in about 1268 with Katarina, daughter of Stephen V of Hungary produced at least four children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Jelisaveta or Elisaveta (??????????), married in 1284 to the Bosnian ban Stefan I Kotromanic. 
1 CONT Ursula, married before 1300 to Pavle Šubic (????? ?????). 
1 CONT Stefan Vladislav II, king of Srem (1316-1325). 
1 CONT Urošic (??????), died as a monk. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT History of Serbia 
1 CONT History of Vojvodina 
1 CONT List of Serbian monarchs 
1 CONT Rulers of Vojvodina 
1 CONT Srem 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Stefan Uroš I King of Serbia
1 CONT 1276–1282 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Stefan Uroš II Milutin 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT John V.A. Fine, Jr., The Late Medieval Balkans, Ann Arbor, 1987. 
1 CONT Miomir Filipovic - Fica, Tri cara i trideset i jedan kralj srpskog naroda, Cikago, 1992. 
1 CONT Drago Njegovan, Prisajedinjenje Vojvodine Srbiji, Novi Sad, 2004. 
1 CONT Small encyclopedia "Sveznanje" published by "Narodno delo", Belgrade, in 1937 which is today in public domain. This article is written from the point of view of that place and time and may not reflect modern opinions or recent dis
1 CONC coveries. 
1 CONT Age, marriage and progeny information from The geneaology and coats of arms of Serbian dynasties and feudals (?????????? ??????? ? ?????? ??????? ????????? ? ????????); editors Aleksa Ivic (1928), Dušan Spasic, Aleksandar Palavest
1 CONC ra and Dušan Mrdenovic (1987); Bata, Belgrade, ISBN 86-7685-007-0 (1928), ISBN 86-7335-050-6 (1987) (in Serbian language). 
0 @N6000000002187576528@ NOTE Stefan Uroš II Milutin of Serbia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Stefan Uroš II Milutin (Serbian: ?????? ???? II ???????), (born around 1253-died in 1321), was a king of Serbia (reigned 1282–1321), and member of Nemanjic dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the youngest son of King Stefan Uroš I and his wife, Queen Helena. Unexpectedly he became king of Serbia after abdication of his brother Stefan Dragutin. He was around 29. Immediately upon his rise to throne he attacked Byz
1 CONC antine lands in Macedonia. In 1282 he conquered northern parts of Macedonia with the city of Skoplje, wnich became his capital. Byzantine emperor Michael VIII Palaiologos began preparations for the war but he died before their com
1 CONC pletion. Next year Milutin advanced with his brother deep in Byzantine territory all the way to Kavala. In 1294 Milutin also gain control in northern Albania and city of Dyrrachion, today Durrës. For the next 15 years there were n
1 CONC o changes in the war. The peace was concluded in 1299 when Milutin kept conquered lands as dowry of Simonis, daughter of emperor Andronikos II Palaiologos.
1 CONT
1 CONT Around 1287 or 1288 Milutin helped his brother Stefan Dragutin to pacify two Bulgarian nobles in today's north-east Serbia, in Branicevo region. But those nobles were vassals of Bulgarian prince of Vidin Shishman who sought reveng
1 CONC e. Shishman attacked Milutin but was defeated and Milutin in return sacked his capital Vidin. But Shishman was vassal of Nogai Khan, Khan of Golden Horde. Nogai Khan threaten to punish Milutin for his insolence, but changed his mi
1 CONC nd when Serbian king sent him gifts and hostages. Among the hostages was his son Stefan Decanski who managed to escape back to Serbia after Nogai Khan's death in 1299.
1 CONT
1 CONT Disputes began between Milutin and Dragutin after peace treaty with Byzantine empire was signed in 1299. Dragutin in the meantime held lands from Branicevo in the east to Bosna river in the West. His capital was Belgrade, for the 
1 CONC first time in Serbian history. The war broke between the brothers and lasted, with sporadic cease-fire, until Dragutin's death in 1314. During this war Milutin appointed Stefan Decanski as regent in Zeta, modern Montenegro. This m
1 CONC eant that Stefan Decanski was to be heir to the throne in Serbia and not Dragutin's son Stefan Vladislav II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon Stefan Dragutin's death in 1314 Milutin conquered most of his lands including Belgrade. But in the 1319 Charles I of Hungary regained control over Belgrade and banovina Macva while Milutin held control in Branicevo. In the ye
1 CONC ar of 1314 Milutin's son Stefan Decanski rebelled against his father, but was captured, blinded and sent to exile in Constantinople. For the rest of Milutin's reign his youngest son Stefan Constantine was considered a heir to the 
1 CONC throne. But in spring 1321 Stefan Decanski returned to Serbia and was pardoned by his father.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the end of Milutin's life many things changed in Serbia. Serbia was one of the strongest countries in the south-eastern Europe except for Hungary. During his reign many court ceremonials were taken over from Byzantine court and
1 CONC  Byzantine culture overflow Serbia. Milutin was also well known as founder of many monasteries: Gracanica monastery, Our Lady of Ljeviš, Church of Vavedenje in Hilandar and many more. After his death a short civil war followed, af
1 CONC ter wnich Serbian throne was succeeded by his eldest son, Stefan Decanski.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family
1 CONT By his first wife, Helena, the daughter of John I Doukas of Thessaly, Stefan Uroš II Milutin had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Stefan Uroš III Decanski, who succeeded as king 
1 CONT Anna (Neda), who married Michael Asen III of Bulgaria 
1 CONT By his second wife, Elizabeth, the daughter of King Stephen V of Hungary or by his third wife Anna, the daughter of George Terter I of Bulgaria, Milutin had:
1 CONT
1 CONT Stefan Constantine, rival king of Serbia from 1321 to 1322 
1 CONT By his fourth wife Simonis, the daughter of Emperor Andronikos II Palaiologos, he had no children.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Stefan Dragutin King of Serbia
1 CONT 1282–1321 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Stefan Uroš III Decanski 
0 @N6000000000945049159@ NOTE Hertug av Jylland (Slesvig) 1218 - 1232. 
1 CONT Samkonge av Danmark 1232 - 1241.
1 CONT Enekonge av Danmark 1241 - 1250.
1 CONT Erik var eldste sønn i Valdemar II's ekteskap med Berengaria, og ble straks etter
1 CONT fødselen utnevnt til Hertug av Jylland. Han avløste i 1226 sin halvbror Valdemar som fange i
1 CONT Schwerin og ble utløst i 1230. Etter brorens død krontes han i Lund til konge sammen med sin
1 CONT far av erkebisp Uffe Pinsedag den 30.05.1232. Ved Valdemars død ble han enekonge, og
1 CONT det ble på et riksmøte i et aktstykke fastsatt regler for landets styre.
1 CONT I hele sin regjeringstid lå Erik i strid med sin yngre bror Abel, hertugen av Slesvig, og
1 CONT med geistligheten. Krigslykken var vekslende, men han fikk til slutt overmakten.
1 CONT Sitt tilnavn fikk han for en plogskatt han utferdet i 1249.
1 CONT I 1250 hjalp han sin bror Abel mot angrep fra tysk hold, men han ble selv tatt til fange
1 CONT av Abel og så halshugget av hans menn på Slien i Slesvig. Hans lemlestede legeme ble kastet
1 CONT i sjøen, men ble berget av fiskere. I 1258 ble det ført til Ringsted kirke av hans bror Christoffer
1 CONT I.
1 CONT Hans tragiske død førte til at han ble dyrket som helgen selv om han aldri ble
1 CONT kanonisert.
0 @N6000000004533643029@ NOTE <b>Erik Plovpenning,</b> 1216-50, Konge,  var Valdemar Sejers og ogBeengjærds førstefødte Søn, der strax efter Fødselen (1216) udnævntestil Hertug i Jylland. Han afløste 1226 sin Halvbroder Valdemar somFange i Schwerin og blev udl
1 CONC øst 1230. Efter Broderens Død kronedes hani Lund til Konge af Ærkebisp Uffe Pinsedag (30. Maj) 1232. E. skalhave studeret i Paris og her have gjort Bekjendtskab med den senerePave Innocents IV. Da Pave Gregor IX rejste et Parti i 
1 CONC Tyskland modKejser Frederik II og søgte at faa ham afsat, tilbød man E. Tronen(1239), hvad han dog afslog. E. synes at have været virksom vedFaderens
1 CONT Side i Styrelsen af Landet. Han ægtede 9. Okt. 1239 Judith ellerJutta, en Datter af Hertug Albrecht I af Sachsen. Medens E. i sineyngre Aar siges at have været letsindig og, skjønt legemlig ikkestærk, hengiven til denne Verdens Gl
1 CONC æder, blev han senere alvorlig oggudfrygtig. Et af de første Breve, som han udstedte efter sinTronbestigelse -- ved Faderens
1 CONT Død 28. Marts 1241 --, indeholder en Erklæring om, at han vil dø iFranciskanernes Dragt og begraves i deres Kloster i Roskilde. VedSiden af sit smukke, vindende Væsen og sin Virkelyst besad E.imidlertid megen Stridbarhed, og under
1 CONC  hans Regering var Danmark i envarig Tilstand af Ufred. Broderen Abel var Hertug i Sønderjylland, menbetragtede dette Land mere som et Arvegods end et kongeligt Len; vedsit Ægteskab med den holstenske
1 CONT Prinsesse Mechtild, ved det Værgemaal over sine umyndige Svogre, somhan en Tid paatog sig, blev hans Stilling farlig, og E., som haabedeat gjenvinde sin Faders Magt over Holsten, var idelig beredt til Kamp.Krigen brød ud 1242 og v
1 CONC arede i 2 Aar, da en Stilstand bragtes tilVeje; men 1246 fornyedes Kampen, og Abel, der havde draget sin BroderChristoffer over paa sit Parti, og som fik Hjælp i Tyskland, hærgede iJylland og paa Fyn, brændte Odense og
1 CONT erobrede Ribe. Denne By gj en vandt dog E., og hans tyskeForbundsfæller faldt ind i Holsten og erobrede Oldeslo. E. besatte nualle sine Brødres Borge og Godser og tog Christoffer og HalvbroderenHertug Knud til Prange. Bevæget af d
1 CONC enne sørgelige Splid var alleredei Efteraaret 1247 E.s Søster Markgrevinde Sophie af Brandenborg dragether op for at mægle mellem Brødrene; hun døde paa Rejsen i Barselsenguden at se Freden bragt til Veje.
1 CONT
1 CONT E.s Stilling var i disse Aaringer saa meget mere truet, som hansamtidig laa i Strid med Biskop
1 CONT Niels Stigsen af Roskilde bl. a. om Besiddelsen af Kjøbenhavn, hvadder bragte denne til at forlade Landet 1245. Forholdet tilGejstligheden, som klagede over Overgreb fra Kronens Side, var i flereHenseender spændt, og Striden med B
1 CONC ispen betegnede tillige etbegyndende Brud med den mægtige Hvide-Slægt, til hvilken han hørte.Endelig maatte Kongen se
1 CONT Lybekkerne slutte sig til hans Fjender og plyndre Landets Kyster; deindtoge og brændte Kjøbenhavn. I Aaret 1249 indtraadte dog enStandsning i Krigen, og Kongen søgte at iværksætte det Estlandstog,som han længe havde forberedt. All
1 CONC erede ved Gjenoprettelsen
1 CONT af Bispedømmet i Reval i Kong Valdemars sidste Aar havde E. væretvirksom, og en Bekæmpelse af Hedningerne inden for dette Land og vedGrænserne laa ham stedse paa Sinde. E. lod i den Anledning udskrive enSkat af hver Plov, hvad der
1 CONC  foranledigede en Opstand af de SkaanskeBønder; fordreven fra Lund og Helsingborg maatte E. drage over tilSjælland, men vendte faa Dage efter tilbage, og nu opnaaedes Fred ogForlig (1249). Skatten har givet Anledning til Kongens T
1 CONC ilnavnPlovpenning. Toget fandt derpaa Sted, saaledes siger i alt FaldHuitfeldt, men til nogen Kamp mod Hedningerne er det næppe kommet.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1250 faldt E. pludselig ind i Sønderjylland og erobrede Slesvig; velvar han ikke heldig i en Kamp mod Friserne, men Abel maatte underkastesig. 20 Adelsmænd besvore paa Hertugens
1 CONT Vegne den sluttede Fred og lovede at forlade Hertugen, om han ikke varKongen tro. Under en Ordstrid med E. viste dog Abel klart, at hans Nagvar lige stærkt, og at han mindedes, hvorledes hans Datter paa Grundaf E.s Overfald «havde
1 CONC  barfodet maatte søge Skjul
1 CONT blandt fattige Kvinder». Han lod E. tage til Fange ved sinKammermester Tyge Bost; mulig har Abel ikke givet Befaling til atdræbe Broderen, men hans Mænd vidste at efterkomme hans Ønsker. Medlænket Haand og Fod førtes E. i en Baad 
1 CONC ned ad Slien; da nærmede enanden Baad sig, i hvilken Kongens arge Fjende Lage Gudmundsen sad. E.indsaa, at Døden forestod, og bad om at maatte skrifte for en Præst.De tilstode ham det og lode en Præst hente, for hvem Kongen ydmygs
1 CONC kriftede; derpaa afhuggede de hans Hoved med et Sværd. Det var omNatten til 10. Avg. 1250. Liget sænkedes i Fjorden, men nogle Fiskerefandt det den næste Dag og førte det til Slesvig, hvor Sortebrødrenejordede det i deres Kirke; s
1 CONC enere flyttedes Liget til St. HansNonnekloster i Slesvig og fandt omsider Plads i Ringsted Kirke. E.sMordere kom snart efter paa forskjellig Maade ynkelig af Dage, og daRygter opkom om Mirakler, der skete ved Graven, begyndte Folk
1 CONC et atanse den myrdede for Helgen. E. blev dog aldrig optaget mellem KirkensHelgene, men han æredes af Folket som saadan, og forskjellige Gildervalgte ham til Patron.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dronning Jutta fødte E. 2 Sønner, der døde unge, og 4 Døtre, Sophie,gift med Kong Valdemar af Sverige, Ingeborg, gift med Kong MagnusLagabøter af Norge, Jutta og Agnes, der gik i Kloster, men senereforlode det. Dronningen ægtede s
1 CONC enere Burchard VIII,
1 CONT Borggreve af Rosenburg ved Harzen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Suhm, Hist. af Danmark X.
1 CONT Hist Tidsskr. 6. R. II, 359 ff.
1 CONT
1 CONT <i>Johannes C. H. R. Steenstrup.</i>
0 @N6000000007255388477@ NOTE [Londal.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Erik Plogpenning Valdemarsson. Født 1216. Død 10.08.1250. Han var
1 CONT           medkonge i Danmark - sammen med sin far - fra 1232, ble kronet 30.05.1232, og
1 CONT           var enekonge i årene 1241-1250.
1 CONT           Allerede mens han lå i vuggen fikk Erik Plogpenning tittel som hertug av Jylland. Den
1 CONT           09.10.1239 giftet Erik Plogpenning seg med Judith (Jutta) av Sachsen. Født 1232.
1 CONT           Død 02.02.1261. Judith (Jutta) var datter av hertug Albrecht I. av Sachsen (død
1 CONT           26.06.1261) og prinsesse Agnes av Østerrike. Erik Plogpenning var nesten under
1 CONT           hele sin regjeringstid i strid med sine hel og halvbrødre, som også hadde fått store
1 CONT           deler av riket i len av faren. 
1 CONT           Erik Plogpenning ble halshugget - da han ble tatt til fange - i uroligheter under et tysk
1 CONT           angrep. Liket ble kastet på sjøen, men ble senere funnet stygt lemlestet av noen
1 CONT           fiskere. Folket ærede Erik Plogpenning som en helgen i ettertid. Hans dronning
1 CONT           Judith (Jutta) giftet seg igjen med Burchard, borggreve av Rosenburg. Erik
1 CONT           Plogpenning fikk med Judith (Jutta) to sønner, men de døde unge, og fire døtre.
1 CONT           Datteren Sofie ble gift med kong Valdemar av Sverige. Datteren Ingeborg ble gift
1 CONT           med kong Magnus Lagabøter Håkonsson, mens døtrene Jutta og Agnes gikk i
1 CONT           klostertjeneste, men forlot dette senere.
1 CONT
1 CONT [Hårfagre og 25 generasjoner frem.FTW]
