0 @N6000000002187757826@ NOTE Theophano (Byzantine Empress)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Theophano was a Byzantine empress. She was the wife of Romanus II; wife and murderer of Nicephorus Phocas; lover of John I Tzimisces ; the mother of Basil II , Constantine VIII and the princess Anna Porphyrogenita, who later marri
1 CONC ed the Russian prince Vladimir.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Becoming Empress
1 CONT     * 2 Partnership with Nicephorus Phocas
1 CONT     * 3 Betrayal
1 CONT     * 4 Downfall
1 CONT     * 5 Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Becoming Empress
1 CONT
1 CONT This beautiful but considerably amoral woman played an important role in Byzantine history. An innkeeper's daughter by the name of Anastaso, the emperor Romanus II fell in love with her around the year 956 and married her. Romanus
1 CONC ' father Constantine VII Porphyrogentius avoided the mistake of preventing his son to marry the girl of his choice--as had several of his precedessors, culminating in their downfalls--by blandly pretending that Anastaso was of nob
1 CONC le birth. After their marriage, she was given the name of Romanus' grandfather's first saintly wife Theophano (whom she resembled not at all).
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Partnership with Nicephorus Phocas
1 CONT
1 CONT On March 15, 963, Emperor Romanus II unexpectedly died at the age of twenty-six. His sons Basil II and Constantine VIII were heirs and Theophano was named regent. However she realized that to secure power she needed to align her i
1 CONC nterest with the strongest general at the time, Nicephorus Phocas. As the army had already proclaimed him as an Emperor in Caesarea, Nicephorus entered Constantinople on August 15, broke the resistance of Joseph Bringas ( a eunuch
1 CONC  palace official who had become Romanus' chief council) in bloody street fights, and on 16 August he was crowned in Hagia Sophia. After that he married Theophano, thereby legitimizing his reign by marring into the Macedonian dynas
1 CONC ty.
1 CONT
1 CONT The marriage proved controversial as Nicephorus had been god-father to one or more of Theophano's children, which placed them within a prohibited spiritual relationship. Nicephorus (who no doubt sincerely loved his beautiful wife)
1 CONC  organised a council at which it was denied that he had ever been god-father to his wife's children.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Betrayal
1 CONT
1 CONT However, not too long after, she became lover to a young and brilliant general, John Tzimisces. They soon began to conspire against Nicephorus . She prepared the assassination and John and his friends implemented it on the night b
1 CONC etween 10 and 11 December 969. The emperor was now John I Tzimisces (969-976)
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Downfall
1 CONT
1 CONT However, Theophano badly miscalculated in the hope of becoming the wife of the new ruler. Slain Nicephorus found his avenger in the Patriarch Polyeuctus, who was determined to punish the crime. He demanded John to repent, to punis
1 CONC h the murderers (his helpers and friends), and to remove Theophano from the court. John was forced to submit to Patriarch’s requests. Only then was he allowed to enter the church and be crowned emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Theophano was first sent into exile to the island of Prinkipo, sometimes known as Prote. However, shortly afterwards, she made a reappearance in the capital, seeking asylum in the Hagia Sophia, where, however, she was forcibly rem
1 CONC oved on the orders of the Chamberlain Basil, who condemned her to exile in distant Armenia. Before this, he granted her request of an audience with the Emperor John, who surprisingly agreed to attend. Once there however, he was su
1 CONC bjected to the former empress, who then physically attacked the chamberlain, landing several telling blows.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is possible that after the succession of her sons to the throne that she was able to return to Constantinople.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT History of the Byzantine State by Georgije Ostrogorski
1 CONT
1 CONT Byzantium: The Apogee by John Julius Norwich
0 @N6000000002187952962@ NOTE Theophanu
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Theophanu (Between 956-958 – June 15, 991) (Greek: Te?fa?? Theophano), also spelled Theophania, Theophana or Theophano, was born in Constantinople, and was the wife of Otto II, Holy Roman Emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Family 
1 CONT 2 Marriage and children 
1 CONT 3 Life as Empress 
1 CONT 4 Sources 
1 CONT 5 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT Otto I, Holy Roman Emperor had requested a Greek princess for his son, Otto, to seal a treaty between the Holy Roman Empire and the Byzantine Empire. The unwise reference by the pope to the ruler in Constantinople as "Greek" in a 
1 CONC letter while Otto's ambassador, Liudprand of Cremona had been in Byzantine had destroyed the first round of negotiations. With the ascension of a new emperor who had not been personally been referred to other as Emperor of Rome, t
1 CONC he treaty negotiations were able to resume. Theophanu duly arrived in 972, arriving in grand style with a magnificent escort and bearing great treasure. However, according to the chronicler Thietmar, she was not the virgo desidera
1 CONC ta, the Imperial princess, that was expected. Theophanu is identified in the marriage contract as the neptis (niece or granddaughter) of Emperor John I Tzimisces (??????? ? ?s?µ?s???). John Tzimisces married Theodora, the sister o
1 CONC f Romanus II, who was a daughter of Romanus and Theophano, who was Romanus' wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT At one time it was believed Theophanu was the daughter of the Emperor Romanos II and his consort Theophano, but no mention is made of her being porphyrogenita (p??f????????t?), purple-born, nor are her parents identified. It is un
1 CONC likely that Theophanu was the daughter of any emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Current research holds that her father was Konstantinos Skleros (???sta?t???? S??????), brother of the pretender Bardas Skleros (???da? S??????) and her mother was Sophia Phokaina (S?f?a F??a??a), niece of Emperor Nikephoros II (?
1 CONC ???f???? ??). Her father's sister of Maria Skleraina (?a??a S????a??a) was the first wife of Tzimisces'. Accordingly she was of Armenian descent.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage and children
1 CONT Theophanu and Otto were married by Pope John XIII on April 14, 972 at Saint Peter's and she was crowned the same day in Rome. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Adelaide, Abbess of Quedlinburg, born November or December 977, died 1040. 
1 CONT Sophia, Abbess of Gandersheim and Essen, born 978, died 1039. 
1 CONT Matilda, born 979, died 1025; who married Ezzo, count palatine of Lotharingia 
1 CONT Otto III, Holy Roman Emperor, born June or July 980 
1 CONT A daughter, a twin to Otto, who died before October 8, 980 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life as Empress
1 CONT Theophanu accompanied her husband on all his journeys, and issued diplomas in her own name as Empress. It is known she was frequently at odds with her mother-in-law, Adelaide of Italy, and this caused an estrangement between Otto 
1 CONC II and Adelaide. According to Odilo, Abbot of Cluny, Adelaide was very happy when "that Greek woman" died.
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert of Metz describes Theophanu as being an unpleasant and talkative woman. Theophanu was also criticized for her decadence, which manifested in her bathing once a day and introducing luxurious garments and jewelry into Germany
1 CONC . She is credited with introducing the fork to Western Europe - chronographers mention the astonishment she caused when she "used a golden double prong to bring food to her mouth" instead of using her hands as was the norm. "The t
1 CONC heologian Peter Damian even asserts that Theophanu had a love affair with John Philagathos, a Greek monk who briefly reigned as Antipope John XVI.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Sarcophagus of Empress TheophanuOtto II died suddenly on December 7, 983 and was buried in Rome. That Christmas Theophanu had their three-year-old son crowned as Otto III, with herself ruling as Empress Regent on his behalf. Henry
1 CONC  II, Duke of Bavaria seized Otto in spring 984, but was forced to surrender the child to his mother. With the cooperation of Willigis, Archbishop of Mainz, and Hildebald, Bishop of Worms, Theophanu reigned until her death in 991.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was buried in the church of Saint Pantaleon at Cologne. The chronicler Thietmar eulogized her as follows: "Though [Theophanu] was of the weak sex she possessed moderation, trustworthiness, and good manners. In this way she pro
1 CONC tected with male vigilance the royal power for her son, friendly with all those who were honest, but with terrifying superiority against rebels."
1 CONT
1 CONT Because Otto III was still a child, his grandmother Adelaide of Italy took over the regency until Otto III became old enough to rule on his own.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Davids, Adelbert. The Empress Theophano: Byzantium and the West at the turn of the first millennium, 2002. ISBN 0-521-52467-9 
1 CONT Hans K. Schulze, Die Heiratsurkunde der Kaiserin Theophanu, Hannover 2007 ISBN 978-3-7752-6124-1 
1 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700 by Frederick Lewis Weis, Line 147-20. 
