0 @N6000000000337165576@ NOTE (Research):John Encyclopædia Britannica Article    Article   Index Entry born Aug. 10, 1296, Luxembourg died Aug. 26, 1346, Crécy, France byname John of Luxembourg , or John of Bohemia , Czech Jan Lucembursky , or Jan S Cech king 
1 CONC of Bohemia from 1310 until his death, and one of the more popular heroic figures of his day, who campaigned across Europe from Toulouse to Prussia.   He was born the son of the future Holy Roman emperor Henry VII of the house of L
1 CONC uxembourg and was made count of Luxembourg in 1310. At about the same time, he also was named king of Bohemia, and on Feb. 7, 1311, he was solemnly crowned at Prague. When his father died in 1313, John was too young to succeed him
1 CONC  as emperor and supported instead the election of Louis the Bavarian as Emperor Louis IV (1314). John subsequently sided with Louis in his struggle against Frederick of Austria (1322); but in later years he was estranged from the 
1 CONC Emperor, especially after Louis's alliance with England against France in the Hundred Years' War. John's own sympathies strongly favoured the French. He had sent his own son, the future emperor Charles IV, to be reared in Paris, a
1 CONC nd he several times fought in the service of France. The Columbia Encyclopedia, Sixth Edition.  2001.   John of Luxemburg    1296-1346, king of Bohemia (1310-46). The son of Holy Roman Emperor Henry VII, he married Elizabeth, sist
1 CONC er of Wenceslaus III of Bohemia, and in 1310 he was chosen king of Bohemia, which had been in virtual anarchy since Wenceslaus's death (1306). As a condition of his accession John was forced to issue a charter guaranteeing the rig
1 CONC hts of the nobility and clergy. Perhaps disappointed that he was not elected to succeed his father, John spent much of his time in foreign wars. During his reign he extended Bohemian control to upper Lusatia and Silesia. He suppor
1 CONC ted the Teutonic Knights in their wars against Lithuania. As a result of his campaigns he ruled parts of Lombardy and Tyrol briefly. He died fighting on the side of the French at Crécy though he had become blind. He was succeeded 
1 CONC by his son, who later became Holy Roman emperor as Charles IV.
0 @N6000000002187840566@ NOTE John I of Bohemia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT John I, Count of Luxemburg.John the Blind (Luxembourgish: Jang de Blannen; German: Johann der Blinde von Luxemburg; Czech: Jan Lucemburský; 10 August 1296 – 26 August 1346) was the Count of Luxembourg from 1309, King of Bohemia, a
1 CONC nd titular King of Poland from 1310. He was the eldest son of the Holy Roman Emperor Henry VII and his wife Margaret of Brabant.
1 CONT
1 CONT John was French by education, but deeply involved in the politics of Germany. In 1310, John married Elisabeth, heiress of Wenceslaus III of Bohemia, and thereby became King of Bohemia and so one of the seven prince-electors of the
1 CONC  Holy Roman Empire. The object of the hostility of the Czech nobility, however, he gave up the administration of Bohemia and embarked on a life of travel, spending time in Luxembourg and the French court. His travels took him to S
1 CONC ilesia, Poland, Lithuania, Tyrol, Northern Italy, Papal Avignon, and Languedoc, where he was governor from 30 November 1338 to November 1340. He lost his eyesight from ophthalmia, while crusading in Lithuania with the Teutonic ord
1 CONC er.
1 CONT
1 CONT He retained his crown even after Elisabeth's death in 1330. His second wife was Beatrice, daughter of Louis I, Duke of Bourbon.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was killed whilst fighting alongside the French against the English at the Battle of Crécy, part of the Hundred Years' War. The chronicler Froissart left the following account of John's last actions:
1 CONT
1 CONT ...for all that he was nigh blind, when he understood the order of the battle, he said to them about him: 'Where is the lord Charles my son?' His men said: 'Sir, we cannot tell; we think he be fighting.' Then he said: 'Sirs, ye ar
1 CONC e my men, my companions and friends in this journey: I require you bring me so far forward, that I may strike one stroke with my sword.' They said they would do his commandment, and to the intent that they should not lose him in t
1 CONC he press, they tied all their reins of their bridles each to other and set the king before to accomplish his desire, and so they went on their enemies. The lord Charles of Bohemia his son, who wrote himself king of Almaine and bar
1 CONC e the arms, he came in good order to the battle; but when he saw that the matter went awry on their party, he departed, I cannot tell you which way. The king his father was so far forward that he strake a stroke with his sword, ye
1 CONC a and more than four, and fought valiantly and so did his company; and they adventured themselves so forward, that they were there all slain, and the next day they were found in the place about the king, and all their horses tied 
1 CONC each to other.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the battle, legend states that John's personal crest (a pair of black wings) and motto Ich dien ("I Serve") were adopted in slightly modified form by Edward, the Black Prince, and since then they have been part of the badge 
1 CONC of the Prince of Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT John was succeeded as King of Bohemia by his eldest son Charles (later Charles IV, Holy Roman Emperor). In Luxembourg, he was succeeded by his son by his second wife, Wenceslaus.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Quotes
1 CONT Before he died at the Battle of Crécy, he cried: "God willing, it will never happen that a Bohemian king runs off a fight!"[citation needed]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and children
1 CONT
1 CONT Coat of Arms of John the Blind, Count of Luxemburg and King of Bohemia.He was married two times:
1 CONT
1 CONT First, to Elizabeth I of Bohemia. In this marriage he had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret (8 July 1313–11 July 1341, Prague), married in Straubing 12 August 1328 to Henry XIV, Duke of Bavaria. 
1 CONT Bonne (21 May 1315–11 September 1349, Maubuisson), married in Melun 6 August 1332 to King John II of France. 
1 CONT Charles IV (14 May 1316–29 November 1378), King of Bohemia and Holy Roman Emperor. 
1 CONT Ottokar ("Otto") (22 November 1318–20 April 1320), Prince of Bohemia. 
1 CONT John Henry (Jan Jindrich) (12 February 1322, Melník–12 November 1375), Margrave of Moravia. 
1 CONT Anna (1323–3 September 1338), twin of Elizabeth, married 16 February 1335 to Otto, Duke of Austria. 
1 CONT Elizabeth (1323–1324), twin of Anna. 
1 CONT Second (December 1334), to Beatrix of Bourbon, daughter of Louis I, Duke of Bourbon. This marriage produced two children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Wenceslaus I of Luxembourg (25 February 1337, Prague–7 December 1383, Luxembourg), Duke of Luxembourg and Brabant. 
1 CONT Bonne 
1 CONT Henry V of Luxembourg   Margaret of Bar   Baldwin of Avesnes   Félicité of Coucy   Henry III, Duke of Brabant   Alice of Burgundy   Louis II of Flanders   Margaret of Braba
1 CONT
