0 @N6000000003051280934@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Philippa of Hanault", retrieved 23 Dec 07]
1 CONT Philippa of Hainault (c. 1314-August 15, 1369) was the Queen consort of Edward III of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa was born in Valenciennes (then in Flanders, now France) and was the daughter of William I, Count of Hainaut and Jeanne of Valois, the granddaughter of Philip III of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT Life as Queen
1 CONT She married Edward at York Minster, on 24 January 1328, eleven months after his accession to the English throne and, unlike many of her predecessors, she did not alienate the English people by retaining her foreign retinue upon he
1 CONC r marriage or bringing large numbers of foreigners to the English court.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa accompanied Edward on his expeditions to the Kingdom of Scotland (1333) and Flanders (1338-40), where she won acclaim for her gentleness and compassion. She is also remembered by history as the tender-hearted woman, who i
1 CONC nterceded with her husband and persuaded him to spare the lives of the Burghers of Calais (1346) whom he had planned to execute as an example to the townspeople. She acted as a regent on several occasions when he was on the contin
1 CONC ent.
1 CONT
1 CONT Children
1 CONT Philippa and Edward had fourteen children, including five sons who lived into adulthood and whose rivalry would eventually bring about the long-running civil wars known as the Wars of the Roses. Their sons are listed below:
1 CONT - Edward, the Black Prince (1330-76)
1 CONT - Lionel of Antwerp, 1st Duke of Clarence (1338-68)
1 CONT - John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster (1340-99)
1 CONT - Edmund of Langley, 1st Duke of York (1341-1402)
1 CONT - Thomas of Woodstock, 1st Duke of Gloucester (1355-97)
1 CONT Another three sons and two daughters died in infancy. There were four surviving daughters, listed below:
1 CONT - Isabella of England (1332-1379)
1 CONT - Joan of England (1334-1348)
1 CONT - Mary Plantagenet (1344-1362)
1 CONT - Margaret Plantagenet (1346-1361)
1 CONT
1 CONT Through her children, Philippa reintroduced the blood of an earlier King to the English royal house. She was descended from Stephen of England through Matilda of Brabant, the wife of Floris IV, Count of Holland. Their daughter Ade
1 CONC laide of Holland married John I of Avesnes, Count of Hainaut, Philippa's paternal great-grandfather. Matilda of Brabant in turn was the great-granddaughter of Stephen through her mother Matilda of Boulogne, the wife of Henry I, Du
1 CONC ke of Brabant.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philippa was also a descendant of Harold II of England through his daughter Gytha of Wessex, married to Vladimir II Monomakh of Kiev, but so was her husband Edward III. The descent came through their mutual great-grandparent Isabe
1 CONC lla of Aragon, married to Philip III of France. Her mother Violant of Hungary was a daughter of Andrew II of Hungary, a grandson of Géza II by Euphrosyne of Kiev, herself a granddaughter of Gytha.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later Life and Death
1 CONT Philippa had grown portly in her later years, and this added to the view most of her English subjects had of her; as a friendly, homely, motherly woman whom the nation greatly loved. Philippa outlived 9 of her 14 children; two of 
1 CONC whom were lost during the Black Death outbreak (1348).
1 CONT
1 CONT On 15 August 1369 Philippa died of an illness akin to dropsy in Windsor Castle, and was buried at Westminster Abbey. By all accounts, her 40 year marriage to Edward had been happy, despite his taking a mistress, Alice Perrers, dur
1 CONC ing the latter part of it.
1 CONT
1 CONT Queens College, Oxford is named after her.It was founded by one of her chaplains Robert de Eglesfield in her honour.
