0 @N6000000006444496658@ NOTE Peter IV of Aragon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Peter IV of Aragon (1319-1387), king of Aragon (1336-1387), the Ceremonious or el del punyalet (the one of the little dagger). He deposed James III of Majorca and rejoined the Balearic Islands and Roussillon under the crown of Ara
1 CONC gon. He wrote the Chronicle of his name.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Majorca Kings Palace at PerpinyàSince 1356, he engaged with Peter I of Castile in what was called the "War of the Two Peters". It ended in 1375 with the Treaty of Almazán, without a winner due to the Black Death and several natura
1 CONC l disasters.
1 CONT
1 CONT Throughout his reign, Peter IV had frequent conflicts with the inquisitor general of Aragon, Nicolas Eymeric.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his reign, the Generalitat was instituted in the Courts of 1358-1359.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Marriage and Children
1 CONT On 1338, he married Maria of Navarra (1326-1347), daughter of Joan II of Navarre. She bore him two daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Constança (1340-1363), who married Frederick III of Sicily. 
1 CONT Joan, Countess of Empuries. 
1 CONT On 1347, he married Leonor of Portugal (1328-1348), daughter of Afonso IV of Portugal. She died one year later of the Black Death.
1 CONT
1 CONT His third marriage was to Elionor of Sicily (1325-1375), daughter of Peter II of Sicily. Four children were born from this marriage:
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan I 
1 CONT Martí I 
1 CONT Eleanor, who married Juan I of Castile and was the mother of Ferdinand I of Aragon. 
1 CONT Alfons (died young). 
1 CONT His last marriage, in 1377, was to Sibila of Fortià, who bore him a daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT Elizabeth (1376-1424), who married her cousin James (Jaume), Count of Urgell. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Alfonso IV King of Aragon
1 CONT 1336–1387 Succeeded by:
1 CONT John I 
1 CONT Count of Barcelona
1 CONT 1336–1387 
1 CONT King of Valencia
1 CONT 1336–1387 
