0 @N6000000002187639206@ NOTE Bohemund III of Antioch
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT  Bohemund III of Antioch (1144–1201), also known as the Stammerer, was Prince of Antioch from 1163 to his death. He was a son of Constance of Antioch by her first husband Raymond of Poitiers.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Early life 
1 CONT 2 Prince of Antioch 
1 CONT 3 Excommunication 
1 CONT 4 Collapse of Jerusalem 
1 CONT 5 Later life and death 
1 CONT 6 Family and children 
1 CONT 7 Sources 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life
1 CONT Bohemund's father was killed at the Battle of Inab in 1149, and his mother ruled as regent until he was old enough to rule on his own. Constance, however, married a second time, to Raynald of Chatillon, who ruled as Prince of Anti
1 CONC och until being taken captive and imprisoned in Aleppo in 1160 (he remained there until 1176). Bohemund was by now of legal age to succeed, but Constance refused; King Baldwin III of Jerusalem intervened and declared Bohemund rule
1 CONC r of the principality. In 1163 Constance asked the Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia for aid in order to maintain her rule; the citizens of Antioch then rioted and exiled her. She died later that year, allowing Bohemund to take full con
1 CONC trol.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Prince of Antioch
1 CONT In 1164, Bohemund and Raymond III of Tripoli marched out to relieve Harim, under siege from Nur ad-Din, but when Nur ad-Din retreated Bohemund led a charge against him. The ensuing battle was a disaster and both Bohemund and Raymo
1 CONC nd were taken prisoner. King Amalric I of Jerusalem hastened back from his invasion of Egypt to take control of the regency of Antioch; Bohemund was freed, for a large ransom, in 1165 with the intervention of Amalric and Byzantine
1 CONC  emperor Manuel I Comnenus, his nominal overlord; Manuel was also his brother-in-law, as he was married to Bohemund's sister Maria of Antioch. Nur ad-Din was always wary of Byzantine intervention in Syria, which may explain his qu
1 CONC ick release of Bohemund. Bohemund then visited Manuel in Constantinople, where he agreed to re-establish a Greek Patriarch in Antioch, Athanasius II. The Latin Patriarch, Aimery of Limoges, protested this and imposed an interdict 
1 CONC on the city. He did not return until Athanasius died in 1170.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1166 the future emperor Andronicus Comnenus, now only governor of Cilicia, arrived in Antioch, having heard of the beauty of Bohemund's sister Philippa. Their subsequent affair angered both Bohemund and Manuel, as Philippa was 
1 CONC the sister of Manuel's wife and thus the relationship was considered incestuous by the church. Andronicus was forced to flee to Jerusalem, where he also seduced Queen Theodora Comnena, an even closer relative.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1172 Bohemund invaded Armenia, in response to Mleh of Armenia's alliance with Nur ad-Din. In 1177, along with Raymond III and Philip, Count of Flanders, who had arrived on pilgrimage, Bohemund besieged Harim, but they could not
1 CONC  recapture it and the siege was abandoned.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1180 Bohemund and Raymond attempted to intervene in the Kingdom of Jerusalem, which was at the time ruled by their kinsman Baldwin IV, a leper. Because Baldwin could have no heirs, it was vital that his sister Sibylla be marrie
1 CONC d to a suitable candidate for the kingship. After the death of her first husband, William of Montferrat, Baldwin had been trying to negotiate another foreign marriage for her. Raymond and Bohemund, both firsts cousin of Baldwin an
1 CONC d Sibylla, brought their forces into the kingdom with the intention of marrying her to one of their supporters, Baldwin of Ibelin. The king pre-empted them by marrying her off to Guy of Lusignan.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Excommunication
1 CONT Around this time Bohemund left his wife Theodora, a niece of the recently-deceased Emperor Manuel, and married a woman named Sibylla, "who had the reputation of practicing evil arts" according to William of Tyre. He was excommunic
1 CONC ated by Pope Alexander III, and Antioch was placed under an interdict, but "to this...he paid slight attention. On the contrary, he continued on his wicked course with redoubled energy." He imprisoned Patriarch Aimery and other bi
1 CONC shops and looted their churches. Opposition to Bohemund was led by Reynald Masoir. Patriarch Heraclius of Jerusalem was sent to mediate in 1181, with Raynald of Chatillon, Raymond III of Tripoli, Arnold of Toroga, and Roger des Mo
1 CONC ulins, but Bohemund refused to acquiesce, and expelled the mediators as well as a number of his own nobles.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Collapse of Jerusalem
1 CONT In 1183 Antioch was harassed by Saladin, with whom Bohemund then negotiated a peace treaty. He also sold Tarsus to Ruben III of Armenia, in order to make Antioch more easily defensible. Meanwhile, in Jerusalem, Baldwin IV was beco
1 CONC ming more and more incapacitated, and Raymond III had Sibylla's son from her first marriage, Baldwin V, crowned co-king. He was supported in this by the nobles' party, including Bohemund. However, Baldwin IV died in 1185 and Baldw
1 CONC in V died as a child soon afterwards. Bohemund, Raymond, and the nobles could not prevent Guy and Sibylla from succeeding to the throne in 1186. Their reign was disastrous. The Kingdom of Jerusalem was all but destroyed by Saladin
1 CONC  at the Battle of Hattin in 1187; Bohemund was not present, but his son Raymond was in the vanguard and escaped with Raymond of Tripoli. Saladin invaded Antioch afterwards, but Bohemund was able to defend his territory with help f
1 CONC rom a Sicilian fleet. Raymond of Tripoli died soon after Hattin, and had named Bohemund's elder son Raymond as his successor, but Bohemund ignored this and instead installed his second son, Bohemund IV, as count.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Later life and death
1 CONT In 1190 Bohemund met the remnants of the German contingent arriving on the Third Crusade; Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor, had died on the way and some of his remains were buried in Antioch. He otherwise played little role in the 
1 CONC crusade, preferring to remain neutral to avoid provoking Saladin. In 1194 Bohemund was captured by Leo II of Armenia. Leo had seized the castle of Bagras, on the northern border of Antioch, which had been captured by Saladin in 11
1 CONC 89. Bohemund and the Knights Templar, its original owners, demanded its return. Leo lured Bohemund to Bagras under pretense of a parley, captured him, and imprisoned him in Sis. Under duress, Bohemund was compelled to cede the Pri
1 CONC ncipality to Leo. Bohemund was subsequently released through the mediation of Henry II of Champagne, King of Jerusalem, but was forced to abandon all claims to the suzerainity of Armenia. In addition, the two entered into a marita
1 CONC l alliance in 1195: Bohemond's son Raymond married Alice of Armenia, the daughter of Leo's brother Ruben III.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bohemund died in 1201, and the succession was disputed between his son Bohemund IV and Raymond-Roupen, son of Raymond and Alice.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and children
1 CONT Orguilleuse d'Harenc (married 1169, died around 1175) 
1 CONT Raymond IV of Tripoli (died 1199), who married Alice (daughter of Ruben III of Armenia) and was the father of Raymond-Roupen of Antioch 
1 CONT Bohemund IV of Antioch (1172 – 1233). 
1 CONT Theodora Comnena, daughter of John Comnenus Ducas, Duke of Cyprus, and Maria Taronitissa (married around 1176) 
1 CONT Constance (died young) 
1 CONT Manuel (1176 – 1211) 
1 CONT Sibylla (married 1180) 
1 CONT Alice (died 1233), married Guy I Embriaco, lord of Gibelet 
1 CONT William 
1 CONT Isabella (married 1199) 
1 CONT Bohemund (died 1244), married the heiress of Botrun 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Steven Runciman, A History of the Crusades, vols. II-III. Cambridge University Press, 1952-54. 
1 CONT William of Tyre, A History of Deeds Done Beyond the Sea, trans. E.A. Babcock and A.C. Krey. Columbia University Press, 1943. 
