1 CONT Duke of Clarence
0 @N6000000002187746112@ NOTE Lionel of Antwerp, 1st Duke of Clarence
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lionel of Antwerp, Duke of Clarence, (November 29, 1338 – October 7, 1368) was the second son of Edward III of England and Philippa of Hainault. He was so called because he was born at Antwerp, Belgium.
1 CONT
1 CONT Betrothed when a child to Elizabeth de Burgh, 4th Countess of Ulster (d. 1363), daughter and heiress of William Donn de Burgh, 3rd Earl of Ulster (d. 1332), he was married to her in 1352, but before this date he had entered into p
1 CONC ossession of her great Irish inheritance. He was called Earl of Ulster from 1347.
1 CONT
1 CONT Having been named as his father's representative in England in 1345 and again in 1346, Lionel joined an expedition into France in 1355, but his chief energies were reserved for the affairs of Ireland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Appointed governor of that country, he landed at Dublin in 1361, and in November of the following year was created Duke of Clarence, while his father made an abortive attempt to secure for him the crown of Scotland. His efforts to
1 CONC  secure an effective authority over his Irish lands were only moderately successful; and after holding a parliament at Kilkenny, which passed the celebrated Statute of Kilkenny in 1367, he dropped the task in disgust and returned 
1 CONC to England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lionel's wife died in Dublin in 1363, having given birth to a daughter, Philippa, whose descendants would one day claim the throne for the House of York. A second marriage was arranged for Lionel with Yolande or Violante, daughter
1 CONC  of Galeazzo Visconti, lord of Pavia (d. 1378); the enormous dowry which Galeazzo promised with his daughter being exaggerated by the rumour of the time. Journeying to fetch his bride, Lionel was received in great state both in Fr
1 CONC ance and Italy, and was married to Violante at Milan in June 1368. Some months were then spent in festivities, during which Lionel was taken ill at Alba, where he died.
1 CONT
1 CONT His only child, Philippa Plantagenet, married in 1368 Edmund Mortimer, 3rd Earl of March (1351-1381). They were parents to Anne de Mortimer, grandparents to Richard Plantagenet, 3rd Duke of York and great-grandparents to Edward IV
1 CONC  and Richard III.
1 CONT
1 CONT The poet Geoffrey Chaucer was at one time a page in Lionel's household.
0 @N6000000004533182842@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Lionel of Antwerp, Duke of Clarence, (November 29, 1338 - October 7,1368) was the second son of Edward III of England, and was so calledbecause he was born at Antwerp, Belgium.
1 CONT
1 CONT Betrothed when a child to Elizabeth (d. 1363), daughter and heiress ofWilliam de Burgh, 3rd Earl of Ulster (d. 1332), he was married to herin 1352, but before this date he had entered into possession of hergreat Irish inheritance.
1 CONC  He was called Earl of Ulster from 1347.
1 CONT
1 CONT Having been named as his father's representative in England in 1345and again in 1346, Lionel joined an expedition into France in 1355,but his chief energies were reserved for the affairs of Ireland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Appointed governor of that country, he landed at Dublin in 1361, andin November of the following year was created Duke of Clarence, whilehis father made an abortive attempt to secure for him the crown ofScotland. His efforts to se
1 CONC cure an effective authority over his Irishlands were only moderately successful; and after holding a parliamentat Kilkenny, which passed the celebrated Statute of Kilkenny in 1367,he dropped the task in disgust and returned to Eng
1 CONC land.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lionel's wife died in Dublin in 1363, having given birth to adaughter, Philippa, whose descendants would one day claim the thronefor the House of York. A second marriage was arranged for Lionel withYolande or Violante, daughter of
1 CONC  Galeazzo Visconti, lord of Pavia (d.1378); the enormous dowry which Galeazzo promised with his daughterbeing exaggerated by the rumour of the time. Journeying to fetch hisbride, Lionel was received in great state both in France a
1 CONC nd Italy,and was married to Violante at Milan in June 1368. Some months werethen spent in festivities, during which Lionel was taken ill at Alba,where he died.
1 CONT
1 CONT His only child, Philippa, married in 1368 Edmund Mortimer, 3rd Earl ofMarch (1351-1381), and through this union Clarence became the ancestorof Edward IV. The poet Chaucer was at one time a page in Lionel'shousehold.
