0 @N6000000002187742986@ NOTE John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster (June 24, 1340–February 3, 1399) was the third surviving son of King Edward III of England and Philippa of Hainault. He gained his name "John of Gaunt" because he was born at Ghent in 1340. The fab
1 CONC ulously wealthy Gaunt exercised tremendous influence over the throne during the minority reign of his nephew, Richard II, and during the ensuing periods of political strife, but took care not to be openly associated with opponents
1 CONC  of the King.
1 CONT
1 CONT John of Gaunt's legitimate male heirs, the Lancasters, included Kings Henry IV, Henry V, and Henry VI. John of Gaunt's illegitimate heirs, the Beauforts, later married into the House of Tudor, which ascended to the throne in the p
1 CONC erson of Henry VII. In addition, Gaunt's legitimate descendants included his daughters Philippa of Lancaster, Queen consort of John I of Portugal and mother of King Edward of Portugal, Elizabeth, Duchess of Exeter, the mother of J
1 CONC ohn Holland, 2nd Duke of Exeter, and Katherine of Lancaster, Queen consort of Henry III of Castile, a grand-daughter of Peter I of Castile and the mother of John II of Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT When John of Gaunt died in 1399, his estates were declared forfeit to the crown, as Richard II had exiled John's less diplomatic heir, Henry Bolingbroke, in 1398. Bolingbroke returned and deposed the unpopular Richard, to reign as
1 CONC  King Henry IV of England (1399–1413), the first of the descendants of John of Gaunt to hold the throne of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Duke of Lancaster 
1 CONT 2 Marriages and descendants 
1 CONT 2.1 Children of John of Gaunt 
1 CONT 3 Popular culture 
1 CONT 4 Links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Duke of Lancaster
1 CONT John was created Duke of Lancaster in 1362, following the death of his father-in-law Henry of Grosmont, 1st Duke of Lancaster. He received half of Henry's lands, the title Earl of Lancaster, and the distinction as the greatest lan
1 CONC downer in the north of England, because of his first marriage to his cousin, Blanche of Lancaster (1359), heiress to the Palatinate of Lancaster. John received the rest of the inheritance only when Blanche's sister, Maud (married 
1 CONC to William of Hainault, Count of Holland and Zealand), died in 1361.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gaunt received the title "Duke of Lancaster" from Edward III on 13 November 1382. John was by then well-established as a fabulously wealthy prince, owning at least thirty castles and vast estates across England and France. His hou
1 CONC sehold was comparable in scale and organisation to that of a monarch.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of his elder brother, Edward, the Black Prince, John of Gaunt became increasingly powerful. He contrived to protect the religious reformer John Wyclif, with whose aims he sympathised. However, Gaunt's ascendancy to
1 CONC  political power coincided with widespread resentment at his influence. At a time when English forces encountered setbacks in the Hundred Years' War against France, and Edward III's rule had started to become domestically unpopula
1 CONC r, due to high taxation and to the king's affair with Alice Perrers, political opinion closely associated the Duke of Lancaster with the failing government of the 1370s. Furthermore, while the king and the Prince of Wales had the 
1 CONC status of 'popular heroes' due to their success on the battlefield, John of Gaunt had never known any such military success, which might have bolstered his reputation.
1 CONT
1 CONT When King Edward III died in 1377 and John's ten-year-old nephew succeeded to the throne as Richard II of England, Gaunt's influence strengthened further. However, mistrust remained, and some suspected him of wanting to seize the 
1 CONC throne for himself. John took pains to ensure that he never became associated with the opposition to Richard's kingship; but as the virtual ruler of England during Richard's minority, he made some unwise decisions on taxation that
1 CONC  led to the Peasants' Revolt in 1381, during which the rebels destroyed his Savoy Palace.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1386, Richard, who had by now assumed more power for himself, dispatched Gaunt to Spain as an ambassador. However, crisis ensued almost immediately, and in 1387, Richard's misrule brought England to the brink of civil war. Only
1 CONC  John of Gaunt, upon his return to England, was able to bring about a compromise between the Lords Appellant and King Richard, ushering in a period of relative stability and harmony. During the 1390s, John of Gaunt's reputation of
1 CONC  devotion to the well-being of the kingdom became much restored. Gaunt died of natural causes on February 3, 1399 at Leicester Castle, with his beloved third wife Katherine by his side.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Marriages and descendants
1 CONT Blanche died in 1369. It is believed the poet Geoffrey Chaucer wrote and dedicated his "Book of the Duchess" to her, as the poem not only mentions the Black Knight, but the "Lady White"; whom we can take to be Blanche, in allegory
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1371, John married Constanza of Castile, daughter of King Peter I of Castile, thus giving him a claim upon the kingdom of Castile, which he would pursue unsuccessfully.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the meantime, John of Gaunt had fathered four children by a mistress, Katherine Swynford (whose sister married Geoffrey Chaucer). Constanza died in 1394. He married Katherine in 1396 or 1397, and their children, the Beauforts, 
1 CONC were 'legitimised' but barred from inheriting the throne. From the eldest son, John, came a granddaughter, Margaret Beaufort, whose son, later King Henry VII of England, would nevertheless claim the throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT John of Gaunt's legitimate son from his first marriage, Henry Bolingbroke, proved less of a diplomat than his father; and Richard II banished Henry from the kingdom in 1398. When John of Gaunt died in 1399, his estates were declar
1 CONC ed forfeit to the crown. This caused Bolingbroke to return; he deposed the unpopular Richard, to reign as King Henry IV of England (1399–1413).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Children of John of Gaunt
1 CONT By Blanche of Lancaster: 
1 CONT Philippa Plantagenet (1360–1426), married King John I of Portugal (1357–1433) 
1 CONT John Plantagenet (1362–1365) 
1 CONT Elizabeth Plantagenet (1364–1426), married (1) John Hastings, 3rd Earl of Pembroke (1372–1389); (2) John Holland, 1st Duke of Exeter (1350-1400); (3) Sir John Cornwall, 1st Baron Fanhope and Lord Milbrook (d. 1443) 
1 CONT Edward Plantagenet (1365–1368) 
1 CONT John Plantagenet (1366–136x) 
1 CONT Henry IV of England (1367–1413), married (1) Mary de Bohun (1369–1394); (2) Joanna of Navarre (1368–1437) 
1 CONT Isabel Plantagenet (1368–136x) 
1 CONT By Constanza of Castile: 
1 CONT Catalina (Catherine) Plantagenet (1372–1418), married King Henry III of Castile (1379–1406) 
1 CONT John Plantagenet (1372–1375) 
1 CONT By Katherine Swynford: 
1 CONT John Beaufort (1373–1410), Earl of Somerset, married Margaret Holland (1385-1429) 
1 CONT Henry Cardinal Beaufort (1375–1447) 
1 CONT Thomas Beaufort (1377–1426), Duke of Exeter, married Margaret Neville 
1 CONT Joan Beaufort (1379–1440), married (1) Robert Ferrers, 3rd Baron Ferrers of Wemme (d. 1396); (2) Ralph Neville, 1st Earl of Westmoreland (1364–1425) 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Popular culture
1 CONT The Lancaster city centre has a pub called The John O'Gaunt, noted for its live jazz music and its large collection of whiskies. An administrative ward on the city council also bears the name.
1 CONT
1 CONT In William Shakespeare's play Richard II, the famous England speech is attributed to John of Gaunt as he lay on his deathbed.
1 CONT
1 CONT This royal throne of kings, this scepter'd isle, 
1 CONT This earth of majesty, this seat of Mars, 
1 CONT This other Eden, demi-paradise, 
1 CONT This fortress built by Nature for herself 
1 CONT Against infection and the hand of war, 
1 CONT This happy breed of men, this little world, 
1 CONT This precious stone set in the silver sea, 
1 CONT Which serves it in the office of a wall, 
1 CONT Or as a moat defensive to a house, 
1 CONT Against the envy of less happier lands, 
1 CONT This blessed plot, this earth, this realm, this England, 
1 CONT This nurse, this teeming womb of royal kings, 
1 CONT Fear'd by their breed and famous by their earth 
1 CONT —Act II, scene i, 42–54 
1 CONT The Tragedy of King Richard II at Wikisource
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Links
1 CONT Information about John of Gaunt
