1 CONT
1 CONT Duke of Lancaster, m. (1) 19 May 1359, Blanche, d. 31 Sept 1369, dau. of Henry, Duke of Lancaster; son of HENRY, Earl of Lancaster, (17-29)m. (2) Jun. 1371, Constance, d. Jun. 1394, eldest dau, and coh. Pedro I "The Cruel", King o
1 CONC f Castile and Leon; m. 3() Jan 1396/7, Catherine (Roet) Swynford, b. 1350; d. 10 May 1403; dau of Sir Paon Roet, a Gascon, and widow of Sir Hugh Swynford. (CP V 320, 736 Chart, VII 415; S. Armitage Smith: John Gaunt, reprint 1964)
1 CONC .
0 @N6000000003463809544@ NOTE John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster (1340-99), English soldier and statesman, the fourth son of King Edward III of England, and brother of Edward, the Black Prince. John was born in March 1340 in Ghent (M.E., Gaunt), now in Belgium. 
1 CONC In 1359 he married Blanche, daughter of Henry, duke of Lancaster; when Henry died, John became duke.
1 CONT John of Gaunt played an important part in the wars of the period between England and France and between England and Spain. He commanded a division of the English army, led by the Black Prince, that defeated the army of Henry (late
1 CONC r Henry II, king of Castile and León) at Nájera in 1367. As a result of his second marriage, to Constance, daughter of Peter the Cruel (king of Castile and León), John laid claim to the throne of Castile. During the Hundred Years'
1 CONC  War, he aided (1370-71) the Black Prince against France and established English rule over most of southern France. After a severe illness forced the return of the Black Prince to England, John took command of the English armies; 
1 CONC by 1380 he had lost much of the territory the English had previously won. In 1386 John invaded Castile, but was defeated by John I, king of Castile and León. John of Gaunt gave up his claim to Castile and León in 1387, when his da
1 CONC ughter married Henry, later Henry III, king of Castile and León.
1 CONT John of Gaunt was also prominent in English affairs. Together with Alice Perrers, his father's mistress, John dominated the English government. He was opposed by Parliament and by the Black Prince. In 1376 Parliament banished Alic
1 CONC e Perrers and curtailed John's powers. The death of the Black Prince that year and the dissolution of Parliament, however, enabled John to regain his power. In 1377, on the death of Edward III and the accession of Richard II (John
1 CONC 's nephew and son of the Black Prince), John gave up his control of the government and thereafter played the role of peacemaker; he also supported the king, by whom he was made (1390) duke of Aquitaine. In 1396, after the death of
1 CONC  his second wife, John married his mistress Catherine Swynford, and Richard legitimized their children the following year. Saddened by the exile (1398) of his son, Henry of Lancaster (later King Henry IV of England), John died on 
1 CONC February 3 of the following year.[Holmes A Court.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Acceded: 13 NOV 1362
1 CONT Notes:
1 CONT and Earl of Richmond. Some say born Jun 1340, but see CP
1 CONT vol.XIV,p.421.
1 CONT Earl of Derby, Lincoln. Duke of Aquitaine. Lord of Beaufort & Nogent.
1 CONT Burke says he died at Ely House, Holborn
1 CONT King of Castile & Leon. Lord of Bergerac & Roche-sur-Yon.
1 CONT The Complete Peerage vol.VII,pp.410-416 & vol.XIV,p.421.
1 CONT John of Gaunt
1 CONT 1340-99, duke of Lancaster, fourth son of EDWARD III of England. He
1 CONT acquired the Lancaster holdings (see LANCASTER, HOUSE OF) through
1 CONT marriage and became one of the most influential nobles in England. He
1 CONT served under his brother EDWARD THE BLACK PRINCE in the HUNDRED YEARS
1 CONT WAR and by his second marriage (1371) gained a claim on the throne of
1 CONT Castile. For a short time John, in effect, ruled England for his aging
1 CONT father; he remained powerful under his nephew RICHARD II. From 1386 to
1 CONT 1388 he fought in vain to make good his Castilian claims. Returning
1 CONT (1389) to England, John helped to restore peace between Richard II and
1 CONT the barons. In 1396 he married Catherine Swynford; they were ancestors
1 CONT of the TUDORS. John was the patron of CHAUCER. His eldest son was
1 CONT HENRY IV.
1 CONT John of Gaunt [Mid. Eng. Gaunt=Ghent, his birthplace], 134099, duke
1 CONT of Lancaster; fourth son of Edward III of England. He married (1359)
1 CONT Blanche, heiress of Lancaster, and through her became earl (1361) and
1 CONT duke (1362) of Lancaster. The Lancaster holdings made him the
1 CONT wealthiest and one of the most influential nobles in England. He
1 CONT served under his brother, Edward the Black Prince, in the Hundred
1 CONT Years War and went (1367) on his campaign to aid Peter the Cruel of
1 CONT Castile. After the death of Blanche he married (1371) Peter's
1 CONT daughter, Constance, and thus gained a claim to the Castilian throne.
1 CONT When the Black Prince became ill during the French campaign of
1 CONT 137071, John took chief command. In 1373 he led his army from Calais
1 CONT to Bordeaux, but the expedition accomplished little. After a truce was
1 CONT reached (1375) he returned to England, where he allied himself with
1 CONT the corrupt court party led by Alice Perrers, mistress of the aging
1 CONT Edward III. For a short time John of Gaunt in effect ruled England.
1 CONT His party was temporarily dislodged from power by the Good Parliament
1 CONT of 1376, but John was soon able to restore his friends and assembled a
1 CONT hand-picked Parliament in 1377. Hostility to the strong clerical
1 CONT party, led by William of Wykeham, caused him to support the movement
1 CONT of John Wyclif. After the accession (1377) of his nephew, Richard II,
1 CONT John remained the most powerful figure in the government, but he
1 CONT devoted himself primarily to military matters. In 1386, allied with
1 CONT John I of Portugal, who married one of his daughters, he led an
1 CONT expedition to make good his Castilian claims against John I of
1 CONT Castile. John of Gaunt finally agreed to peace in 1388, transferred
1 CONT his claims to his daughter by Constance of Castile, and married her to
1 CONT the future Henry III of Castile. He returned to England in 1389, was
1 CONT made duke of Aquitaine, and helped to restore peace between Richard II
1 CONT and the hostile barons led by Thomas of Woodstock, duke of Gloucester.
1 CONT In 1396, John of Gaunt married Catherine Swynford, many years his
1 CONT mistress, and had his children by her, under the name of Beaufort,
1 CONT declared legitimate. He died soon after the king had exiled his eldest
1 CONT son, the duke of Hereford (later Henry IV, first of the royal line of
1 CONT Lancaster). John is also remembered as the patron of the poet Geoffrey
1 CONT Chaucer.
