0 @N6000000002188005102@ NOTE Svarn
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Grand Duchy of LithuaniaŠvarn the Lightning (Shvarno, Švarnas, Ioann) was the Knyaz of Halych, Grand Prince of Lithuania 1268 - 1269, Prince of Chelm 1264 - 1269. He died in 1269 and was buried in Chelm.
1 CONT
1 CONT His mother was Anna Mstislavna of Novgorod (died before 1252) and his father was Daniel of Halych (born 1201, died 1264) who served as Prince of Halych-Volynia 1211 - 1213 and 1229 - 1264 and Prince of Volynia 1215 - 1264 and was 
1 CONC crowned by a papal archbishop in Dorohychyn 1253 as the 1st King of Halych-Volynia. He married Mindowhowna, daughter of Mindaugas, in 1255.
1 CONT
1 CONT He had several brothers:
1 CONT
1 CONT Irakli Danylovich (1223 - 1240) 
1 CONT Lev Danylovich (1228 - 1301), Prince of Belz 1245 - 1264, Prince of Halych 1264 - 1269; he moved his capital from Halych to the newly-founded city of Lviv (Lwow, Lemberg) 
1 CONT Roman Danylovich (1230 - 1261), Prince of Black Ruthenia (Navahradak) 1255? - 1260?, and Slonim 
1 CONT Mstislav Danylovich (died after 1300), Prince of Lutsk 1265 - 1289, Prince of Volynia 1289 - aft 1300 
1 CONT He also had several sisters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Pereyaslava (died 12 April 1283, married Prince Ziemowit of Masovia in 1248) 
1 CONT Ustinia (married Prince Andrei II of Vladimir-Suzdal in 1250/1251) 
1 CONT Sofia Danylovna (married Graf Heinrich V von Schwarzburg-Blankenburg in 1259) 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT List of rulers of Halych and Volhynia 
1 CONT List of Belarusian rulers 
1 CONT List of Lithuanian rulers 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Vaisvilkas Grand Prince of Lithuania
1 CONT 1267-1269 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Traidenis 
0 @N6000000002187757289@ NOTE Stefan Uroš I of Serbia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Stefan Uroš I (?????? ???? I) (d. May 1, 1277) was king of Serbia from 1243 to 1276, succeeding his brother Stefan Vladislav.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life
1 CONT Stefan Uroš was the youngest son of Stefan Prvovencani and Anna, the daughter of Enrico Dandolo, Doge of Venice.
1 CONT
1 CONT In spring 1243 the Serbians rebelled and deposed their King Stefan Vladislav I, replacing him with his younger brother Stefan Uroš I. The new king remained on good terms with his predecessor, who is mentioned in some of his charte
1 CONC rs.
1 CONT
1 CONT The reign of Stefan Uroš I coincided with the decline of Serbia's primary rivals in the Balkans, Epirus and Bulgaria. This helped Serbia become an influential local power. That development was actively fostered by its king, who en
1 CONC couraged rapid economic development. Saxon miners from Hungary were introduced to work and develop the Serbian silver mines at Brskovo and Rudnik. The Saxon communities were allowed a level of self-government and the right to wors
1 CONC hip in Catholic-rite churches.
1 CONT
1 CONT Economic prosperity was also fostered by the related intensification of trade with the Dalmatian cities of Dubrovnik and Kotor. The increase in the mining of silver and in trade naturally led to the introduction of larger quantiti
1 CONC es of royal coinage, modeled after the Venetian standard.
1 CONT
1 CONT Stefan Uroš I was forced, however, to undertake military action against several of his neighbors. In 1252–1253 he clashed with Dubrovnik, and shortly afterward his attempt to assert his authority over Zahumlje drove the local prin
1 CONC ce into the arms of the Hungarians, whose vassal he became. Into these conflicts Dubrovnik drew its allies, the Bulgarians, who invaded deep into Serbian territory in 1254. Eventually Stefan Uroš made separate peace agreements wit
1 CONC h his neighbors and the crisis passed.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the second half of the 1260s a new war broke out with Dubrovnik, which was secretly favored by the Serbian queen. A treaty was signed in 1268, specifying the amount of protection money that Dubrovnik was expected to supply 
1 CONC annually to the Serbian king. The arrangement remained largely unbroken for the next century.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1268 the Serbian king invaded the Hungarian possessions south of the Danube in Macva, what is now northern Serbia. In spite of some initial succes, Stefan Uroš was captured by the Hungarians and forced to purchase his release. 
1 CONC A peace treaty was signed between the two kingdoms, and Stefan Uroš's son Stefan Dragutin was married to Catherine (Katalin), the daughter of the future king Stephen V of Hungary.
1 CONT
1 CONT By the end of his reign, Stefan Uroš apparently succeeded in suppressing the autonomy of Zahumlje, where the local princes became virtually indistinguishable from the rest of the nobility. In his effort to achieve centralization, 
1 CONC the king appears to have alienated his eldest son by refusing to grant him an appanage. The conflict between father and son exacerbated, and the king apparently considered making his younger son, the future Stefan Milutin, his hei
1 CONC r.
1 CONT
1 CONT Worried about the inheritance and his very life, Stefan Dragutin finally demanded to be associated on the throne in 1276. When Stefan Uroš refused, Dragutin rebelled and received help from his Hungarian relatives. The allies defea
1 CONC ted the Serbian king and Stefan Uroš was forced to abdicate and retire to his monastic foundation of Sopocani, where he died in c. 1277.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family
1 CONT By his wife Helena, who was either an Angevin princess or a daughter of the Latin Emperor of Constantinople, Stefan Uroš I had at least three sons:
1 CONT
1 CONT Stefan Dragutin, who succeeded as king 
1 CONT Stefan Uroš II Milutin, who succeeded as king in 1282 
1 CONT Stefan 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Stefan Vladislav I King of Serbia
1 CONT 1243–1276 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Stefan Dragutin 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT John V.A. Fine, Jr., The Late Medieval Balkans, Ann Arbor, 1987. 
0 @N6000000000337130761@ NOTE (Research):Amadeus VIII Encyclop‘dia Britannica Article    born 1383, Chamb‚ry, Savoy [now in France] died Jan. 7, 1451, Geneva [now in Switzerland]   byname Amadeus The Peaceful,  Italian Amedeo Il Pacifico, French Am‚d‚e Le Pais
1 CONC ible,  also called Pope Felix V count (1391-1416) and duke (1416-40) of Savoy, first member of the house of Savoy to assume the title of duke. His 42-year reign saw the extension of his authority from Lake Neuchƒtel on the north t
1 CONC o the Ligurian coast, and under the title of Felix V he was an antipope for 10 years (1439-49).   The sudden death of Amadeus VII in 1391 left his eight-year-old son under a regency. In 1393 Amadeus married Mary, daughter of Phili
1 CONC p the Bold, duke of Burgundy. During the early 1400s he increased the territories of Savoy, laying claim to Geneva in 1401 and buying other nearby lands in 1402 and 1406. In 1416 the Holy Roman emperor Sigismund elevated the count
1 CONC y of Savoy to the rank of a duchy.   In 1434, after 42 years on the throne, Amadeus retired to a monastery at Ripaille. His retirement was only partial, however, and he continued to exercise power, with his son Louis (Ludovico) ac
1 CONC ting as his lieutenant. During that period Amadeus' daughter Margherita was betrothed to Louis III of Anjou, pretender to the throne of Naples. Louis III died suddenly in 1434, and Amadeus briefly claimed Naples for Margherita but
1 CONC  in the end abandoned the kingdom to Alfonso V of Aragon. In 1440 Amadeus abdicated. One adventure remained to the aging duke: when Pope Eugenius IV broke off relations with the rebellious Council of Basel in 1439, the mutinous pr
1 CONC elates elected Amadeus pope under the name of Felix V. Amadeus-Felix resigned 10 years later, under pressure from the kings of France, England, and Sicily, and was a cardinal for his last two years.
0 @N6000000006127680598@ NOTE Felix V. (* 4. Dezember 1383; † 7. Januar 1451) - eigentlich Graf Amadeus VIII., "der Friedfertige", von Savoyen - war 1439-1449 der letzte katholische Gegenpapst.1416 von Kaiser Sigismund zum Herzog erhoben und auch sonst politis
1 CONC ch durchaus erfolgreich, übergab er 1434 seinem Sohn die Amtsgeschäfte und zog sich mit sechs Rittern in ein Kloster zurück, wo er den Moritzorden, einen Ritterorden von mäßiglangem Bestand gründete.Als Papst Eugen IV. 1439 das Ko
1 CONC nzil von Basel nach Ferrara verlegte, spaltete sich das Konzil und der in Basel verbliebene Teil setzte am 5. November Eugen ab und wählte in einem nur wenige Stunden dauernden Konklave Amadeus zum Papst. Erresidierte in Genf, Lau
1 CONC sanne und Basel, wurde aber nur in Aragonien, Ungarn, Bayern und der Schweiz anerkannt, weswegen er am 7. April 1449 resignierte, wobei ihm der Rücktritt mit der Gewährung mehrerer Privilegien erleichtert wurde. Bis zuseinem Tod 1
1 CONC 451 war er Kardinalbischof von Sabina und Genf. Er gilt als der letzte historische Gegenpapst.
0 @N6000000002188378489@ NOTE Isabella of Aragon, Countess of Urgel
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabella of Aragon, Countess of Urgel (12 March 1409-29 August 1443) was the wife of Infante Pedro, Duke of Coimbra. She is also referred to as the Duchess of Coimbra.
1 CONT Coat-of-Arms of Isabella's grandfather Peter of Aragon, Count of Urgel
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
