0 @N6000000002188005392@ NOTE Albert III, Elector of Brandenburg
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert III (German Albrecht Achilles), (9 November 1414, Tangermunde–11 March 1486, Frankfurt am Main), Margrave of Brandenburg, given the cognomen Achilles because of his knightly qualities, was the third son of Frederick I of Br
1 CONC andenburg of Hohenzollern, elector of Brandenburg, later Burgrave of Nuremberg.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early life
1 CONT     * 2 Reign
1 CONT     * 3 Dynastic marriages of his children
1 CONT     * 4 Family and children
1 CONT     * 5 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life
1 CONT
1 CONT After passing some time at the court of the emperor Sigismund, he took part in the war against the Hussites, and afterwards distinguished himself whilst assisting the German king, Albert II, against the Poles.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reign
1 CONT
1 CONT On the division of territory which followed his father's death in 1440, Albert received the margravate of Ansbach. Although his resources were very meagre, he soon took a leading place among the German princes, and was especially 
1 CONC prominent in resisting the attempts of the towns to obtain self-government.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1443, he formed a league directed mainly against Nuremberg, over which town members of his family had formerly exercised the rights of burgrave. It was not until 1448, however, that he found a pretext for attack. After initial 
1 CONC military successes he was defeated at the Battle of Pillenreuther Weiher (Pillenreuther bishop's lands) resulting in the Treaty of Bamberg 22 June 1450 which forced Albert to return all of the conquered territory and to recognize 
1 CONC the independence of Nuremberg and its associated towns.
1 CONT
1 CONT He supported the emperor Frederick III in his struggle with the princes who desired reforms in Germany, and in return for this loyalty received many marks of favour from Frederick, including extensive judicial rights which aroused
1 CONC  considerable irritation among neighbouring rulers.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1457, he arranged a marriage between his eldest son John, and Margaret, daughter of William III, landgrave of Thuringia, who inherited the claims upon Hungary and Bohemia of her mother, a granddaughter of the emperor Sigismund.
1 CONC  The attempt to secure these thrones for the Hohenzollerns through this marriage failed, and a similar fate befell Albert's efforts to revive in his own favour the disused title of duke of Franconia.
1 CONT
1 CONT The sharp dissensions which existed among the princes over the question of reform culminated in open warfare in 1460, when Albert was confronted with a league under the leadership of the Count Palatine, Frederick I, and Louis IX, 
1 CONC Duke of Bavaria-Landshut. Worsted in this struggle, which was concluded in 1462, Albert made an alliance with his former enemy, George of Podebrady, King of Bohemia, a step which caused Pope Paul II to place him under the ban.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1470, Albert, who had inherited Bayreuth on the death of his brother John in 1464, became Margrave of Brandenburg owing to the abdication of his remaining brother, the elector Frederick II. He was soon actively engaged in its a
1 CONC dministration, and by the Treaty of Prenzlau in 1472 he brought Pomerania also under his supremacy. Having established his right to levy a tonnage on wines in the mark, he issued in February 1473 the Achillea, which decreed that t
1 CONC he margravate of Brandenburg should descend in its entirety to the eldest son, while the younger sons should receive the Franconian possessions of the family.
1 CONT
1 CONT After treating in vain for a marriage between one of his sons and Mary, daughter and heiress of Charles the Bold, duke of Burgundy, Albert handed over the government of Brandenburg to his eldest son John, and returned to his Franc
1 CONC onian possessions.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Dynastic marriages of his children
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1474, he married his daughter Barbara to Henry XI, Duke of Glogau, who left his possessions on his death in 1476 to his widow with reversion to her family, an arrangement which was resisted by Henry's kinsman, John II, Duke of 
1 CONC Sagan. Aided by Matthias Corvinus, king of Hungary, John invaded Brandenburg, and the Pomeranians seized the opportunity to revolt. Under these circumstances Albert returned to Brandenburg in 1478, compelled the Pomeranians to rec
1 CONC ognize his supremacy, and, after a stubborn struggle, secured a part of Duke Henry's lands for his daughter in 1482.
1 CONT
1 CONT His main attention was afterwards claimed by the business of the Empire, and soon after taking part in the election of Maximilian as king of the Romans he died at Frankfurt in March 1486. He left a considerable amount of treasure.
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert was a man of relentless energy and boundless ambition, who by reason of his physical and intellectual qualities was one of the most prominent princes of the 15th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and children
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert was married twice. First, he married 12 November 1446 with Margarete of Baden, daughter of Markgrave Jakob I of Baden and Catherine of Lorraine. From this marriage he had following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Wolfgang, born and died in 1450.
1 CONT    2. Johann Cicero, Elector of Brandenburg.
1 CONT    3. Friedrich, died young.
1 CONT    4. Ursula (25 September 1450–25 October 1508, Breslau), married Duke Heinrich I of Münsterberg.
1 CONT    5. Elisabeth, (29 October 1451, Ansbach–28 March 1524, Nürtingen), married Eberhard II, Duke of Württemberg.
1 CONT    6. Margareta (18 April 1453–27 April 1509), Abbess of St.Klara.
1 CONT
1 CONT Margarete died 24 October 1457 and in 1458 Albert married Anna of Saxony, daughter of Frederick II, Elector of Saxony and Margarete of Austria. They children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Frederick I, Margrave of Brandenburg-Ansbach, Margrave of Brandenburg in Ansbach since 1486 and Bayreuth since 1495.
1 CONT    2. Amalie (1 October 1461, Plassenburg–3 September 1481, Baden-Baden), married Kaspar, Pfalzgraf of Zweibrücken.
1 CONT    3. Anna, born and died in 1462.
1 CONT    4. Barbara (30 May 1464, Ansbach–4 September 1515, Ansbach), married:
1 CONT          1. in Berlin 11 October 1472 to Duke Henry XI of Glogau;
1 CONT          2. in Frankfurt (Oder) 20 August 1476 to King Ladislaus II of Bohemia and Hungary.
1 CONT    5. Albrecht, born and died in 1466.
1 CONT    6. Sibylle (31 May 1467, Ansbach–9 July 1524, Kaster), married Duke Wilhelm IV of Jülich and Berg.
1 CONT    7. Siegmund, Margrave of Brandenburg in Bayreuth, (27 September 1468, Ansbach–26 February 1495, Ansbach).
1 CONT    8. Albrecht, born and died in 1470.
1 CONT    9. Georg (30 December 1472, Berlin – 5 December 1476, Kadolzburg).
1 CONT   10. Dorothea (12 December 1471, Berlin–13 February 1520, Bamberg), Abbess in Bamberg.
1 CONT   11. Elisabeth (8 April 1474, Ansbach–25 April 1507, Römhild), married Count Hermann VIII of Henneberg-Aschach.
1 CONT   12. Magdalene (29 July 1476, Berlin–before 4 February 1480).
1 CONT   13. Anastasia (14 March 1478, Ansbach–4 July 1534, Ilmenau), married Count Wilhelm VII of Henneberg-Schleusingen.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
