0 @N6000000002188309217@ NOTE Ernest, Duke of Austria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT   (Redirected from Ernest of Austria (Habsburg))
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Brass tomb effigy of Ernest, Duke of Austria
1 CONT
1 CONT Ernest the Iron, officially Ernest, Duke of Inner Austria (German: Ernst der Eiserne; 1377 - June 10, 1424) was the ruler of Styria, Carinthia and Carniola (collectively Inner Austria) from 1406 until his death. He was a member of
1 CONC  the Habsburg dynasty, of the Leopoldian line, whose head of the family he was from 1411 to 1424.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ernest was born in Bruck an der Mur, Styria, the third son of Leopold III, Duke of Inner Austria. After the death of his father in the Battle of Sempach in 1386, he stood under the guardianship of Albert III. In 1401 he accompanie
1 CONC d the German King Rupert on his campaign in Italy. Upon the death of their eldest brother William, Duke of Inner Austria in 1406, the remaining three brothers agreed about the future partition of their patrimony. In the separation
1 CONC  agreement of 1406, Ernest received Styria, Carinthia and Carniola, and jointly with his elder brother duke Leopold IV (the head of the Leopoldian line), held the guardianship over young Albert V, Duke of Austria. In 1407, conflic
1 CONC ts between Leopold and Ernest resulted in a civil war that lasted until May 1409. When Leopold died without a male heir in 1411, Ernest became the uncontested head of the Leopoldian family. In 1414, he became the last Duke to be e
1 CONC nthroned according to the traditional rite in Carinthia, and from that time on called himself Archduke. He was the first Habsburg to actually use this title, which had been invented by Rudolf IV.
1 CONT Ernest the Iron with his sons.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was made a member of the Order of the Dragon, but later became bitter with Emperor Sigismund from 1412 onwards when his brother Frederick IV, Duke of Further Austria (ruler of Tirol) was banned by the Emperor in 1417, Ernest fi
1 CONC rst attempted to gain control over Frederick's territories himself, but then came to an agreement with him and successfully defended Tirol against the Emperor's pretensions.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ernest died at Bruck an der Mur, and was buried in the Cistercian monastery of Rein. His nickname the Iron only came into use after his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and children
1 CONT
1 CONT On 14 January 1392, Ernest married his first wife Margaret of Pomerania. She was a daughter of Boguslaw V, Duke of Pomerania and his second wife Adelheid of Brunswick-Grubenhagen. They had no children. She died in 1407 or 1410 acc
1 CONC ording to contradictory necrologies.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 25 January 1412, Ernest married his second wife Cymburgis of Masovia, who was his equal in vitality and with whom he had nine children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Duke Frederick V (21 September 1415 – 19 August 1493)
1 CONT    2. Margarete (1416/17 – 12 February 1486), married on 3 June 1431 to Frederick II, Elector of Saxony
1 CONT    3. Duke Albrecht VI (18 December 1418 – 2 December 1463)
1 CONT    4. Alexander (d. 1420)
1 CONT    5. Rudolf (d. before 1424)
1 CONT    6. Katharina (1424 – 11 September 1493), married on 15 July 1447 to Charles I, Margrave of Baden-Baden
1 CONT    7. Leopold (d. before 1424)
1 CONT    8. Anna (d. 11 November 1429)
1 CONT    9. Ernst II (d. 10 August 1432)
1 CONT
1 CONT As the ruler of Inner Austria and the founder of the older Styrian Line of the Habsburg family, which, by his son Duke Frederick V survived the Albertinian-Austrian and the Tyrolean Lines, he became the ancestor of all later Habsb
1 CONC urg emperors.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT William Duke of Inner Austria
1 CONT (Styria, Carinthia and Carniola)
1 CONT 1406–1424 Succeeded by
1 CONT Frederick V &
1 CONT Albert VI
