0 @N6000000002187757349@ NOTE John, Duke of Burgundy
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Duke John I aka Jean de Valois and Jean de Bourgogne (May 28, 1371, Dijon – September 10, 1419, on the bridge of Montereau), also known as the Fearless (French: sans peur) was Duke of Burgundy from 1404 to 1419. John was the son o
1 CONC f Philip II, the Bold and Margaret III, Countess of Flanders. As heir apparent, he used the title of Count of Nevers from 1384 to 1405, when after his accession he ceded it to his brother Philip.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Family and early life 
1 CONT 2 John vs Orléans 
1 CONT 3 John vs Armagnac 
1 CONT 4 John vs the Dauphin 
1 CONT 5 External links 
1 CONT 6 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and early life
1 CONT In 1385, John married Margaret of Bavaria, daughter of Albrecht of Bavaria, Count of Holland and Hainaut, to consolidate his position in the Low Countries, after cancelling his engagement with Catherine of Valois, daughter of king
1 CONC  Charles V of France. They had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Catherine (1391–1414, Ghent) 
1 CONT Mary (1393 – October 30, 1463, Monterberg bei Kalkar). She married Adolph II, Duke of Cleves. They were the great-grandparents of Johann III, Duke of Cleves, father of Anne of Cleves who was fourth Queen consort of Henry VIII of E
1 CONC ngland. 
1 CONT Marguerite, duchess of Guyenne (1394 – February 2, 1441, Paris), married on August 30, 1404 Louis of Valois the Dauphin (heir of king Charles VI of France), then on October 10, 1422 Arthur de Richemont, the future Duke of Brittany
1 CONC
1 CONT Philip III, Duke of Burgundy (1396–1467) 
1 CONT Isabelle (d. September 18, 1412, Rouvre), married at Arras on July 22, 1406 to Olivier de Châtillon-Blois, Count of Penthièvre and Périgord 
1 CONT Jeanne (b. 1399, Bouvres), d. young 
1 CONT Anne of Burgundy (1404 – November 14, 1432, Paris), married John, Duke of Bedford 
1 CONT Agnes of Burgundy (1407 – December 1, 1476, Château de Moulins), married Charles I, Duke of Bourbon 
1 CONT John also had several illegitimate children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Before his accession to the Duchy, John was one of the principal leaders of the French forces sent to aid King Sigismund of Hungary in his war against Sultan Bayezid I. John fought in the battle of Nicopolis (September 25, 1396) w
1 CONC ith such enthusiasm and bravery that he was given the nickname of Fearless (Sans-Peur). Nevertheless he was taken prisoner and released only in the next year, against an enormous ransom paid by his father.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT John vs Orléans
1 CONT John was invested as duke of Burgundy in 1404 and almost immediately entered into open conflict against Louis of Orléans, younger brother of the increasingly mad Charles VI. Both men attempted to fill the power vacuum left by the 
1 CONC demented king.
1 CONT
1 CONT John played a game of marriages, exchanging his daughter Marguerite for Michelle of Valois, who would marry his heir Philip III. He did not overlook, however, the importance of the middle class of merchants and tradesman or the Un
1 CONC iversity of Paris.
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis tried to gain the favor of Queen Isabella, and may have become her lover. After a game of hide and seek in which his son-in-law, the Dauphin, was successively kidnapped and recovered by both parties, the Duke of Burgundy man
1 CONC aged to gain appointment by royal decree – during one of the King's "absent" periods when mental illness manifested itself – as guardian of the Dauphin and the king's children. This did not improve the relations between John and L
1 CONC ouis.
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon the two rivals descended into making open threats. Their uncle, John, Duke of Berry, secured a vow of solemn reconciliation, but three days later, on November 23, 1407 Louis was brutally assassinated in the streets of Paris. 
1 CONC He was attacked after mounting his horse by a party of men who literally amputated his arms so that he was defenseless. The order, no one doubted, had come from the Duke of Burgundy, who shortly admitted to the deed and declared i
1 CONC t to be a justifiable act of "tyrannicide". After an escape from Paris and a few skirmishes against the Orléans party, John managed to recover the king's favour. In the treaty of Chartres, signed on March 9, 1409, the king absolve
1 CONC d the Duke of Burgundy of the crime, and he and Louis's son Charles pledged a reconciliation. A later edict renewed John's guardianship of the Dauphin.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT John vs Armagnac
1 CONT Even with the Orléans dispute resolved to his favour, John would not have an easy life. Charles of Orléans gathered allies, among them Bernard VII, Count of Armagnac, to support his claims for the property that had been confiscate
1 CONC d from him. Peace was solemnly sworn in 1410, and John returned to Burgundy, and Bernard remained in Paris and reportedly shared the queen's bed. Armagnac's party was not contented with political power, and, after a series of riot
1 CONC s and attacks against the citizens, John was recalled to the capital. However, he was sent back to Burgundy in 1413.
1 CONT
1 CONT At this time king Henry V of England invaded French territory and threatened to attack Paris. John participated in the peace negotiations, but with dubious intent. Although he talked of helping his sovereign, his troops took no pa
1 CONC rt in the Battle of Agincourt (in 1415), where two of his brothers, Antoine, Duke of Brabant, and Philip II, Count of Nevers, died fighting for France.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Assassination of the Duke of Burgundy, John the Fearless, on the Bridge of Montereau, in 1419. – facsimile of a miniature in the "Chronicles" of Monstrelet, manuscript of the fifteenth century, in the Library of the Arsenal of Pa
1 CONC ris.[edit]
1 CONT John vs the Dauphin
1 CONT Two years later, John's troops set about the task of gaining Paris. On May 30, 1418, he captured the city, but not before the Dauphin, the future Charles VII of France, had escaped. John then installed himself in the city and made
1 CONC  himself protector of the King. Although not an open ally of the English, John did nothing to prevent the surrender of Rouen in 1419. With the whole of northern France in English hands and Paris occupied by Burgundy, the Dauphin t
1 CONC ried to bring about a reconciliation with John. They met in July and swore peace on the bridge of Pouilly, near Melun. On the grounds that peace was not sufficiently assured by the Pouilly meeting, a fresh interview was proposed b
1 CONC y the Dauphin to take place on September 10, 1419 on the bridge at Montereau. John of Burgundy was present with his escort for what he considered a diplomatic meeting. He was, however, assassinated by the Dauphin's companions. He 
1 CONC was later buried in Dijon.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also: Dukes of Burgundy family tree – Hundred Years' War - Dukes of Burgundy - Counts of Burgundy
1 CONT
